window.ChemistryATAR = [{"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-atoms-accounting", "title": "Atoms, ions, isotopes and weighted atomic mass", "scope": "Separate nuclear identity from charge · Weighted means need fractional abundance · Check the meaning of the result", "parent": "che11-atoms", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Atoms, ions, isotopes and weighted atomic mass</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Separate nuclear identity from charge</li><li>Weighted means need fractional abundance</li><li>Check the meaning of the result</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Atoms, ions and the three scales", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemistry connects a visible observation with a particle explanation and a symbolic representation. A flame colour is a macroscopic observation. An electron changing energy level is the particle explanation. An atomic symbol and electron configuration represent the atom symbolically. An atom contains a compact positive nucleus and electrons distributed around it. Protons have charge +1, neutrons 0 and electrons −1 in relative units. Protons and neutrons each have approximately one atomic mass unit. An electron has much smaller mass. Electrical neutrality means equal numbers of protons and electrons, not an absence of charged particles. Chemical reactions redistribute electrons and rearrange atoms without changing the nuclei.", "diagram": "atom"}, {"title": "Atomic number, mass number and ionic charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "Atomic number Z counts protons and fixes the element. Mass number A counts protons plus neutrons in one isotope. Therefore neutrons = A − Z. For an ion with signed charge q, electrons = Z − q: a 2+ ion has two fewer electrons than its neutral atom. For chlorine-37, Z = 17, so there are 17 protons and 20 neutrons. Its chloride ion has 18 electrons. Never use a decimal periodic-table atomic weight as a mass number. The decimal value describes an isotopic average, whereas a particular nucleus contains whole numbers of protons and neutrons. Write isotope symbols with mass number at upper left and atomic number at lower left.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Isotopes and relative atomic mass", "kind": "learn", "text": "Isotopes share atomic number but differ in neutron number. Their neutral atoms have the same electron configuration, so ordinary chemical behaviour is similar. Their masses and some physical properties differ. Relative atomic mass Aᵣ compares the mean mass per atom with one twelfth of a carbon-12 atom. It is a dimensionless weighted average. Multiply each isotopic mass by its fractional abundance, then add. If abundances are percentages, divide the sum by 100. Aᵣ need not equal a whole number and does not mean an individual atom contains a fractional neutron. Check that abundances total 100% and that your average lies between the isotope masses.", "diagram": "isotopes"}, {"title": "Separate nuclear identity from charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "Atomic number is the proton count and fixes the element. Mass number counts protons plus neutrons; ionic charge reflects the imbalance between protons and electrons. Losing electrons makes a cation without changing its nucleus. An isotope differs in neutron number, not ordinarily in the chemical electron arrangement of a neutral atom. In a symbol with mass, atomic number and charge, read each separately before calculating. The mass number of one isotope is not the same quantity as the weighted relative atomic mass of a sample.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Weighted means need fractional abundance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiply each isotope mass by its fractional abundance and sum. If percentages total 100, divide them by 100 before multiplying. An average must fall between the isotope masses and be nearer the more abundant isotope. Relative atomic mass is dimensionless because it compares a mean atomic mass with a reference; molar mass uses g mol⁻¹. A mass-spectrum peak position and its intensity provide different evidence: position relates to mass-to-charge ratio, while intensity can support relative abundance under the appropriate model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the meaning of the result", "kind": "learn", "text": "Using integer mass numbers gives an approximate average, whereas measured isotope masses can produce a more precise value. A fictional two-isotope calculation should state that assumption. Atomic number cannot be averaged into a new element, and an ion does not lose protons merely because it has positive charge. Explain accounting with numbers and identify the counted particle. This is more precise than giving an unexplained answer whose signs could come from an incorrect rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Isotope and ion accounting", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Isotope and ion accounting", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Weighted isotopic average", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Weighted isotopic average", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Isotope and ion accounting", "prompt": "A sulfur-34 ion has charge 2−. Determine its numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons. Explain why sulfur-32 has similar chemical properties.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Sulfur has atomic number 16, so 16 protons. [1]", "Neutrons = 34 − 16 = 18. [1]", "The 2− ion has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons. [1]", "Both neutral isotopes have the same electron configuration and valence arrangement. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Weighted isotopic average", "prompt": "An element has isotope masses 10.01 u and 11.01 u with abundances 20.0% and 80.0%. Calculate its relative atomic mass.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Convert to fractions 0.200 and 0.800. [1]", "Aᵣ = 10.01(0.200) + 11.01(0.800). [1]", "Aᵣ = 10.81, dimensionless. It lies between the isotopic masses. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-atoms-accounting-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional sample has isotopes of mass 10.0 and 11.0 with abundances 20.0% and 80.0%. Calculate its relative atomic mass. Then determine electron and neutron counts for ¹¹₅X³⁺. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Weighted mean = 10.0×0.200 + 11.0×0.800 = 10.8, dimensionless.", "[2] Atomic number 5 gives five protons; 3+ means three fewer electrons, so two electrons.", "[2] Mass number 11 gives 11−5 = six neutrons; isotopic mass and ionic charge affect different accounting."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-atoms-accounting-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why forming a 2− ion does not change an element’s atomic number, and distinguish relative atomic mass from the mass number of an isotope. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A 2− ion has gained two electrons relative to the neutral atom; the proton count remains unchanged.", "[2] Atomic number is proton count, whereas mass number is the integer proton-plus-neutron count of one isotope.", "[2] Relative atomic mass is an abundance-weighted comparison for a sample, usually not an integer and not an isotope’s particle count."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What changes when a neutral atom forms a 2− ion?", "o": ["It gains two electrons.", "It gains two protons.", "It loses two neutrons.", "Its atomic number necessarily increases by two."], "a": 0, "why": "Charge changes through electron accounting without changing the nuclear element identity."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-atoms-evidence", "title": "Atomic models and experimental evidence", "scope": "An observation can challenge a model without showing every detail · New evidence modifies rather than merely decorates a model · Evaluate a representation by what it predicts", "parent": "che11-atoms", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Atomic models and experimental evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>An observation can challenge a model without showing every detail</li><li>New evidence modifies rather than merely decorates a model</li><li>Evaluate a representation by what it predicts</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Dalton and Thomson: models meet new evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dalton used patterns in chemical combination to propose atoms and fixed whole-number ratios in compounds. His claim that all atoms of an element have identical mass needed revision after isotopes were established. Thomson showed that cathode rays were deflected by electric and magnetic fields and consisted of negative particles found in different materials. This supported electrons as subatomic components and contradicted an indivisible atom. His model placed electrons within a diffuse positive region. In an exam, connect each observation to a specific inference, then state what the evidence does not establish. Discovering electrons alone does not demonstrate a tiny nucleus.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rutherford and Chadwick: the nuclear model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Most alpha particles passed through a thin gold foil with little deflection, indicating that most atomic volume offered no concentrated obstacle. A small fraction deflected strongly or returned, supporting a very small, dense, positive nucleus containing most of the mass. Rutherford’s results did not directly locate electrons in particular energy levels. Chadwick’s evidence for neutral particles helped account for nuclear mass without adding positive charge. The modern nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Describe both the common and rare scattering observations. Saying only “particles bounced back” loses the connection between most atoms being largely empty space and mass being concentrated centrally.", "diagram": "scattering"}, {"title": "An observation can challenge a model without showing every detail", "kind": "learn", "text": "Most alpha particles passing through a thin foil supported the idea that atoms are largely empty space at the scale of their mass distribution. Rare large deflections indicated a small concentrated region of positive charge and mass, challenging Thomson’s diffuse-positive-charge model. Do not say that every particle bounced back or that scattering directly photographed orbitals. Link each observation to the feature inferred, then identify what that experiment did not determine.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "New evidence modifies rather than merely decorates a model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Thomson’s electron evidence challenged an indivisible-atom account. Rutherford’s scattering supported a nuclear structure, and Chadwick’s neutron evidence helped explain nuclear mass beyond proton count. These contributions concerned different measurements and questions. A timeline is useful only if the evidence and resulting change are explicit. Later electron models addressed energy and spatial probability beyond a simple classical planetary picture. No one experiment established every feature of the modern atom.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate a representation by what it predicts", "kind": "learn", "text": "A ball-and-shell diagram can support particle counting but implies misleading scale and fixed paths if taken literally. A model should be judged by its explanation of observations and its limits. An unusual result requires checking apparatus, background and alternatives before discarding an otherwise useful account. Scientific development combines new measurements and critical comparison, not an assumption that a newer picture must be correct because it looks more complicated.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Evidence and model limits", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Evidence and model limits", "prompt": "State two observations from Rutherford’s scattering experiment and explain the nuclear model they support.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Most particles passed through with little deflection. [1]", "This supports most atomic volume being largely empty space. [1]", "A small fraction underwent large deflections. [1]", "This supports a small dense positively charged nucleus. Fixed electron shells are not established by this experiment. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-atoms-evidence-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain how both common straight-through paths and rare large alpha-particle deflections supported Rutherford’s nuclear model. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Most particles passing through implied that most atomic volume offered little concentrated mass/positive charge.", "[2] Rare large deflections required a small concentrated positive, massive region capable of strong interaction.", "[2] Together the observations challenged diffuse positive charge; they did not determine detailed electron orbitals."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-atoms-evidence-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student says Rutherford discovered every feature of the modern atom. Evaluate the claim using one earlier and one later contribution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Thomson’s electron evidence had already challenged indivisibility and identified negative particles.", "[2] Later neutron and quantised/electron-model evidence addressed nuclear mass and electron behaviour not fixed by scattering.", "[2] Rutherford’s contribution was crucial but limited: models develop through different experiments and do not all arise from one measurement."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What did rare large deflections particularly support?", "o": ["A small concentrated region of positive charge and mass.", "Uniform positive charge throughout the entire atom.", "Every alpha particle hitting a large solid atom.", "A direct measurement of every electron orbital."], "a": 0, "why": "Large deflections required concentrated interactions; most straight paths supported mostly empty volume."}], "subtitle": "12 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-atoms-electrons", "title": "Electron configurations and ion formation", "scope": "Write the neutral configuration before adjusting charge · Orbital occupancy follows an energy model · Isoelectronic does not mean identical", "parent": "che11-atoms", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Electron configurations and ion formation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Write the neutral configuration before adjusting charge</li><li>Orbital occupancy follows an energy model</li><li>Isoelectronic does not mean identical</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Electron configurations and shell capacity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electron configurations describe the distribution of electrons among energy levels and, when required, subshells. For the first 20 elements, useful shell configurations include Na 2,8,1; Cl 2,8,7; Ca 2,8,8,2. The formula 2n² gives a shell’s maximum capacity, not the order in which every shell fills. The third shell can hold 18 electrons, but potassium and calcium begin occupying 4s before 3d fills. Shell diagrams are bookkeeping models. They do not show electrons following circular planetary tracks. The number of occupied main shells connects to the period, while main-group valence electrons connect to typical bonding capacity.", "diagram": "shells"}, {"title": "Subshell notation and ion formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The labels s, p and d identify subshells with capacities 2, 6 and 10 respectively. Sodium is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s¹. Its Na⁺ ion is 1s²2s²2p⁶ because it loses its outer 3s electron. Chlorine is 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁵ and Cl⁻ becomes 1s²2s²2p⁶3s²3p⁶. Losing electrons does not change atomic number. Main-group ions often have noble-gas configurations, but “atoms want an octet” is an informal shortcut, not a physical cause. Stability depends on the energy of the resulting system. Use electrostatic attraction and electron arrangement to explain bonding rather than giving particles human intentions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Write the neutral configuration before adjusting charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "Count electrons from atomic number for a neutral atom, then account for electrons lost or gained. Shell and subshell representations answer different levels of detail: a shell total is not a complete subshell configuration. For main-group examples, valence arrangement supports common ion formation and chemical similarities. A stable-looking outer shell is a model-based explanation of tendencies, not a purpose consciously pursued by an atom. Check the total electron count against the stated charge.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Orbital occupancy follows an energy model", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Aufbau ordering, Pauli restriction and Hund arrangement provide a practical ground-state framework within the required scope. The order of filling and the order of electron removal need care, especially for transition-metal examples. When an example involves familiar main-group ions, show which outer-shell electrons change rather than inventing a proton transfer. A configuration diagram represents occupation, not miniature planets following visible circular tracks. The syllabus’s required notation should be practised explicitly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Isoelectronic does not mean identical", "kind": "learn", "text": "Species with equal electron counts can have different nuclear charges, sizes and chemical roles. Na⁺ and Ne both have ten electrons, but one has eleven protons and positive charge while the other is neutral with ten protons. Increasing nuclear attraction across an isoelectronic set can affect radius. Do not infer equal size merely from a matching configuration. A clear comparison identifies electron count, proton count and the attraction relevant to the requested property.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "che11-atoms-electrons-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For sodium (Z = 11), write the neutral shell distribution and that of Na⁺. Compare Na⁺ with neon (Z = 10). (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Neutral sodium has 11 electrons with shell distribution 2,8,1.", "[2] Na⁺ has lost one electron, giving ten and distribution 2,8.", "[2] Na⁺ and Ne are isoelectronic but have different proton counts/charge; equal electrons do not make them the same species."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-atoms-electrons-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why an ion’s electron configuration alone is insufficient to identify its element. Use one appropriate isoelectronic comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Element identity is determined by proton count, not electron configuration alone.", "[2] For example Ne and Na⁺ both have ten electrons but have ten and eleven protons respectively.", "[2] Charge/proton information distinguishes the species and affects attraction; matching electron counts does not guarantee equal radius or chemical behaviour."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Na⁺ and Ne share which feature?", "o": ["Ten electrons.", "The same atomic number.", "The same ionic charge.", "Guaranteed identical radius."], "a": 0, "why": "They are isoelectronic but differ in proton count and charge."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-atoms-spectroscopy", "title": "Line spectra, flame tests and atomic absorption", "scope": "Absorption and emission describe opposite transitions · Calibration converts response into an amount · Combine measurements and uncertainty", "parent": "che11-atoms", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Line spectra, flame tests and atomic absorption</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Absorption and emission describe opposite transitions</li><li>Calibration converts response into an amount</li><li>Combine measurements and uncertainty</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Mass spectrometry: how the evidence is produced", "kind": "learn", "text": "A sample is converted into gas-phase ions, separated according to mass-to-charge ratio m/z and detected. In a simple elemental spectrum of singly charged ions, peak positions approximately identify isotope masses and relative peak intensities indicate relative abundance. For multiply charged ions, m/z is smaller than the ionic mass number: a 40 u ion carrying charge 2+ appears near m/z = 20. A peak is evidence of detected ions, not a picture of the atom. Do not interpret every peak in a molecular spectrum as an isotope: fragmentation may also produce smaller ions. This module uses simple elemental isotope spectra when calculating Aᵣ.", "diagram": "mass-spectrum"}, {"title": "Bohr, quantised energy and line spectra", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bohr’s model introduced allowed electron energies. An electron absorbs a photon only when the photon energy matches an allowed upward energy difference. An excited electron can later fall to a lower level and emit a photon carrying that difference. Different energy gaps produce different wavelengths, so atomic spectra contain distinct lines rather than a continuous rainbow. The model explains hydrogen’s line spectrum but fixed circular orbits do not provide an adequate general picture of many-electron atoms. Modern orbital models describe probabilities. In flame tests, heating excites atomic species and emitted visible light gives characteristic colours. Electrons release energy on downward transitions, not on the upward excitation.", "diagram": "energy-levels"}, {"title": "Flame tests, contamination and identification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Clean the test apparatus, test a known reference and compare the observed colour under consistent conditions. Sodium gives a strong yellow emission that can mask another element. Lithium is crimson, potassium lilac, calcium orange-red, barium green and copper compounds often blue-green. A colour is supporting evidence, not always a unique identification of a mixture. A convincing explanation names energy absorption, movement to higher allowed levels, return to lower levels and emission of particular wavelengths. Contamination, mixtures and weak emissions limit interpretation. Do not treat flame intensity as a reliable concentration measurement without calibration and controlled conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Atomic absorption spectroscopy", "kind": "learn", "text": "AAS atomises a prepared sample and passes light of a selected element-specific wavelength through the atomic vapour. Ground-state atoms absorb matching photons as electrons move to higher levels. A detector measures the transmitted intensity and the instrument determines absorbance. Standards of known concentration establish a calibration relationship over a validated range. Interpolate an unknown’s absorbance, then apply its dilution factor. Absorbance is logarithmically related to incident and transmitted intensity, not simply their difference. Blanks, suitable standards and repeated measurements help control contamination and uncertainty. AAS commonly measures concentration of a selected element, not an entire molecular structure.", "diagram": "aas"}, {"title": "Calibration uncertainty and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A calibration line should include a blank and relevant standards, with the unknown inside the validated range. Diluting an over-range unknown can prevent unjustified extrapolation. Sample preparation may introduce contamination or losses even if the instrument itself repeats precisely. Flame colour and AAS response are observations; naming the element is an inference supported by reference comparison. In a written answer, distinguish these roles and give specific evidence. If an absorbance is repeated without independent sample preparation, the result assesses instrumental repeatability more than the full method’s reliability.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Absorption and emission describe opposite transitions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Absorption raises an electron to a higher allowed energy state when an appropriate photon is absorbed. Emission occurs when an electron moves to a lower state and releases a photon corresponding to the energy difference. Discrete gaps produce line spectra rather than every possible colour. The gap, not the absolute energy of one level, determines photon energy. A brighter line is not automatically a higher-energy photon; intensity and wavelength describe different information.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Calibration converts response into an amount", "kind": "learn", "text": "Atomic absorption uses absorption at an element-relevant wavelength with appropriate atomisation and calibrated standards. A blank helps account for background, and standards must span a useful range. If a diluted sample is measured, multiply the diluted concentration by the dilution factor to recover the original concentration. Extrapolating well beyond the standards or ignoring contamination weakens the result. A matching signal is analytical evidence under the method, not a universal proof of the compound’s complete identity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combine measurements and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mass spectrometry and atomic absorption answer different questions: isotope/ion mass-to-charge evidence differs from calibrated elemental concentration. Flame colours can support identification but contamination and overlapping appearances limit certainty. A good evaluation proposes clean apparatus, suitable controls and repeat measurements tied to the actual problem. Repeating a contaminated preparation may reproduce the same bias. Distinguish random scatter from systematic calibration error and state how the proposed protection improves the inference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: AAS and dilution", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: AAS and dilution", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Emission reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: AAS investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "AAS and dilution", "prompt": "Standards give absorbance 0.100 at 2.00 mg L⁻¹ and 0.300 at 6.00 mg L⁻¹. A 10.0-fold diluted sample gives 0.200. Assume linear calibration. Find the original concentration and name one reliability improvement.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The absorbance is halfway between the standards. [1]", "The diluted concentration is 4.00 mg L⁻¹. [1]", "Original concentration = 4.00 × 10.0 = 40.0 mg L⁻¹. [1]", "Repeat sample preparation and measurement and compare results, or use several standards spanning the unknown. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Emission reasoning", "prompt": "Explain the origin of a line in an atomic emission spectrum and why an element produces several separate lines.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["An excited electron falls from a higher allowed energy level to a lower one. [1]", "A photon carries the energy difference. [1]", "Different permitted transitions have different energy gaps. [1]", "Each gap corresponds to a particular wavelength, producing distinct lines. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "AAS investigation", "prompt": "A diluted water sample lies above the highest AAS calibration standard. A student extrapolates and reports an exact concentration. Evaluate the method, explain a better procedure and identify why an atomisation measurement does not reveal the full original molecular structure.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Extrapolation beyond the validated range is not justified. [1]", "Dilute the sample to bring its response within the standard range. [1]", "Use a blank and several relevant standards. [1]", "Repeat preparation and measurement to assess variation. [1]", "Apply the additional dilution factor to the interpolated result. [1]", "AAS measures selected atomic absorption after atomisation, not intact molecular connectivity. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-atoms-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-atoms-spectroscopy-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A sample diluted from 10.00 mL to 100.0 mL gives an AAS concentration of 2.40 mg L⁻¹. Calculate the original concentration and explain why calibration standards are needed. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Dilution factor = 100.0/10.00 = 10.00.", "[2] Original concentration = 2.40×10.00 = 24.0 mg L⁻¹.", "[2] Standards relate instrument response to known concentration within an appropriate range; a blank/background and method suitability remain important."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-atoms-spectroscopy-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare absorption and emission line processes and evaluate a flame-test claim that colour alone proves the complete identity of a compound. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Absorption promotes electrons using appropriate photon energy; emission releases photons on lower-energy transitions.", "[2] Quantised energy gaps explain characteristic lines, not a continuous range or intensity equalling photon energy.", "[2] Flame colours can overlap or be contaminated and usually provide limited elemental evidence, not full compound identification; use complementary evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "An electron emits a photon when it does what?", "o": ["Moves to a lower allowed energy state.", "Moves upwards without absorbing energy.", "Changes the atom’s proton count.", "Produces any wavelength regardless of the energy gap."], "a": 0, "why": "Emission photon energy corresponds to the difference between allowed states."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-atoms-periodicity", "title": "Periodicity and ionisation-energy evidence", "scope": "Nuclear attraction is balanced against distance and shielding · Exceptions reveal subshell and pairing effects · Successive energies reveal a change in shell", "parent": "che11-atoms", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Periodicity and ionisation-energy evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Nuclear attraction is balanced against distance and shielding</li><li>Exceptions reveal subshell and pairing effects</li><li>Successive energies reveal a change in shell</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Periodic table: the organising evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Elements appear in increasing atomic number. Across a period the same main energy level fills while nuclear charge increases. Main-group elements within a group share a characteristic number of valence electrons and therefore similar chemical patterns. Group 1 atoms commonly form +1 ions, Group 2 +2, Group 13 +3, Group 16 −2 and Group 17 −1. Group 14 often shares electrons rather than forming simple ±4 monatomic ions. Valency describes combining capacity and is not automatically equal to an ionic charge. Noble gases have filled outer shells and low ordinary reactivity. Periodicity follows repeating electron arrangements, not repeating mass numbers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Atomic radius across and down", "kind": "learn", "text": "Across Period 3, nuclear charge increases while electrons enter the same main shell. Shielding by inner shells changes relatively little, so effective attraction on outer electrons increases and atomic radius generally decreases. Down a main group, occupied shells increase. Outer electrons lie farther from the nucleus and experience greater shielding, so radius increases despite more protons. A full explanation balances nuclear charge, distance and shielding. “More protons means smaller atoms” is incomplete when comparing atoms in different periods. When comparing isoelectronic ions, electron count is the same and the ion with more protons generally has the smaller radius.", "diagram": "periodic-trends"}, {"title": "First ionisation energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "First ionisation energy is the energy required to remove one electron from each gaseous atom in one mole of gaseous atoms: Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻. It is positive because attraction must be overcome. It generally increases across a period and decreases down a group. Period 3 contains departures: Al’s outer 3p electron is easier to remove than Mg’s 3s electron, and pairing repulsion makes an electron in S easier to remove than in P. Explain these comparisons through subshell energy or electron pairing. Do not claim the trend is perfectly monotonic. A very large jump in successive ionisation energies can reveal the number of valence electrons.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Electronegativity and bonding predictions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electronegativity describes an atom’s tendency to attract shared electrons in a bond. It generally increases across a period and decreases down a group because nuclear attraction, shielding and distance change. It differs from ionisation energy, which concerns removing an electron from a gaseous atom. An electronegativity difference creates unequal sharing and partial charges in a polar covalent bond. A partial charge is not a full ionic charge. Whether a molecule is polar also depends on geometry. Noble-gas electronegativity values are commonly omitted in basic tables. Use a provided data table consistently rather than mixing numerical scales.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Successive ionisation evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Successive ionisation energies increase as more electrons are removed because the remaining electrons experience a more positive ion. A particularly large jump can indicate removal begins from an inner shell after valence electrons are exhausted. For a main-group atom with a jump between the second and third removals, two valence electrons are a plausible inference. The conclusion depends on identifying the sequence correctly, not choosing the biggest raw number. State the electron arrangement inferred and connect the jump to closer, more strongly attracted inner electrons. Successive removal involves increasingly charged gaseous ions, unlike first ionisation from neutral atoms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Nuclear attraction is balanced against distance and shielding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Across a main-group period the proton count rises while electrons are added to the same principal shell, so increased effective nuclear attraction generally reduces radius and raises first ionisation energy. Down a group the outer shell is farther from the nucleus and more shielded, commonly increasing radius and reducing first ionisation energy. Explain the balance, not simply the direction of the arrow. Nuclear charge alone cannot explain both trends without distance and shielding.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Exceptions reveal subshell and pairing effects", "kind": "learn", "text": "First ionisation energy does not rise perfectly smoothly across every period. Subshell energy and electron pairing can create departures from the broad trend. A measured pattern should therefore be explained using the electron arrangement rather than denied because it contradicts a memorised line. Electronegativity concerns attraction for bonding electrons, whereas ionisation energy concerns removing an electron from a gaseous species. They are related to attraction but are not the same experimental quantity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Successive energies reveal a change in shell", "kind": "learn", "text": "A large jump after several electrons have been removed indicates that the next removal involves a much more strongly bound electron, often from an inner shell in a main-group interpretation. Identify where the jump occurs and connect it with the original valence count, with appropriate scope limits. Values are energies per mole of the specified removal process, not numbers of electrons. Use the supplied sequence and avoid assigning a group merely from the largest absolute value without considering its position.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A periodic comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A periodic comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A periodic comparison", "prompt": "Explain why chlorine has a smaller atomic radius and a greater first ionisation energy than sodium.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Both atoms have three occupied main shells. [1]", "Chlorine has greater nuclear charge with similar inner-shell shielding. [1]", "The greater effective attraction draws outer electrons closer, decreasing radius. [1]", "More energy is required to remove an outer electron against this attraction. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-atoms-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-atoms-periodicity-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional main-group atom has successive ionisation energies 600, 1,200, 1,900 and 9,500 kJ mol⁻¹. Infer the likely valence-electron count and explain the jump. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The large jump occurs between removal of the third and fourth electrons.", "[2] This supports three outer/valence electrons in the stated main-group interpretation.", "[2] The fourth removal involves a more strongly attracted inner-shell electron with less shielding/shorter distance, not a sudden change in atomic number."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-atoms-periodicity-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the general radius trend across a period and down a group, then distinguish electronegativity from first ionisation energy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Across a period increased effective nuclear attraction in the same principal shell generally decreases radius.", "[2] Down a group extra shells/distance and shielding generally increase radius despite more protons.", "[2] Electronegativity concerns bonding-electron attraction; first ionisation energy removes one electron from a gaseous atom and is a different quantity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "A large jump after the third successive removal suggests what?", "o": ["Three outer electrons in a suitable main-group interpretation.", "Three neutrons in every isotope.", "A nucleus gains protons during ionisation.", "The fourth electron is automatically farther from the nucleus."], "a": 0, "why": "A jump indicates a change to more strongly bound electrons after the outer set is removed."}, {"q": "Which changes when a neutral sodium atom forms Na⁺?", "o": ["Proton number", "Electron number", "Atomic number", "Neutron number"], "a": 1, "why": "Na loses one outer electron; its nucleus is unchanged."}, {"q": "Aᵣ is best described as", "o": ["The mass number of every atom", "A weighted isotopic average relative to carbon-12", "The neutron count", "A mass measured in grams"], "a": 1, "why": "Aᵣ is a dimensionless relative average."}, {"q": "An emission photon is produced when an electron", "o": ["Falls to a lower energy level", "Moves to a higher level", "Becomes a neutron", "Increases atomic number"], "a": 0, "why": "The photon carries the decrease in atomic electronic energy."}, {"q": "Down Group 1 atomic radius generally", "o": ["Decreases", "Increases", "Stays constant", "Becomes zero"], "a": 1, "why": "Additional occupied shells and shielding increase radius."}, {"q": "Which can reveal isotopic abundance?", "o": ["A simple elemental mass spectrum", "A pH meter", "A conductivity reading alone", "A thermometer"], "a": 0, "why": "Peak positions and relative intensities support isotope analysis."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-materials-separation", "title": "Substances, mixtures and separation methods", "scope": "Match a method to a property difference · A sequence must preserve the desired material · Purity needs suitable evidence", "parent": "che11-materials", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Substances, mixtures and separation methods</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Match a method to a property difference</li><li>A sequence must preserve the desired material</li><li>Purity needs suitable evidence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Pure substances and mixtures", "kind": "learn", "text": "A pure element contains one type of atom. A pure compound contains elements chemically combined in a fixed proportion. A mixture contains substances physically combined in variable proportions. Oxygen gas contains O₂ molecules and remains an element. A sodium chloride crystal is a compound built from ions, with a 1:1 ratio rather than discrete NaCl molecules. Air is a mixture of gases. A sample can be homogeneous and still be a mixture, such as salt solution. Fixed composition and characteristic properties support purity; appearance alone does not establish it. Physical separation exploits property differences without requiring chemical conversion.", "diagram": "mixtures"}, {"title": "Separation through particle size and solubility", "kind": "learn", "text": "Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid because particles cannot pass through suitable pores. Dissolved ions are far smaller and pass with the solvent. To separate sand and salt, add water to dissolve salt, filter the sand, wash its residue and evaporate or crystallise salt from the filtrate. Each step depends on a stated property difference. Washing removes solution trapped in a residue, improving purity but potentially affecting recovery if the desired solid is slightly soluble. Explain which material is residue and which is filtrate. A method earns scientific credit through the property that makes it work, not merely naming equipment.", "diagram": "filtration"}, {"title": "Distillation, density and chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "Distillation vaporises a more volatile component and condenses its vapour. Fractional distillation improves separation of miscible liquids with closer boiling points through repeated vaporisation and condensation. A separating funnel separates immiscible liquids by density and distinct phases, not dissolved substances within one phase. Chromatography separates components by different interactions with stationary and mobile phases. Choose a method using the actual mixture: salt water, ethanol–water and oil–water require different approaches. Some mixtures need a sequence. A narrow melting range or a single chromatographic spot can support purity, but one test alone may fail to detect impurities with similar properties.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Match a method to a property difference", "kind": "learn", "text": "Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a fluid through particle-size differences. Evaporation can recover a dissolved nonvolatile solute; distillation can collect a volatile component through vaporisation and condensation. A separating funnel uses immiscibility and density differences. None works simply because the mixture has two components: the relevant physical difference must exist. Dissolved salt passes through ordinary filter paper with water, so filtering is not a method for removing its individual dissolved ions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A sequence must preserve the desired material", "kind": "learn", "text": "For salt, sand and water, filter to separate sand, then recover dissolved salt by removing water or collect water by appropriate distillation. State which fraction contains each component after each stage. Washing and drying affect purity and recovered mass. Separation is based on physical properties and ordinarily does not establish a chemical reaction. A diagram should label filtrate, residue and collected product rather than using the word substance for every fraction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Purity needs suitable evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "A clear liquid can contain several dissolved species, and one visible phase does not establish a pure substance. Chromatographic spots, boiling behaviour or other evidence can support purity under specified conditions but are not unlimited proofs. Material losses and contamination affect yield and interpretation. An evaluation should trace where a component could be lost or remain mixed, then propose a targeted technique or check. Repeating an unsuitable separation does not overcome the wrong property assumption.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A separation sequence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A separation sequence", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A separation sequence", "prompt": "Describe how to obtain dry sand and dry salt from a mixture of the two.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Add water to dissolve salt while sand remains insoluble. [1]", "Filter, retaining sand as residue. [1]", "Wash the sand to remove trapped salt solution. [1]", "Dry the washed sand. [1]", "Evaporate water or crystallise salt from the filtrate, then dry the recovered crystals. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-materials-separation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Describe how to recover sand and salt separately from an aqueous mixture of both. Explain why filtration alone cannot recover the dissolved salt. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Filter to retain insoluble sand as residue, washing/drying appropriately for recovery.", "[2] Salt remains in the filtrate as dissolved ions and can be recovered by appropriate evaporation/crystallisation.", "[2] Dissolved ions pass through ordinary filter paper; particle-size filtration does not remove them as a solid residue."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-materials-separation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A clear collected liquid is called pure solely because it has no visible particles. Evaluate the claim and propose one relevant check. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Dissolved substances may be invisible, so clarity is not proof of chemical purity.", "[2] Use an appropriate analytical comparison such as chromatography or suitable boiling/other property evidence.", "[2] State the method’s limits and control contamination; one observation or one spot does not rule out every impurity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which component remains in the filtrate of salt and sand in water?", "o": ["Dissolved salt.", "All sand necessarily passes through.", "Salt is removed because each ion is visibly large.", "The filtrate must be a pure element."], "a": 0, "why": "Ordinary filtration retains insoluble particles; dissolved ions remain in the liquid."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-materials-nano", "title": "Nanomaterials: surface area, evidence and applications", "scope": "Geometry explains surface-area change · Surface effects support both uses and concerns · Evaluate the comparison fairly", "parent": "che11-materials", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Nanomaterials: surface area, evidence and applications</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Geometry explains surface-area change</li><li>Surface effects support both uses and concerns</li><li>Evaluate the comparison fairly</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Nanomaterials and surface area", "kind": "learn", "text": "Nanomaterials contain particles approximately 1–100 nm in size. As size decreases, the surface-area-to-volume ratio increases, so a larger proportion of atoms is near the surface. A cube of side L has area 6L², volume L³ and ratio 6/L. Surface interactions can therefore contribute more strongly to reactivity, catalysis and adsorption. Nanoscale optical or electrical properties may also differ through size-dependent electronic behaviour. A nanoparticle is not a new element simply because it is small. Particle diagrams exaggerate scale. Compare equal total masses when judging the effect of dividing a material into smaller particles.", "diagram": "nano"}, {"title": "Nanotechnology: benefits, evidence and risk", "kind": "learn", "text": "A nanoscale catalyst may improve reaction rate by exposing more active sites, and nanomaterials can alter coatings, sensors or drug delivery. Benefits must be judged against evidence about exposure, persistence and effects on organisms. A bulk material’s safety record does not automatically establish a nanoparticle’s safety. Compare dose, particle size, surface chemistry and routes of exposure. A reasoned evaluation states a benefit, a plausible concern, the evidence needed and a practical control. Distinguish an observed effect from a hypothesis and a commercial claim. Science informs a decision, while economic and ethical priorities also influence the final choice.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Geometry explains surface-area change", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a cube, surface area is 6l² and volume is l³, so surface-area-to-volume ratio is 6/l. Smaller particles therefore expose more surface relative to their amount. Splitting a fixed volume into many small pieces increases total exposed area without creating additional mass. Be clear whether a question asks about one particle or a fixed mass of material. A ratio has inverse-length units, not simply square units or a percentage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Surface effects support both uses and concerns", "kind": "learn", "text": "Greater accessible surface can increase the opportunity for surface reactions, useful in some catalysts and materials. Nanometre-scale properties can also depend on composition, shape and other effects, so surface area alone does not predict every performance measure. Increased reactivity or mobility can be relevant to exposure assessment, but nano does not mean automatically toxic or automatically safe. Evidence must concern the specific material, dose, route and conditions rather than a general size label.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate the comparison fairly", "kind": "learn", "text": "To test a size effect, keep composition, mass, temperature and relevant exposure conditions comparable. Agglomeration can reduce accessible surface even when nominal particle size is small. A promotional claim should separate measured improvement from extrapolated benefits and include uncertainties or trade-offs. A useful recommendation names the intended application and compares evidence for efficiency, resource use and risk, without implying that scientific data alone settle every social preference.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Nanoparticle calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Nanoparticle calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Nanoparticle calculation", "prompt": "Compare surface-area-to-volume ratios of cubes with sides 100 nm and 20 nm. Explain one catalytic implication.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["SA/V = 6/L. [1]", "Ratios are 0.060 nm⁻¹ and 0.300 nm⁻¹. [1]", "The smaller cube has five times the ratio. [1]", "For equal material amount, more exposed surface can provide more catalytic active sites. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-materials-nano-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Calculate surface-area-to-volume ratios for cubes of side 1.0 mm and 0.10 mm, then explain the change. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] SA/V = 6/l, so the 1.0 mm cube has 6.0 mm⁻¹.", "[2] The 0.10 mm cube has 60 mm⁻¹, ten times larger.", "[2] Smaller size increases exposed surface relative to volume; it does not create mass or prove every property improves."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-materials-nano-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A manufacturer claims its nanoparticles are safer and more effective simply because they are small. Evaluate the claim and propose a fair performance comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Small size can increase accessible surface, which may affect reactivity/performance but is not a universal benefit.", "[2] Safety depends on specific composition, exposure, dose and conditions; small size alone proves neither safety nor harm.", "[2] Compare equal amounts/composition under controlled conditions and evaluate accessible area/agglomeration and relevant risk evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "For a cube, what happens to SA/V when side length is halved?", "o": ["It doubles.", "It halves.", "It stays unchanged.", "The mass necessarily doubles."], "a": 0, "why": "SA/V = 6/l, so the ratio is inversely proportional to side length."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-materials-ionic-metallic", "title": "Ionic and metallic bonding: structure and properties", "scope": "Ionic formulae express charge balance · Mobile charge explains conductivity · Different deformation responses follow the structure", "parent": "che11-materials", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ionic and metallic bonding: structure and properties</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Ionic formulae express charge balance</li><li>Mobile charge explains conductivity</li><li>Different deformation responses follow the structure</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Electrostatic attraction: the basis of bonding", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemical bonds involve electrostatic attraction between positive and negative charge. Ionic bonding is attraction between oppositely charged ions throughout a lattice. Metallic bonding is attraction between positive atomic cores and delocalised electrons. Covalent bonding is attraction between shared electron density and the nuclei of bonded atoms. Electron transfer explains the formation of ions, but transfer itself is not the attractive force holding a crystal together. Bonding lowers the system’s energy relative to appropriately separated particles. Simple diagrams distinguish local electron sharing from extended structures. A line in a structural formula represents a shared electron pair, not a tiny solid rod.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ionic formulae and polyatomic ions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A neutral ionic formula has total positive charge equal to total negative charge. Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ give MgCl₂. Al³⁺ and O²⁻ give Al₂O₃, using the smallest whole-number ratio. Calcium and nitrate give Ca(NO₃)₂; parentheses show two entire nitrate groups. A sulfate ion remains SO₄²⁻ during ordinary formula construction. Never alter internal subscripts to balance charge. Ionic formulae express ratios in a large lattice, not molecule counts. Common ions include NH₄⁺, OH⁻, NO₃⁻, CO₃²⁻, HCO₃⁻, SO₄²⁻ and PO₄³⁻. A Roman numeral specifies a variable metal oxidation state, as in iron(III) chloride, FeCl₃.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ionic lattices and high melting point", "kind": "learn", "text": "Each ion in an ionic crystal interacts with oppositely charged neighbours in a three-dimensional regular arrangement. Melting requires enough energy to disrupt this ordered network of strong electrostatic attractions, so ionic substances commonly have high melting points. A flat grid represents only a slice of the lattice. Do not describe the solid as many isolated cation–anion pairs. Comparisons between ionic melting points also depend on ionic charges, sizes and lattice arrangement. A greater charge and shorter separation can strengthen attraction, but a full ranking may require data. Dissolving involves both separation of lattice ions and interactions with the solvent.", "diagram": "ionic"}, {"title": "Ionic conductivity and brittleness", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a solid ionic crystal, ions occupy fixed positions and cannot migrate through the material, so it does not conduct electricity through mobile ions. In a molten ionic substance or a sufficiently soluble aqueous solution, ions can move and carry charge. Do not say the ions become electrons or the solid contains no charge. When a force shifts lattice layers, ions of like charge may become adjacent and repel, causing fracture. This explains brittleness. Conductivity requires mobile charged particles and an appropriate pathway. A conductivity answer should identify the carrier and whether it can move in the specified physical state.", "diagram": "ionic-shift"}, {"title": "Metallic bonding and conductivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "A metal contains positive atomic cores in an extended arrangement and delocalised valence electrons that can move through the structure. An applied potential difference causes net electron movement and electrical conduction. Energy also transfers through mobile electrons and lattice vibrations, contributing to thermal conductivity. The model represents collective attraction, not one fixed electron pair connecting each neighbour. Many metals have high melting points because metallic attraction is strong, but there are important exceptions such as mercury. Avoid an absolute statement that all metals have high melting points. A diagram’s negative dots represent electrons, not anions dissolved between metal atoms.", "diagram": "metallic"}, {"title": "Malleability, ductility and alloys", "kind": "learn", "text": "Metallic attraction is not tied to one directional pair of atoms. Layers of positive cores can move while attraction to delocalised electrons maintains cohesion. Metals can therefore be hammered into sheets or drawn into wires without behaving like brittle ionic crystals. Alloying introduces atoms of different sizes that distort the regular arrangement and obstruct easy movement of layers. This often increases hardness or strength, although exact properties depend on composition and structure. Explain an alloy’s property through the altered structure rather than saying it is simply “stronger bonding.” Alloys are typically mixtures, whereas an ionic compound has a fixed chemical composition.", "diagram": "alloy"}, {"title": "Valency, charge and compound names", "kind": "learn", "text": "Valency counts combining capacity in the relevant model, while oxidation state and ionic charge are different ideas. Magnesium commonly gives Mg²⁺, requiring two chloride ions in MgCl₂. Nitrogen in ammonia makes three N–H bonds but is not a free N³⁺ ion. Roman numerals distinguish a metal oxidation state, as in copper(II) oxide CuO versus copper(I) oxide Cu₂O. In an ionic name, state the cation then anion and retain a polyatomic ion’s identity. In a covalent structural argument, count shared pairs rather than treating every atom as a fully separated ion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ionic formulae express charge balance", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ionic solid consists of a lattice rather than separate neutral molecules. Its formula gives the simplest whole-number ratio balancing ion charges. Ca²⁺ and NO₃⁻ combine as Ca(NO₃)₂, with brackets preserving the polyatomic ion. Changing a coefficient or subscript within the nitrate changes the represented species. The formula does not describe a two-ion covalent bond or a molecule floating in the lattice. Name the ions and balance their total charge before writing the ratio.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Mobile charge explains conductivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Solid ionic lattices have ions fixed in positions and generally do not conduct through freely moving ions. Molten material or a suitable aqueous solution permits ionic movement. Metals conduct through mobile delocalised electrons in a structure of positive metal centres; they do not require melting to free their charge carriers. Both explanations involve attraction, but the moving species differs. State the carrier and whether it can move in the specified state rather than attributing conductivity vaguely to strong bonds.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Different deformation responses follow the structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Sliding ionic layers can bring like charges adjacent, causing repulsion and brittle fracture. Metallic layers can move while non-directional attraction between positive centres and delocalised electrons remains, supporting malleability. Alloy atoms can disrupt regular layer movement and alter properties; the exact effect depends on composition and structure. A high melting point alone does not identify a substance because several structures can share it. Combine conductivity, state and deformation evidence with the proposed model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reference: required cation names and formulae", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Use charge balance to construct an ionic formula. A Roman numeral distinguishes the metal oxidation state. These names/formulae cover the cation set specified in the Year 11 syllabus appendix.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ammonium</td><td>NH₄⁺</td></tr><tr><td>caesium</td><td>Cs⁺</td></tr><tr><td>copper(I)</td><td>Cu⁺</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogen</td><td>H⁺</td></tr><tr><td>lithium</td><td>Li⁺</td></tr><tr><td>potassium</td><td>K⁺</td></tr><tr><td>rubidium</td><td>Rb⁺</td></tr><tr><td>silver</td><td>Ag⁺</td></tr><tr><td>sodium</td><td>Na⁺</td></tr><tr><td>barium</td><td>Ba²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>calcium</td><td>Ca²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>cobalt(II)</td><td>Co²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>copper(II)</td><td>Cu²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>iron(II)</td><td>Fe²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>lead(II)</td><td>Pb²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>magnesium</td><td>Mg²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>manganese(II)</td><td>Mn²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>nickel(II)</td><td>Ni²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>strontium</td><td>Sr²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>zinc</td><td>Zn²⁺</td></tr><tr><td>aluminium</td><td>Al³⁺</td></tr><tr><td>chromium(III)</td><td>Cr³⁺</td></tr><tr><td>iron(III)</td><td>Fe³⁺</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Reference: required anions with charge 1−", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Retain each polyatomic ion’s internal formula. A negative charge indicates the whole ion’s electron excess, not an extra atom. Use parentheses when more than one polyatomic group is required.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>bromide</td><td>Br⁻</td></tr><tr><td>chloride</td><td>Cl⁻</td></tr><tr><td>cyanide</td><td>CN⁻</td></tr><tr><td>dihydrogenphosphate</td><td>H₂PO₄⁻</td></tr><tr><td>ethanoate (acetate)</td><td>CH₃COO⁻</td></tr><tr><td>fluoride</td><td>F⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogencarbonate</td><td>HCO₃⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogensulfate</td><td>HSO₄⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydroxide</td><td>OH⁻</td></tr><tr><td>iodide</td><td>I⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitrate</td><td>NO₃⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitrite</td><td>NO₂⁻</td></tr><tr><td>permanganate</td><td>MnO₄⁻</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Reference: required anions with charges 2− and 3−", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>Distinguish sulfate from sulfite, and nitrate from nitrite: a one-oxygen difference changes the ion’s identity. Hydrogenphosphate HPO₄²⁻ differs from dihydrogenphosphate H₂PO₄⁻.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>carbonate</td><td>CO₃²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>chromate</td><td>CrO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>dichromate</td><td>Cr₂O₇²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogenphosphate</td><td>HPO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>oxalate</td><td>C₂O₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>oxide</td><td>O²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfate</td><td>SO₄²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfide</td><td>S²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>sulfite</td><td>SO₃²⁻</td></tr><tr><td>nitride</td><td>N³⁻</td></tr><tr><td>phosphate</td><td>PO₄³⁻</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Ionic formula construction", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Ionic formula construction", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Structure from observations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Structure from observations", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Unfamiliar structure evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Ionic formula construction", "prompt": "Write formulae for aluminium sulfate and calcium phosphate, showing how charges balance.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻ require 2:3 to balance +6 and −6. [1]", "Al₂(SO₄)₃. [1]", "Ca²⁺ and PO₄³⁻ require 3:2 to balance +6 and −6. [1]", "Ca₃(PO₄)₂. Internal ion subscripts remain unchanged. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Structure from observations", "prompt": "A solid has a high melting point, is brittle, does not conduct when solid and conducts when molten. Identify its likely bonding and explain the conductivity change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ionic bonding is consistent with the full evidence set. [1]", "The solid contains charged ions fixed in a lattice. [1]", "Melting disrupts the ordered lattice, allowing ions to move. [1]", "Mobile ions carry electrical charge through the liquid. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-materials-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Unfamiliar structure evidence", "prompt": "Solid X has a high melting point and conducts as a solid. Solid Y also melts at high temperature but conducts only when molten. Propose a bonding model for each, explain the charge carriers and identify why high melting point alone is insufficient.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["X is consistent with metallic structure, though graphite is an important alternative requiring more evidence. [1]", "Mobile delocalised electrons explain solid conduction in these possible models. [1]", "Y is consistent with an ionic lattice. [1]", "Its solid ions cannot migrate. [1]", "Molten ions can move and conduct. [1]", "High melting point can arise from ionic, metallic or network bonding, so the full evidence set matters. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-materials-ionic-metallic-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "An unknown solid is brittle, does not conduct as a solid but conducts when molten. Propose a structure and explain all three observations. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] An ionic lattice is consistent with fixed ions preventing solid-state ionic conduction.", "[2] In the melt ions are mobile charge carriers, enabling conduction.", "[2] Layer displacement can place like charges adjacent and cause repulsion/fracture; observations support but do not universally prove identity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-materials-ionic-metallic-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Write the formula for aluminium sulfate from Al³⁺ and SO₄²⁻, then compare metallic and ionic solid conductivity. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Balance +6 and −6 using two Al³⁺ and three SO₄²⁻: Al₂(SO₄)₃.", "[2] Metals have mobile delocalised electrons that can carry charge in the solid.", "[2] Ionic solids generally have fixed ions and lack mobile ionic carriers; melting/appropriate dissolution changes their mobility."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why does a typical metal conduct as a solid?", "o": ["Its delocalised electrons can move through the structure.", "Its positive centres freely dissolve without a solvent.", "Every lattice contains separate neutral molecules.", "Ionic charge balance creates moving neutrons."], "a": 0, "why": "The relevant carriers in metallic conduction are delocalised electrons."}], "subtitle": "24 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-materials-covalent", "title": "Molecular substances, networks and carbon allotropes", "scope": "Identify what is separated during a physical change · Carbon allotropes share an element but differ in structure · Properties provide converging evidence", "parent": "che11-materials", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Molecular substances, networks and carbon allotropes</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Identify what is separated during a physical change</li><li>Carbon allotropes share an element but differ in structure</li><li>Properties provide converging evidence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Covalent molecular substances", "kind": "learn", "text": "Within a molecule, shared electron pairs are attracted to both bonded nuclei. Between separate molecules, intermolecular attractions act. Melting or boiling a molecular substance generally overcomes enough intermolecular attraction for particles to rearrange or separate; it does not normally break all covalent bonds inside each molecule. Molecular substances often have lower melting points than networks and lack mobile charged particles, so they commonly do not conduct in solid or liquid states. Some molecular substances ionise when reacting with water, producing a conducting solution. Describe the actual state and chemistry rather than assuming every molecular solution is a non-electrolyte.", "diagram": "molecular"}, {"title": "Covalent networks and carbon allotropes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diamond has a three-dimensional network in which each carbon bonds to four neighbours. Strong covalent bonding throughout the network explains hardness and very high thermal stability. It lacks mobile charged particles for ordinary electrical conduction. Graphite has sheets with each carbon bonded to three neighbours and delocalised electrons within the sheets. It conducts along the layers, and relatively weak attraction between layers permits sliding. Fullerenes contain discrete cage molecules, so forces between cages differ from diamond’s continuous network. Allotropes are different structures of the same element, not isotopes. A model must distinguish intralayer bonds from interlayer attraction.", "diagram": "carbon"}, {"title": "Structure–property explanations that earn marks", "kind": "learn", "text": "A complete explanation follows structure, force or carrier, then observed property. For sodium chloride: an extended lattice of oppositely charged ions; strong electrostatic attraction; substantial energy needed to melt. For conductivity: mobile ions in the melt carry charge, while solid ions are fixed. For graphite: delocalised electrons in sheets permit conduction; weak interlayer forces permit sliding. “Strong bonds” alone omits the structure and often names the wrong force. Use unfamiliar data to identify a plausible structure, then check all properties together. One property such as high melting point cannot uniquely distinguish an ionic crystal from a covalent network.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A structure–property comparison table", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ionic: extended ions; solid ions fixed; molten ions mobile; generally high melting point and brittleness. Metallic: positive cores plus delocalised electrons; solid conduction and layer movement supported by collective attraction. Molecular: discrete molecules; intermolecular forces determine phase separation; ordinary neat substances lack mobile ions or electrons. Network: extended covalent bonds; generally high thermal stability, with conductivity dependent on electronic structure. Graphite is an important conducting network exception. Apply all observations before choosing a model. A high melting point and insolubility alone do not uniquely identify one class.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Identify what is separated during a physical change", "kind": "learn", "text": "A covalent molecular substance contains discrete molecules with strong bonds within them and attractions between them. Boiling usually overcomes intermolecular attractions without breaking each molecule’s covalent bonds. A covalent network has extended bonding, so substantial structural disruption requires breaking strong covalent connections. This distinction explains very different melting or boiling behaviour even though both materials involve covalent bonds. Use the represented particles and the particular change instead of saying all covalent substances melt easily.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Carbon allotropes share an element but differ in structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Diamond has an extended three-dimensional covalent structure, with each carbon bonded to four others in the ideal model. Graphite has bonded layers and delocalised electrons, with weaker attractions between layers. Graphite can conduct in relevant directions and layers can slide; diamond lacks the same mobile electron system under ordinary conditions. The difference is arrangement and bonding, not different atomic numbers. A drawing should distinguish connections within a layer from attraction between layers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Properties provide converging evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Low boiling point and poor electrical conductivity are consistent with many molecular substances but do not identify one molecule. High hardness and poor conduction can support a network interpretation, while graphite shows why a universal network-does-not-conduct rule fails. Explain each observation with the model and consider exceptions. A structure–property answer should name the structure, attraction or carrier, energy/mobility consequence and observed property in a connected chain.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Diamond and graphite", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Melting versus bond breaking", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Diamond and graphite", "prompt": "Explain why graphite conducts electricity and is soft while diamond does not ordinarily conduct and is hard.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Graphite contains delocalised electrons within sheets. [1]", "These mobile electrons carry charge. [1]", "Weak attractions between sheets permit sliding. [1]", "Diamond forms a continuous three-dimensional covalent network. [1]", "Strong bonds throughout resist deformation. [1]", "Its electrons are localised in bonds, leaving no mobile charge carriers. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Melting versus bond breaking", "prompt": "A student says boiling methane breaks C–H bonds. Evaluate the statement and identify the relevant attraction.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The statement confuses bonds within molecules with forces between molecules. [1]", "Methane molecules remain intact during ordinary boiling. [1]", "Energy overcomes intermolecular dispersion attractions sufficiently for vaporisation. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-materials-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-materials-covalent-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare boiling a molecular liquid with disrupting a covalent network, specifying the attractions or bonds affected. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A molecular liquid has strong intramolecular bonds but attractions between discrete molecules.", "[2] Boiling primarily overcomes intermolecular attractions, leaving molecular covalent structure intact.", "[2] Disrupting an extended network requires breaking covalent connections, generally requiring much more energy; avoid a universal rule for all materials."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-materials-covalent-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why graphite can conduct and act as a lubricant while diamond has different ordinary properties. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Graphite’s layers contain delocalised electrons that support conductivity in relevant directions.", "[2] Weaker interlayer attractions permit layer sliding, supporting lubrication.", "[2] Diamond’s three-dimensional covalent network resists deformation and does not have graphite’s ordinary mobile-electron structure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is usually overcome when a molecular substance boils?", "o": ["Intermolecular attractions.", "Every covalent bond within every molecule.", "The nucleus of each atom.", "The atomic-number identity of the element."], "a": 0, "why": "Boiling separates molecules; it does not ordinarily decompose them into atoms."}, {"q": "Molten NaCl conducts through", "o": ["Mobile ions", "Free protons", "Mobile neutral molecules", "Photons"], "a": 0, "why": "The charged ions can migrate in the melt."}, {"q": "Which has a continuous covalent network?", "o": ["Methane", "Diamond", "Sodium chloride", "Water"], "a": 1, "why": "Diamond has a three-dimensional network of covalently bonded carbons."}, {"q": "Filtration of salt water normally", "o": ["Removes dissolved salt", "Allows dissolved ions through", "Changes Na⁺ to sodium", "Separates water molecules by charge"], "a": 1, "why": "Dissolved ions pass through ordinary filter pores."}, {"q": "An alloy often becomes harder because", "o": ["Atoms lose their nuclei", "Different sizes obstruct layer movement", "All electrons disappear", "It becomes an ionic compound"], "a": 1, "why": "Distortion impedes movement within the structure."}, {"q": "A nanomaterial typically contains particles of size", "o": ["1–100 nm", "1–100 m", "Only exactly 1 nm", "At least 1 mm"], "a": 0, "why": "SCSA specifies approximately the 1–100 nm range."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-names", "title": "Hydrocarbon representations, names and branching", "scope": "Connectivity is more informative than a molecular formula · Choose the parent before assigning branches · Representations are equivalent only when they preserve connections", "parent": "che11-hydrocarbons", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Hydrocarbon representations, names and branching</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Connectivity is more informative than a molecular formula</li><li>Choose the parent before assigning branches</li><li>Representations are equivalent only when they preserve connections</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Carbon and molecular representations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carbon commonly forms four covalent bonds. Single, double and triple bonds count as one, two and three shared pairs respectively when checking valency. Carbon atoms can form chains, branches and rings. A hydrocarbon contains only carbon and hydrogen, whereas the wider category of organic compounds can also contain oxygen, nitrogen and other elements. A molecular formula states atom counts but does not show connectivity. A condensed structural formula such as CH₃CH₂CH₃ identifies the carbon sequence. A displayed formula shows bonds and atoms explicitly. Choose the representation requested in the question; a molecular formula cannot identify one structural isomer uniquely.", "diagram": "hydrocarbon"}, {"title": "Alkanes and the homologous series", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acyclic saturated alkanes contain only C–C single bonds and have general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. Consecutive members differ by CH₂ and show related chemical properties. Methane is CH₄, ethane C₂H₆ and propane C₃H₈. The formula assumes an open chain with no rings. A cycloalkane has a different hydrogen count despite containing only single bonds. Carbon’s tetrahedral local bonding means a displayed straight chain is a connectivity diagram, not a claim that all atoms lie on one straight line. Check each carbon has four bonds when drawing a structure. A terminal carbon in an unbranched alkane is usually CH₃.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming straight chains to eight carbons", "kind": "learn", "text": "The stems are meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-, hex-, hept- and oct- for one through eight carbons. Add -ane for an alkane. The longest continuous carbon chain determines the parent name. A bent drawing may still be an unbranched chain. Follow connected atoms instead of counting how many appear in a horizontal row. CH₃(CH₂)₄CH₃ has six carbons and is hexane. Parent-chain selection precedes numbering and branch naming. Formula C₆H₁₄ alone cannot distinguish hexane from a branched isomer. A name must communicate connectivity clearly enough that another person can draw the same structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Simple branching and locants", "kind": "learn", "text": "A one-carbon branch is methyl and a two-carbon branch is ethyl. Select the longest continuous chain, then number to give the substituents the lowest set of locants at the first point of difference. CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is 2-methylbutane, not 3-methylbutane and not a pentane chain. Repeated branches use di-, tri- and appropriate locants, as in 2,3-dimethylpentane. Separate numbers by commas and numbers from words by hyphens. Alphabetise different branch names while ignoring multiplying prefixes. There may be several candidate longest chains; choose a valid chain according to naming conventions, then check total carbon count and valency.", "diagram": "branches"}, {"title": "Connectivity is more informative than a molecular formula", "kind": "learn", "text": "A molecular formula records atom totals, while condensed and displayed structures show how atoms connect with different levels of detail. Carbon ordinarily forms four bonds in the neutral hydrocarbon examples; a double bond counts twice towards that valence. Check each carbon before naming. A string with the right atom count can still depict impossible connectivity. A full displayed formula must show all required atoms and bonds, not merely a molecular formula placed between dashes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choose the parent before assigning branches", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identify the longest appropriate carbon chain, number it to give the required lowest locants, then identify and name substituents. The visually straight line on the page is not necessarily the longest chain. Repeated substituents require multiplicative prefixes and each position, while punctuation separates numbers and names clearly. The name must return the same structure when redrawn. Naming from a guessed formula without checking the actual chain is a common source of apparent isomers that are duplicates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Representations are equivalent only when they preserve connections", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rotating a molecule or reversing the direction in which a chain is drawn does not create a structural isomer. Compare bonded neighbours and branch positions rather than page orientation. If asked for a displayed structure, make every bond attach to the correct atom: condensed groups still represent carbon-centred connections. In an exam, include locants where needed and distinguish a naming error from a valid alternative drawing of the same compound.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A branched structure", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A branched structure", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Names and structures", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A branched structure", "prompt": "Name CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃ and give its molecular formula.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The longest continuous chain has five carbons: pentane. [1]", "The methyl branch has lowest locant 2, giving 2-methylpentane. [1]", "Six carbons and an acyclic saturated structure give C₆H₁₄. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Names and structures", "prompt": "Write condensed structures for 3-methylpent-1-ene and 2,2-dimethylbutane. Verify carbon valency.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["3-methylpent-1-ene: CH₂=CHCH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃. [2]", "2,2-dimethylbutane: CH₃C(CH₃)₂CH₂CH₃. [2]", "Check the branched central carbons have four total bonds. This check supports the structures but is not an additional mark."], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q5", "allocations": [2, 2, 0]}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-names-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Name CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ and explain the parent chain and branch position. Give its molecular formula. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The longest chain has four carbons, so the parent is butane.", "[2] A methyl branch is at carbon 2 using the lower locant: 2-methylbutane.", "[2] Five total carbons in a saturated acyclic alkane give C₅H₁₂; verify each carbon’s four bonds."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-names-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two drawings reverse the same branched chain from left to right. Explain how to decide whether they are different structural isomers, and what a full displayed formula must include. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Compare actual atom connectivity and substituent positions, not drawing orientation.", "[2] Reversal/rotation preserving bonded neighbours represents the same molecule, not another structural isomer.", "[2] A full displayed formula shows all atoms and bonds with correct carbon valence; a molecular formula alone does not establish connectivity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the name of CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃?", "o": ["2-methylbutane.", "Pentane regardless of connectivity.", "3-methylbutane as the preferred numbering.", "2-ethylpropane using an unnecessarily short parent chain."], "a": 0, "why": "The longest chain is four carbons and the methyl branch receives locant 2."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-isomers", "title": "Alkenes, structural isomers and benzene", "scope": "Structural isomers change connections · A double bond changes the bonding model · Benzene is not three independent ordinary alkene bonds", "parent": "che11-hydrocarbons", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Alkenes, structural isomers and benzene</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Structural isomers change connections</li><li>A double bond changes the bonding model</li><li>Benzene is not three independent ordinary alkene bonds</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Alkenes and the double bond", "kind": "learn", "text": "An alkene contains a C=C bond. An acyclic alkene with one double bond has formula CₙH₂ₙ. The double bond is a region of higher electron density and supports characteristic addition reactions. Choose the longest parent chain containing the double bond and number from the end giving that bond its lowest locant. CH₃CH=CHCH₂CH₃ is pent-2-ene. CH₂=C(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ is 2-methylbut-1-ene. The double bond receives numbering priority over an alkyl branch in these simple structures. A general formula helps check atom count but cannot by itself prove that a compound is an alkene because rings can give the same formula.", "diagram": "alkene"}, {"title": "Structural isomers and checking completeness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Structural isomers share a molecular formula but have different connectivity. Butane and 2-methylpropane both have C₄H₁₀. Position isomers differ in the location of a group or bond, as in pent-1-ene and pent-2-ene. Rotating a drawing or bending an identical chain does not produce a new structural isomer. To enumerate candidates, work systematically through distinct carbon skeletons and permitted bond positions. Redraw or name each candidate to identify duplicates. In Year 12, restricted rotation around a double bond adds cis–trans isomerism when each double-bonded carbon has two different attached groups. Structural and geometric differences are separate ideas.", "diagram": "isomers"}, {"title": "Benzene: a delocalised ring model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Benzene is C₆H₆ with a planar ring of six carbon atoms. Each carbon has three sigma-bond connections and contributes to a delocalised electron system extending around the ring. Its six C–C bonds are equivalent and intermediate in length between typical single and double bonds. A hexagon containing alternating bonds is a useful representation, but benzene does not literally alternate between two different structures. A circle inside the hexagon emphasises delocalisation. The extra stability means benzene commonly undergoes substitution that preserves the ring electron system rather than ordinary alkene-like addition. Explain this limitation when using a simple structural diagram.", "diagram": "benzene"}, {"title": "A complete small-isomer example", "kind": "learn", "text": "C₅H₁₂ has three acyclic alkane constitutional isomers: pentane CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂CH₃, 2-methylbutane CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃ and 2,2-dimethylpropane C(CH₃)₄. These represent five-, four- and three-carbon longest paths respectively. Moving a methyl branch from position 2 to position 3 of a four-carbon drawing gives the same named skeleton after reversing numbering, not a fourth isomer. Count five total carbons and twelve hydrogens in every candidate and check four bond units per carbon. Use a systematic skeleton method so symmetry does not create duplicates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Structural isomers change connections", "kind": "learn", "text": "Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different atom connectivity. Butane and 2-methylpropane illustrate chain isomerism. An alkene’s double-bond position can also distinguish structures, while reversing a chain may reproduce a previously counted example. Draw candidates, check atom totals and valence, then remove duplicates using connectivity. The general CₙH₂ₙ formula applies to a suitable acyclic monoalkene, but also fits other classes such as cycloalkanes; a formula alone does not uniquely prove a double bond.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A double bond changes the bonding model", "kind": "learn", "text": "An alkene contains a carbon–carbon double bond with electron density and geometry different from an alkane’s single bond. The model supports addition reactions that change the bond and add groups. Restricted rotation is relevant to later cis–trans reasoning, not a claim that all alkenes necessarily have two geometric forms. A representation must show which carbons form the double bond and preserve carbon valence after a reaction or naming change.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Benzene is not three independent ordinary alkene bonds", "kind": "learn", "text": "Benzene’s ring has a delocalised electron system and equivalent carbon–carbon bonding in the standard model. Alternating-line drawings are representations, not evidence of rapid physical switching between isolated structures. Its characteristic substitution behaviour differs from a simple alkene’s readily illustrated addition. Explain the delocalised ring and the reaction context, rather than concluding that any formula with several unsaturations must react identically with bromine under identical conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Benzene versus ethene", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Benzene versus ethene", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Benzene versus ethene", "prompt": "Explain why a diagram with alternating single and double bonds is incomplete for benzene, and compare its bromine reaction with ethene.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Benzene has delocalised electrons around its ring. [1]", "All six C–C bonds are equivalent, not alternating different lengths. [1]", "Ethene readily adds bromine across C=C. [1]", "Benzene typically undergoes catalysed substitution that retains aromaticity. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-isomers-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Draw the two structural isomers of C₄H₁₀ and explain why reversing a butane drawing does not add a third. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Show CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ with a four-carbon chain.", "[2] Show (CH₃)₃CH with branched connectivity, correctly accounting for C₄H₁₀.", "[2] Reversing the first structure preserves all bonded neighbours, so it is the same structure rather than another isomer."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-isomers-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare the bonding model of benzene with that of an ordinary alkene and link this to a reaction distinction. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Benzene has a delocalised ring system with equivalent C–C bonding in the usual model.", "[2] An ordinary alkene contains a localised C=C and undergoes relevant addition reactions.", "[2] Benzene commonly undergoes substitution under appropriate conditions; an alternating drawing does not make it three independent alkene sites."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which change creates a structural isomer?", "o": ["Changing atom connectivity while retaining the molecular formula.", "Rotating an unchanged drawing.", "Reversing a straight-chain drawing with the same neighbours.", "Changing only the font used for a formula."], "a": 0, "why": "Structural isomerism concerns connectivity, not the drawing’s orientation."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-properties", "title": "Hydrocarbon properties, combustion and fuel evidence", "scope": "Dispersion comparison needs molecular size and contact · Balance combustion using atom conservation · Compare fuels per a stated basis", "parent": "che11-hydrocarbons", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Hydrocarbon properties, combustion and fuel evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Dispersion comparison needs molecular size and contact</li><li>Balance combustion using atom conservation</li><li>Compare fuels per a stated basis</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Dispersion forces and physical properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hydrocarbons are largely non-polar and attract each other through dispersion forces. Within a comparable homologous series, larger molecules usually have more polarisable electron clouds and stronger dispersion attraction, raising boiling point. Branching often reduces intermolecular surface contact and lowers boiling point among alkane isomers. Melting points depend strongly on crystal packing and symmetry and do not always follow the same simple pattern. There is no universal rule that every aromatic compound boils above every alkane or every alkane above every alkene of similar carbon count. Use molecular size, shape and actual data rather than an unsupported category ranking.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Complete and incomplete combustion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Complete combustion with sufficient oxygen converts hydrocarbon carbon to CO₂ and hydrogen to H₂O. Balance carbon first, hydrogen second and oxygen last. Methane: CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g). Limited oxygen may produce CO, carbon soot and mixtures of products. A specified equation producing CO only can be balanced, but real incomplete combustion is often more complex. Carbon monoxide is toxic and soot contributes to particulate pollution. Water’s state depends on conditions: hot combustion products contain vapour, while standard combustion enthalpies often specify liquid water. State symbols influence thermochemical interpretation.", "diagram": "combustion"}, {"title": "Fuel choices and an evidence-based comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Fuels can be compared by energy per mole, per mass or per useful service. These are different comparisons. A fuel with more energy per mole may contain much heavier molecules. Complete combustion still generates CO₂, even if a fuel is described as renewable. A fair life-cycle assessment considers feedstock growth, land use, transport, processing energy and combustion, as well as conversion efficiency and infrastructure. Evaluate one concrete benefit and one limitation using a stated metric. Do not label a fuel carbon-neutral solely because it is a biofuel. Relate claims to evidence and identify which stages an estimate includes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Dispersion comparison needs molecular size and contact", "kind": "learn", "text": "Nonpolar hydrocarbons have dispersion forces. Larger molecules generally have more polarisable electron clouds and stronger intermolecular attraction; shape and contact also influence comparisons. For suitable isomers, branching can reduce contact and boiling point, but melting depends more strongly on packing and is not the same rule. State the compared molecules and forces. A longer chain does not make an alkane hydrogen-bond merely because it contains many hydrogen atoms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Balance combustion using atom conservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon produces carbon dioxide and water with sufficient oxygen in the ideal equation. Incomplete combustion can form carbon monoxide and/or carbon with water under limited oxygen; actual mixtures depend on conditions. Balance carbon first, hydrogen next and oxygen last, then check every atom. Changing a formula’s subscript to balance changes the substance and is invalid. Coefficients describe ratios of reacting amounts, not the energy released by one arbitrary mass.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare fuels per a stated basis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Energy per mole and energy per gram can give different rankings. Convert using molar mass and retain units before comparing. Emissions depend on the boundary: combustion carbon, production, transport and land-use impacts are different components. A clean-looking flame does not establish no carbon dioxide, and a renewable source does not imply no environmental trade-offs. A justified recommendation compares relevant performance and evidence for the intended use instead of selecting one favourable number alone. Biogas contains methane together with other gases, commonly including carbon dioxide, and its composition affects energy per measured volume. Biodiesel contains fatty-acid alkyl esters and bioethanol is ethanol; they are not interchangeable hydrocarbon molecules. Compare fossil fuels such as coal, oil-derived petroleum fuels and natural gas with these biofuels using energy output, combustion products, purpose and a common mass or energy basis. Renewable origin does not remove combustion CO₂ or upstream impacts. Social, economic, cultural and political values can influence adoption alongside the chemistry, so a justified recommendation identifies both evidence and the criterion being prioritised.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Combustion balancing", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Combustion balancing", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: A physical-property claim", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Combustion balancing", "prompt": "Write a balanced equation for complete combustion of liquid octane, producing CO₂(g) and H₂O(g).", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Carbon requires 8CO₂ per C₈H₁₈; hydrogen requires 9H₂O. [1]", "The products contain 25 oxygen atoms per fuel molecule, requiring 12.5O₂. [1]", "2C₈H₁₈(l) + 25O₂(g) → 16CO₂(g) + 18H₂O(g). [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A physical-property claim", "prompt": "Evaluate the statement: “All larger hydrocarbons have higher melting points, so carbon count alone predicts melting point.”", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Carbon count can increase dispersion attraction in comparable compounds. [1]", "Melting also depends on molecular symmetry and crystal packing. [1]", "The absolute claim is unjustified; use structural comparisons and experimental data. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-properties-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Balance complete propane combustion and calculate the amount of CO₂ formed from 0.250 mol propane under complete conversion. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, with atoms balanced.", "[2] The propane:CO₂ molar ratio is 1:3.", "[2] n(CO₂) = 0.250×3 = 0.750 mol, assuming sufficient oxygen and complete conversion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-properties-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why an alkane boiling-point comparison needs specific intermolecular reasoning, then evaluate “a smokeless fuel produces no carbon dioxide.” (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alkanes have dispersion forces, with size/polarisability and shape/contact affecting strength.", "[2] Boiling separates molecules rather than breaking all C–H bonds; hydrogen atoms alone do not create hydrogen bonding.", "[2] Complete combustion can produce invisible CO₂ without soot, so lack of smoke does not establish zero carbon emissions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does a balanced equation coefficient ratio compare?", "o": ["Amounts in moles of the represented species.", "Always equal masses of each species.", "A licence to change molecular subscripts.", "A guarantee of zero emissions if the flame is clear."], "a": 0, "why": "Stoichiometric coefficients are mole ratios; masses require molar-mass conversion."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-reactions", "title": "Addition, substitution and chemical-test evidence", "scope": "Addition preserves the carbon skeleton in the simple model · Substitution replaces an atom and produces another product · Observation and inference must be separated", "parent": "che11-hydrocarbons", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Addition, substitution and chemical-test evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Addition preserves the carbon skeleton in the simple model</li><li>Substitution replaces an atom and produces another product</li><li>Observation and inference must be separated</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Addition reactions of alkenes", "kind": "learn", "text": "In addition, the C=C becomes a C–C single bond and new groups attach to its two carbons. Ethene + bromine gives 1,2-dibromoethane: CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br. Hydrogenation adds H₂ using a suitable metal catalyst such as nickel, converting an alkene to an alkane. Acid-catalysed hydration adds H and OH to produce an alcohol. Track which atoms are added and retain all carbons. An unsymmetrical alkene can give regioisomeric products; use the conditions and conventions given in the question. Do not assume every conceivable product forms in equal amount.", "diagram": "addition"}, {"title": "Bromine observations and controlled testing", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bromine solution is coloured and can be decolourised rapidly by reaction with an alkene under suitable conditions. The bromine atoms become part of an organic addition product. An alkane ordinarily does not react rapidly in the same test without ultraviolet light. A fair comparison uses similar sample amounts, solvent, temperature and lighting. Decolourisation supports a reactive unsaturation or another bromine-consuming process, so the wider chemical context matters. Never identify a functional group from colour loss alone if other reactive substances are possible. State both the observed colour change and the chemical reason, supported by an appropriate structural equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substitution of alkanes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Substitution replaces a hydrogen with another atom or group. Methane reacts with chlorine under UV light: CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl. UV initiates a radical process by helping split a chlorine molecule homolytically. Further substitutions can occur, so the product is not necessarily pure chloromethane. For an exam at this level, name the reaction type, write a balanced equation and identify required conditions. The C–C skeleton remains unchanged in simple substitution. Compare this with addition, which consumes a double bond without removing a hydrogen. Molecular formulae alone may hide the reaction site, so structural formulae are often preferable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substitution of benzene", "kind": "learn", "text": "Benzene reacts with bromine using an appropriate Lewis-acid catalyst such as FeBr₃: C₆H₆ + Br₂ → C₆H₅Br + HBr. A ring hydrogen is replaced, and the aromatic delocalised system is retained in the product. This differs from an alkene’s rapid bromine addition without such a catalyst. Do not use fluorine as a routine classroom example because its reactivity makes the comparison unhelpful. Catalysts and light requirements belong with the equation rather than being omitted. Distinguish chemical stability from complete unreactivity: benzene can react, but its favoured pathways and conditions differ from those of isolated alkenes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reaction equations and exam representations", "kind": "learn", "text": "An organic equation should preserve every atom and show the functional part of the structure changing. Use a displayed or condensed structure when asked to show connectivity, place conditions above the reaction arrow and include coproducts such as HCl in substitution. Check carbon valency after changing a double bond to a single bond. In a combustion equation, a molecular formula is usually sufficient to count atoms, but obey the question’s exact representation requirement. A balanced formula can still describe the wrong reaction. First identify the reactant class and reaction type, then construct products and balance atom counts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Addition versus substitution in an exam", "kind": "learn", "text": "For addition, retain the entire alkene skeleton, change C=C to C–C and attach the reagent’s atoms across the original double-bond carbons. For substitution, replace an atom such as H and include the small coproduct, for example HBr. Conditions distinguish dark bromine addition to an alkene, UV alkane halogenation and catalysed benzene halogenation. State observations as visible changes, then separately infer products through equations. A chemical name is an inference rather than a visual observation. If a question asks all atoms and bonds, use a displayed drawing rather than a molecular formula.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Addition preserves the carbon skeleton in the simple model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adding bromine across ethene’s double bond yields a dibromoalkane, with the C=C becoming a single bond and one bromine attached to each former double-bond carbon. Other addition reagents produce their corresponding products under suitable conditions. Track every atom and check four bonds at each carbon. Adding two atoms to one carbon while leaving an impossible valence is not an acceptable reaction structure, even when the molecular formula appears plausible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Substitution replaces an atom and produces another product", "kind": "learn", "text": "Alkane halogen substitution under suitable photochemical conditions replaces hydrogen with halogen and produces hydrogen halide. Benzene halogen substitution requires appropriate conditions/catalysis and preserves the ring’s aromatic system in the conventional account. An equation should include the substituted organic product and the other product, with correct conditions where requested. Reaction type is determined by the structural change, not simply the presence of bromine in the reagent list.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Observation and inference must be separated", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bromine decolourisation under controlled conditions supports a reaction associated with suitable unsaturation, but colour change alone does not identify every unknown structure. Compare initial colour, final colour and any phase changes without claiming that an inferred compound name is directly visible. Light conditions, contamination and other reactive groups matter. A fair unknown comparison uses the same solvent, reagent amount and conditions, then combines evidence rather than treating one test as complete identification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Addition and observations", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Addition and observations", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Testing unknown hydrocarbons", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Addition and observations", "prompt": "Draw a condensed equation for ethene reacting with bromine and explain the observed change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br. [2: correct double-bond change and product connectivity]", "The coloured bromine solution becomes colourless as bromine is consumed. [1]", "The process is addition across C=C. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q3", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Testing unknown hydrocarbons", "prompt": "Two labelled samples are hexane and hex-1-ene, but labels are lost. Design a chemical comparison, predict observations and products and identify a condition that would spoil the interpretation.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Use a small controlled bromine test under suitable non-UV conditions with matched sample conditions. [1]", "Hex-1-ene consumes bromine and loses its colour. [1]", "CH₂=CH(CH₂)₃CH₃ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCHBr(CH₂)₃CH₃. [1]", "Hexane does not react rapidly under those same dark conditions. [1]", "UV could initiate alkane substitution and undermine the comparison. [1]", "Reference/blank comparisons and controlled amounts strengthen interpretation. Practical work requires approved supervision. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-hydrocarbons-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-reactions-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Write the ethene–bromine addition equation in condensed form and explain the structural change and expected colour evidence under suitable conditions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br, with the carbon framework and atoms conserved.", "[2] The double bond becomes single and one bromine attaches to each carbon; product valence is correct.", "[2] Bromine colour is lost under appropriate controlled conditions; the observation supports reaction but alone does not identify every unknown."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-hydrocarbons-reactions-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare a simple alkene addition with alkane bromination under appropriate light. Include the other product of substitution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Alkene addition attaches reagent atoms across C=C and changes it to a single bond.", "[2] Alkane substitution replaces H with Br, for example CH₄ + Br₂ → CH₃Br + HBr under suitable light.", "[2] Distinguish structural changes/conditions; using the same halogen does not make the reaction types identical."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which equation represents substitution?", "o": ["CH₄ + Br₂ → CH₃Br + HBr under suitable light.", "CH₂=CH₂ + Br₂ → CH₂BrCH₂Br.", "C + O₂ → CO₂ as alkene addition.", "A change of ink colour with no chemical information."], "a": 0, "why": "Substitution replaces an atom and forms another product; addition adds across a multiple bond."}, {"q": "An acyclic alkane with six carbons has formula", "o": ["C₆H₁₂", "C₆H₁₄", "C₆H₆", "C₆H₁₀"], "a": 1, "why": "CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ gives 14 hydrogens."}, {"q": "Benzene usually reacts with bromine by", "o": ["Catalysed substitution", "Rapid uncatalysed alkene addition", "Neutralisation", "Precipitation"], "a": 0, "why": "Substitution preserves the aromatic delocalised system."}, {"q": "The parent chain for an alkene must", "o": ["Contain the double bond", "Always be horizontal", "Exclude branches entirely", "Have eight carbons"], "a": 0, "why": "Choose the longest suitable chain containing C=C."}, {"q": "Structural isomers have", "o": ["Different molecular formulae", "The same connectivity", "The same molecular formula but different connectivity", "Different proton counts in carbon"], "a": 2, "why": "Connectivity distinguishes structural isomers."}, {"q": "Ordinary hydrocarbon boiling mainly overcomes", "o": ["C–H bonds", "Nuclear forces", "Dispersion forces between molecules", "All C–C bonds"], "a": 2, "why": "The molecules ordinarily remain chemically intact."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-quantities-energy-moles", "title": "Moles, molar mass, particles and composition", "scope": "Specify the entity counted · Composition is a mass fraction, not an atom fraction · Use units to catch a wrong pathway", "parent": "che11-quantities-energy", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Moles, molar mass, particles and composition</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Specify the entity counted</li><li>Composition is a mass fraction, not an atom fraction</li><li>Use units to catch a wrong pathway</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "The mole and particle identity", "kind": "learn", "text": "A mole contains 6.022 × 10²³ specified entities using the course data-booklet precision. Specify whether these entities are atoms, molecules, ions or formula units. One mole of CO₂ molecules contains one mole of carbon atoms and two moles of oxygen atoms. One mole of CaCl₂ formula units corresponds to one mole of calcium ions and two moles of chloride ions in an ideal fully dissociated solution. Particle count N = nNₐ. A mole is a counting unit, not a fixed mass. Equal amounts in moles of different substances contain equal entity counts but generally have different masses.", "diagram": "mole"}, {"title": "Molar mass and n = m/M", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molar mass M is mass per mole, usually g mol⁻¹. Add atomic masses for every atom in the formula, including atoms multiplied by parentheses. For Ca(OH)₂, M = 40.08 + 2(16.00 + 1.008) = 74.096 g mol⁻¹ using these stated values. Amount n = m/M, mass m = nM. Keep extra digits during calculations and round the final result to an appropriate precision. Dimensional checking gives g ÷ (g mol⁻¹) = mol. Do not substitute a sample mass into a molar-mass expression or assume that a large mass means a large amount without considering M.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Percentage composition", "kind": "learn", "text": "The mass fraction of an element in a compound is its contribution to formula molar mass divided by the total molar mass. Percentage = element contribution / M × 100. For water, oxygen contributes about 16.00 of 18.016 g mol⁻¹, so oxygen is approximately 88.81% by mass. This differs from atomic percentage, where one of the three atoms is oxygen. A subscript gives an atom ratio, not a mass percentage. When using analytical composition data later to find an empirical formula, convert masses to moles before comparing element ratios. Check that a complete set of mass percentages totals approximately 100%.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Percentage composition worked reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Using stated values Ca 40.08, C 12.01 and O 16.00, M(CaCO₃) = 100.09 g mol⁻¹. Calcium’s mass percentage is 40.08/100.09 × 100 = 40.04%. Oxygen’s contribution is three atoms: 48.00/100.09 × 100 = 47.96%. The percentages describe mass shares, while the formula gives atom numbers 1:1:3. A student who uses one oxygen atomic mass overlooks the subscript. Your working should show the complete molar-mass sum so the marking pathway is visible and the final check can confirm the element percentages total approximately 100%.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Specify the entity counted", "kind": "learn", "text": "A mole refers to a stated entity count: atoms, molecules, formula units or ions. One mole of CaCl₂ formula units contains one mole of calcium ions and two moles of chloride ions in the stoichiometric accounting. Total atoms and molecules are not interchangeable. Use n = m/M with consistent mass units and a molar mass for the actual formula. If the compound is hydrated, omitting its water changes the molar mass and calculated amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Composition is a mass fraction, not an atom fraction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Percentage by mass uses the mass contribution of an element in a formula divided by the formula’s molar mass. Two hydrogen atoms do not imply that hydrogen is two thirds of the mass of water. Count atoms, multiply by appropriate atomic masses and use the total in the denominator. Percentages should sum approximately to 100 when all components are included, with rounding considered. A composition calculation does not by itself establish the molecule’s connectivity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use units to catch a wrong pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiplying grams by g mol⁻¹ cannot produce moles; dividing mass by molar mass can. Particle number follows n times Avogadro’s constant, with entity labels retained. Relative atomic mass and relative molecular mass are dimensionless comparisons, while molar mass has g mol⁻¹ units. Keep intermediate values unrounded and report the final answer appropriately. A chemically sensible value and an explicit counted entity are part of the explanation, not optional decoration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Moles to particles", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Moles to particles", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Moles to particles", "prompt": "Calculate the amount of CO₂ in 8.80 g using M(CO₂) = 44.0 g mol⁻¹, then calculate the number of oxygen atoms.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(CO₂) = 8.80/44.0 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "Each CO₂ molecule contains two O atoms, so n(O atoms) = 0.400 mol. [1]", "N = 0.400 × 6.022 × 10²³. [1]", "N = 2.41 × 10²³ oxygen atoms. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-moles-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Using M(H₂O) = 18.0 g mol⁻¹ and Nₐ = 6.022×10²³ mol⁻¹, calculate molecules and hydrogen atoms in 9.00 g water. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(H₂O) = 9.00/18.0 = 0.500 mol.", "[2] Molecules = 0.500×6.022×10²³ = 3.01×10²³ to three significant figures.", "[2] Two H atoms per molecule gives 6.02×10²³ hydrogen atoms, not the same count as molecules."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-moles-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Using C = 12.0 and O = 16.0 g mol⁻¹, calculate carbon percentage by mass in CO₂ and explain why it is not one third. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] M(CO₂) = 12.0 + 2×16.0 = 44.0 g mol⁻¹.", "[2] Carbon percentage = 12.0/44.0 ×100 = 27.3%.", "[2] Atom ratios are not mass ratios because C and O atoms have different masses; retain the actual mass contributions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "One mole of CaCl₂ corresponds to how much chloride in its formula ratio?", "o": ["Two moles of chloride ions.", "One mole of chloride ions.", "Two moles of calcium atoms and no chloride.", "Three moles of CaCl₂ molecules automatically."], "a": 0, "why": "Each formula unit contains two chloride ions in the ionic stoichiometric account."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-quantities-energy-stoichiometry", "title": "Balanced equations and mass stoichiometry", "scope": "Balance before converting quantities · State the conversion assumptions · Working can earn credit even when arithmetic goes wrong", "parent": "che11-quantities-energy", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Balanced equations and mass stoichiometry</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Balance before converting quantities</li><li>State the conversion assumptions</li><li>Working can earn credit even when arithmetic goes wrong</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Balanced chemical equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A balanced equation conserves each element’s atom count and total charge where ionic species appear. Change coefficients, never internal formula subscripts. Aluminium oxide formation is 4Al(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Al₂O₃(s). Coefficients give ratios of particles and amounts in moles. They do not directly give gram ratios because molar masses differ. State symbols identify physical state and may matter to energy changes and reactions. Start with correct chemical formulae, then balance. A formula such as AlO is not repaired by changing coefficients because it represents the wrong compound. Conservation of mass assumes the full relevant system, including gases, is accounted for.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A mass-to-mass calculation pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use a balanced equation to connect known and unknown amounts. First convert the known mass to moles. Second use the coefficient ratio to obtain moles of the requested species. Third multiply by that species’ molar mass. For 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, magnesium and magnesium oxide have a 1:1 mole ratio, even though their masses are unequal. Write the ratio explicitly as n(MgO)/n(Mg) = 2/2. This avoids applying a coefficient to grams directly. State which reagent is in excess or compare both if amounts are given. Theoretical product calculations assume the stated reaction proceeds as described.", "diagram": "stoichiometry"}, {"title": "Conservation, observations and gas loss", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reaction can appear to lose mass if gas escapes from the measured container. The total mass of a properly defined closed system remains conserved in an ordinary chemical reaction. If acid reacts with carbonate in an open beaker, CO₂ leaves and the beaker’s measured mass decreases. That change can measure reaction progress if other losses are controlled. Conversely, burning magnesium in air can increase the collected solid mass because oxygen is incorporated. Always identify the system boundary. Do not infer that matter is destroyed from a balance reading without considering incoming and outgoing material.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Significant figures, units and reasonableness", "kind": "learn", "text": "Measured data limit numerical precision. Keep unrounded intermediate values and round the final result sensibly, normally to the least precise relevant measured input. Stoichiometric coefficients are exact and do not limit significant figures. Convert millilitres to litres for molar concentration calculations and joules to kilojoules for a kJ mol⁻¹ result. A percentage cannot exceed 100% for an ideal pure-product yield, though a wet or impure sample can give an apparent value above 100%. Estimate the order of magnitude before trusting a calculator. Include units at each major step so an incorrect factor of 1000 is visible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Balance before converting quantities", "kind": "learn", "text": "A balanced equation conserves atoms and, where appropriate, charge. Coefficients provide mole ratios, so mass-to-mass calculations pass through moles of the known species, the stoichiometric ratio and mass of the target. A 2:1 coefficient ratio is not generally a 2:1 mass ratio. Write the balanced equation and identify the species beside each number. Changing subscripts to force balance changes chemical identity and invalidates the calculation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "State the conversion assumptions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A theoretical calculation often assumes the stated reactant is fully converted with other requirements satisfied. If two reactant amounts are supplied, a limiting check is needed before selecting the product amount. Gas loss can reduce the measured mass of an open vessel without violating overall conservation: escaped matter belongs to the wider system. Explain the chosen boundary and account for products rather than treating a decreasing balance reading as evidence that atoms vanished.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Working can earn credit even when arithmetic goes wrong", "kind": "learn", "text": "Show substituted values, the ratio and units in distinct linked steps. Do not leave empty brackets or unexplained arrows in place of numbers. Keep precision until the final result and check magnitude against the starting amount. A complete answer states the requested quantity and any crucial condition. This approach helps identify whether an error is chemical, unit-based or numerical rather than hiding it behind a single calculator result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Mass stoichiometry", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Mass stoichiometry", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Mass stoichiometry", "prompt": "Magnesium burns in excess oxygen: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. Find MgO mass from 4.86 g Mg, using M(Mg) = 24.3 and M(MgO) = 40.3 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(Mg) = 4.86/24.3 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "n(MgO)/n(Mg) = 2/2 = 1. [1]", "n(MgO) = 0.200 mol. [1]", "m(MgO) = 0.200 × 40.3 = 8.06 g. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-stoichiometry-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Using 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO, M(Mg) = 24.3 and M(MgO) = 40.3 g mol⁻¹, calculate MgO formed from 4.86 g Mg with excess oxygen. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(Mg) = 4.86/24.3 = 0.200 mol.", "[2] The balanced 2:2 ratio gives n(MgO) = 0.200 mol.", "[2] m(MgO) = 0.200×40.3 = 8.06 g, assuming complete conversion and oxygen excess."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-stoichiometry-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "An open reaction vessel loses measured mass while gas bubbles form. Explain how this is compatible with conservation and how a closed-system measurement could help. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Gas evolution can carry matter beyond the vessel/balance boundary.", "[2] Atoms are conserved across reactants and products including escaped gas; vessel mass alone is not total-system mass.", "[2] A suitable safely contained/monitored system can include gas in the accounting, with appropriate pressure and apparatus constraints."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why convert mass to moles before using an equation ratio?", "o": ["Coefficients relate molar amounts, not generally equal mass ratios.", "Every reactant has identical molar mass.", "Subscripts may be changed freely to match masses.", "Mass is never conserved in a gas reaction."], "a": 0, "why": "Molar mass connects measured mass to the equation’s mole ratio."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-quantities-energy-limiting", "title": "Limiting reagents and excess amounts", "scope": "Compare amount divided by coefficient · Theoretical completion differs from equilibrium yield · Check leftover amounts and conservation", "parent": "che11-quantities-energy", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Limiting reagents and excess amounts</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Compare amount divided by coefficient</li><li>Theoretical completion differs from equilibrium yield</li><li>Check leftover amounts and conservation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Limiting reagent and excess material", "kind": "learn", "text": "When amounts of two reactants are supplied, compare n/coefficient for each. The smaller value limits how far the reaction can proceed. In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 3.00 mol H₂ and 2.00 mol O₂ give ratios 1.50 and 2.00. Hydrogen limits, 3.00 mol water forms and 1.50 mol oxygen is consumed, leaving 0.50 mol oxygen. A smaller mass does not necessarily identify the limiting reagent. Compute the excess remaining from initial amount minus amount consumed, then convert units if required. This method becomes especially important in Year 12 chemical synthesis and percentage-yield calculations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare amount divided by coefficient", "kind": "learn", "text": "For aA + bB → products, compare n(A)/a and n(B)/b. The smaller value limits the theoretical reaction extent. Comparing raw masses or raw mole amounts alone can give the wrong answer when coefficients differ. Multiply the limiting extent by each product coefficient, then subtract the consumed amount from any excess reactant. At an exact stoichiometric match neither reactant remains in the ideal completion model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Theoretical completion differs from equilibrium yield", "kind": "learn", "text": "A limiting-reagent calculation estimates a maximum under the stated complete-conversion assumptions. A reversible industrial reaction can stop at an equilibrium composition before complete limiting-reactant consumption. Recovery and purification can lower isolated yield further. Distinguish maximum product, actual product and leftover amounts. The useful calculation does not establish reaction speed or prove that a plant will obtain its maximum on every pass.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check leftover amounts and conservation", "kind": "learn", "text": "An excess result must be nonnegative and correspond to the stoichiometric consumption implied by the limiting reactant. If the calculation gives a negative leftover, revisit the ratio selection or units. Products cannot be calculated independently from both reactants and then added, since both contribute to the same reaction. A clear table of initial, consumed and remaining amounts helps trace the argument, with the chemical equation at its head.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-extent"}, {"title": "Interactive limiting-reagent model", "kind": "interactive", "model": "limiting"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: A limiting reaction", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A limiting reaction", "prompt": "For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, start with 4.00 mol H₂ and 1.50 mol O₂. Find the limiting reagent, water formed and excess remaining.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Compare n/coefficient: hydrogen 2.00; oxygen 1.50. [1]", "O₂ is limiting. [1]", "Water formed = 2 × 1.50 = 3.00 mol. [1]", "Hydrogen consumed = 3.00 mol, leaving 1.00 mol. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-limiting-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, a theoretical completion model begins with 0.400 mol N₂ and 0.900 mol H₂. Determine the limiting reactant, NH₃ and excess amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n/coefficient: N₂ = 0.400; H₂ = 0.900/3 = 0.300, so H₂ limits.", "[2] n(NH₃) = 2×0.300 = 0.600 mol maximum.", "[2] N₂ consumed = 0.300 mol, leaving 0.100 mol; ideal model leaves no H₂."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-limiting-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why selecting the smaller starting mass as the limiting reactant can fail, and distinguish the resulting theoretical yield from actual equilibrium production. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Masses must be converted using each molar mass and compared through stoichiometric coefficients.", "[2] Smaller mass need not provide the smaller n/coefficient, so raw mass comparison is insufficient.", "[2] The calculated maximum assumes completion; equilibrium, loss and side reactions can lower actual recovered product and do not determine rate."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "For N₂ + 3H₂, which comparison determines limitation?", "o": ["n(N₂)/1 against n(H₂)/3.", "Starting masses without molar conversion.", "Only which gas has the smaller atomic number.", "Adding separately calculated product amounts from both reactants."], "a": 0, "why": "Amounts divided by coefficients compare the maximum common reaction extent."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-quantities-energy-enthalpy", "title": "Enthalpy, bond energies and energy profiles", "scope": "Choose the system before assigning the sign · Bond breaking absorbs; bond formation releases · Scale thermochemical equations consistently", "parent": "che11-quantities-energy", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Enthalpy, bond energies and energy profiles</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Choose the system before assigning the sign</li><li>Bond breaking absorbs; bond formation releases</li><li>Scale thermochemical equations consistently</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "System, surroundings and enthalpy sign", "kind": "learn", "text": "The reacting substances are the system, while the solvent, container and wider environment can form the surroundings. An exothermic reaction transfers heat from system to surroundings and has ΔH < 0. An endothermic reaction absorbs heat and has ΔH > 0. If a well-insulated solution surrounding an exothermic process warms, the system has lost energy even though the measured temperature rises. Temperature and enthalpy are different quantities. A phase change can transfer energy at nearly constant temperature while particles rearrange. Sign conventions follow the defined system; write the definition before assigning a sign.", "diagram": "enthalpy"}, {"title": "Bond breaking and bond formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Breaking an existing bond requires energy input. Forming a bond releases energy. The overall reaction enthalpy reflects the difference between energy needed to separate reactant bonding and energy released as product bonding forms. Using mean bond energies, ΔH ≈ ΣE(bonds broken) − ΣE(bonds formed). An exothermic reaction releases more during formation than it absorbs during breaking. It is incorrect to say energy comes out simply because a fuel’s bonds are broken. Mean bond energies are approximate gas-phase averages; they do not exactly describe every molecule or phase. Count the number and type of bonds carefully before substituting values.", "diagram": "bond-energy"}, {"title": "Thermochemical equations and amount scaling", "kind": "learn", "text": "A thermochemical equation specifies a balanced reaction, states and the enthalpy change for the written amounts. CH₄(g) + 2O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l), ΔH = −890 kJ means the reaction as written releases 890 kJ per mole of methane consumed. Doubling all coefficients doubles ΔH. Reversing the reaction reverses the sign. Producing gaseous rather than liquid water gives a different ΔH. If a question asks “energy released,” report a positive magnitude while making the direction clear. If it asks ΔH for the system, include the negative sign for an exothermic process.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Energy profile and activation energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "An energy profile plots system potential energy against reaction progress, not elapsed time. Reactants and products occupy different levels, and the highest point represents a transition-state region along the pathway. Activation energy Eₐ is the energy difference from reactants to that barrier. ΔH is products minus reactants. An exothermic reaction has products lower; an endothermic reaction has products higher. A reaction may be energetically favourable overall yet slow because Eₐ is large. A catalyst supplies a different pathway with lower barriers while leaving reactant and product energies, hence ΔH, unchanged.", "diagram": "energy-profile"}, {"title": "Choose the system before assigning the sign", "kind": "learn", "text": "An exothermic reaction transfers energy from the reacting system to the surroundings and has negative ΔH under the stated convention. An endothermic reaction absorbs energy and has positive ΔH. A warming solution often provides evidence of an exothermic process, but the measured temperature concerns the surroundings rather than a direct reading of reaction enthalpy. Define the boundary and connect energy transfer to the sign. Heat and temperature are different quantities.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Bond breaking absorbs; bond formation releases", "kind": "learn", "text": "A mean bond-energy estimate uses energy to break reactant bonds minus energy released when product bonds form. It is not correct that breaking bonds releases the stored energy by itself. Values are average gas-phase estimates, so the calculation may differ from measured enthalpy involving particular molecules and phases. Count bonds from balanced structures, not only molecular formula letters. A negative net result means formation releases more than breaking absorbs in the model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Scale thermochemical equations consistently", "kind": "learn", "text": "If an equation represents two moles of a product with a specified ΔH, forming one mole has half that change under the same conditions. Reversing the reaction reverses the sign; multiplying every coefficient multiplies ΔH. An energy profile distinguishes activation barrier from reactant–product enthalpy difference. A catalyst lowers a kinetic barrier but does not change the enthalpy of the same overall reaction. Label the quantities rather than treating every vertical difference as ΔH.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Reaction energy and mass", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Reaction energy and mass", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Mean bond-energy estimate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Reaction energy and mass", "prompt": "Methane combustion has ΔH = −890 kJ per mole CH₄. Calculate the heat released by 3.20 g CH₄ using M = 16.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["n(CH₄) = 3.20/16.0 = 0.200 mol. [1]", "System heat = 0.200 × (−890) = −178 kJ. [1]", "Heat released is 178 kJ; the system ΔH is negative. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Mean bond-energy estimate", "prompt": "For H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl, use H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243 and H–Cl 431 kJ mol⁻¹. Estimate ΔH and explain the sign.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Breaking: 436 + 243 = 679 kJ. [1]", "Formation: 2 × 431 = 862 kJ released. [1]", "ΔH ≈ 679 − 862 = −183 kJ per written reaction. [1]", "More energy is released on bond formation than absorbed on breaking, so the reaction is exothermic. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-enthalpy-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A reaction releases 120 kJ when the written equation forms 2.00 mol product. Determine ΔH for the written equation and the energy change for 0.500 mol product. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Release from the reacting system gives ΔH = −120 kJ for the written equation.", "[2] Energy per mole product = −120/2.00 = −60.0 kJ mol⁻¹.", "[2] For 0.500 mol, change = −30.0 kJ under the same stated process/conditions, with the sign convention explicit."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-enthalpy-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A bond-energy model requires 800 kJ mol⁻¹ to break bonds and releases 950 kJ mol⁻¹ on formation. Calculate the net estimate and evaluate one limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Estimate ΔH = energy absorbed in breaking − energy released in formation.", "[2] ΔH ≈ 800−950 = −150 kJ mol⁻¹ on the stated reaction basis.", "[2] Mean gas-phase bond energies and actual molecular/phase conditions can differ; bond breaking itself is endothermic."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the effect of reversing a thermochemical equation?", "o": ["The enthalpy-change sign reverses.", "The sign stays fixed regardless of direction.", "A catalyst changes the product–reactant enthalpy difference.", "Breaking bonds becomes automatically energy-releasing."], "a": 0, "why": "Reversing swaps initial and final states, reversing their enthalpy difference."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 1 – Chemical fundamentals", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-quantities-energy-calorimetry", "title": "Calorimetry, fuel comparisons and uncertainty", "scope": "The measured heat belongs to specified surroundings · Heat loss changes a calculated magnitude · Compare on a consistent basis", "parent": "che11-quantities-energy", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Calorimetry, fuel comparisons and uncertainty</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>The measured heat belongs to specified surroundings</li><li>Heat loss changes a calculated magnitude</li><li>Compare on a consistent basis</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Calorimetry and q = mcΔT", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat gained by a solution is estimated using q = mcΔT, where m is solution mass, c specific heat capacity and ΔT = final − initial temperature. With c in J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, q is in joules. For an insulated reaction, q(reaction) ≈ −q(solution), with additional corrections if the apparatus absorbs heat. Divide reaction heat by moles reacted to estimate molar ΔH. Heat loss to surroundings often makes an exothermic measured temperature rise too small, so the estimated ΔH is less negative than the true value. State assumptions: solution density, heat capacity, negligible apparatus heat and complete reaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Comparing fuel energy and emissions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fuel’s molar combustion energy must be divided by molar mass to compare kJ g⁻¹. For CO₂ per unit energy, use the balanced combustion equation to determine moles of CO₂, then divide its mass by useful or released energy as specified. Methane produces one mole of CO₂ per mole burned; ethanol produces two, but comparing molecule counts alone ignores energy output. Complete combustion, incomplete combustion and real device efficiency affect practical performance. Biofuel production can have land-use and processing impacts. A comparison should state its basis and avoid confusing combustion-stage emissions with full life-cycle greenhouse-gas effects.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluating a fuel calorimetry investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Control water amount, burner distance and initial conditions; repeat trials and measure actual fuel mass consumed. Record maximum temperature consistently and minimise draughts. In an open flame setup, substantial heat escapes and incomplete combustion can occur. Soot may indicate a different combustion pathway, and fuel evaporation can exaggerate apparent fuel consumption. Repetition improves estimates of random variation but does not remove systematic heat loss. An improved method must address a specific limitation, such as an insulated calorimeter with a known heat capacity. Explain how the error affects the calculated energy per gram, rather than listing “human error.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apparatus heat and a corrected model", "kind": "learn", "text": "If the calorimeter has heat capacity Ccal, an improved energy estimate is q(surrounding measured parts) = mcΔT + CcalΔT. Then the reaction heat is its negative under the insulation and completion assumptions. Omitting calorimeter heat underestimates exothermic heat release magnitude. Heat escaping to the room adds a separate loss not captured merely by including Ccal. Explain the direction through the measured temperature rise and energy balance. Use a correction only when supplied or justified data support it; inventing an apparatus constant would create false precision.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The measured heat belongs to specified surroundings", "kind": "learn", "text": "q = mcΔT estimates the heat change of a material with stated mass and specific heat capacity. In a simplified insulated solution calorimeter, reaction heat is approximately the negative of the solution’s heat change. Temperature rise gives positive q for the solution and negative reaction heat. Use the solution’s mass rather than automatically using only the reactant solid mass. If apparatus heat capacity matters, its contribution must be included in the energy accounting.", "diagram": "focused-calorimetry"}, {"title": "Heat loss changes a calculated magnitude", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat transferred to the room or apparatus reduces the observed temperature change relative to the ideal fully captured model. For an exothermic process this often leads to a calculated ΔH that is less negative in magnitude than the true process value, subject to the stated method. Evaporation, incomplete burning and soot can create other biases in fuel experiments. Explain the route from error to measured ΔT to calculated q and final result, rather than saying human error or heat was lost without a direction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare on a consistent basis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat released by a mass of fuel can be expressed per gram or per mole, requiring mass/molar conversions. Different fuel masses or water amounts must not be compared using temperature rise alone. Repeats assess variability but do not remove systematic heat loss. Insulation, suitable apparatus and controlled geometry address particular limitations, with safety and method constraints respected. A conclusion should use the calculated values and qualified boundary, not claim exact industrial performance from a simple school calorimeter.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Calorimetry with assumptions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Calorimetry with assumptions", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Fuel comparison and uncertainty", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Calorimetry with assumptions", "prompt": "A reaction of 0.0500 mol warms 100.0 g solution from 20.0 to 26.0 °C. Use c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹ and neglect apparatus heat. Find molar ΔH.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["ΔT = 6.0 K. [1]", "q(solution) = 100.0 × 4.18 × 6.0 = 2508 J. [1]", "q(reaction) = −2.508 kJ. [1]", "ΔH = −2.508/0.0500 = −50.16 kJ mol⁻¹, approximately −50 kJ mol⁻¹ at the limiting precision. [1]", "Assume negligible external heat loss and solution heat capacity as specified. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Fuel comparison and uncertainty", "prompt": "Fuel A releases 1000 kJ mol⁻¹ and has molar mass 50.0 g mol⁻¹; B releases 1500 kJ mol⁻¹ and has mass 100.0 g mol⁻¹. Compare energy per gram, then evaluate an open-flame experimental claim of exact agreement with these values.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["A: 1000/50.0 = 20.0 kJ g⁻¹. [1]", "B: 1500/100.0 = 15.0 kJ g⁻¹. [1]", "A has greater released energy per mass despite B having greater molar energy. [1]", "Open flame heat loss can lower measured energy magnitude. [1]", "Incomplete combustion or evaporation can introduce additional bias. [1]", "Repeat and improve heat accounting; exact agreement cannot be assumed without uncertainty and method evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-quantities-energy-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-calorimetry-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A fictional solution of mass 100.0 g warms by 5.00 °C. Using c = 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹ and 0.0500 mol reacted, calculate the simplified molar enthalpy change. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] q(solution) = 100.0×4.18×5.00 = 2090 J = 2.09 kJ.", "[2] q(reaction) ≈ −2.09 kJ under the stated heat-capture assumptions.", "[2] ΔH ≈ −2.09/0.0500 = −41.8 kJ mol⁻¹; ignore apparatus/loss only as an explicit model assumption."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-quantities-energy-calorimetry-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A fuel calorimeter loses heat to the room. Explain the likely bias in calculated exothermic enthalpy magnitude and why repeating alone does not remove it. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Less released heat reaches the measured water, reducing observed ΔT.", "[2] Calculated q magnitude is too small and reaction ΔH commonly less negative than the fully captured value.", "[2] Repeats can assess scatter but retain a systematic loss; insulation/calibration/apparatus accounting addresses that pathway."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "If a solution warms in the simplified reaction calorimeter, reaction q is approximately what?", "o": ["Negative, opposite the positive solution heat change.", "Positive because every warming reaction absorbs energy.", "Equal to temperature with no mass or heat capacity.", "Independent of the chosen system boundary."], "a": 0, "why": "The surroundings gain heat while the exothermic reacting system loses it."}, {"q": "Balanced coefficients directly express ratios of", "o": ["Grams only", "Moles of species", "Densities", "Temperatures"], "a": 1, "why": "Convert grams to moles before applying stoichiometric coefficients."}, {"q": "An exothermic system has", "o": ["Positive ΔH", "Negative ΔH", "Zero bond formation", "No activation barrier"], "a": 1, "why": "The system transfers heat to its surroundings."}, {"q": "Bond breaking", "o": ["Always releases heat", "Requires energy", "Creates mass", "Is the sole cause of exothermicity"], "a": 1, "why": "Bond formation releases energy; the overall balance determines ΔH."}, {"q": "For 2H₂ + O₂, 1 mol O₂ can form", "o": ["1 mol H₂O", "2 mol H₂O", "0.5 mol H₂O", "4 mol H₂O"], "a": 1, "why": "The O₂:H₂O coefficient ratio is 1:2 with sufficient hydrogen."}, {"q": "Heat loss in exothermic calorimetry usually makes measured ΔH", "o": ["More negative", "Less negative", "Always positive", "Independent of temperature"], "a": 1, "why": "A reduced temperature rise underestimates released heat magnitude."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-shape-imf-lewis", "title": "Lewis structures: electrons, bonds and lone pairs", "scope": "Start with the complete valence count · Multiple bonds preserve electron accounting · An ion needs an explicit charge representation", "parent": "che11-shape-imf", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Lewis structures: electrons, bonds and lone pairs</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Start with the complete valence count</li><li>Multiple bonds preserve electron accounting</li><li>An ion needs an explicit charge representation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Lewis structures: counting valence electrons", "kind": "learn", "text": "A Lewis structure represents valence electrons as dots or bonding lines. First total the valence electrons, adding electrons for negative charge and subtracting for positive charge. Choose a sensible central atom, connect surrounding atoms with single bonds, complete outer shells where appropriate and allocate remaining electrons. Hydrogen has a two-electron shell and forms one ordinary bond. The usual octet pattern is useful for common main-group molecules but has exceptions. For CO₂, 4 + 2(6) = 16 valence electrons and the final structure is O=C=O with two lone pairs on each oxygen. Check both electron count and atom connectivity.", "diagram": "lewis"}, {"title": "Bonding pairs, lone pairs and multiple bonds", "kind": "learn", "text": "A bonding pair is shared between atoms. A lone pair belongs primarily to one atom in the Lewis representation. A single, double or triple bond contains one, two or three shared pairs. For VSEPR, each bond connection occupies one electron domain irrespective of bond multiplicity. Water has two O–H bonding domains and two lone-pair domains around oxygen. Ammonia has three bonding domains and one lone-pair domain around nitrogen. CO₂ has two bonding domains around carbon and no central lone pairs. Always count domains around the atom whose geometry you are describing, not the total electrons in the whole molecule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Lewis electrons must actually be shown", "kind": "learn", "text": "For NH₃, nitrogen contributes five valence electrons and three hydrogens contribute three, totaling eight. Three N–H bonding pairs use six, leaving one lone pair. For H₂O, eight total valence electrons form two bonding pairs and two lone pairs. For CO₂, sixteen total electrons give two double bonds and two lone pairs on each oxygen. A displayed bond line counts as two electrons. Verify the drawing’s electron total and show the lone pairs when a Lewis structure is requested. A molecular-shape sketch without dots is not a complete Lewis answer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Start with the complete valence count", "kind": "learn", "text": "Count valence electrons from every atom and adjust for net charge. A negative charge adds electrons to the represented ion; a positive charge subtracts them. Choose a suitable skeleton, distribute bonding electrons and remaining lone pairs, then check the total and required valence patterns. Hydrogen normally has a duet in these examples, not an octet. A diagram omitting lone-pair electrons is not a complete Lewis structure when their representation is requested.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Multiple bonds preserve electron accounting", "kind": "learn", "text": "A double or triple bond shares more than one pair between the same atoms. It uses the corresponding electrons but counts as one electron-density region for ordinary VSEPR geometry. Do not add a multiple bond without checking electron totals and terminal/central valence. Lewis structures represent electron-pair connectivity and distribution, not the exact three-dimensional shape or every feature of electron delocalisation. Use them as the starting model for a separate shape explanation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "An ion needs an explicit charge representation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Enclose a polyatomic Lewis structure appropriately and show its overall charge, while maintaining correct atom connectivity. Formal charge or resonance considerations can qualify simple structures where relevant, but the introductory focus is correct electron count and bonds/lone pairs. A molecular formula alone cannot show whether a lone pair is present. Check the completed drawing against the stated species before applying shape or polarity: an incorrect electron model makes a later confident explanation unreliable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "che11-shape-imf-lewis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Draw the Lewis structure of NH₃ and show the total valence count. Explain why its lone pair must be represented. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] N contributes five and three H contribute three, giving eight valence electrons.", "[2] Three N–H single bonds use six electrons; one lone pair on N uses the remaining two.", "[2] H has its duet and the N lone pair influences electron-region arrangement/molecular shape; omitting it makes the requested Lewis structure incomplete."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-lewis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain electron counting for H₂O and distinguish a double bond’s electron pairs from its number of VSEPR regions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] H₂O has 6+2 = eight valence electrons.", "[2] Two O–H bonds use four electrons and two O lone pairs use four.", "[2] A double bond contains two bonding pairs but is one electron-density region in ordinary VSEPR counting; pair count and region count are different."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "A double bond counts as how many ordinary VSEPR electron regions?", "o": ["One.", "Two because it contains two bonding pairs.", "Zero because it is covalent.", "Four regardless of the molecule."], "a": 0, "why": "Geometry uses regions of electron density, not the raw number of shared pairs."}], "subtitle": "12 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-shape-imf-shape", "title": "VSEPR shape and molecular polarity", "scope": "Electron-region geometry differs from molecular shape · Molecular polarity requires a vector comparison · The model simplifies rather than guarantees every angle", "parent": "che11-shape-imf", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>VSEPR shape and molecular polarity</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Electron-region geometry differs from molecular shape</li><li>Molecular polarity requires a vector comparison</li><li>The model simplifies rather than guarantees every angle</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "VSEPR: repulsion and geometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Valence-shell electron-pair repulsion models regions of electron density as arranging themselves to reduce repulsion around a central atom. Two domains favour a linear arrangement at 180°, three a trigonal planar arrangement near 120°, and four a tetrahedral arrangement near 109.5°. Electron-domain geometry includes lone pairs; molecular shape describes the positions of atoms. A tetrahedral domain arrangement can therefore give tetrahedral, trigonal pyramidal or bent molecular shapes depending on lone pairs. VSEPR predicts approximate shapes rather than exact atom sizes or all bond angles. A flat drawing uses wedges or other cues to represent three-dimensional arrangement.", "diagram": "vsepr"}, {"title": "Linear and trigonal planar examples", "kind": "learn", "text": "CO₂ is linear because carbon has two bonding domains and no lone pairs. The double bonds count as domains, not four separate directions. Boron trifluoride has three bonding domains and no central lone pair, giving a trigonal planar structure. Boron’s incomplete octet in this molecule is an important reminder that not every stable structure follows a simple octet rule. All atoms in a trigonal planar central arrangement lie approximately in one plane. Identify the central atom, count domains, give geometry and state the angle. Geometry and bond polarity must then be considered together when deciding overall molecular polarity.", "diagram": "linear-planar"}, {"title": "Tetrahedral, pyramidal and bent molecules", "kind": "learn", "text": "CH₄ has four bonding domains, giving a tetrahedral shape. NH₃ has three bonds and one lone pair, so its atoms form a trigonal pyramid. H₂O has two bonds and two lone pairs, so it is bent. Lone-pair electron density occupies more space near the central atom than a bonding pair, tending to compress bond angles. Approximate familiar values are 109.5° for methane, 107° for ammonia and 104.5° for water. These values illustrate a trend rather than a universal formula for every molecule with the same domain count. The lone pairs are essential to explaining the shapes.", "diagram": "shapes"}, {"title": "Bond polarity and partial charges", "kind": "learn", "text": "A covalent bond becomes polar when its atoms attract shared electrons unequally. The more electronegative atom receives δ− and the other δ+. In H–Cl, chlorine is δ−. The shared electrons remain part of the covalent bonding; partial charges are not complete electron transfer into free ions. A bond-dipole arrow often points toward the more electronegative end, with a crossed tail at the positive end. State the arrow convention if your diagram uses one. Compare electronegativity using the data provided. Polarity depends on electron distribution, not whether the substance is currently dissolved in water.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Molecular polarity and vector cancellation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Overall polarity depends on the vector sum of bond dipoles and molecular symmetry. CO₂ has polar C=O bonds but a linear symmetric shape, so the equal opposite dipoles cancel and the molecule is non-polar. Water has polar O–H bonds in a bent arrangement; they do not cancel, so it is polar. CH₄ is symmetric and non-polar, while CH₃Cl is not equivalently substituted and is polar. “Contains a polar bond” is therefore insufficient to establish a polar molecule. Draw the actual geometry, indicate relevant dipoles and explain cancellation or lack of cancellation.", "diagram": "polarity"}, {"title": "Model limitations and unfamiliar molecules", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lewis structures are two-dimensional electron bookkeeping diagrams. VSEPR approximates spatial arrangement through repulsion. Dipole arrows represent charge distribution and vector direction. None shows realistic atom scale, electron motion or every energetic contribution. For an unfamiliar molecule, count electron domains, predict shape, assess bond polarities and cancellation, then identify intermolecular forces. If the molecule is an ion, include its charge and recognise that interactions can differ from those of a neutral molecule. Explain how the evidence supports the model and which predictions are qualitative. A diagram is useful only when its labels and reasoning are understood.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Electron-region geometry differs from molecular shape", "kind": "learn", "text": "VSEPR models repulsion between electron-density regions around a central atom. Four regions have a tetrahedral arrangement; with four bonded atoms the molecular shape is tetrahedral, with three plus one lone pair it is pyramidal, and with two plus two lone pairs it is bent. Molecular shape describes atom positions rather than invisible lone-pair vertices. State both region count and bonding/lone-pair distribution before naming the shape. Multiple bonds count as one region for this purpose.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Molecular polarity requires a vector comparison", "kind": "learn", "text": "Bond polarity reflects unequal sharing due to electronegativity difference. Molecular polarity also depends on how bond dipoles combine in the three-dimensional structure. CO₂ has polar bonds but its linear symmetric arrangement supports cancellation; H₂O’s bent arrangement does not cancel its bond dipoles. A molecule is not automatically polar because it contains one polar bond, nor automatically nonpolar because its overall charge is zero. Draw direction or explain symmetry explicitly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The model simplifies rather than guarantees every angle", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ideal geometries provide approximate angles, while lone pairs and bonding details can change actual values. VSEPR can make useful predictions within the specified scope but is not a precise calculation of every molecular structure. For unfamiliar examples, work from the supplied Lewis structure and region count rather than memorising an unrelated molecule. The exam explanation should connect electron arrangement, atom geometry and dipole outcome in the requested order.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Shape and polarity of water", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Shape and polarity of water", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Ammonia model", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Shape and polarity of water", "prompt": "Use electron domains to explain water’s shape and polarity.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["O has two bonding domains and two lone-pair domains. [1]", "Four domains have an approximately tetrahedral electron-domain arrangement. [1]", "With only two bonded atoms, the molecular shape is bent. [1]", "O–H bonds are polar toward oxygen. [1]", "The bent arrangement prevents dipole cancellation, giving a polar molecule. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A non-polar molecule with polar bonds", "prompt": "Explain why CO₂ is non-polar despite containing polar C=O bonds.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["O is more electronegative, so each C=O bond is polar. [1]", "CO₂ is linear and symmetric. [1]", "The equal bond dipoles oppose and cancel. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ammonia model", "prompt": "Draw a Lewis structure of NH₃, predict its molecular shape and identify its intermolecular forces.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["N has three N–H bonding pairs and one lone pair; the drawing accounts for eight valence electrons. [2]", "Trigonal pyramidal shape. [1]", "It is polar and can form hydrogen bonds between molecules. [1]", "Dispersion forces and dipole–dipole interactions also occur. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q5", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-shape-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why CO₂ is nonpolar but H₂O is polar even though both contain polar bonds. Include shape. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CO₂ has two electron regions about C and a linear symmetric O=C=O shape.", "[2] Equal opposite C–O bond dipoles cancel, producing no net molecular dipole.", "[2] Water has four regions including two lone pairs and a bent shape, so O–H dipoles do not cancel."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-shape-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "For a central atom with three bonds and one lone pair, state electron-region arrangement and molecular shape, then explain the distinction. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Four regions have an approximately tetrahedral electron-region arrangement.", "[2] Three bonded atoms plus one lone pair give a trigonal pyramidal molecular shape.", "[2] Shape describes atom positions while region arrangement includes the lone pair; ideal angles can be modified by repulsion details."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why is linear CO₂ nonpolar?", "o": ["Its equal opposite bond dipoles cancel.", "Its C–O bonds are necessarily nonpolar.", "Every molecule containing oxygen is nonpolar.", "Its total charge alone determines all molecular polarity."], "a": 0, "why": "Molecular polarity depends on bond polarity and geometry, not the presence of polar bonds alone."}], "subtitle": "20 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-shape-imf-forces", "title": "Dispersion, dipoles, hydrogen bonding and ion–dipole", "scope": "Dispersion is present even when another force is named · Hydrogen bonding needs correct connectivity · Ion–dipole concerns a full ionic charge", "parent": "che11-shape-imf", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Dispersion, dipoles, hydrogen bonding and ion–dipole</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Dispersion is present even when another force is named</li><li>Hydrogen bonding needs correct connectivity</li><li>Ion–dipole concerns a full ionic charge</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Dispersion forces in every molecule", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electron distributions fluctuate, creating temporary dipoles that can induce dipoles in neighbouring particles. These dispersion forces occur in all atoms and molecules, including non-polar species and substances that also hydrogen bond. Larger, more polarisable electron clouds generally produce stronger dispersion attraction. Shape affects how closely molecules can contact one another. Do not equate dispersion strength simply with the number of bonds or with molecular mass in unrelated substances. A small hydrogen-bonding molecule need not boil above every large non-polar molecule. Compare the complete combination of forces and molecular size.", "diagram": "dispersion"}, {"title": "Permanent dipole–dipole attraction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Polar molecules have permanent uneven charge distributions. Opposite partial charges on neighbouring molecules attract, producing dipole–dipole interactions in addition to dispersion forces. A useful diagram shows δ+ near δ− between molecules, not unlike charges within a single bond being labelled as an intermolecular force. Molecules move and rotate, so not every orientation is equally favourable at each instant. Dipole–dipole attraction often raises boiling point when comparing similarly sized molecules, but shape, polarisability and hydrogen bonding must also be considered. Polarity does not automatically imply that a molecule can donate a hydrogen bond.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydrogen bonding: donor and acceptor", "kind": "learn", "text": "A common ATAR hydrogen-bond model involves a hydrogen bonded covalently to N, O or F interacting with a lone pair on N, O or F of another molecule. An O–H bond is a covalent bond within a molecule. An O···H attraction between molecules is a hydrogen bond. Water and ethanol can both donate and accept. A ketone can accept hydrogen bonds from water through oxygen but cannot hydrogen bond to an identical ketone as a donor because it lacks O–H or N–H. All these molecules also experience dispersion forces. The dotted line in the model must connect the correct donor hydrogen and acceptor atom.", "diagram": "hydrogen-bond"}, {"title": "Ion–dipole differs from dipole–dipole", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ion interacting with a polar molecule experiences ion–dipole attraction. Na⁺ in water is stabilised by the oxygen-rich partial-negative region, while Cl⁻ interacts with partial-positive hydrogen regions. Dipole–dipole attraction instead refers to permanent partial charges on neutral polar molecules. A water-solubility comparison for an ionic salt needs ion–dipole language, not merely hydrogen bonding. A charge label should distinguish a full ionic charge from δ notation. The named attraction must connect the actual interacting species, because generic force labels without chemical context do not fully explain a property.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Dispersion is present even when another force is named", "kind": "learn", "text": "Instantaneous and induced dipoles support dispersion attractions in all molecules. Polar molecules also have permanent dipole–dipole attraction; suitable N–H, O–H or F–H donors and appropriate acceptor lone pairs allow hydrogen bonding. These forces are not mutually exclusive labels. Identify every relevant attraction for the specific species before comparing strength. Merely containing hydrogen or nitrogen does not guarantee a donor, and a carbon-bound hydrogen is not the usual strong hydrogen-bond donor in these syllabus examples.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydrogen bonding needs correct connectivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ethanol molecule can donate through O–H and accept at oxygen lone pairs. An ether oxygen can accept hydrogen bonds from water but lacks an O–H donor of its own. Drawing the dotted attraction between two positively polarised hydrogen atoms misrepresents the interaction. Show the donor H towards an appropriate electronegative acceptor and keep covalent bonds distinct from intermolecular notation. A correct site explanation is more useful than calling every dotted line a weak bond.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Ion–dipole concerns a full ionic charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hydrated ions interact with oriented polar water molecules. The oxygen-rich negative end points towards cations, while positively polarised hydrogens orient towards anions. This ion–dipole attraction differs from ordinary molecular dipole–dipole attraction because one participant carries net charge. Explaining dissolution requires balancing solute separation and new solute–solvent interactions, not asserting that water chemically converts every ion into a molecule. Name the specific attraction and orientation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Intermolecular site identification", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Intermolecular site identification", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Intermolecular site identification", "prompt": "Explain why propanone can hydrogen bond with water but does not form ordinary donor–acceptor hydrogen bonds between identical propanone molecules.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Its carbonyl oxygen has lone pairs and can accept a hydrogen bond. [1]", "Water provides an O–H donor hydrogen. [1]", "Propanone has no N–H, O–H or F–H group to act as that donor to itself. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-forces-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare ethanol and an ether’s hydrogen-bond roles with water. Explain why having oxygen alone does not make them identical donors. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ethanol has an O–H group that can donate and oxygen lone pairs that can accept suitable hydrogen bonds.", "[2] An ether oxygen can accept from water but lacks its own O–H donor in the ordinary example.", "[2] Donor/acceptor connectivity matters; both also have dispersion and other appropriate attractions, not one exclusive force."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-forces-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Describe water orientation around Na⁺ and Cl⁻ and name the principal ion–water attraction. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Water’s partially negative oxygen end orients towards Na⁺.", "[2] Its partially positive hydrogen ends orient towards Cl⁻.", "[2] The relevant attraction is ion–dipole between full ionic charge and molecular dipole, distinguished from molecule–molecule dipole attraction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which way does water principally orient around a cation?", "o": ["Its oxygen end towards the cation.", "Both hydrogen ends towards the positive ion by electrostatic attraction.", "No orientation because water is nonpolar.", "The nucleus changes into the cation."], "a": 0, "why": "The partially negative oxygen end is attracted to positive ionic charge."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-shape-imf-properties", "title": "Boiling point, vapour pressure and solubility", "scope": "Use the energy required to separate molecules · Vapour pressure reflects dynamic escape and return · Solubility and melting are not one-force rankings", "parent": "che11-shape-imf", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Boiling point, vapour pressure and solubility</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use the energy required to separate molecules</li><li>Vapour pressure reflects dynamic escape and return</li><li>Solubility and melting are not one-force rankings</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Boiling point explained through forces", "kind": "learn", "text": "A liquid boils when its vapour pressure equals external pressure. Stronger intermolecular attractions usually require a higher temperature to achieve this, because more energy is required for molecules to escape into the vapour. Compare ethanol and dimethyl ether of similar molecular formula: ethanol can form hydrogen bonds between its molecules, while the ether lacks a hydrogen-bond donor, so ethanol has a substantially higher boiling point. Explain the species, forces and energy link. Boiling ordinary molecular liquids does not break their internal covalent bonds. Use “overcomes intermolecular attractions” rather than “breaks the molecules.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vapour pressure and temperature", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a closed container, molecules escape from liquid and return by condensation. At equilibrium, vapour pressure depends on temperature and substance. At the same temperature, a liquid with weaker intermolecular attraction generally has a greater equilibrium vapour pressure. Raising temperature increases the fraction of molecules able to escape, increasing vapour pressure. The equilibrium remains dynamic even when no visible change occurs. Evaporation can occur below boiling point; boiling forms bubbles throughout the liquid when vapour pressure reaches external pressure. A lower external pressure lowers the boiling temperature. Keep external pressure distinct from the substance’s equilibrium vapour pressure.", "diagram": "vapour"}, {"title": "Melting and solubility need more than a rule", "kind": "learn", "text": "Melting involves disrupting a solid’s ordered arrangement and depends on crystal structure, molecular symmetry and attraction. Boiling trends cannot always be transferred directly to melting. Dissolution requires breaking some solute–solute and solvent–solvent interactions while forming solute–solvent interactions, with entropy also contributing. “Like dissolves like” is a useful pattern, not a complete mechanism. Polar and hydrogen-bonding groups can favour water solubility, while a large non-polar carbon region can oppose it. Use a specific interaction such as hydrogen bonding or ion–dipole attraction. A molecule may be polar yet only sparingly soluble.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A four-part exam explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "To compare molecular properties, begin with the relevant structure. Next identify molecular polarity and any hydrogen-bond donor and acceptor sites. Then name the intermolecular attractions, including dispersion. Finally connect their combined strength to the energy required for separation or to solvent interactions. This sequence avoids jumping from a formula directly to a boiling-point claim. Match detail to the question: a three-mark comparison might credit the structural distinction, the force difference and the energy consequence. For substantially different molecular sizes, explicitly consider polarisability rather than assuming hydrogen bonding must always dominate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the energy required to separate molecules", "kind": "learn", "text": "Stronger relevant intermolecular attractions generally require more energy for vaporisation and can raise boiling point in a fair comparison. Size/polarisability, hydrogen-bond capacity, dipole and shape can all matter. Do not rank any hydrogen-bonding molecule above every larger non-hydrogen-bonding molecule without examining the actual species. The explanation concerns separation of molecules, not universal breaking of covalent bonds. A boiling-point answer needs the named forces and why their overall effect differs.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Vapour pressure reflects dynamic escape and return", "kind": "learn", "text": "At a fixed temperature in a closed equilibrium system, vapour pressure depends on the balance of molecular escape and condensation. Increasing temperature increases the proportion with sufficient energy to escape and typically raises vapour pressure. At comparable temperature stronger attractions commonly lower vapour pressure for comparable substances. Boiling occurs when vapour pressure reaches the external pressure. State the fixed condition; a warmer strongly attracted liquid need not have lower pressure than every colder weakly attracted one.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solubility and melting are not one-force rankings", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dissolution depends on solute–solute, solvent–solvent and solute–solvent interactions and thermodynamic factors. Like dissolves like is a guide, not a complete explanation. Melting depends on solid packing as well as interactions, so an isomer boiling trend need not predict the same melting trend. Apply the functional sites and chain size explicitly. A full comparison links structure to interactions and the particular property rather than repeating stronger forces for every possible result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A boiling comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A boiling comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Vapour pressure reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: An unfamiliar molecular comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A boiling comparison", "prompt": "Ethanol and dimethyl ether both have formula C₂H₆O. Explain why ethanol’s boiling point is higher.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ethanol has an O–H group, while dimethyl ether does not. [1]", "Ethanol molecules can hydrogen bond to one another. [1]", "The ether has dispersion and dipole–dipole interactions but no identical-molecule hydrogen-bond donor. [1]", "More energy is required to separate ethanol molecules, increasing boiling point. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Vapour pressure reasoning", "prompt": "At the same temperature, liquid A has weaker intermolecular attractions than similarly sized liquid B. Predict their relative vapour pressures and justify.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["A has the greater equilibrium vapour pressure. [1]", "Its molecules require less energy to escape intermolecular attraction. [1]", "A larger fraction enters the vapour at the given temperature until dynamic equilibrium is established. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "An unfamiliar molecular comparison", "prompt": "Compare CH₄, CH₃Cl and CH₃OH for molecular polarity and possible intermolecular forces. Explain why a boiling comparison must also consider molecular size.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["CH₄ is symmetric and non-polar, with dispersion between molecules. [1]", "CH₃Cl is unsymmetrically substituted and polar. [1]", "It has dispersion and dipole–dipole interactions. [1]", "CH₃OH is polar and has an O–H hydrogen-bond donor plus oxygen acceptor. [1]", "Methanol therefore also hydrogen bonds between molecules. [1]", "Polarisability and size affect dispersion, so a force label alone is not a universal boiling ranking. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-shape-imf-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-properties-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare ethanol and propane of similar molar mass for boiling point, naming the relevant forces and the physical change. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Both have dispersion attractions; ethanol also has polarity and suitable intermolecular hydrogen bonding.", "[2] Ethanol’s overall attractions in this comparison require more energy to separate molecules, supporting a higher boiling point.", "[2] Boiling overcomes intermolecular attraction without ordinarily breaking all covalent bonds inside the molecules."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-shape-imf-properties-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the effect of increasing temperature on a liquid’s equilibrium vapour pressure, then distinguish boiling-point reasoning from melting-point reasoning. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] More molecules have sufficient energy to escape as temperature rises, and equilibrium vapour pressure generally increases.", "[2] Boiling depends on vapour pressure reaching external pressure.", "[2] Melting also depends on solid packing; a boiling/isomer trend cannot automatically determine melting without structural evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "At a fixed temperature, stronger attractions commonly have what effect on comparable liquids?", "o": ["Lower equilibrium vapour pressure.", "Always more molecules escaping at the same rate regardless of attraction.", "Automatic decomposition into atoms.", "A guarantee of identical melting points."], "a": 0, "why": "Escape is less favourable under stronger attractions in a suitable same-temperature comparison."}, {"q": "Water has how many electron domains around oxygen?", "o": ["2", "3", "4", "6"], "a": 2, "why": "Two bonding domains and two lone pairs give four domains."}, {"q": "A double bond counts in VSEPR as", "o": ["One domain", "Two directions", "Four domains", "No electron density"], "a": 0, "why": "Multiple bonding along one atom connection occupies one domain."}, {"q": "Dispersion forces occur in", "o": ["Non-polar molecules only", "All molecules", "Only ionic solids", "Only water"], "a": 1, "why": "Temporary fluctuations can occur in every molecular electron cloud."}, {"q": "CO₂ is non-polar because", "o": ["Its bonds are non-polar", "It has no electrons", "Its linear bond dipoles cancel", "O has lower electronegativity"], "a": 2, "why": "Geometry cancels its polar bond contributions."}, {"q": "Boiling a molecular liquid normally", "o": ["Breaks all covalent bonds", "Overcomes intermolecular attractions", "Changes nuclei", "Requires ion formation"], "a": 1, "why": "Molecules ordinarily remain intact in the vapour."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-tlc", "title": "TLC, retention factors and identification", "scope": "Retention depends on both phases · Measure from the same origin · A matching value supports but does not prove identity", "parent": "che11-chromatography-gases", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>TLC, retention factors and identification</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Retention depends on both phases</li><li>Measure from the same origin</li><li>A matching value supports but does not prove identity</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Two phases in chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chromatography uses a stationary phase that remains in place and a mobile phase that carries components through or over it. Substances interact differently with the two phases, so they spend different proportions of time moving. A component more strongly retained by the stationary phase usually moves more slowly under the same conditions. Separation depends on chemical interactions, not simply particle size or mass. The choice of solvent and stationary material changes the result. Two substances may fail to separate under one set of conditions and separate under another. Label the phases and direction of movement in any explanatory diagram.", "diagram": "tlc"}, {"title": "Thin-layer chromatography procedure", "kind": "learn", "text": "TLC uses a thin stationary coating such as silica on a plate. Place small sample spots on a pencil baseline above the starting solvent level. The mobile solvent rises by capillary action. Mark the solvent front promptly after removing the plate and visualise spots if needed. If the baseline is submerged, samples can dissolve directly into the reservoir and undermine separation. Pencil avoids introducing soluble ink dyes. Use the same plate, solvent and conditions for samples and reference standards. Large overloaded spots may overlap, reducing interpretability. Practical decisions should be explained through their effects on separation and measurement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Retention factor calculations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For TLC, Rf = distance travelled by centre of component spot / distance travelled by solvent front, both measured from the same baseline. Rf is dimensionless and is normally between 0 and 1. A spot 3.0 cm from the baseline with solvent front 6.0 cm gives Rf = 0.50. Measuring from the bottom of the plate instead introduces a systematic error. Rf depends on the phase materials and conditions and is not an intrinsic constant valid in every solvent. Similar Rf values support identity when references run under matching conditions, but co-migration does not prove two samples contain the same substance.", "diagram": "rf"}, {"title": "Purity and mixture identification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Several separated spots show that a sample contains at least several detectable components under those conditions. One spot supports apparent purity but does not prove absolute purity: substances can co-migrate or fail to be detected. Compare unknown spots with known standards run together. A shared position is evidence that a component may match, strengthened by repeating with a different solvent system. Do not count spot darkness as mole quantity without a validated calibration. An exam answer should distinguish observation, inference and limitation: “two spots” is observed; “at least two detectable components” is inferred; hidden overlap remains possible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choosing phases changes retention", "kind": "learn", "text": "On a common polar silica TLC phase with a suitable mobile solvent, stronger polar stationary interactions often give lower Rf. In a reversed-phase HPLC arrangement, the relatively non-polar stationary material can retain non-polar substances more strongly. These are contextual tendencies, not one rule for all chromatography. The solvent competes for interactions and changing it can alter separation. When comparing retention times or Rf, state the stationary phase, mobile conditions and relevant molecular groups. Do not identify a compound by retention data from a different column or solvent without verification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Retention depends on both phases", "kind": "learn", "text": "A component distributes between a stationary phase and a moving phase. Its movement depends on relative interactions with both, not merely whether it is polar in isolation. In ordinary silica TLC a strongly adsorbed component may move less under a specified solvent, while changing the mobile phase can alter that ranking. Name the stationary and mobile conditions before predicting a result. A chromatographic spot is evidence of a separated component under those conditions, not a complete molecular identification.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Measure from the same origin", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rf equals the distance travelled by the component from the origin divided by the solvent-front distance from the same origin. The ratio is dimensionless and usually between zero and one for an ordinary separated spot within the front. Do not measure from the plate edge if the origin was higher, or use spot diameter as travel distance. Mark the solvent front before it disappears and compare standards under matching conditions. Overloading or submerging the origin can distort interpretation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A matching value supports but does not prove identity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Two compounds can co-migrate or have similar Rf under one set of conditions. A sample with one spot can contain unresolved components. Use appropriate reference standards, another solvent system or complementary evidence where needed. If an unknown matches a standard on the same plate, this is stronger comparative evidence than using a value obtained in a different system. Explain the limit rather than declaring every single spot pure with certainty.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Rf and comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Rf and comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: A chromatography claim", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Rf and comparison", "prompt": "A TLC spot travels 4.20 cm and the solvent front 7.00 cm from the baseline. Calculate Rf. A reference has the same Rf under these conditions. Is identity proven?", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Rf = 4.20/7.00. [1]", "Rf = 0.600, dimensionless. [1]", "Matching retention supports a possible identity under the same conditions. [1]", "It does not prove identity because different substances can co-migrate. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A chromatography claim", "prompt": "A sample gives one HPLC peak. Evaluate the claim that it is certainly pure and suggest a stronger investigation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["One detected peak supports apparent purity under the chosen conditions. [1]", "Components may overlap or fail to be detected. [1]", "Use another separation condition or independent analytical method. [1]", "Compare standards and blank runs under matched conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-tlc-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A spot travels 3.20 cm from the origin and the solvent front 8.00 cm. Calculate Rf and explain why a match with a standard is not complete proof of identity. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Rf = 3.20/8.00.", "[2] Rf = 0.400, dimensionless and measured from the same origin.", "[2] Different compounds can co-migrate; matching conditions and additional evidence improve but do not make one ratio universal proof."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-tlc-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A student measures the spot from the bottom of the plate but the front from the origin line. Evaluate the calculation and propose two procedural protections. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distances use different reference points, making the ratio invalid for the stated definition.", "[2] Measure both from the origin and promptly mark the front.", "[2] Keep the origin above solvent, use suitable loading and compare a standard under matching conditions; identify the protection relevant to the described error."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Rf is calculated using what?", "o": ["Spot travel divided by solvent-front travel from the same origin.", "Spot diameter divided by plate width.", "Different origin points for numerator and denominator.", "A mass in grams divided by concentration."], "a": 0, "why": "Both distances share a reference point, and their ratio is dimensionless."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-instrumental", "title": "GC, HPLC and quantitative chromatograms", "scope": "Choose a technique from the sample’s properties · Peak area needs an appropriate response relationship · Combine identity and quantity evidence carefully", "parent": "che11-chromatography-gases", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>GC, HPLC and quantitative chromatograms</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Choose a technique from the sample’s properties</li><li>Peak area needs an appropriate response relationship</li><li>Combine identity and quantity evidence carefully</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Gas chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "GC uses a carrier gas as mobile phase and a stationary material within a column. A vaporised sample’s components interact differently with the stationary phase and travel to a detector at different retention times. Components must be sufficiently volatile and thermally stable under the chosen conditions. A chromatogram displays detector response against time. Peaks correspond to detected components, while a reference comparison helps identify them. Retention time depends on column and operating conditions. Molecular mass alone does not determine the order because volatility and stationary-phase affinity both matter. Calibration is necessary for quantitative use of peak areas.", "diagram": "chromatogram"}, {"title": "High-performance liquid chromatography", "kind": "learn", "text": "HPLC pumps a liquid mobile phase through a packed column under high pressure. It can analyse less volatile or heat-sensitive compounds that may not suit GC. Separation reflects differences in interactions with the stationary and mobile phases. In reversed-phase HPLC, a relatively non-polar stationary phase can retain non-polar components more strongly, but the specific mobile-phase composition also matters. Avoid a universal rule that “polar always comes first” without specifying the system. A detector records peaks. Changing solvent composition, flow rate or column material changes retention, so unknown and standard data must be comparable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reading a chromatogram quantitatively", "kind": "learn", "text": "Peak retention time is commonly used in identification comparisons. Peak area can support quantity when detector response and calibration are known. Equal peak areas do not automatically mean equal masses for different substances because response factors can differ. Overlapping peaks can hide components. A blank run can reveal contamination, and replicate injections can assess instrumental repeatability. To estimate composition from area, state the assumptions or use supplied response-factor data. A chromatogram does not directly show molecular structure. Combine it with known references, chemical tests and other analytical evidence as the question requires.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combining analytical evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "To identify an unknown, compare independent evidence rather than relying on one clue. TLC may suggest several components, GC may separate volatile constituents, and a gas-producing reaction can quantify one reactive component. Each method has a domain and limitations. An appropriate method depends on sample amount, volatility, stability, expected concentration and available calibration. Evaluate sensitivity and specificity separately: detecting a small amount is different from uniquely identifying it. Report a supported conclusion with its uncertainty. A persuasive exam response uses actual supplied peak times, distances or gas volumes rather than repeating a generic technique description.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choose a technique from the sample’s properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "GC uses a gaseous mobile phase and suits components that can vaporise without unacceptable decomposition under the method. HPLC uses a liquid mobile phase and can analyse suitable less volatile or heat-sensitive components. Both involve separation through phase interactions, with detectors recording signals. Retention time depends on conditions and does not uniquely identify a compound in every instrument. The technique must fit the analyte, separation requirement and available calibration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Peak area needs an appropriate response relationship", "kind": "learn", "text": "A larger peak area often indicates more analyte under calibrated conditions, but different compounds can have different detector responses. Equal areas therefore need not mean equal amounts without suitable response factors. Standards, blanks and a calibration range allow quantity to be related to the signal. Peak height and area are not interchangeable when widths differ. An unresolved combined peak can also distort apparent purity and amount, so integration needs chemical interpretation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combine identity and quantity evidence carefully", "kind": "learn", "text": "Retention matching against a standard under the same conditions supports identification; area calibration supports quantification. These are different inferences. Changing flow, temperature or solvent can alter retention and separation. A sample requiring both trace quantification and structural identification may need complementary methods. Evaluate the actual purpose and uncertainty, not simply label an instrument modern and therefore infallible. A clear chromatogram includes time, response, relevant units and the calibration basis. Applications include forensic analysis, drug testing in urine or blood, monitoring air and water pollutants and food-additive or quality testing. Method choice also depends on sample amount and required sensitivity, not only volatility. A trace peak needs calibration, blanks, contamination control and suitable identification evidence. In a forensic claim, co-elution, sample handling and reference conditions limit the inference; detecting a signal alone does not establish the source or circumstances. Explain what the method measures and which conclusion the data support, then identify any additional evidence needed.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Interpreting detector areas", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Interpreting detector areas", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Choosing an analytical method", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Interpreting detector areas", "prompt": "Two GC peaks have calibrated response factors of 2.0 area units per mg for A and 1.0 for B. Their areas are both 10.0 units. Find mass percentage of A.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["m(A) = 10.0/2.0 = 5.0 mg. [1]", "m(B) = 10.0/1.0 = 10.0 mg. [1]", "Total mass = 15.0 mg. [1]", "A mass percentage = 5.0/15.0 × 100 = approximately 33%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Choosing an analytical method", "prompt": "A heat-sensitive dissolved compound is to be separated from related impurities. Compare HPLC with GC, explain what one HPLC peak can establish and give a method improvement.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["HPLC uses a liquid mobile phase and need not vaporise the sample in the same way as GC. [1]", "GC generally requires adequate volatility and thermal stability under its conditions. [1]", "HPLC may therefore be more suitable for this compound. [1]", "One peak supports apparent purity under the conditions. [1]", "Co-elution or detector insensitivity may hide impurities. [1]", "Use alternative conditions, reference standards and an independent method to strengthen the conclusion. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-instrumental-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A calibration gives peak area 200 units for 2.00 mg L⁻¹ and 400 for 4.00 mg L⁻¹, with a linear zero-intercept model. A sample gives 300 units. Calculate its concentration and state one assumption. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Response factor = 200/2.00 = 100 area units per mg L⁻¹.", "[2] Sample concentration = 300/100 = 3.00 mg L⁻¹.", "[2] Assume the same analyte/conditions, linear calibrated response and no unresolved interference; the result concerns the tested sample."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-instrumental-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare GC and HPLC for a heat-sensitive nonvolatile sample, then evaluate equal peak areas as proof of equal component amounts. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] HPLC can suit a compatible nonvolatile/heat-sensitive analyte because the mobile phase is liquid.", "[2] GC requires suitable vaporisation and thermal stability under the conditions, so it may be inappropriate here.", "[2] Detector response differs between compounds; equal areas need suitable response factors before implying equal amounts."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why do equal chromatographic peak areas not always mean equal amounts?", "o": ["Different compounds can have different detector response factors.", "Every detector always responds identically to every species.", "Peak area is unrelated to calibration under any circumstances.", "Retention time measures atomic number directly."], "a": 0, "why": "Calibration must account for the analyte and detector response."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-model", "title": "Kinetic theory, gas pressure and temperature", "scope": "Pressure is a collision model · Control the variables in a gas-law explanation · Average motion differs from every particle’s motion", "parent": "che11-chromatography-gases", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Kinetic theory, gas pressure and temperature</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Pressure is a collision model</li><li>Control the variables in a gas-law explanation</li><li>Average motion differs from every particle’s motion</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Kinetic theory of an ideal gas", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ideal-gas model treats particles as moving continuously and randomly, with negligible particle volume relative to the container and negligible intermolecular attraction. Collisions are elastic in the ideal model. Gas pressure arises from particles transferring momentum to container walls, not from the particles being “compressed” internally. Temperature in kelvin relates to average kinetic energy. Particles have a distribution of speeds; they do not all move at the same speed. Real gases deviate more at high pressure and low temperature because volume and attractions become significant. Use the model to explain trends while stating these limitations.", "diagram": "gas-particles"}, {"title": "Pressure and volume at constant temperature", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, reducing volume increases the frequency of collisions with each unit area of the container wall, so pressure rises. The average particle kinetic energy remains constant because temperature is fixed. The ideal relationship is inverse, P ∝ 1/V, or P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ when a numerical gas-law calculation is requested. Halving volume doubles pressure under the assumptions. A explanation should not claim particles move faster solely because the container gets smaller. Distinguish collision frequency with walls from the energies of the particles.", "diagram": "boyle"}, {"title": "Temperature at constant volume or pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "At fixed amount and volume, raising kelvin temperature increases average particle kinetic energy. Wall collisions become more forceful and more frequent, increasing pressure. At fixed pressure, the volume expands as temperature rises, reducing collision frequency per area enough to balance the greater particle energy. Proportional gas relationships require kelvin, where 0 °C = 273.15 K. Doubling degrees Celsius does not generally double absolute temperature. State which variables remain constant before applying a relationship. In an exam explanation, connect the temperature change to particle motion and then to the observed pressure or volume.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Pressure is a collision model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gas pressure arises from particles colliding with container walls and transferring momentum. At fixed amount and volume, raising absolute temperature increases average translational kinetic energy and typically raises pressure. Heating does not make individual gas particles expand. The model treats particles as widely separated with negligible volume and attractions, so its predictions are approximations. Real gases depart more strongly from ideal behaviour at high pressure or low temperature, when particle volume and attractions become significant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Control the variables in a gas-law explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "At constant temperature and amount, decreasing volume increases collision frequency per unit wall area and pressure rises. At constant pressure and amount, increased temperature produces expansion. Specify what is held constant before using a relationship: pressure and volume are not inversely proportional in every process. Convert Celsius to kelvin before temperature ratios. A temperature of 20 °C is 293.15 K, and doubling Celsius to 40 °C does not double absolute temperature or ideal-gas pressure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Average motion differs from every particle’s motion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Particles have a distribution of speeds and continually change velocities through collisions. Raising temperature increases average kinetic energy; it does not give every particle the same energy. At the same temperature gases have the same average translational kinetic energy, but lighter particles generally move faster. Diffusion is net spreading through random motion, rather than purposeful movement towards an empty region. Distinguish this model from a diagram in which particle size is exaggerated for visibility.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Pressure reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Pressure reasoning", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Pressure reasoning", "prompt": "A sealed fixed-volume vessel is heated. Explain the pressure increase using kinetic theory.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Average kinetic energy rises with absolute temperature. [1]", "Particles collide with walls more frequently and transfer more momentum per collision on average. [1]", "Greater momentum transfer per unit area per time raises pressure. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-model-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "An ideal gas at 300 K and 100 kPa is heated to 450 K in a sealed rigid container. Calculate pressure and explain the particle mechanism. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Amount and volume are constant, so P₂/P₁ = T₂/T₁.", "[2] P₂ = 100 × 450/300 = 150 kPa.", "[2] Average kinetic energy rises and wall collisions transfer momentum more frequently/strongly; particles themselves do not expand."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-model-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A sealed gas at 100 kPa occupies 2.00 L. It is compressed to 1.00 L at constant temperature. Calculate pressure and identify a condition under which ideal behaviour becomes less reliable. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Constant amount and temperature imply P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.", "[2] P₂ = 100 × 2.00/1.00 = 200 kPa.", "[2] High pressure or low temperature increases the importance of particle volume or intermolecular attractions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which temperature scale is needed for ideal-gas temperature ratios?", "o": ["Kelvin.", "Celsius without conversion.", "Any scale because zero is identical.", "A pH scale."], "a": 0, "why": "Gas laws use absolute temperature, measured in kelvin."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-quantities", "title": "Gas amounts, STP, stoichiometry and collection", "scope": "Match the gas conditions and units · Connect gas amount to the balanced reaction · Collected gas may include water vapour", "parent": "che11-chromatography-gases", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Gas amounts, STP, stoichiometry and collection</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Match the gas conditions and units</li><li>Connect gas amount to the balanced reaction</li><li>Collected gas may include water vapour</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Amount of gas and the ideal gas equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "At fixed temperature and volume, adding more gas particles increases wall-collision frequency and pressure. The ideal-gas equation PV = nRT combines amount, pressure, volume and temperature. With P in kPa and V in L, R = 8.314 kPa L mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ is numerically equivalent to the booklet’s 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ because 1 kPa L = 1 J. Year 11 SCSA explicitly requires qualitative kinetic relationships and gas volumes at STP. The general equation is useful preparation and data-booklet literacy; numerical use is identified where included. Never mix pressure or volume units without converting.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "SCSA standard temperature and pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2026 SCSA Chemistry data booklet defines STP as 0.0 °C and 100.0 kPa. One mole of an ideal gas occupies 22.71 L under those conditions. Thus n = V/22.71 when volume is in litres. Do not substitute the familiar 22.4 L value associated with a different pressure convention. The molar volume applies to a gas under the specified conditions, not liquids, solids or a gas at any temperature. Convert millilitres to litres before using the formula. In an exam, use the data booklet supplied for that examination if a future edition changes its constants.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Gas stoichiometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "A balanced reaction gives mole ratios, which connect gas volumes under the same temperature and pressure. For 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(g), hydrogen and oxygen volumes react in a 2:1 ratio if measured comparably. If water condenses, its liquid volume does not follow the gas ratio. For a solid producing CO₂ at STP, convert solid mass to moles, apply the reaction ratio, then multiply gas moles by 22.71 L mol⁻¹. Distinguish initial total gas volume from the volume of one requested product and check limiting reactants if more than one quantity is supplied.", "diagram": "gas-stoich"}, {"title": "Gas collection and experimental limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "A gas syringe measures volume directly but leaks, friction and reading resolution can affect results. Collection over water can dissolve some gas and includes water vapour, so correction may be needed for accurate dry-gas quantities. Temperature and pressure must be known or controlled to compare volumes. Delay after mixing can miss early gas formation. A leak generally underestimates collected gas, leading to a low calculated amount. Repeating trials assesses variation but will not cure a consistent leak. Select an improvement that addresses the specific error and describe the direction of its effect on the reported result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Worked ideal-gas extension", "kind": "learn", "text": "Extension: for 0.100 mol ideal gas at 298 K and 100 kPa, V = nRT/P = (0.100 × 8.314 × 298)/100 = 2.48 L using kPa and L. This differs from its 2.271 L volume at SCSA STP because temperature is higher. Year 11’s explicit gas-volume requirement focuses on STP plus qualitative kinetic relationships; this calculation introduces the general equation found in the booklet. Always state the assumptions and convert temperature to kelvin. The numerical constant works here because kPa L is equivalent to joules.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Match the gas conditions and units", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2026 SCSA data book defines STP as 0.0 °C and 100.0 kPa and gives molar volume 22.71 L mol⁻¹. Use that value for these specified conditions, rather than importing 22.4 L mol⁻¹ from another pressure convention. For other conditions use PV = nRT with compatible units. R = 8.314 works with pressure in kPa and volume in litres, or pressure in pascals and volume in cubic metres; do not combine those systems accidentally.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Connect gas amount to the balanced reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "First calculate the gas amount, then use the coefficient ratio to find the requested reactant or product amount. Equal gas volumes have the coefficient ratio only when gases are compared at the same temperature and pressure and ideal behaviour is a suitable approximation. A liquid volume does not enter this rule. Report the requested quantity, not simply the first gas mole result, and show the equation and ratio so the calculation can be assessed.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Collected gas may include water vapour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Gas collected over water is a mixture of the target gas and water vapour. Where water-vapour pressure is supplied and the gas is otherwise suitable for this method, subtract it from total pressure to obtain the target gas partial pressure. A water-soluble or water-reactive gas can be poorly suited to collection over water. Cooling a gas changes its measured volume, so record conditions at measurement. Leakage generally reduces collected amount and can cause underestimation of a reacting sample.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive gas model: fixed amount", "kind": "interactive", "model": "gas"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Gas volume from a solid", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Gas volume from a solid", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: STP volume ratio", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Gas volume from a solid", "prompt": "CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. Find CO₂ volume at SCSA STP from 5.00 g CaCO₃, M = 100.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(CaCO₃) = 5.00/100.0 = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "The reactant:CO₂ mole ratio is 1:1. [1]", "n(CO₂) = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "V = 0.0500 × 22.71 = 1.14 L at STP. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "STP volume ratio", "prompt": "At the same temperature and pressure, 150 mL hydrogen reacts with 60.0 mL oxygen. Which gas limits and how much hydrogen remains? Use 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The gas volume ratio is H₂:O₂ = 2:1. [1]", "60.0 mL O₂ requires 120 mL H₂. [1]", "Oxygen limits because sufficient hydrogen is present. [1]", "150 − 120 = 30 mL H₂ remains, at the stated common conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-chromatography-gases-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-quantities-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Calculate the amount in 4.542 L of ideal gas at SCSA STP, then the mass if it is CO₂ with M = 44.0 g mol⁻¹. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n = V/Vm = 4.542/22.71 = 0.2000 mol.", "[2] m = nM = 0.2000 × 44.0 = 8.80 g.", "[2] The molar volume applies at 0.0 °C and 100.0 kPa; identify the supplied conditions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-chromatography-gases-gas-quantities-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A gas collected over water has total pressure 103.0 kPa, water-vapour pressure 3.0 kPa, volume 2.00 L and temperature 300 K. Use R = 8.314 kPa L mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ to calculate dry-gas amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Pgas = 103.0 − 3.0 = 100.0 kPa.", "[2] n = PV/RT = (100.0 × 2.00)/(8.314 × 300).", "[2] n = 0.0802 mol; the calculation uses target-gas partial pressure, not total mixture pressure."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What molar volume does the 2026 SCSA data book give at its stated STP?", "o": ["22.71 L mol⁻¹.", "22.4 L mol⁻¹ for every pressure.", "22.71 mol L⁻¹.", "A universal value independent of temperature."], "a": 0, "why": "SCSA STP is 0.0 °C and 100.0 kPa."}, {"q": "TLC Rf is measured from", "o": ["The plate top", "The same baseline for spot and solvent", "Different reference points", "The solvent reservoir floor"], "a": 1, "why": "Both distances must begin at the sample baseline."}, {"q": "SCSA 2026 STP molar volume is", "o": ["22.4 L", "24.0 L", "22.71 L", "1.00 L"], "a": 2, "why": "The booklet specifies 0 °C and 100 kPa, giving 22.71 L mol⁻¹."}, {"q": "Compressing an ideal gas at constant temperature increases pressure because", "o": ["Average speed necessarily increases", "Wall collision frequency per area increases", "Each atom shrinks", "New gas is formed"], "a": 1, "why": "Temperature fixes the average kinetic energy in this comparison."}, {"q": "GC normally uses which mobile phase?", "o": ["A gas", "A stationary solid", "A vacuum with no particles", "A permanent liquid reservoir"], "a": 0, "why": "A carrier gas moves components through the column."}, {"q": "Equal peak areas for different chemicals imply equal mass", "o": ["Always", "Only with appropriate response calibration assumptions", "Never under any circumstances", "Only if colours match"], "a": 1, "why": "Detector responses can differ between compounds."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-water", "title": "Water, hydration, solubility and solution structure", "scope": "Hydration requires a species-specific explanation · Dissociation and molecular dissolution differ · Apply structure without making absolute rules", "parent": "che11-solutions-acidity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Water, hydration, solubility and solution structure</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Hydration requires a species-specific explanation</li><li>Dissociation and molecular dissolution differ</li><li>Apply structure without making absolute rules</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Water’s bent polar structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water has two O–H bonds and two lone pairs around oxygen, giving a bent molecular shape. Oxygen attracts bonding electrons more strongly, so O is δ− and H is δ+. The bond dipoles do not cancel. Water molecules can form extensive hydrogen-bond interactions because each has donor hydrogens and lone-pair acceptor sites. This helps explain water’s unusually high boiling point for a small molecule and substantial surface tension. The hydrogen bonds continually reorganise in a liquid. A diagram with fixed neighbouring molecules is a snapshot, not a rigid structure that every liquid-water molecule occupies permanently.", "diagram": "water"}, {"title": "Ice density and surface tension", "kind": "learn", "text": "In ordinary ice, hydrogen bonding holds water molecules in a relatively open arrangement. Liquid water can pack more closely as the network reorganises, so ordinary ice is less dense and floats. This is not because water molecules themselves grow larger on freezing. Surface molecules experience an imbalance of cohesive attractions, contributing to surface tension and a tendency to minimise surface area. A detergent can change interfacial behaviour by interacting with water and the interface. Explain a property through molecular arrangement and forces, not by saying water is “special.” Density is mass divided by volume and should be distinguished from viscosity.", "diagram": "ice"}, {"title": "Dissolving ionic substances", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water’s oxygen-rich δ− end orients toward cations, while δ+ hydrogen ends orient toward anions. Ion–dipole attractions stabilise separated hydrated ions. Dissolution requires overcoming part of the lattice attraction and reorganising solvent interactions, while hydration forms new interactions. Whether a salt dissolves appreciably depends on the overall energetic and entropy balance, not merely the presence of water. NaCl(s) → Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) describes dissociation without creating new nuclei. A hydration diagram should show appropriate orientations around both charge types. The dissolved ions remain charged and can migrate, explaining electrolyte conductivity.", "diagram": "hydration"}, {"title": "Molecular solutes and solvent interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polar molecular solute may form dipole interactions or hydrogen bonds with water. Ethanol can hydrogen bond through its O–H group and oxygen lone pairs. A hydrocarbon such as hexane has dispersion interactions but cannot compensate sufficiently for disruption of water’s strong interactions, so water solubility is low. Longer alcohol carbon chains can reduce water solubility despite retaining an O–H group. Dissolution does not always involve ion formation: sugar dissolves mainly as molecules and is not a strong electrolyte. Distinguish dissolve, dissociate and ionise when explaining why a solution does or does not conduct.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Unsaturated, saturated and supersaturated", "kind": "learn", "text": "At a stated temperature, an unsaturated solution can dissolve more solute. A saturated solution is at its equilibrium solubility, often in contact with excess undissolved solid. A supersaturated solution contains more dissolved solute than the equilibrium amount and can crystallise when disturbed or seeded. It is a metastable condition, not a solution simply containing a lot of visible solid. Solubility varies with substance and temperature; many solid solutes become more soluble when heated, but this is not universal. Gases often become less soluble as temperature rises. Use a supplied solubility curve rather than assuming every substance behaves like potassium nitrate.", "diagram": "solubility"}, {"title": "Hydration requires a species-specific explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water is polar: oxygen carries partial negative charge and hydrogen partial positive charge. In hydration of a cation, water’s oxygen end faces towards the positive ion; for an anion, hydrogen ends orient towards it. These attractions are ion–dipole interactions, not covalent bonds newly joining every water molecule to every ion. Dissolution also requires disruption of solute and solvent interactions, so the existence of hydration alone does not guarantee unlimited solubility.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Dissociation and molecular dissolution differ", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ionic solid such as NaCl separates into hydrated ions when it dissolves. A molecular solute can dissolve as neutral molecules; some substances also react or ionise in water. Use the actual species rather than describing every aqueous solution as ions. For NaCl(aq), dissolved mobile Na⁺ and Cl⁻ carry charge; water polarity does not make dry crystalline salt conduct. The lattice fixes ions in position, while the aqueous state permits migration under an electric field.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apply structure without making absolute rules", "kind": "learn", "text": "A small organic molecule with suitable polar sites can form favourable interactions with water, while a large nonpolar hydrocarbon region can reduce solubility. Hydrogen bonding is relevant only where the structure permits it. Temperature, pressure for gases and the balance of all interactions matter. A solubility statement needs conditions and evidence. Avoid the claim that all polar substances dissolve fully or that all ionic compounds are soluble; lattice interactions can oppose hydration strongly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-hydration"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-water-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why aqueous NaCl conducts electricity but solid NaCl does not, and describe water orientation around Na⁺. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The solid has charged ions fixed in a lattice and lacks mobile charge carriers.", "[2] In solution Na⁺ and Cl⁻ can move and carry charge.", "[2] Water’s partially negative oxygen end points towards Na⁺, producing ion–dipole attraction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-water-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare dissolving glucose molecules and dissociating sodium chloride, then explain why polarity alone cannot prove unlimited solubility. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Glucose can dissolve as neutral molecules; NaCl separates into hydrated Na⁺ and Cl⁻.", "[2] Solute–solvent interactions must be considered alongside disruption of solute–solute and solvent–solvent interactions.", "[2] Structure, lattice or molecular attractions and conditions limit solubility; polar is not a universal fully soluble label."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which end of water preferentially faces a hydrated cation?", "o": ["The oxygen end.", "The hydrogen ends because they are partially negative.", "Neither because water has no polarity.", "Both ends become covalently bonded to the ion in every solution."], "a": 0, "why": "The partially negative oxygen is attracted to a positive ion."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-concentration", "title": "Concentration, ppm, preparation and dilution", "scope": "Distinguish the amount of each species · Prepare a measured solution rather than guessing its volume · Use ppm with its mass basis", "parent": "che11-solutions-acidity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Concentration, ppm, preparation and dilution</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Distinguish the amount of each species</li><li>Prepare a measured solution rather than guessing its volume</li><li>Use ppm with its mass basis</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Molar concentration and mass concentration", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molar concentration c = n/V uses solute moles and total solution volume in litres. Mass concentration in g L⁻¹ = solute mass / solution volume in litres. For 0.100 mol solute in 250.0 mL solution, c = 0.100/0.2500 = 0.400 mol L⁻¹. The denominator is final solution volume, not necessarily the initial water volume added. Relate the two units through molar mass: g L⁻¹ = (mol L⁻¹)(g mol⁻¹). Record the chemical species because a solution’s formula-unit concentration differs from individual ion concentrations. Unit conversion is part of the reasoning, not a hidden calculator step.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Parts per million", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mass-based ppm expresses mg solute per kg solution. In a dilute aqueous solution with density close to 1.00 kg L⁻¹, the numerical value is approximately mg L⁻¹. This approximation is not valid for every solvent or concentrated solution. A 2.00 mg solute in 0.500 kg solution gives 4.00 ppm by mass. To convert g L⁻¹ to mol L⁻¹, divide by molar mass. To convert ppm to another concentration, identify the definition and any density assumption first. In water-quality questions, distinguish the mass of an ion or element from the mass of an entire compound used to supply it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Making a solution and dilution", "kind": "learn", "text": "A volumetric flask establishes an accurate final volume. Dissolve a measured solute, transfer quantitatively with washings, allow temperature to equilibrate and fill to the calibration mark. Mix thoroughly. Dilution conserves solute amount, so c₁V₁ = c₂V₂ for the same solute when no reaction or loss occurs. A pipette transfers a measured aliquot into a flask and solvent is added to the final mark. Using a beaker’s markings gives poorer volumetric accuracy. Overfilling a flask makes the actual concentration lower than intended if the solute amount is unchanged. Explain the concentration effect through n/V.", "diagram": "volumetric"}, {"title": "Individual ion concentrations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For complete dissociation, multiply the compound’s concentration by the ion coefficient. A 0.200 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ solution has [Ca²⁺] = 0.200 and [Cl⁻] = 0.400 mol L⁻¹. After mixing solutions without reaction, calculate each ion’s total moles and divide by the total solution volume, assuming volumes are additive. If precipitation occurs, subtract reacting ion amounts before dividing. Ion charge does not multiply the concentration: the “2+” in Ca²⁺ denotes charge, not two calcium ions. Parentheses in Al₂(SO₄)₃ produce two aluminium ions and three sulfate ions per formula unit in the ideal dissociation model.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish the amount of each species", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molar concentration c = n/V uses solution volume in litres. The dissolved formula amount and each ion amount need not be equal: 0.100 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ gives 0.100 mol L⁻¹ Ca²⁺ and 0.200 mol L⁻¹ Cl⁻ if fully dissociated without another reaction. After mixing, calculate actual amounts and use the new total volume when appropriate. The concentration does not remain unchanged merely because no precipitate forms. Identify whether a question asks formula concentration or one ion’s concentration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Prepare a measured solution rather than guessing its volume", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a solid standard, calculate the mass, weigh accurately, dissolve and transfer quantitatively, rinse transfer vessels into the flask, then make to the calibration mark at the appropriate temperature and mix. The final solution volume is specified, not the amount of solvent initially added. For dilution without reaction, solute amount is conserved and c₁V₁ = c₂V₂. Consistent volume units cancel in this ratio, but n = cV still requires litres for c in mol L⁻¹.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use ppm with its mass basis", "kind": "learn", "text": "The data book defines ppm as milligrams of solute per kilogram of solution. The approximation ppm ≈ mg L⁻¹ is justified only when the solution density is sufficiently close to 1.00 kg L⁻¹ for the required precision. State the basis rather than silently applying it to all solvents. Concentration calculations should show conversion of milligrams to grams or millilitres to litres where needed, carry extra digits through intermediate work and round only the final result.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Solution preparation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Solution preparation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Dilution calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Dilution calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Ions after mixing", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Water-quality concentration", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 4}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 5}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Solution preparation", "prompt": "Find the mass of NaCl needed to make 250.0 mL of 0.200 mol L⁻¹ solution, using M = 58.44 g mol⁻¹. Describe the final volume step.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n = cV = 0.200 × 0.2500 = 0.0500 mol. [1]", "m = nM = 0.0500 × 58.44. [1]", "m = 2.92 g. [1]", "Dissolve and transfer quantitatively, then add water to the flask’s final 250.0 mL mark and mix. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Dilution calculation", "prompt": "What volume of 2.00 mol L⁻¹ stock is needed to make 100.0 mL of 0.150 mol L⁻¹ solution?", "marks": 3, "steps": ["c₁V₁ = c₂V₂ conserves solute moles. [1]", "V₁ = (0.150 × 100.0)/2.00. [1]", "V₁ = 7.50 mL; dilute this aliquot to 100.0 mL total solution. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ions after mixing", "prompt": "Mix 20.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ with 30.0 mL water. Assume additive volumes and no reaction. Find calcium and chloride concentrations.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Initial n(Ca²⁺) = 0.0200 × 0.100 = 0.00200 mol. [1]", "n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 0.00200 = 0.00400 mol. [1]", "[Ca²⁺] = 0.00200/0.0500 = 0.0400 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "[Cl⁻] = 0.00400/0.0500 = 0.0800 mol L⁻¹. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Water-quality concentration", "prompt": "A water sample contains 3.00 mg lead in 2.00 kg solution. Calculate mass ppm, describe how AAS can support monitoring and explain why filtration alone does not remove dissolved ions.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["ppm = 3.00/2.00 = 1.50. [1]", "Use prepared samples and element-specific AAS with calibration standards. [1]", "Include a blank and relevant concentration range. [1]", "Apply dilution factors and report uncertainty. [1]", "Dissolved ions pass through ordinary filter pores. [1]", "A separate validated removal method is needed for those ions; final monitoring must verify its effectiveness. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-concentration-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Find the amount of chloride ions in 25.0 mL of 0.200 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂, assuming complete dissociation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(CaCl₂) = cV = 0.200 × 0.0250 = 0.00500 mol.", "[2] One formula unit gives two Cl⁻, so n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 0.00500.", "[2] n(Cl⁻) = 0.0100 mol; distinguish ion amount from dissolved formula amount."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-concentration-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Calculate the volume of 0.500 mol L⁻¹ stock needed to prepare 250.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ solution and describe the final preparation step. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] c₁V₁ = c₂V₂, so V₁ = (0.100 × 250.0)/0.500.", "[2] V₁ = 50.0 mL of stock, transferred with suitable volumetric apparatus.", "[2] Dilute to the flask’s final 250.0 mL mark and mix; do not simply add 250.0 mL of water."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "When can ppm be approximated by mg L⁻¹?", "o": ["When solution density is sufficiently close to 1.00 kg L⁻¹.", "For every solvent at every density.", "Only when no solute is present.", "Because milligrams and moles are identical."], "a": 0, "why": "The ppm definition uses kilograms of solution, so conversion to volume requires density."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 6 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-precipitation", "title": "Solubility rules, ionic equations and precipitation", "scope": "Predict and then state the observations · Write the species that actually change · Calculate remaining amounts after reaction", "parent": "che11-solutions-acidity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Solubility rules, ionic equations and precipitation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Predict and then state the observations</li><li>Write the species that actually change</li><li>Calculate remaining amounts after reaction</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Solubility rules and precipitation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use the SCSA data booklet’s solubility rules to predict which ion combination forms a precipitate. All nitrates are listed soluble. Most chlorides are soluble, with exceptions including AgCl and PbCl₂. Most sulfates are soluble, with BaSO₄, SrSO₄ and PbSO₄ listed insoluble and others slightly soluble. These are practical classifications, not a statement of literally zero solubility. Mixing AgNO₃ and NaCl can give white AgCl. Identify the precipitate formula by balanced charges, retain its solid state and separate spectator ions from reacting species. Refer to the current booklet rather than memorising an incomplete rule.", "diagram": "precipitation"}, {"title": "Net ionic equations and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Write correct molecular products and states, separate aqueous strong electrolytes into ions, then cancel species unchanged on both sides. Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) is the net ionic equation. Nitrate and sodium are spectators in this example. Do not split a solid precipitate, liquid water or a weak molecular acid as though fully ionised. Balance atoms and charge in the final equation. Observation describes what is seen, such as “a white precipitate forms,” while the equation explains reacting species. A colourless solution alone does not imply no ions. Different ion combinations can produce precipitates with characteristic colours.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solution stoichiometry and water treatment", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a reaction involving solutions, convert known concentrations and volumes to moles using n = cV, apply the balanced ratio and calculate the requested mass, concentration or volume. If two reactant amounts are supplied, check which limits. Water treatment combines methods for different contaminants: filtration removes suspended solids, desalination reduces dissolved salts and disinfection controls microbes. Each method has costs and constraints. Heavy-metal monitoring may use AAS and calibration, while precipitation can remove some ions. A proposed treatment needs evidence of removal effectiveness, safe final concentrations and handling of waste, rather than a claim that one process removes every impurity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Slightly soluble is not zero soluble", "kind": "learn", "text": "The SCSA booklet distinguishes soluble, slightly soluble and insoluble practical ranges. Calcium sulfate is listed slightly soluble rather than being grouped with all highly soluble sulfates. A precipitate prediction must therefore refer to the supplied rules and concentrations when those matter. A saturated solution can contain dissolved ions even when excess solid remains at the bottom. The solid and aqueous ions are different physical parts of the system. It is incorrect to infer that an insoluble-labelled substance has no particles dissolved under any circumstances.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Predict and then state the observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use the supplied solubility rules to decide whether a combination can form a sparingly soluble product under the stated conditions. Formation of an insoluble solid is an inference supported by observations such as a clear solution becoming cloudy or a precipitate of a stated colour appearing. Do not replace the observation with only a compound name. Slightly soluble does not mean precisely zero dissolution, and dilute conditions can affect whether a visible precipitate forms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Write the species that actually change", "kind": "learn", "text": "For AgNO₃(aq) mixed with NaCl(aq), the net ionic equation is Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s). Sodium and nitrate remain aqueous spectators in this model and cancel from the complete ionic equation. Check both atom counts and total charge on each side. A net ionic equation is not obtained by removing charges or splitting the solid product into aqueous ions. State symbols communicate the precipitation and are part of the chemical account.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Calculate remaining amounts after reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "If precipitating ions are not in the coefficient ratio, one is limiting and the other remains in excess. Use their initial amounts, subtract the reacted amount and divide by the appropriate final solution volume if concentration is requested. Spectator-ion amounts remain, although their concentrations can change through mixing. Treat the simplified complete-precipitation calculation as a model; advanced equilibrium solubility may require additional data beyond the qualitative rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Precipitate amount", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Precipitate amount", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Precipitate amount", "prompt": "Mix 25.0 mL 0.200 mol L⁻¹ AgNO₃ with excess NaCl. Calculate AgCl mass, M = 143.32 g mol⁻¹, and give the net ionic equation.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(Ag⁺) = 0.0250 × 0.200 = 0.00500 mol. [1]", "Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s). [2: species/states and balance]", "n(AgCl) = 0.00500 mol. [1]", "m = 0.00500 × 143.32 = 0.717 g. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q2", "allocations": [1, 2, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-precipitation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Write the net ionic equation and one suitable observation when aqueous silver nitrate and sodium chloride form silver chloride. Check charge. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s).", "[2] Initially clear solutions become cloudy/a white precipitate appears.", "[2] One Ag and one Cl each side; total reactant charge +1 − 1 = 0 equals neutral solid product."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-precipitation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Mix 10.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ AgNO₃ with 20.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ NaCl. Assuming complete AgCl precipitation and additive volumes, calculate chloride concentration remaining. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(Ag⁺) = 0.00100 mol and n(Cl⁻) = 0.00200 mol; 1:1 reaction makes Ag⁺ limiting.", "[2] Remaining Cl⁻ = 0.00200 − 0.00100 = 0.00100 mol.", "[2] c(Cl⁻) = 0.00100/0.0300 = 0.0333 mol L⁻¹."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which species belong in the AgCl precipitation net ionic equation?", "o": ["Ag⁺, Cl⁻ and AgCl(s).", "Only sodium and nitrate spectators.", "Uncharged Ag and Cl atoms in solution.", "AgCl(aq) split into ions as the precipitate."], "a": 0, "why": "The net ionic equation retains the species that change."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-acids", "title": "Acids, bases, pH and neutralisation", "scope": "Separate concentration from strength · Use the logarithmic pH scale precisely · Connect neutralisation to the reacting amounts", "parent": "che11-solutions-acidity", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Acids, bases, pH and neutralisation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Separate concentration from strength</li><li>Use the logarithmic pH scale precisely</li><li>Connect neutralisation to the reacting amounts</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Arrhenius acids, bases and strength", "kind": "learn", "text": "In aqueous solution an Arrhenius acid increases hydrogen-ion concentration and an Arrhenius base increases hydroxide-ion concentration. H⁺(aq) is convenient notation for hydrated proton behaviour; H₃O⁺ is a more explicit representation. HCl is a strong acid that ionises essentially completely in dilute water. Ethanoic acid ionises only partly and is weak. NaOH dissociates to give OH⁻. NH₃ reacts with water reversibly to form NH₄⁺ and OH⁻. Strength describes degree of ionisation, while concentration describes amount per volume. A concentrated weak acid and a dilute strong acid are both possible. Neither word directly specifies the pH by itself.", "diagram": "acid-strength"}, {"title": "pH, indicators and the logarithmic scale", "kind": "learn", "text": "pH = −log₁₀[H⁺] for the course’s concentration-based calculations. At 25 °C, neutral water has [H⁺] = [OH⁻] and pH approximately 7. Each unit decrease in pH corresponds to a tenfold rise in [H⁺]. A pH 3 solution has 100 times the hydrogen-ion concentration of a pH 5 solution. Indicators change colour over a transition range and give approximate classification rather than exact pH. A calibrated pH meter provides a numerical reading. Neutrality depends on equal hydrogen and hydroxide concentrations, and neutral pH can differ from 7 at another temperature. State the temperature assumptions in more advanced comparisons.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Characteristic acid and base reactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acid with a suitable reactive metal can form a salt and hydrogen, for example Mg + 2H⁺ → Mg²⁺ + H₂. Acid with carbonate forms CO₂ and water: CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O. Acid with hydroxide gives H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Hydroxide with ammonium gives NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ → NH₃ + H₂O. Predictions should include states, charge balance and observations such as effervescence, a metal dissolving or a temperature change. Not all metals react readily with dilute acid, and acid type matters. Avoid giving “gas produced” without identifying which gas and the reaction responsible.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Weak acid neutralisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a weak acid such as ethanoic acid, do not split all reactant molecules into free H⁺ and ethanoate when writing a net ionic neutralisation. CH₃COOH(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → CH₃COO⁻(aq) + H₂O(l) represents the weak molecular acid reacting with hydroxide. Its incomplete initial ionisation does not reduce the total stoichiometric acid amount that can react as equilibrium readjusts. A strong acid’s simplified net equation is H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O. Choose the equation that accurately represents the acid species under the given conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate concentration from strength", "kind": "learn", "text": "An acid increases the hydronium concentration in water, while a base can accept protons or supply hydroxide under the relevant model. Strong describes extensive ionisation for a specified acid reaction; concentrated describes a large amount per solution volume. A dilute strong acid and a concentrated weak acid are possible. In straightforward introductory calculations, identify the acid/base and its ionisation assumption before converting concentration into hydrogen- or hydroxide-ion concentration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the logarithmic pH scale precisely", "kind": "learn", "text": "pH = −log₁₀[H⁺], where the school calculation uses concentration in mol L⁻¹ as a suitable approximation. A tenfold increase in [H⁺] lowers pH by one. At 25 °C a neutral solution has equal hydrogen and hydroxide concentrations and approximately pH 7. Temperature changes the neutral pH through water’s ionisation equilibrium. Do not treat a pH difference of one as a one-unit concentration difference or assume all acids have the same pH.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Connect neutralisation to the reacting amounts", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a strong acid and strong base, H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l) describes the net reaction. Acid–carbonate reaction also produces carbon dioxide; an observation is effervescence, while carbon dioxide identity requires suitable evidence. Equal solution volumes are neutralising only when concentration and stoichiometry make reacting amounts equivalent. A salt is not automatically neutral in water, a distinction developed in Year 12 salt hydrolysis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Reference: common molecular names and formulae", "kind": "reference", "html": "<p>These common formulas support naming, equations and acid/base interpretation. Formula and proton count alone do not establish complete ionisation. Molecular names here cover the syllabus appendix set.</p><div class=\"che-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ammonia</td><td>NH₃</td></tr><tr><td>water</td><td>H₂O</td></tr><tr><td>hydrogen peroxide</td><td>H₂O₂</td></tr><tr><td>ethanoic acid</td><td>CH₃COOH</td></tr><tr><td>hydrochloric acid</td><td>HCl</td></tr><tr><td>nitric acid</td><td>HNO₃</td></tr><tr><td>carbonic acid</td><td>H₂CO₃</td></tr><tr><td>sulfuric acid</td><td>H₂SO₄</td></tr><tr><td>sulfurous acid</td><td>H₂SO₃</td></tr><tr><td>phosphoric acid</td><td>H₃PO₄</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Comparing pH", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Comparing pH", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Carbonate reaction", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Comparing pH", "prompt": "Calculate the pH of a strong monoprotic acid with [H⁺] = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹. Compare [H⁺] with a pH 5.00 solution.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["pH = −log₁₀(2.00 × 10⁻³). [1]", "pH = 2.699. [1]", "At pH 5.00, [H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻⁵ mol L⁻¹. [1]", "The first solution has 200 times the hydrogen-ion concentration. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Carbonate reaction", "prompt": "Write a net ionic equation for acid reacting with carbonate and describe one observation and its cause.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["CO₃²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) → CO₂(g) + H₂O(l). [2: correct species and atom/charge balance]", "Effervescence or bubbling occurs. [1]", "Carbon dioxide gas forms and escapes. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-solutions-acidity-q6", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-acids-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Calculate pH of 0.0100 mol L⁻¹ HCl under the complete-ionisation approximation and compare [H⁺] with a pH 3.00 solution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] [H⁺] = 0.0100 mol L⁻¹; pH = −log₁₀(0.0100) = 2.00.", "[2] At pH 3.00, [H⁺] = 0.00100 mol L⁻¹.", "[2] The pH 2.00 solution has ten times the hydrogen-ion concentration, not a one-unit concentration increase."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-solutions-acidity-acids-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Write a net ionic strong-acid/strong-base neutralisation equation and explain why equal volumes need not produce a neutral mixture. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l).", "[2] Amount is concentration × volume, so equal volume does not ensure equal reacting amounts.", "[2] Apply the balanced ratio and check excess acid/base; at equivalence consider the resulting species rather than assuming every salt solution is neutral."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Can an acid be both dilute and strong?", "o": ["Yes: strength and concentration describe different properties.", "No: strong always means concentrated.", "No: dilute means it cannot ionise.", "Only if pH is exactly 7."], "a": 0, "why": "Strength concerns the extent of ionisation; concentration concerns amount per volume."}, {"q": "The concentration of Cl⁻ in 0.10 mol L⁻¹ CaCl₂ is", "o": ["0.05", "0.10", "0.20", "2.00"], "a": 2, "why": "Each formula unit supplies two chloride ions."}, {"q": "A weak acid is one that", "o": ["Is always dilute", "Ionises partly in water", "Contains no hydrogen", "Cannot react with a base"], "a": 1, "why": "Strength and concentration are separate properties."}, {"q": "For dilute water density 1 kg L⁻¹, 5 ppm is approximately", "o": ["5 mg L⁻¹", "5 g L⁻¹", "5 mol L⁻¹", "5 kg L⁻¹"], "a": 0, "why": "Mass ppm is mg per kg of solution."}, {"q": "Ordinary ice floats because", "o": ["Its molecules contain fewer atoms", "Its open structure makes it less dense", "It contains no hydrogen bonds", "It is a different element"], "a": 1, "why": "Hydrogen bonding creates an open solid arrangement."}, {"q": "A pH 3 solution has compared with pH 5", "o": ["Twice [H⁺]", "100 times [H⁺]", "One hundredth [H⁺]", "The same [H⁺]"], "a": 1, "why": "Two pH units represent a factor of 10²."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-graphs", "title": "Reaction-rate graphs and quantitative evidence", "scope": "Rate is a change over time · Read slopes and plateaus carefully · Design comparisons with a meaningful denominator", "parent": "che11-rates-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Reaction-rate graphs and quantitative evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Rate is a change over time</li><li>Read slopes and plateaus carefully</li><li>Design comparisons with a meaningful denominator</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Rate as change per time", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reaction rate measures how quickly a reactant is consumed or a product forms. An average rate uses change divided by elapsed time. Gas volume rate might have units mL s⁻¹, mass-loss rate g s⁻¹ and concentration rate mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹. Product rate is positive when its quantity rises. For a decreasing reactant amount, quote the positive disappearance rate or clearly state the signed derivative convention. Initial rate refers to the beginning, while an average over a long interval may hide changing rate. The measured quantity must reliably represent reaction progress. Do not compare raw volumes collected over unequal times without calculation.", "diagram": "rate-graph"}, {"title": "Graph gradients and completion", "kind": "learn", "text": "A steeper product–time graph means greater product-formation rate. A tangent gives instantaneous rate; a secant between two points gives interval average rate. A plateau can indicate completion or another limit on net observable change, depending on context. Two trials can reach the same final product amount at different speeds if they begin with the same limiting-reactant amount. A catalyst can shorten the time without increasing that final amount. Label axes with variables and units, choose a sensible scale and use a smooth fit where the process warrants it. A curve is not a series of straight segments simply because measurements are discrete.", "diagram": "rate-curves"}, {"title": "Inverse time as a rate proxy", "kind": "learn", "text": "If each trial reaches the same fixed visible endpoint after the same chemical amount has changed, 1/time can serve as a comparative rate proxy with units s⁻¹. It is not automatically an amount-per-time rate and relies on a consistent endpoint and chemistry. Different observers or obscuring colours may change the endpoint criterion, affecting validity. A gas-volume slope provides more direct formation-rate evidence when collection is reliable. State what the proxy measures and why shorter time implies faster progress under the matched conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A conclusion with numeric evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose controlled trials give mean initial rates 0.40, 0.60 and 0.82 mL s⁻¹ as concentration rises across three specified levels. Describe an increasing trend and refer to the measured values. Collision theory predicts more reacting particles per volume and more frequent encounters. Do not claim exact proportionality merely from these three numbers unless uncertainty and the model support it. Report spread and any unusual trial. A scientific conclusion should answer the testable question within its investigated range and distinguish the evidence from extrapolated predictions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rate is a change over time", "kind": "learn", "text": "Average reaction rate is a change in a measured quantity divided by the time interval. For a product amount–time graph, a rising slope gives formation rate; for a reactant concentration–time graph, consumption may be reported as the negative of the slope so rate is positive. State the measured quantity and units. A gas volume rate in mL s⁻¹ is not automatically a mole rate without conditions or conversion. A final amount describes extent, not how rapidly it was reached.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read slopes and plateaus carefully", "kind": "learn", "text": "An instantaneous rate is represented by the tangent slope at a specified time, whereas a secant gives an average over an interval. Many batch reactions slow as reactants are consumed, but do not assume every rate graph has that shape. A plateau can indicate exhaustion of a limiting reagent, equilibrium or another specified stopping condition. Two curves can have different initial rates but the same final yield, or different final yields with comparable early slopes. Use the particular graph evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Design comparisons with a meaningful denominator", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rate comparisons require matched amounts, conditions and measurement methods unless the changed variable is deliberate. Larger absolute gas volumes can arise from larger initial samples rather than intrinsically faster chemistry. If normalising by mass or concentration, explain why that denominator addresses the question. Include axes, units, suitable time resolution and repeats. Describe uncertainty and anomalies without hiding them by connecting every point as if it were exact.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Average and changing rate", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Average and changing rate", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Separating rate from final amount", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Average and changing rate", "prompt": "CO₂ volume rises from 12.0 mL at 10.0 s to 30.0 mL at 40.0 s. Find average rate over the interval and explain why it may differ from initial rate.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["ΔV = 18.0 mL and Δt = 30.0 s. [1]", "Average rate = 18.0/30.0 = 0.600 mL s⁻¹. [1]", "Reactant concentrations or available surface can decrease as reaction proceeds. [1]", "This interval average therefore need not equal the slope at the start. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Separating rate from final amount", "prompt": "Equal carbonate masses with identical acid excess give gas curves reaching the same plateau at different times. Propose a controlled explanation, identify two variables to check and explain what the plateau cannot prove.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["A difference in particle surface area, temperature or catalyst could affect rate. [1]", "A suitable mechanism changes collision frequency or successful-collision fraction. [1]", "Check temperature and particle size while keeping other reactant conditions matched. [1]", "Equal limiting amounts can give equal final product amounts. [1]", "The faster curve does not imply greater theoretical yield. [1]", "A plateau alone cannot exclude gas leakage, dissolution or incomplete measurement; validate collection. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-graphs-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A reaction produces 12.0 mL of gas at 10.0 s and 30.0 mL at 40.0 s. Calculate average volume rate over this interval and explain its limit. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] ΔV = 30.0 − 12.0 = 18.0 mL and Δt = 40.0 − 10.0 = 30.0 s.", "[2] Average rate = 18.0/30.0 = 0.600 mL s⁻¹.", "[2] This is an interval average, not necessarily the instantaneous rate at either endpoint or a molar rate."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-graphs-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two gas–time curves reach 50 mL, but A reaches the plateau earlier and has a steeper initial slope. Compare initial rate and final amount, then identify one controlled condition. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A has a faster initial volume formation rate because its initial slope is steeper.", "[2] Both have the same measured final gas volume under matched measurement conditions.", "[2] Control relevant starting amounts/concentration, temperature or gas pressure; rate and final extent are distinct."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does the tangent slope on a product amount–time graph represent?", "o": ["Instantaneous product formation rate at that time.", "The final yield at every time.", "Only the activation energy.", "The total mass of the apparatus."], "a": 0, "why": "The tangent measures local change per unit time."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-collisions", "title": "Collision theory: concentration, temperature and area", "scope": "A collision must meet more than one requirement · Temperature changes the energetic distribution · Surface area matters where reaction occurs at a surface", "parent": "che11-rates-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Collision theory: concentration, temperature and area</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A collision must meet more than one requirement</li><li>Temperature changes the energetic distribution</li><li>Surface area matters where reaction occurs at a surface</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Collision theory", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reactant particles must collide with sufficient energy and a suitable orientation to react. Activation energy is the minimum energy barrier for the pathway. Not every collision produces products. A rate explanation should state how a change affects collision frequency or the fraction of collisions meeting the energy requirement. Increasing collision count without suitable energy does not ensure a proportionate rate rise. Collision theory is a simplified model for explaining trends, not a microscopic movie of every chemical step. Reactions with different pathways can respond differently, so use the actual reactants, phases and conditions in the question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concentration and gas pressure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Increasing concentration places more reacting particles in a given volume, generally increasing collision frequency and rate. For gases at fixed temperature, compressing a given amount increases concentration and collision frequency. An inert gas added at fixed volume does not increase reactant concentration and need not increase reaction rate. Pressure is therefore relevant through reacting gas partial pressures and particle density, not simply a gauge reading. A complete explanation names the reacting particles and the volume or concentration change. Do not state that concentration increases particle kinetic energy; average kinetic energy is determined by temperature in this model.", "diagram": "collisions"}, {"title": "Temperature and energy distribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Higher temperature raises average particle kinetic energy and usually increases collision frequency. More importantly, it increases the fraction of particles with energy at or above Eₐ, often causing a substantial rate increase. A qualitative energy-distribution diagram has a broader lower peak at higher temperature with a larger high-energy tail beyond the fixed Eₐ. The area under each distribution represents the same total particle population in a controlled comparison. Eₐ does not itself fall simply because the reactants are warmer. The particles’ energy distribution changes. Explain both the motion and successful-collision consequences.", "diagram": "distribution"}, {"title": "Surface area of a solid", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dividing a solid exposes more surface to other reactants at the same total mass. More sites are available for collisions, so reaction rate can increase. A powdered carbonate can react faster than one large piece of the same material. The total product amount is unchanged if the same limiting amount reacts completely. Control mass and composition when comparing particle sizes. A larger surface area may also affect heat transfer and gas release, so design the investigation carefully. “Smaller particles move faster” does not explain the central surface effect for a reacting solid. It is the accessible surface that changes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A collision must meet more than one requirement", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reaction requires suitable reacting particles to collide with sufficient energy to overcome the activation barrier and suitable orientation for the rearrangement. More collisions can increase rate, but total collision frequency is not identical to successful-collision frequency. An explanation should name the changed factor and connect it to effective collisions. Merely repeating that particles collide faster does not distinguish concentration, surface area and temperature effects.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Temperature changes the energetic distribution", "kind": "learn", "text": "Increasing temperature increases average kinetic energy and the proportion of collisions with energy at least the activation energy. The resulting increase in successful collisions is a central reason rates commonly rise substantially. Concentration increase raises reacting particle density and encounter frequency under otherwise comparable conditions. For gases, compression can increase concentration. None of these descriptions requires the activation energy itself to decrease merely because a solution is heated or concentrated.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Surface area matters where reaction occurs at a surface", "kind": "learn", "text": "Breaking a solid into smaller pieces increases exposed surface for the same amount and can increase contact with another reactant. It does not change the total number of formula units initially present or the reaction’s balanced ratio. If the same limiting amount reacts completely, final theoretical product can be unchanged while rate increases. The importance of stirring, passivation and access to the surface depends on the actual system; apply the model to the stated evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Temperature explanation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Temperature explanation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Temperature explanation", "prompt": "Explain why heating usually increases reaction rate without changing activation energy for the same uncatalysed pathway.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Average particle kinetic energy increases. [1]", "Collision frequency can increase. [1]", "A greater fraction of collisions has energy at or above Eₐ and appropriate orientation. [1]", "The pathway barrier is unchanged; the distribution of reactant energies changes. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-collisions-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why powdered calcium carbonate can react faster than an equal mass of larger pieces in excess acid, without necessarily changing final CO₂ amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Powder exposes more surface area to acid for the same amount of solid.", "[2] More reacting surface permits more effective encounters per time under comparable conditions.", "[2] The initial carbonate amount and stoichiometry are unchanged, so complete reaction with excess acid has the same theoretical CO₂ amount."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-collisions-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare how raising temperature and increasing concentration can increase rate. State whether either necessarily lowers activation energy. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Higher temperature increases the proportion of collisions with sufficient energy and also affects encounter motion.", "[2] Higher concentration increases reacting particle density and collision frequency under comparable conditions.", "[2] Neither change alone necessarily lowers activation energy; that is a different mechanism from catalytic pathway change."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can heating increase rate markedly?", "o": ["A greater proportion of collisions have sufficient energy.", "Every molecule gains exactly identical energy.", "The reaction equation changes its coefficients automatically.", "Activation energy always becomes zero."], "a": 0, "why": "The energy distribution shifts so a larger fraction can overcome the barrier."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-catalysis", "title": "Energy profiles, catalysts and applications", "scope": "A catalyst provides another reaction pathway · Read an energy profile as a model · Choose catalysts from evidence and constraints", "parent": "che11-rates-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Energy profiles, catalysts and applications</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A catalyst provides another reaction pathway</li><li>Read an energy profile as a model</li><li>Choose catalysts from evidence and constraints</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Activation energy and the transition state", "kind": "learn", "text": "An energy profile shows reactants, a high-energy transition-state region and products along reaction progress. Eₐ measures the barrier above reactants. Reverse activation energy is the barrier above products. Their difference matches ΔH for a simple common pathway. A large Eₐ can make an exothermic reaction slow at room temperature. “Energy released” and “energy required to initiate successful reaction” are different quantities. A profile is schematic and does not show reaction speed on its horizontal axis. Read vertical energy differences rather than line length or horizontal distance.", "diagram": "energy-profile"}, {"title": "Catalysts and unchanged reaction energetics", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst participates in steps that provide an alternative pathway with lower activation barriers and is regenerated overall. At a given temperature, a greater fraction of collisions can proceed successfully. It does not supply extra reactant, change the balanced net equation or lower ΔH. In a reversible equilibrium, it speeds both directions and does not change the equilibrium composition. In a completion experiment with fixed limiting reagent, it changes rate but not the theoretical total product. A catalysed energy curve must begin and end at the same energies as the uncatalysed one.", "diagram": "catalysed-profile"}, {"title": "Enzymes, metal surfaces and nanoparticles", "kind": "learn", "text": "Enzymes are protein catalysts with structures that allow specific interactions with substrates and lower the reaction barrier. High temperature or unsuitable pH can disrupt their functioning. Metal surfaces can provide sites where reactants adsorb and react through a different pathway. Nanoparticles can expose a high proportion of surface atoms, increasing active sites per material amount. A catalyst is not necessarily permanently unchanged at every step; regeneration is the overall feature. Poisoning or degradation can reduce practical performance. Compare activity, operating conditions, selectivity and reuse when evaluating industrial catalysts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Catalytic converters and human endeavour", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalytic converter promotes reactions that reduce harmful exhaust components, including oxidation of CO to CO₂ and conversion of nitrogen oxides toward nitrogen. The aim is lower pollutant emissions, not elimination of all greenhouse-gas production. For example, 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂ converts toxic CO but still forms CO₂. Performance depends on operating temperature and exhaust composition. An evaluation should distinguish pollutants, use balanced chemical examples and consider resource cost and catalyst durability. A claim of “zero emissions” is not supported merely because a vehicle uses a catalyst.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A catalyst provides another reaction pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst participates in a mechanism and is regenerated overall. The alternative pathway has a lower activation barrier, increasing the fraction of successful reactions at the same temperature. It does not change the initial and final energy levels of the overall reaction or its enthalpy change. Some catalysts require a surface, suitable temperature or specific reactants. They can be deactivated or poisoned, so describing them as never involved and impossible to affect is inaccurate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read an energy profile as a model", "kind": "learn", "text": "The vertical difference between reactants and products represents ΔH; the difference between reactants and the relevant barrier represents forward activation energy. An exothermic profile ends lower, but it can still have a substantial barrier. The horizontal axis is reaction progress, not clock time. A catalyst lowers the barrier for both forward and reverse pathways without changing equilibrium constant at fixed temperature; it helps a reversible system approach equilibrium sooner rather than guaranteeing more product at equilibrium.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choose catalysts from evidence and constraints", "kind": "learn", "text": "An industrial catalyst must provide useful activity under feasible conditions and may require selectivity, durability, safety and recoverability. A laboratory observation of faster change alone does not establish every mechanistic claim. Compare temperatures and amounts fairly and distinguish an increased rate from an altered final composition. Where an enzyme is a catalyst, its structure and environment influence activity. Use the actual context to explain a benefit or limitation rather than stating catalysts make everything better.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Reading an energy profile", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Reading an energy profile", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Catalyst misconceptions", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Reading an energy profile", "prompt": "Reactants lie at 40 kJ, transition state at 120 kJ and products at 10 kJ on a schematic energy scale. Find forward Eₐ, reverse Eₐ and ΔH.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Forward Eₐ = 120 − 40 = 80 kJ. [1]", "Reverse Eₐ = 120 − 10 = 110 kJ. [1]", "ΔH = 10 − 40 = −30 kJ. [1]", "Products are lower, so the written reaction is exothermic. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Catalyst misconceptions", "prompt": "A student says a catalyst increases the final gas volume and lowers reaction enthalpy. Evaluate both statements for fixed limiting reactant undergoing complete reaction.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The catalyst increases rate through an alternative lower-barrier pathway. [1]", "It does not add reactant, so the same theoretical final gas amount forms. [1]", "Reactant and product energy levels remain unchanged. [1]", "Therefore ΔH is unchanged. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-catalysis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "An uncatalysed reaction has Ea = 80 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔH = −30 kJ mol⁻¹. A catalyst reduces the forward barrier to 45 kJ mol⁻¹. Compare the energy profiles and equilibrium effect. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reactant and product levels, and ΔH = −30 kJ mol⁻¹, remain unchanged.", "[2] The alternative forward barrier is lower by 35 kJ mol⁻¹, increasing successful reaction at the same temperature.", "[2] At fixed temperature the equilibrium constant/composition is not changed by a catalyst alone; equilibrium is approached faster."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-catalysis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that an exothermic reaction needs no activation energy and a catalyst must increase its equilibrium yield. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Exothermic describes lower product energy, not absence of a forward activation barrier.", "[2] A catalyst changes pathway/barrier and reaction rate, not overall ΔH.", "[2] It accelerates forward and reverse processes without changing fixed-temperature equilibrium yield solely by catalysis."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does a catalyst change in the usual energy-profile model?", "o": ["The pathway and activation barrier.", "The reaction’s overall enthalpy in every case.", "Only the product amount permitted by stoichiometry.", "The fixed-temperature equilibrium constant."], "a": 0, "why": "Initial and final energy levels are unchanged for the same overall reaction."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "11", "unit": "Unit 2 – Molecular interactions and reactions", "icon": "⚗️", "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-inquiry", "title": "Investigation design, uncertainty and scientific claims", "scope": "Turn a question into an operational investigation · Distinguish precision, accuracy and systematic error · Evaluate conclusions using the data actually collected", "parent": "che11-rates-inquiry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Investigation design, uncertainty and scientific claims</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Turn a question into an operational investigation</li><li>Distinguish precision, accuracy and systematic error</li><li>Evaluate conclusions using the data actually collected</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "A testable question and hypothesis", "kind": "learn", "text": "A useful investigation question identifies an independent variable and a measurable dependent variable, for example acid concentration and initial CO₂-production rate with a fixed carbonate mass and particle size. A hypothesis predicts the direction of the relationship and gives a chemical reason. “More acid reacts faster because collision frequency rises” can be tested across several concentrations. State controls such as temperature, total solution volume, solid mass, surface area and apparatus. A dependent variable must be defined operationally, such as slope over the first 20 s, rather than the vague phrase “how much reaction.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Valid, reliable and safe measurements", "kind": "learn", "text": "Validity concerns whether the method answers the intended question without confounding variables. Reliability concerns consistency and the evidence from repeats. Accuracy concerns closeness to a reference or true value, while precision concerns the spread or resolution of measurement. Repeating a biased method may produce precise but inaccurate results. Plan enough levels and repeats to reveal a pattern, use appropriate equipment and assess hazards before any practical work. These screens teach analysis and planning; practical work involving chemicals or heat requires the school’s approved supervision and procedures. Link a risk control to a specific hazard rather than giving an unrelated generic safety sentence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Random error, systematic error and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Random effects produce trial-to-trial scatter, such as small timing variation. Systematic effects bias results in a consistent direction, such as a miscalibrated balance or persistent gas leak. Repeats can reduce uncertainty in a mean arising from random variation but do not remove a consistent bias. Resolution sets a measurement limit, and uncertainty propagates through calculations. For a burette titre obtained from two readings, both readings contribute. Explain error direction through the calculation: gas leakage gives low measured volume and therefore low calculated gas amount. “Human error” does not identify a scientific mechanism or justify a direction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Data processing and anomalous values", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tabulate raw data with headings and units, preserve meaningful precision and calculate suitable averages or rates. Plot the independent variable horizontally and the dependent variable vertically. A possible anomaly deserves investigation, not automatic deletion because it does not fit a preferred trend. Check recording, apparatus and conditions and report any justified exclusion transparently. Use numeric evidence in a conclusion, such as “initial rate rose from 0.50 to 0.90 mL s⁻¹.” State whether the trend supports the hypothesis within the tested range. Avoid claiming causation from uncontrolled observations or extrapolating far beyond the measurements.", "diagram": "data-error"}, {"title": "Evidence, source quality and scientific reports", "kind": "learn", "text": "Evaluate a claim through method quality, sample size, controls, calibration, reproducibility and relevance of the evidence. Distinguish a primary measurement from an advertisement and a model prediction from an observation. A report presents the question, method, results, analysis, conclusion and specific limitations in a logical order. Chemical formulae, equations and diagrams should support the argument. An improvement should address a named limitation and explain its expected effect. In extended response, consider benefits and constraints and conclude in proportion to the evidence. Uncertainty is part of scientific reasoning, not a reason to ignore well-supported results.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Turn a question into an operational investigation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Specify the independent variable, dependent measurement and controlled conditions. For a rate experiment, define exactly how rate will be estimated, such as a slope over a stated interval rather than an ambiguous time until finished. Use a practical range, repeats, risk controls and consistent starting procedure. A control condition helps interpret the comparison, but controlling everything is impossible; state the factors that matter for this reaction and measurement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish precision, accuracy and systematic error", "kind": "learn", "text": "Repeats reveal random variation and can improve the precision of an average; they do not automatically remove a consistent calibration error or gas leak. Accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference, while precision concerns agreement or dispersion. Resolution sets a reading limit, but it is not the only uncertainty. Record raw values, units and justified significant figures. Calculate first with sufficient digits and round final results so uncertainty is not disguised by excessive decimal places.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate conclusions using the data actually collected", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identify a trend with values or graph evidence, explain a plausible chemical mechanism and distinguish this from proof of causation if the design is inadequate. An anomalous result needs an evidence-based investigation; do not delete it only because it spoils a line. An improvement should address the named limitation. Saying use better equipment is too vague unless the equipment’s resolution, calibration or collection mechanism explains why the proposed change improves the answer. For numerical summaries, the mean is the sum divided by the number of observations and the median is the middle ordered value, or the mean of two middle values for an even count. The interquartile range is Q₃ − Q₁ under the specified quartile convention and describes the central half of the data. For results 2, 3, 4, 5, 16, the mean is 6 while the median is 4: an extreme value affects them differently. A frequency table records counts. Use a histogram for an appropriate continuous distribution, a bar chart for categories and a scatter plot for paired quantitative variables; choose representations that match the question rather than joining unrelated categories.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Controlled rate investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Controlled rate investigation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Error and improvement", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Controlled rate investigation", "prompt": "Design a comparison of carbonate particle size and acid reaction rate. Identify variables, measurement and one improvement to reliability.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Independent variable: particle size at several defined levels. [1]", "Dependent variable: initial gas-volume rate using consistent time intervals. [1]", "Keep carbonate mass/composition and acid concentration/volume constant. [1]", "Keep temperature and collection apparatus consistent. [1]", "Repeat each level and compare means and spread. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Error and improvement", "prompt": "A gas-collection tube leaks consistently during each trial. Explain the effect on calculated initial rate and whether repeated trials alone solve it.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Measured gas volume is systematically too low. [1]", "The estimated formation rate is therefore too low. [1]", "Repeating the same leaking method does not remove the systematic bias. [1]", "Check and seal connections or replace the apparatus and verify it is gas-tight. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che11-rates-inquiry-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-inquiry-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A student changes acid concentration and temperature together, then concludes concentration caused faster gas production. Evaluate the inference and propose a correction. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Temperature is a confounding variable because it also affects successful collision frequency.", "[2] The results cannot isolate the concentration effect in this design.", "[2] Hold temperature constant while varying concentration with matched other relevant conditions and repeat measurements."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che11-rates-inquiry-inquiry-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Three repeat gas measurements agree closely, but a confirmed leak affects all trials. Explain precision versus accuracy and one suitable improvement. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Close agreement supports precision/repeatability within this method.", "[2] A consistent leak can reduce collected gas in all trials and create a systematic underestimate despite precision.", "[2] Seal and leak-test the collection apparatus; repeating alone does not remove the systematic problem."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does repeating a measurement fail to guarantee?", "o": ["Removal of systematic error.", "Evidence about random variation.", "A basis for estimating repeatability.", "More than one observation."], "a": 0, "why": "A shared calibration error or leak can persist across every repeat."}, {"q": "A catalyst changes", "o": ["ΔH", "The pathway and activation barriers", "The stoichiometric coefficients", "The initial reactant mass automatically"], "a": 1, "why": "The alternative pathway speeds reaction without changing net energetics."}, {"q": "Higher concentration generally increases rate through", "o": ["Higher average kinetic energy", "More frequent reactant collisions", "Changing proton numbers", "Lower particle masses"], "a": 1, "why": "Concentration changes particle density, while temperature governs kinetic energy."}, {"q": "A tangent to a product–time graph gives", "o": ["Instantaneous formation rate", "Final product mass only", "Molar mass", "Activation energy directly"], "a": 0, "why": "Its gradient is the local change per time."}, {"q": "Repeats alone are least effective at removing", "o": ["A persistent calibration bias", "Random timing scatter", "Variation in averages", "Evidence about repeatability"], "a": 0, "why": "A systematic bias requires correction of its cause."}, {"q": "Equal mass of powder reacts faster than a lump mainly because", "o": ["More surface is exposed", "Its atoms are lighter", "The final yield must increase", "Its Eₐ becomes zero"], "a": 0, "why": "More accessible surface allows more reactant collisions."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-equilibrium-dynamic", "title": "Dynamic equilibrium: systems, rates and concentrations", "scope": "Equilibrium is dynamic rather than stopped · Closure applies to matter exchange · Describe rates and concentrations separately", "parent": "che12-equilibrium", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Dynamic equilibrium: systems, rates and concentrations</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Equilibrium is dynamic rather than stopped</li><li>Closure applies to matter exchange</li><li>Describe rates and concentrations separately</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Open, closed and isolated systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "An open system exchanges matter and energy with its surroundings. A closed system exchanges energy but not matter. An isolated idealisation exchanges neither. A sealed vessel can therefore be closed even if heat enters through its walls. Reversible reactions can establish dynamic equilibrium in a closed system when conditions are stable. An open system is not necessarily incapable of every steady state, but a steady flow condition differs from the closed-system equilibrium studied here. Define the system boundary and whether gases can escape. If a product is continuously removed, its concentration behaviour and reaction extent differ from those in a sealed mixture.", "diagram": "closed-system"}, {"title": "Reversible changes at the particle scale", "kind": "learn", "text": "A reversible process can proceed in both directions under the relevant conditions. In a sealed liquid–vapour system, evaporation and condensation occur simultaneously. In N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), bonds break and form in forward and reverse reactions. Early behaviour depends on starting composition. Starting with mainly reactants usually gives a large forward rate and a small reverse rate; as products accumulate, the reverse rate grows. Equilibrium is reached when rates become equal, not when particles stop reacting. Molecules continue changing identities while average macroscopic properties stay constant. Use a reversible arrow to distinguish the two possible directions.", "diagram": "dynamic-equilibrium"}, {"title": "Rates, concentrations and equilibrium position", "kind": "learn", "text": "At equilibrium, forward and reverse rates are equal, so net concentration changes cease. Reactant and product concentrations are constant but need not be equal. A product-favoured equilibrium can contain substantially more products, yet the two opposing rates still match. Equilibrium position describes relative composition under the conditions, while rate describes how fast the system reaches that composition. An equal-rate graph does not imply the reaction has “finished.” Explain which quantity is plotted: a concentration graph approaches plateaus, whereas a rate graph approaches a common rate. Their vertical values mean different things.", "diagram": "equilibrium-graphs"}, {"title": "Forward and reverse activation energies", "kind": "learn", "text": "For one schematic pathway, forward Eₐ is transition-state energy minus reactant energy, and reverse Eₐ is transition-state energy minus product energy. ΔH = forward Eₐ − reverse Eₐ. For an exothermic forward reaction, the reverse barrier measured from lower-energy products is larger. Heating can increase both rates but does not necessarily increase them by the same factor. In an exothermic forward system, the endothermic reverse direction is favoured by a temperature rise, changing the final composition. Avoid the incorrect explanation that one direction entirely stops after conditions change. Both continue throughout re-equilibration.", "diagram": "reversible-energy"}, {"title": "Equilibrium is dynamic rather than stopped", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a closed system at equilibrium, forward and reverse reaction rates are equal. Particles continue to react and concentrations remain constant because the opposing changes balance. Equal rates do not require equal reactant and product concentrations. A concentration–time plateau alone does not prove equilibrium: depletion or a stopped reaction can also produce a plateau. Combine the graph with information about reversibility, closure and continuing processes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Closure applies to matter exchange", "kind": "learn", "text": "A suitable closed system prevents relevant substances escaping or entering while allowing energy exchange where specified. An open container losing a gaseous product can behave differently because that product is removed. Specify the chemical equation and state symbols when deciding whether the model applies. A diagram with the same number of particles on each side illustrates one possible composition, not a rule that equilibrium creates equal numbers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Describe rates and concentrations separately", "kind": "learn", "text": "Immediately after a disturbance, concentrations or volume can change before composition readjusts through reaction. At the new equilibrium the opposing rates are again equal, but neither their common value nor the concentrations must equal the original values. Avoid saying the reaction reverses completely or the system wants to compensate. Explain the changed concentrations, altered collision opportunities and resulting net reaction until rate equality is restored.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "che12-equilibrium-dynamic-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A closed reversible reaction has constant unequal concentrations of reactant and product. Explain whether this can be equilibrium and name evidence beyond a concentration plateau. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Unequal concentrations are compatible with equilibrium.", "[2] Forward and reverse rates must be equal and nonzero in a dynamic system.", "[2] Evidence must support reversibility and system closure/ongoing opposing processes; a plateau alone could also reflect exhaustion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-dynamic-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why a reversible reaction losing gaseous product through an open outlet need not attain the same equilibrium as a sealed vessel. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Product removal changes its amount/concentration in the reacting system.", "[2] Reverse-reaction opportunities are reduced relative to a comparable sealed system, supporting net further product formation.", "[2] Ongoing matter loss means the same closed-system equilibrium conditions are not maintained."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "At dynamic equilibrium, what must be equal?", "o": ["Forward and reverse reaction rates.", "All species concentrations.", "Reactant and product masses.", "The number of particles shown on each side of every equation."], "a": 0, "why": "Constant concentrations result from balanced opposing rates, not equal concentrations."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-equilibrium-concentration", "title": "Equilibrium responses to concentration changes", "scope": "Predict direction from the balanced equation · Use a reaction quotient where appropriate · Separate the immediate jump from readjustment", "parent": "che12-equilibrium", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Equilibrium responses to concentration changes</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Predict direction from the balanced equation</li><li>Use a reaction quotient where appropriate</li><li>Separate the immediate jump from readjustment</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Le Châtelier’s principle", "kind": "learn", "text": "If an equilibrium system is disturbed, its net response tends to counteract the imposed change as it approaches a new equilibrium. Identify the perturbation, predict the net direction, explain its effect and state the new composition. The response only partially counteracts the disturbance; it does not necessarily restore the original concentrations or pressure. A useful explanation also considers collision frequency and the initial imbalance of rates. Le Châtelier is a qualitative prediction tool and should not replace clear reasoning about which species or variable was changed. A catalyst accelerates re-equilibration without causing a position shift.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Adding or removing a dissolved species", "kind": "learn", "text": "For Fe³⁺(aq) + SCN⁻(aq) ⇌ FeSCN²⁺(aq), adding thiocyanate increases its concentration immediately. Forward collision frequency increases before composition readjusts, so forward rate initially exceeds reverse rate. More complex forms until the rates match again. Removing a product favours net product formation. Removing a reactant favours the reverse direction. On a concentration–time graph, the directly added species has an instantaneous upward jump, while species not directly changed respond smoothly afterward. Distinguish the imposed jump from the subsequent reaction response. Adding a solution also changes volume unless the question explicitly neglects dilution.", "diagram": "concentration-shift"}, {"title": "Equilibrium investigations and observation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A visible colour change can indicate a composition shift, but intensity also depends on dilution and optical path length. For a coloured complex equilibrium, use matched volumes and containers and control temperature. Explain a shift using an equation and relevant species, not colour alone. Repeated measurements and calibration strengthen quantitative claims. An experiment should distinguish a reversible response from irreversible precipitation or decomposition. A sound conclusion identifies conditions and limitations. In an exam, cite the actual observed colour or concentration data, then connect them to forward/reverse rate imbalance and eventual equal rates.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Predict direction from the balanced equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a fixed-temperature equilibrium, adding a reacting species commonly produces a net change that consumes some of that addition; removing it favours a net change that replenishes some. Identify whether the species is a reactant or product in the written equation. Le Châtelier’s principle predicts direction, but a molecular explanation names how concentration changes alter opposing reaction opportunities. The system does not generally restore every concentration to its original value.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use a reaction quotient where appropriate", "kind": "learn", "text": "For A + B ⇌ C, Qc = [C]/([A][B]) at a given instant. Compare Qc with fixed-temperature Kc: Qc < Kc supports net forward reaction and Qc > Kc net reverse reaction. Stoichiometric coefficients become powers in the expression. Adding a species can change Q immediately while K remains unchanged if temperature is constant. Do not confuse a temporary concentration ratio with a new equilibrium constant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate the immediate jump from readjustment", "kind": "learn", "text": "If A is injected into a rigid vessel, [A] rises immediately; B and C do not instantly jump merely because A was added. Subsequent forward reaction consumes A and B and forms C in the coefficient ratio. The final A concentration can remain above its original value even though some addition is consumed. Annotate graph segments clearly: physical addition and chemical readjustment are different events.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Graph interpretation investigation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Graph interpretation investigation", "prompt": "A gaseous equilibrium with more reactant-side gas moles is compressed at fixed temperature. Describe the concentration graph stages and explain why a measured colour change needs careful interpretation.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["All gas concentrations jump up immediately. [1]", "Net reaction then favours the side with fewer gas moles. [1]", "Chemical concentration changes follow stoichiometric ratios after the imposed jump. [1]", "A new constant-composition equilibrium is reached with equal opposing rates. [1]", "Kc remains constant at fixed temperature. [1]", "Colour intensity is affected by concentration and optical path; distinguish compression from composition shift using matched measurement conditions. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-concentration-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For A + B ⇌ C at constant temperature, A is added without changing volume. Predict the immediate concentration change, net direction and effect on Kc. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] [A] immediately increases; [B] and [C] need not jump at that instant.", "[2] Qc decreases, so net forward reaction consumes some A and B and forms C.", "[2] Kc remains unchanged because temperature is fixed; original concentrations are not necessarily restored."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-concentration-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "For A + B ⇌ C with Kc = 4.0, current concentrations are [A] = 1.0, [B] = 1.0 and [C] = 2.0 mol L⁻¹. Calculate Qc and predict net direction. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Qc = [C]/([A][B]) = 2.0/(1.0 × 1.0) = 2.0.", "[2] Qc < Kc, so net forward reaction is favoured until equilibrium is approached.", "[2] C increases while A and B decrease in a 1:1:1 ratio in the fixed-volume system."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Adding a reactant at fixed temperature changes which quantity immediately?", "o": ["The reaction quotient, rather than the equilibrium constant.", "Kc because every disturbance changes Kc.", "Only the atomic masses.", "Every concentration by an identical instantaneous amount."], "a": 0, "why": "Kc is temperature-dependent; current concentration ratios can change independently."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-equilibrium-pressure", "title": "Gas equilibrium: volume, pressure and inert gases", "scope": "Count gaseous coefficients, not all species · Inert gas effects depend on the constraint · Interpret concentration graphs after compression", "parent": "che12-equilibrium", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Gas equilibrium: volume, pressure and inert gases</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Count gaseous coefficients, not all species</li><li>Inert gas effects depend on the constraint</li><li>Interpret concentration graphs after compression</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Volume changes for gaseous equilibria", "kind": "learn", "text": "Decreasing volume raises the concentrations and partial pressures of all gaseous species immediately. If the two sides have different gaseous stoichiometric amounts, net reaction commonly favours the side with fewer gas moles. For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, compression favours ammonia. If both sides have equal gaseous mole totals, volume change need not shift the equilibrium position, although individual rates can change. Count gaseous coefficients only; solids and liquids are not part of the gas-mole comparison. On a graph, all gas concentrations jump on compression before the slower chemical response. Kc remains unchanged at fixed temperature.", "diagram": "pressure-shift"}, {"title": "Inert gas and pressure distinctions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adding an inert gas at fixed volume and temperature increases total pressure but leaves reacting species’ concentrations and partial pressures unchanged in the ideal model. Thus equilibrium position is unchanged. If inert gas is added at constant total pressure, the container expands, reducing reactant partial pressures and potentially shifting toward more gas moles. The words “pressure increases” alone do not tell you the cause. Identify whether volume, temperature or gas composition changed. This distinction is common in demanding exam explanations because a simple “high pressure favours fewer moles” slogan can produce an incorrect prediction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Collision explanations after compression", "kind": "learn", "text": "For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, compression immediately raises all reacting gas concentrations. Collision frequencies for both directions increase, but the reactant-side rate is enhanced more strongly for this equilibrium’s kinetic response, producing net ammonia formation. As reactants fall and ammonia rises relative to that immediate compressed state, opposing rates readjust and become equal. Do not claim only one direction speeds up. A quantitative rate law cannot always be read directly from an overall equation, so this is qualitative collision reasoning, not an invented elementary rate expression.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Count gaseous coefficients, not all species", "kind": "learn", "text": "A decrease in volume at constant temperature raises gas concentrations and pressure. A gas equilibrium can then shift towards the side with fewer total gaseous stoichiometric amounts. Solids and liquids are excluded from that count. If gaseous coefficients sum equally on both sides, compression does not predict a composition shift in the ideal model, even though pressure and reaction rates can change. State the balanced equation before counting.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Inert gas effects depend on the constraint", "kind": "learn", "text": "Adding a chemically inert gas at constant volume raises total pressure but leaves reacting-gas partial pressures unchanged in the ideal model, so no equilibrium shift is predicted. At constant total pressure, adding inert gas can expand the vessel and lower reacting-gas partial pressures; that dilution may shift a gas equilibrium towards the side with more gas particles. Merely saying pressure increased is insufficient without explaining how reacting partial pressures changed.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interpret concentration graphs after compression", "kind": "learn", "text": "Compression immediately raises concentrations of all contained gases. The subsequent equilibrium readjustment can lower some from that immediate value and raise others further. A product concentration decreasing from its compressed value does not necessarily fall below its original concentration. Distinguish total pressure, partial pressure, amount and concentration throughout the answer; a decrease in gas amount is not automatically a decrease in concentration when volume also changes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: An inert gas comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: An inert gas comparison", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Compression of ammonia equilibrium", "prompt": "For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), temperature is fixed while volume halves. Predict the immediate concentration changes, net direction and Kc change.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["All reacting gas concentrations immediately double. [1]", "There are four gas moles on the left and two on the right per equation. [1]", "Net response favours ammonia, the fewer-gas side. [1]", "The system approaches a new equilibrium with equal forward/reverse rates. [1]", "Kc remains unchanged because temperature is fixed. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "An inert gas comparison", "prompt": "An inert gas is added to an equilibrium mixture at constant temperature and fixed volume. Explain why increased total pressure does not necessarily shift equilibrium.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Reactant and product amounts and volume remain unchanged. [1]", "Their concentrations and partial pressures therefore remain unchanged. [1]", "Their equilibrium ratio and position remain unchanged despite higher total pressure. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-pressure-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), predict compression at constant temperature and compare inert-gas addition at constant volume. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reactants have four gaseous coefficient units and products two; compression favours net NH₃ formation.", "[2] Compression changes reacting partial pressures, then composition readjusts.", "[2] Inert gas at constant volume changes total pressure without changing ideal reacting partial pressures, so no shift is predicted."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-pressure-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why compression does not shift H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) in the ideal model, while it can change the reaction rates. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Both sides have two gaseous coefficient units.", "[2] Compression scales the numerator and denominator of the concentration ratio equally, leaving Qc unchanged at equilibrium.", "[2] Reacting concentrations rise and encounter rates can change, but equal forward/reverse rates can remain compatible with unchanged equilibrium composition."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What must be specified when discussing addition of an inert gas?", "o": ["Whether volume or total pressure is held constant.", "Only the colour of the vessel.", "That inert gas always becomes a reactant.", "That total pressure equals every reacting partial pressure."], "a": 0, "why": "The constraint determines whether reacting partial pressures change."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-equilibrium-temperature", "title": "Temperature, catalysts and reaction energetics", "scope": "Temperature changes equilibrium and rates · Catalysis changes approach rather than fixed-temperature K · Use models without inventing mechanism", "parent": "che12-equilibrium", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Temperature, catalysts and reaction energetics</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Temperature changes equilibrium and rates</li><li>Catalysis changes approach rather than fixed-temperature K</li><li>Use models without inventing mechanism</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Temperature and the sign of ΔH", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an exothermic forward reaction, increasing temperature favours the endothermic reverse direction, lowering product fraction and usually lowering its forward Kc. Decreasing temperature favours the exothermic direction. For an endothermic forward reaction, these predictions reverse. You may represent heat as a conceptual product or reactant to aid reasoning, but heat is not a concentration term in Kc. Heating generally increases kinetic rates while possibly decreasing desired equilibrium yield. State both consequences when discussing industrial conditions. Temperature is the course variable that changes the equilibrium constant for a specified reaction.", "diagram": "temperature-shift"}, {"title": "Catalyst at equilibrium", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst supplies a different pathway with lower activation barriers for forward and reverse processes. Both rates increase while their equality at equilibrium is preserved. Concentrations, equilibrium position, Kc and ΔH are unchanged if temperature and other conditions remain fixed. A catalyst can make industrial production practical because equilibrium is approached faster, but it does not improve the theoretical equilibrium yield solely by being added. A rate graph can show both rates rising together on catalyst addition, whereas a concentration graph stays level. A catalyst in a real plant may allow different operating conditions, which can indirectly alter yield; distinguish this from its direct effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Heating: both rates change unequally", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heating increases the fraction of particles able to overcome each directional activation barrier. Both forward and reverse rates increase initially. For an exothermic forward reaction, the reverse endothermic barrier is larger in the simple energy-profile model, so its successful-collision fraction is more sensitive to temperature and the reverse rate increases more strongly. Composition shifts toward reactants until rates match. This explains the direction through energy distribution and rate imbalance, while Le Châtelier predicts the same response. State that constant concentrations return at a new equilibrium, not that reaction stops.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Temperature changes equilibrium and rates", "kind": "learn", "text": "Raising temperature commonly increases both forward and reverse rates, but the equilibrium composition depends on the relative energetic response. For an exothermic forward reaction, heating favours net reverse change and lowers the forward-equation equilibrium constant. For an endothermic forward reaction, heating favours net forward change and increases that constant. Use the equation’s stated direction and enthalpy sign; reversing the equation reverses the thermal interpretation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Catalysis changes approach rather than fixed-temperature K", "kind": "learn", "text": "A catalyst supplies an alternative pathway and accelerates opposing processes. It does not change the reactant/product energy difference or the equilibrium constant for the same reaction at the same temperature. A catalyst can nevertheless be practically valuable by reaching an economically useful state sooner. Separate rate, equilibrium yield and actual production per unit time when evaluating an industrial condition.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use models without inventing mechanism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Writing heat on one side is a mnemonic for predicting a temperature shift, not a claim that heat is a substance with a concentration included in Kc. Collision theory explains changes in successful collisions; Le Châtelier predicts direction. A complete answer links the chosen condition to rate and equilibrium effects independently. In industry, a compromise temperature can provide sufficient rate while retaining acceptable equilibrium yield.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Heating a coloured equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Heating a coloured equilibrium", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Heating a coloured equilibrium", "prompt": "For N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g), ΔH > 0. NO₂ is brown and N₂O₄ is colourless. Predict the colour change on heating and the direction of Kc change.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The forward direction is endothermic. [1]", "Heating favours net NO₂ formation. [1]", "The mixture becomes more intensely brown if other observational conditions are controlled. [1]", "The forward Kc increases with temperature. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-temperature-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For an exothermic forward reaction, explain the effects of heating on forward rate, equilibrium product proportion and Kc. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Forward rate commonly increases because more collisions have sufficient energy.", "[2] Heating favours the endothermic reverse direction, reducing equilibrium product proportion in this comparison.", "[2] Kc for the written exothermic forward equation decreases; rate increase does not imply greater equilibrium yield."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-temperature-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why adding a catalyst to a reversible reaction at fixed temperature can improve production speed without increasing equilibrium yield. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] An alternative lower-barrier pathway increases reaction rates.", "[2] Both directions are affected, so equilibrium is reached sooner.", "[2] Overall ΔH and fixed-temperature K are unchanged; equilibrium composition is not improved by catalysis alone."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which change can change Kc for the same written reaction?", "o": ["Temperature change.", "Adding a catalyst alone at fixed temperature.", "Adding inert gas at fixed volume and temperature.", "Rewriting a concentration graph axis without altering the system."], "a": 0, "why": "Equilibrium constants depend on temperature for a specified reaction."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-equilibrium-constant", "title": "Kc, heterogeneous systems and carbonate chemistry", "scope": "Build the expression from the reaction · Keep the equation convention visible · Apply carbonate equilibria to a connected system", "parent": "che12-equilibrium", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Kc, heterogeneous systems and carbonate chemistry</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Build the expression from the reaction</li><li>Keep the equation convention visible</li><li>Apply carbonate equilibria to a connected system</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Writing a homogeneous Kc expression", "kind": "learn", "text": "For aA(aq) + bB(aq) ⇌ cC(aq) + dD(aq), Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ). Use equilibrium concentrations and exponents from balanced coefficients. Kc is reaction-specific and temperature-specific. It is not a rate constant. For N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, Kc = [NH₃]²/([N₂][H₂]³). Reversing the equation reciprocates Kc, and multiplying coefficients raises Kc to that factor. The course commonly uses concentration-based expressions; more advanced thermodynamic constants use activities. Follow the notation and units conventions requested by the assessment instead of assuming a coefficient is an additive multiplier.", "diagram": "kc"}, {"title": "Heterogeneous equilibria", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a heterogeneous equilibrium, pure solids and pure liquids have effectively constant activities and are omitted from the course Kc expression. For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), the expression is Kc = [CO₂]. Adding more of a pure solid does not change the equilibrium gas concentration while both required solid phases remain present. If a phase is completely used up, the earlier equilibrium description may no longer apply. Do not omit every liquid-labelled or dissolved species indiscriminately: an aqueous solute’s concentration belongs in the expression. Solvent water is normally omitted when its activity is treated as constant.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "What a large or small Kc means", "kind": "learn", "text": "A large Kc indicates the written equilibrium favours products in the sense of its concentration expression; a small Kc indicates reactant favouring. It does not tell you the speed, activation energy or complete conversion. Values should be interpreted with stoichiometry and concentration units in mind. A reaction with large Kc can be slow. A very small amount of product can still exist at equilibrium when Kc is small. Year 12 requires writing expressions and qualitatively interpreting constants. Simple numerical substitution is useful practice; lengthy equilibrium-solving algebra beyond supplied information is labelled extension where used.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concentration graphs after a disturbance", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read a graph in stages: initial equilibrium, instantaneous imposed change, transient net response and final equilibrium. Direct addition creates a jump only for the species directly changed if volume is unchanged. Compression creates jumps in all gas concentrations. Temperature changes usually cause smooth composition adjustment unless volume also changes. A catalyst causes no concentration shift. Final concentrations generally differ from the original ones, but forward and reverse rates are equal again. Use stoichiometric ratios to compare changes due solely to reaction. Do not measure a total graph jump as if it were all chemical consumption.", "diagram": "equilibrium-graphs"}, {"title": "Ocean carbonate chemistry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rising atmospheric CO₂ can increase dissolved CO₂ and carbonic-acid-related chemistry: CO₂(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H₂CO₃(aq), followed by acid ionisation. Additional H⁺ reacts with carbonate, CO₃²⁻ + H⁺ ⇌ HCO₃⁻, lowering available carbonate ions. This can reduce conditions favouring calcium carbonate formation in marine organisms. Ocean acidification means a decrease in pH; it does not require seawater to become pH below 7. Real seawater contains many coupled equilibria and biological influences, so a simple equation is a partial model. Relate the directional chemistry to evidence rather than claiming every organism responds identically.", "diagram": "carbonate"}, {"title": "Build the expression from the reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, the concentration expression uses [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ/([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ) for included solution or gas species in the school model. Pure solids and pure liquids have effectively constant activity and are omitted. Their omission does not mean they are absent or chemically irrelevant. Changing the amount of an existing pure solid alone does not change K or equilibrium dissolved concentrations while that phase remains present under the specified conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Keep the equation convention visible", "kind": "learn", "text": "Reversing the reaction gives the reciprocal constant; multiplying all coefficients by a factor raises the original constant to that factor. A large K indicates products favoured at equilibrium for that equation convention, not a rapid reaction or complete absence of reactants. Substitute equilibrium values into K, or use nonequilibrium values only when explicitly calculating Q. Avoid mixing initial amounts with final equilibrium concentrations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Apply carbonate equilibria to a connected system", "kind": "learn", "text": "Dissolved carbon dioxide, carbonic-acid chemistry and carbonate species are connected through equilibria. Changing CO₂ exchange or hydrogen-ion concentration can alter species proportions and carbonate availability. A diagram of these links is not a claim that every process has the same equilibrium constant or rate. State the disturbance, relevant equation and consequence, then qualify the result with system conditions rather than asserting that all carbonate solids dissolve in every environment. The syllabus connects ocean carbonate chemistry with international responses to greenhouse emissions. The Kyoto Protocol is a historical international agreement addressing greenhouse-gas emissions; the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change assesses scientific evidence about climate change, impacts and responses. An assessment body and a negotiated agreement have different roles. Use carbonate equilibria to explain why increased dissolved CO₂ can increase acidity and reduce carbonate availability for calcification, then distinguish chemical evidence from the economic, social and political decisions about emissions. These named syllabus examples do not imply that one equilibrium equation alone establishes every biological outcome.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive equilibrium quotient", "kind": "interactive", "model": "equilibrium"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A Kc expression and value", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A Kc expression and value", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Solid addition", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Ocean reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A Kc expression and value", "prompt": "For H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g), equilibrium concentrations are 0.200, 0.100 and 0.800 mol L⁻¹ respectively. Write and evaluate Kc.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Kc = [HI]²/([H₂][I₂]). [1]", "Kc = 0.800²/(0.200 × 0.100). [1]", "Kc = 32.0 for the specified temperature and concentration convention. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Solid addition", "prompt": "For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), both solids are present. Write Kc and predict the effect of adding more CaCO₃ at fixed temperature and volume.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Kc = [CO₂]. [1]", "Pure solid activities are treated as constant and omitted. [1]", "Adding more solid does not change equilibrium CO₂ concentration under these assumptions. [1]", "Kc is fixed by temperature; the conclusion assumes both solid phases remain available. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ocean reasoning", "prompt": "Explain how additional dissolved CO₂ can reduce carbonate availability without making seawater acidic in the pH-below-7 sense.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Additional CO₂ increases carbonic-acid-related acid equilibria. [1]", "Hydrogen-ion concentration increases and pH falls. [1]", "H⁺ reacts with CO₃²⁻ to form HCO₃⁻, lowering carbonate availability. [1]", "A pH decrease is acidification even if the final pH remains above 7. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-equilibrium-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-constant-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ Ca²⁺(aq) + CO₃²⁻(aq), write the concentration expression and evaluate adding more solid while the solid phase is already present. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] K = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻] in the stated school concentration model.", "[2] Pure solid CaCO₃ is omitted because its activity is effectively constant.", "[2] At fixed temperature adding more of the existing pure solid alone does not change the equilibrium ion concentrations while that phase remains."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-equilibrium-constant-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "For A ⇌ 2B with Kc = 4.0, determine the constant for 2B ⇌ A and explain why the original Kc does not describe rate. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Reversing the equation gives K′ = 1/Kc.", "[2] K′ = 1/4.0 = 0.25 for the reverse convention.", "[2] K describes the equilibrium concentration relationship; kinetics and activation barriers govern how quickly that state is reached."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why are pure solids omitted from a heterogeneous equilibrium expression?", "o": ["Their activity is effectively constant while the phase is present.", "They never react.", "Their mass must always be zero.", "Every solid has the same molar mass."], "a": 0, "why": "Omission reflects the activity convention, not chemical inactivity."}, {"q": "At dynamic equilibrium", "o": ["All reactions stop", "Forward and reverse rates are equal", "Reactant and product concentrations must be equal", "All products disappear"], "a": 1, "why": "Constant macroscopic composition follows equal opposing rates."}, {"q": "Which directly changes Kc for the same equation?", "o": ["A catalyst", "Adding reactant", "Changing temperature", "Adding more pure solid"], "a": 2, "why": "Kc depends on temperature for a specified reaction."}, {"q": "A catalyst at equilibrium", "o": ["Shifts toward products", "Changes ΔH", "Speeds both directions without changing composition", "Stops the reverse reaction"], "a": 2, "why": "The equilibrium position is unchanged."}, {"q": "For CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), Kc contains", "o": ["Both solids", "Only [CO₂]", "No terms", "[CaO]/[CaCO₃] only"], "a": 1, "why": "Pure solid activities are omitted."}, {"q": "Large Kc tells us the reaction is", "o": ["Always fast", "Product favoured, not necessarily fast", "Irreversible in every case", "Endothermic necessarily"], "a": 1, "why": "Equilibrium composition and kinetic rate are different."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-acids-buffers-models", "title": "Brønsted–Lowry models, conjugates and amphiprotic species", "scope": "Identify actual proton transfer · Conjugate pairs differ by one proton · Amphiprotic is a capability demonstrated in reactions", "parent": "che12-acids-buffers", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Brønsted–Lowry models, conjugates and amphiprotic species</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Identify actual proton transfer</li><li>Conjugate pairs differ by one proton</li><li>Amphiprotic is a capability demonstrated in reactions</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Why acid–base models changed", "kind": "learn", "text": "Davy related acid behaviour to replaceable hydrogen. Arrhenius described acids and bases through H⁺ and OH⁻ production in water, explaining many aqueous observations. Brønsted–Lowry broadened the model to proton transfer: an acid donates a proton and a base accepts one. NH₃(g) + HCl(g) → NH₄Cl(s) illustrates acid–base chemistry without water as solvent. The wider model accounts for ammonia’s basicity without requiring an OH group in its formula. Models are judged by explanatory scope and evidence. Explain what a new model adds rather than saying earlier models were entirely useless.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Proton donation and acceptance", "kind": "learn", "text": "In HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻, HCl donates H⁺ and water accepts it. In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, ammonia accepts H⁺ while water donates it. The proton transfers without its original bonding electron pair, so charges change appropriately. The species losing H⁺ has charge reduced by one unit; the species gaining it has charge increased by one. An acid need not be electrically neutral and a base need not be negative. Identify roles in the actual reaction rather than assigning one permanent role to every species.", "diagram": "proton-transfer"}, {"title": "Conjugate acid–base pairs", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conjugate pair differs by one proton. After donation, an acid becomes its conjugate base; after acceptance, a base becomes its conjugate acid. In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, the pairs are NH₄⁺/NH₃ and H₂O/OH⁻. They are not NH₃/OH⁻, which are both bases in their respective directions. Show each pair’s formula and one-proton difference. Charge must differ by one. A strong acid’s conjugate base has very little tendency to accept protons from water under ordinary conditions. This helps explain why chloride salts of non-hydrolysing cations are often approximately neutral.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiprotic species", "kind": "learn", "text": "An amphiprotic species can donate or accept a proton depending on its reaction partner. HCO₃⁻ can donate H⁺ to form CO₃²⁻ or accept H⁺ to form H₂CO₃. Water can form OH⁻ or H₃O⁺. A species having both hydrogen and a negative charge is a candidate, but those features alone are not proof; the chemical structure and proton-transfer behaviour matter. Write two equations to demonstrate the two roles. The broader term amphoteric describes acting as an acid or base, whereas amphiprotic explicitly refers to proton transfer. Use the model and terminology requested by the question.", "diagram": "amphiprotic"}, {"title": "Monoprotic and polyprotic acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "A monoprotic acid can donate one relevant proton per molecule, while a polyprotic acid can donate more in successive steps. Ethanoic acid is monoprotic despite containing four hydrogens, because only its acidic O–H proton is readily donated in the course context. H₃PO₄ has three successive proton-donation steps, each with a different equilibrium. Do not equate the total hydrogen subscript with acid proticity. Each later donation from an increasingly negative species is typically less favourable. In complete-neutralisation stoichiometry, the number of donated protons affects the acid:OH⁻ ratio, but it does not mean all ionisation steps are complete in water.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Identify actual proton transfer", "kind": "learn", "text": "A Brønsted–Lowry acid donates a proton and a base accepts it. In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, ammonia is the base and water the acid in the forward direction. A substance’s role depends on the reaction partner, rather than an immutable label based on whether it contains hydrogen. Include charge and atoms to show the transferred proton. Water can act as acid or base in different reactions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conjugate pairs differ by one proton", "kind": "learn", "text": "An acid and its conjugate base differ by one H⁺, so charge changes by one as well. H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ and HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻ are separate conjugate pairs. NH₄⁺/NH₃ is another. Species that merely coexist in an equation are not necessarily a pair. To find a conjugate base remove H⁺; to find a conjugate acid add H⁺, then check the new charge.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiprotic is a capability demonstrated in reactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "An amphiprotic species can donate or accept a proton. Hydrogen carbonate can donate to form carbonate or accept to form carbonic acid. This does not require both processes to be equally favourable under every condition. Use a balanced equation to demonstrate each role and distinguish amphiprotic from simply having several hydrogen atoms. In water, competing acid/base tendencies can determine the net solution behaviour.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Conjugate pairs", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Conjugate pairs", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Conjugate pairs", "prompt": "For HSO₄⁻ + HCO₃⁻ ⇌ SO₄²⁻ + H₂CO₃, identify the forward acid/base and both conjugate pairs.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["HSO₄⁻ donates H⁺ and is the acid. [1]", "HCO₃⁻ accepts H⁺ and is the base. [1]", "HSO₄⁻/SO₄²⁻ is one conjugate pair. [1]", "H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ is the other. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-models-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Identify acid, base and conjugate pairs in NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₄⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq). (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] NH₃ accepts H⁺ and is the base; H₂O donates H⁺ and is the acid.", "[2] NH₄⁺/NH₃ is one conjugate acid/base pair.", "[2] H₂O/OH⁻ is the other; each pair differs by one H⁺ and one charge unit."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-models-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Write reactions showing HCO₃⁻ acting as a base with H⁺ and as an acid with OH⁻. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃ demonstrates proton acceptance.", "[2] HCO₃⁻ + OH⁻ → CO₃²⁻ + H₂O demonstrates proton donation.", "[2] Both conserve atoms and charge; the two roles establish amphiprotic capability, not equal tendency."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What distinguishes a conjugate acid/base pair?", "o": ["One proton and one charge unit.", "One electron only in every case.", "The same charge and hydrogen count.", "Any two species on opposite sides of an equation."], "a": 0, "why": "Proton loss removes H⁺, changing composition and charge together."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-acids-buffers-strength", "title": "Acid strength, concentration, Ka and Kw", "scope": "Strength concerns equilibrium extent · Water’s ionisation links the ions · Use the approximation justified by the question", "parent": "che12-acids-buffers", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Acid strength, concentration, Ka and Kw</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Strength concerns equilibrium extent</li><li>Water’s ionisation links the ions</li><li>Use the approximation justified by the question</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Strength and concentration are separate", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acid strength describes degree of ionisation at equilibrium under stated conditions. Concentration describes total amount per solution volume. A dilute strong acid may have lower [H⁺] than a concentrated weak acid. Compare strength under suitable matched conditions or use Ka. At equal analytical concentration, a strong monoprotic acid generally has greater [H⁺] than a weak monoprotic acid. Conductivity also depends on ion concentration and mobility, so it alone is not a universal strength ranking across unmatched solutions. A particle diagram should show mostly ions for a strong dilute acid and a mixture dominated by un-ionised molecules for a typical weak acid.", "diagram": "acid-strength"}, {"title": "Acidity constant Ka", "kind": "learn", "text": "For HA(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + A⁻(aq), Ka = [H₃O⁺][A⁻]/[HA] when solvent activity is treated as constant. Larger Ka generally indicates stronger acid at the same temperature. The equilibrium concentration of un-ionised HA is used, not automatically the original total concentration. The Year 12 syllabus asks for strength represented through equations and acidity constants. Qualitative comparison and expression-writing are core. Approximate weak-acid equilibrium calculations are useful extension where explicitly labelled; do not treat every added algebraic method as a separate syllabus requirement. Ka does not describe how fast ionisation reaches equilibrium.", "diagram": "ka"}, {"title": "Water and Kw", "kind": "learn", "text": "Water weakly self-ionises: 2H₂O(l) ⇌ H₃O⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq). In course notation, Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ at 25 °C. Neutrality means [H⁺] = [OH⁻], giving each concentration 1.00 × 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹ at that temperature. Adding acid raises [H⁺] and shifts the ionisation equilibrium, reducing [OH⁻]. Kw depends on temperature, so neutral pH is not universally 7. A low ionic concentration explains water’s weak conductivity compared with electrolyte solutions. “Pure water has no ions” is incorrect.", "diagram": "water-ionisation"}, {"title": "Strength concerns equilibrium extent", "kind": "learn", "text": "A strong acid is extensively ionised in the specified aqueous context; a weak acid establishes appreciable undissociated acid at equilibrium. Concentration refers to amount per volume. Ka quantifies the acid dissociation relationship, so comparing Ka at the same temperature supports strength comparisons. A larger Ka means stronger acid in that comparison; a smaller pKa means larger Ka. Do not infer strength solely from a supplied concentration or from how dangerous a substance sounds.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Water’s ionisation links the ions", "kind": "learn", "text": "At 25 °C the school value Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴. Raising one concentration reduces the other in the equilibrium relation. Neutral means [H⁺] = [OH⁻], not no ions. Kw varies with temperature, so pH + pOH = 14.00 is a 25 °C approximation rather than a temperature-independent law. State temperature when applying it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the approximation justified by the question", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a dilute strong monoprotic acid well above water’s own contribution, [H⁺] approximately equals acid concentration. Weak-acid calculations require the dissociation equilibrium and cannot automatically use that equality. A polyprotic acid has successive stages with potentially different strengths, so multiplying concentration by every hydrogen in its formula is not generally justified. The response should explain the assumption before performing logarithms.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Strength evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Strength evidence", "prompt": "Two monoprotic acids have Ka values 1.0 × 10⁻³ and 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ at the same temperature. Identify the stronger acid and explain why this alone does not specify pH.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The acid with Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻³ is stronger. [1]", "Its equilibrium favours ionised products more strongly. [1]", "pH also depends on concentration, so Ka alone is insufficient. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-strength-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "At 25 °C, [H⁺] = 2.00 × 10⁻⁴ mol L⁻¹. Calculate [OH⁻] using Kw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ and identify acidic/basic character. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] [OH⁻] = Kw/[H⁺].", "[2] [OH⁻] = (1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴)/(2.00 × 10⁻⁴) = 5.00 × 10⁻¹¹ mol L⁻¹.", "[2] [H⁺] exceeds [OH⁻], so the solution is acidic at the stated conditions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-strength-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Two monoprotic acids have equal concentration at the same temperature but Ka values 1.0 × 10⁻³ and 1.0 × 10⁻⁶. Compare strength and explain why concentration is not the criterion. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The acid with Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻³ is stronger in this comparison.", "[2] Its dissociation equilibrium more strongly favours ion formation.", "[2] Equal analytical concentration does not mean equal ionisation extent or equal [H⁺]."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "At 25 °C, a neutral aqueous solution has what relation?", "o": ["[H⁺] = [OH⁻].", "Neither ion is present.", "Every dissolved salt must be absent.", "Kw is zero."], "a": 0, "why": "Neutrality is equality of the relevant ion concentrations."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-acids-buffers-ph", "title": "Strong-acid/base pH and excess after reaction", "scope": "Do the reaction before calculating the logarithm · Choose the correct ion and temperature relation · Report a chemically interpretable result", "parent": "che12-acids-buffers", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Strong-acid/base pH and excess after reaction</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Do the reaction before calculating the logarithm</li><li>Choose the correct ion and temperature relation</li><li>Report a chemically interpretable result</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "pH and strong-acid calculations", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a dilute strong monoprotic acid, assume near-complete ionisation and use [H⁺] ≈ analytical acid concentration when water’s own contribution is negligible. Then pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]. If pH is given, [H⁺] = 10⁻ᵖᴴ. A very dilute acid near 10⁻⁷ mol L⁻¹ requires considering water rather than naively applying this approximation. Do not automatically multiply every polyprotic acid concentration by its total proton count; successive ionisation can be incomplete. For sulfuric acid, the first ionisation is strong and the second depends on equilibrium. Follow any explicit simplifying assumption given in a numerical question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong bases and excess after reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a strong hydroxide, obtain [OH⁻] from dissociation stoichiometry, then [H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] and calculate pH. At 25 °C, pH + pOH = 14.00 in the course approximation. Ba(OH)₂ gives two OH⁻ per formula unit. When acid and base mix, calculate their reacting mole amounts first, determine excess H⁺ or OH⁻ and divide by the combined solution volume. Do not average the initial pH values. Logarithmic pH values are not additive, and neutralisation changes the species. Equivalence composition may involve salt hydrolysis if weak acid or base products are present.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Do the reaction before calculating the logarithm", "kind": "learn", "text": "When acid and base are mixed, calculate their reacting amounts and use the balanced stoichiometry to find any excess. Divide excess ion amount by final solution volume before finding pH or pOH. Averaging the two original pH values is generally invalid because pH is logarithmic and the species react. If the equivalence mixture contains hydrolysing species, its pH requires their chemistry rather than a blind excess-ion calculation.", "diagram": "focused-ph"}, {"title": "Choose the correct ion and temperature relation", "kind": "learn", "text": "For excess strong acid use pH = −log[H⁺]. For excess strong base at 25 °C, pOH = −log[OH⁻] and pH = 14.00 − pOH using the stated Kw. Convert volumes to litres for cV. The final pH need not lie between both starting numerical pH values by a simple arithmetic rule; dilution, reaction and speciation determine it.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Report a chemically interpretable result", "kind": "learn", "text": "Show the equation, initial amounts, limiting species, excess and total volume. Carry sufficient intermediate digits so subtraction near equivalence is not rounded to zero prematurely. Very dilute solutions need consideration of water’s contribution, and activities can matter outside the simple classroom approximation. If a result is implausible, check the sign of the logarithm and whether the calculated concentration belongs to H⁺ or OH⁻.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Strong-base pH", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Strong-base pH", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Excess acid after mixing", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Excess acid after mixing", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Strong-base pH", "prompt": "Find pH of 0.0100 mol L⁻¹ Ba(OH)₂ at 25 °C, assuming complete dissociation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["[OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0100 = 0.0200 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "[H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0200 = 5.00 × 10⁻¹³. [1]", "pH = −log₁₀(5.00 × 10⁻¹³). [1]", "pH = 12.301. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Excess acid after mixing", "prompt": "Mix 20.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ HCl with 15.0 mL 0.100 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Find final pH at 25 °C with additive volumes.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(H⁺) = 0.00200 mol and n(OH⁻) = 0.00150 mol. [1]", "The reaction is 1:1, leaving 0.000500 mol H⁺. [1]", "Total volume = 0.0350 L. [1]", "[H⁺] = 0.000500/0.0350 = 0.0142857 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "pH = 1.845. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-ph-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Mix 20.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ HCl with 10.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Assuming additive volumes and complete reaction, calculate pH. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(H⁺) = 0.00200 mol; n(OH⁻) = 0.00100 mol, leaving 0.00100 mol H⁺.", "[2] [H⁺] = 0.00100/0.0300 = 0.03333 mol L⁻¹.", "[2] pH = −log(0.03333) = 1.48."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-ph-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "At 25 °C a strong-base solution has [OH⁻] = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol L⁻¹. Calculate pOH and pH, stating the temperature assumption. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] pOH = −log(2.00 × 10⁻³) = 2.699.", "[2] pH = 14.00 − 2.699 = 11.30.", "[2] The 14.00 sum follows the supplied 25 °C Kw approximation; it is not universal at all temperatures."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What should precede pH calculation after acid/base mixing?", "o": ["Stoichiometric reaction and excess-ion calculation.", "Averaging starting pH values.", "Ignoring total volume.", "Adding both H⁺ and OH⁻ as if they cannot react."], "a": 0, "why": "Reacting amounts determine the remaining ion concentration."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-acids-buffers-salts", "title": "Salt hydrolysis and competing acid/base tendencies", "scope": "Spectator ions need a chemical justification · Write the hydrolysis equation · Compare competing tendencies where necessary", "parent": "che12-acids-buffers", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Salt hydrolysis and competing acid/base tendencies</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Spectator ions need a chemical justification</li><li>Write the hydrolysis equation</li><li>Compare competing tendencies where necessary</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Salt hydrolysis: weak-acid conjugate bases", "kind": "learn", "text": "A salt can dissociate into ions that then react with water. Ethanoate accepts a proton: CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻, making an ethanoate salt solution basic if the cation is effectively non-hydrolysing. Na⁺ is a spectator in this model. Show the net ionic hydrolysis equation and identify the proton acceptor. The fact that a salt forms in neutralisation does not guarantee a neutral final solution. A weak acid’s conjugate base has appreciable basicity compared with a strong acid’s conjugate base. Actual pH also depends on concentration and equilibrium strength.", "diagram": "salt-hydrolysis"}, {"title": "Salt hydrolysis: weak-base conjugate acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ammonium can donate a proton: NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺. Therefore NH₄Cl solution is acidic in the course model; chloride has negligible basic hydrolysis. Salts of a strong acid and a strong base with non-hydrolysing ions are approximately neutral at 25 °C. For ions from polyprotic acids, an amphiprotic species may both donate and accept, and relative acid/base tendencies determine the net behaviour. HCO₃⁻ is commonly mildly basic, while H₂PO₄⁻ is commonly acidic. Do not force every salt into a simplistic one-direction rule without considering its ion species.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiprotic salts need two tendencies", "kind": "learn", "text": "H₂PO₄⁻ can donate H⁺ to become HPO₄²⁻ or accept H⁺ to become H₃PO₄. Which tendency dominates in water depends on the relevant acid/base constants; its solution is commonly acidic in the course context. HCO₃⁻ also has both roles but commonly gives a mildly basic solution. Show the possible equations when the question asks for amphiprotic behaviour. For a numerical or unfamiliar comparison, use supplied equilibrium data rather than a charge-only rule. Negative charge does not guarantee that every ion’s solution is basic, just as having hydrogen does not guarantee predominant acidity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Spectator ions need a chemical justification", "kind": "learn", "text": "A salt dissolves into ions, and some react with water. Ions derived from a strong acid or strong base often have negligible hydrolysis in the school model; they do not all produce neutral solutions by definition. Acetate, the conjugate base of a weak acid, can accept H⁺ from water and form OH⁻. Ammonium, the conjugate acid of a weak base, can donate H⁺ to water and form H₃O⁺.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Write the hydrolysis equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "For CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻, the net basic tendency follows formation of hydroxide. For NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺, the net acidic tendency follows hydronium formation. Keep weak equilibria as reversible equations and check atoms and charge. The sodium or chloride counterion does not need to be forced into the net equation when it is a spectator under these assumptions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Compare competing tendencies where necessary", "kind": "learn", "text": "A salt containing both a weak-acid conjugate base and a weak-base conjugate acid cannot be classified solely by one ion. Relative acid/base tendencies or supplied constants determine the net effect. Amphiprotic ions similarly can have competing proton donation and acceptance. State what data would resolve an uncertain comparison, rather than assuming equal ion counts imply neutrality. Distinguish salt hydrolysis from dissociation: releasing ions and reacting with water are separate processes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A basic salt", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A basic salt", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A basic salt", "prompt": "Explain why aqueous sodium ethanoate is basic, using a net ionic equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The salt provides CH₃COO⁻ in solution. [1]", "CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻. [1]", "Ethanoate accepts a proton from water. [1]", "OH⁻ formation increases basicity; Na⁺ is a spectator in this model. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-salts-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why sodium acetate solution is basic, using a hydrolysis equation and identifying the spectator ion. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻.", "[2] Acetate accepts a proton from water and generates OH⁻, producing a basic tendency.", "[2] Na⁺ is a spectator in this school hydrolysis model; salt dissociation alone is not the hydrolysis reaction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-salts-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the acidic tendency of NH₄Cl(aq), then state why a salt with both weak-acid and weak-base conjugates needs further comparison. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ produces hydronium; chloride has negligible hydrolysis here.", "[2] Ammonium is the conjugate acid of a weak base and can donate a proton.", "[2] When both ions hydrolyse, compare their relative tendencies/constants before deciding net pH."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can ammonium chloride solution be acidic?", "o": ["NH₄⁺ can donate a proton to water.", "Chloride always produces a strong acid by itself.", "All salts have pH zero.", "Dissociation eliminates every water molecule."], "a": 0, "why": "The ammonium hydrolysis equilibrium forms hydronium."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-acids-buffers-buffers", "title": "Buffers: reactions, equilibrium and capacity", "scope": "A buffer contains a reacting conjugate pair · Explain the chemical response without claiming zero change · Capacity is finite and direction-dependent", "parent": "che12-acids-buffers", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Buffers: reactions, equilibrium and capacity</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A buffer contains a reacting conjugate pair</li><li>Explain the chemical response without claiming zero change</li><li>Capacity is finite and direction-dependent</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "What makes a buffer", "kind": "learn", "text": "A buffer typically contains a weak acid and a substantial amount of its conjugate base, or a weak base and its conjugate acid. It resists pH change when small amounts of strong acid or base are added. An ethanoic acid/ethanoate mixture supplies both HA and A⁻. Merely mixing two arbitrary acids is not sufficient. A buffer does not hold pH perfectly constant or work indefinitely. Its components react with the added species, reducing the resulting change in free hydrogen-ion concentration. The relevant SCSA treatment is qualitative mechanism and capacity, so the explanations emphasise equations rather than requiring an unstated quantitative buffer formula.", "diagram": "buffer"}, {"title": "Buffer response to added acid", "kind": "learn", "text": "For HA/A⁻, added H⁺ is consumed mainly by A⁻: A⁻ + H⁺ → HA. In the weak-acid equilibrium HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, increasing H⁺ causes net reverse reaction. The base component decreases and the acid component increases, so the pH changes somewhat rather than remaining exactly fixed. A good explanation names which component reacts, gives the equation and connects consumption of added H⁺ to a smaller pH change. If little A⁻ remains, the buffer cannot absorb much additional acid. Compare with plain water, which lacks a substantial reservoir of the matching conjugate component.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Buffer response to added base and capacity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Added OH⁻ is consumed by the weak acid: HA + OH⁻ → A⁻ + H₂O. The acid component decreases and the base component increases. Buffer capacity depends on the amounts of both components available, not just the starting pH. A more concentrated conjugate mixture can usually absorb more added acid/base before a large pH shift. Dilution reduces amounts per unit volume and practical capacity even when the ratio remains similar. Once a component is substantially exhausted, added strong reagent controls pH more directly. Match a qualitative capacity comparison to actual amounts and additions specified in the question.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "An acid–base explanation in an unfamiliar context", "kind": "learn", "text": "Identify the species, compare before/after formulae to locate the proton, then assign donor and acceptor. Identify conjugate pairs and any hydrolysis reaction. If a numerical pH is requested, determine whether complete ionisation, equilibrium or excess after stoichiometric reaction is applicable. For a buffer question, name the reservoir species that consumes the added reagent and explain finite capacity. Use states and balanced charges. A persuasive answer applies the model to the actual formulas provided, rather than merely quoting the definition of an acid. Check that every proposed conjugate differs by exactly one H⁺.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Buffer equilibrium rate explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "After OH⁻ reacts with HA, HA concentration decreases and A⁻ concentration increases. For HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, forward ionisation tendency initially falls relative to the reverse tendency, and the system readjusts. The overall buffer consumes most of the small added base while free [H⁺] changes less than in an unbuffered solution. For added acid, increased H⁺ increases reverse encounter tendency and A⁻ consumes it. These collision and species explanations supply the mechanism that a bare “the system opposes change” sentence omits. The final pH is slightly changed, not identical.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A buffer contains a reacting conjugate pair", "kind": "learn", "text": "A typical acidic buffer contains a weak acid HA and appreciable conjugate base A⁻. Added H⁺ is consumed mainly by A⁻ to form HA; added OH⁻ is consumed mainly by HA to form A⁻ and water. Both components must be available in useful amounts. A weak acid alone is not automatically an effective buffer against substantial additions in both directions, and a mixture of strong acid and strong base is not necessarily a buffer.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explain the chemical response without claiming zero change", "kind": "learn", "text": "The added acid/base is largely converted into another buffer component, limiting the change in free hydrogen-ion concentration. Equilibrium readjusts, but pH changes somewhat: buffers resist change rather than freezing pH exactly. Show the relevant net equation for the particular addition. Dilution reduces amounts per volume and can reduce practical capacity even when the conjugate ratio remains similar in an appropriate approximation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Capacity is finite and direction-dependent", "kind": "learn", "text": "Buffer capacity depends on component amounts and their proportions. If an addition consumes almost all A⁻ or HA, the solution can lose effective resistance in that direction. Calculate the neutralisation amounts before considering any equilibrium approximation. This topic requires qualitative buffer explanation under the course; an advanced formula is not a substitute for showing which species consumes the added acid or base and why the capacity can be exceeded.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-buffer"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Two buffer additions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Salt and buffer reasoning", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Two buffer additions", "prompt": "An ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer receives a small amount of HCl in one trial and NaOH in another. Give equations and explain its response in each.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["With acid: CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃COOH. [1]", "The conjugate base consumes most added H⁺. [1]", "The pH fall is reduced, not eliminated. [1]", "With base: CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O. [1]", "The weak acid consumes added OH⁻. [1]", "Capacity is finite because either component can be depleted. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Salt and buffer reasoning", "prompt": "Compare solutions of NaCl, NH₄Cl and sodium ethanoate at 25 °C. Then explain why sodium ethanoate alone is not equivalent in capacity to a prepared ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["NaCl is approximately neutral in the non-hydrolysing-ion model. [1]", "NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺ gives acidic ammonium chloride. [1]", "CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COOH + OH⁻ gives basic sodium ethanoate. [1]", "A prepared buffer has substantial weak acid and conjugate base amounts. [1]", "The weak acid consumes added OH⁻ and the conjugate base consumes added H⁺. [1]", "Ethanoate alone lacks the same substantial acid reservoir, so its response/capacity differs. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-acids-buffers-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-buffers-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Write the buffer reactions for added H⁺ and OH⁻ in an ethanoic-acid/ethanoate buffer and explain the pH response. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃COOH consumes added acid.", "[2] CH₃COOH + OH⁻ → CH₃COO⁻ + H₂O consumes added base.", "[2] Most addition is converted into buffer components, limiting but not eliminating the pH change while capacity remains."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-acids-buffers-buffers-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A buffer contains 0.020 mol HA and 0.010 mol A⁻. Assess addition of 0.015 mol H⁺ using the principal neutralisation model. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] H⁺ reacts with A⁻ in a 1:1 ratio.", "[2] Only 0.010 mol A⁻ is available, so 0.005 mol of the added H⁺ is beyond that component’s neutralising capacity.", "[2] Effective resistance to this acid addition is overwhelmed; do not claim unchanged pH or an intact useful conjugate ratio."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What does a buffer do within its capacity?", "o": ["Limits pH change by consuming much of added acid/base.", "Guarantees absolutely unchanged pH for any addition.", "Makes every solution neutral.", "Prevents all chemical reactions."], "a": 0, "why": "The conjugate components react with additions, and capacity is finite."}, {"q": "Conjugate acid–base pairs differ by", "o": ["One H⁺", "One electron only", "One O atom", "One neutron"], "a": 0, "why": "Proton transfer creates the conjugate species."}, {"q": "NH₄Cl is commonly acidic because", "o": ["Cl⁻ is a strong base", "NH₄⁺ donates a proton to water", "Na⁺ forms", "It is necessarily concentrated"], "a": 1, "why": "Ammonium hydrolysis forms hydronium."}, {"q": "A buffer contains", "o": ["Any two neutral salts", "A substantial conjugate acid/base pair", "Only pure water", "Only a strong acid"], "a": 1, "why": "Both components provide capacity for consuming added acid or base."}, {"q": "At 25 °C Kw is", "o": ["1.00 × 10⁻⁷", "1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴", "14 mol L⁻¹", "Always independent of temperature"], "a": 1, "why": "Kw is the product [H⁺][OH⁻] at the stated temperature."}, {"q": "Ethanoic acid is monoprotic because", "o": ["It has only one hydrogen atom", "Only one relevant acidic proton is donated", "It fully ionises", "It cannot form a conjugate base"], "a": 1, "why": "Total hydrogens and acid proticity are different counts."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-titrations-technique", "title": "Titration standards, apparatus and measurement", "scope": "Use apparatus for its measurement purpose · Standardisation creates a reliable concentration · Endpoint requires careful technique and interpretation", "parent": "che12-titrations", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Titration standards, apparatus and measurement</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use apparatus for its measurement purpose</li><li>Standardisation creates a reliable concentration</li><li>Endpoint requires careful technique and interpretation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Purpose of volumetric analysis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Volumetric analysis determines an unknown amount from the volume of a known-concentration reagent required by a balanced reaction. Acid–base titration relies on a suitable reaction and a measurable endpoint close to stoichiometric equivalence. The known solution is the titrant, usually delivered from a burette. The analyte aliquot commonly sits in a conical flask. At equivalence, reactant amounts match the equation’s mole ratio, not necessarily equal mole amounts. A 2:1 OH⁻:diprotic-acid reaction needs two hydroxide moles per acid mole. Establish the equation before interpreting titre data.", "diagram": "titration-apparatus"}, {"title": "Primary standards and standardisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A suitable primary standard has known purity and composition, is stable during storage and weighing, and reacts predictably. Accurate weighing and quantitative transfer establish its amount, while a volumetric flask defines solution volume. Some reagents, including NaOH solutions, can change through water uptake or reaction with CO₂, so their concentration is commonly determined by standardisation against a reliable standard. A label on a bottle is not a substitute for verified concentration. Distinguish making a solution of calculated nominal concentration from measuring its actual concentration. Report the standardisation data and precision that support subsequent unknown calculations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Burette readings and delivered volume", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the meniscus at eye level to reduce parallax, using the appropriate reference for the liquid. Delivered titre = final reading − initial reading; the initial value need not be zero. Remove air bubbles from the tip before recording the start. A funnel left in the burette can add unmeasured drops during a trial. Read and record all digits warranted by the instrument, even a final zero. A titre involves two readings, so both contribute uncertainty. Use repeated close titres according to the laboratory’s stated criterion, and justify treatment of outliers rather than silently selecting convenient values.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rinsing equipment with purpose", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rinse a burette with the titrant to avoid diluting it with residual water. Rinse a pipette with the analyte solution to avoid diluting the measured aliquot. A conical flask can be rinsed with distilled water because added water changes volume but not the analyte moles already transferred. Rinsing that flask with analyte and leaving residue adds unmeasured analyte, causing a larger titre. Equipment rules follow amount conservation. Explain the causal chain from contamination or dilution to moles and calculated concentration. Merely saying “rinse for accuracy” does not demonstrate understanding of which error is prevented.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Concordance and uncertainty", "kind": "learn", "text": "Close titres suggest repeatability but do not prove absence of bias. Exclude a rough trial from the final mean when it was intentionally used to locate the endpoint, and use the stated criterion for concordant analytical trials. A mean of 18.60, 18.65 and 18.62 mL is 18.623… mL, reported consistently with equipment precision, commonly 18.62 mL. If each burette reading has a specified maximum uncertainty ±0.05 mL, a conservative titre uncertainty is ±0.10 mL; other statistical treatments may be appropriate when specified. Larger titres can reduce relative volume uncertainty, provided the method remains suitable.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A SCSA concordance example", "kind": "learn", "text": "The 2025 SCSA examination report identifies titres differing by 0.10 mL or less as concordant. Values 21.10, 21.16 and 21.18 mL span 0.08 mL and satisfy that comparison. Their mean is 21.146… mL, normally reported as 21.15 mL for appropriate burette precision. A rough trial is handled separately when it was used to locate the endpoint. Concordance demonstrates consistency, while an uncalibrated instrument or wrong concentration can still bias every titre. Follow specific instructions if an assessment provides a different selection rule.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use apparatus for its measurement purpose", "kind": "learn", "text": "A volumetric pipette delivers a fixed measured aliquot, a burette delivers a measured variable titre and a volumetric flask defines a final prepared volume. Rinse the pipette with the solution it will deliver and the burette with titrant, after appropriate cleaning, so residual water does not dilute those solutions. Rinse the conical flask with distilled water: extra water there changes volume but not analyte amount. Read a suitable meniscus at eye level and record initial and final burette readings.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Standardisation creates a reliable concentration", "kind": "learn", "text": "A suitable primary standard has known purity, stability and a composition that supports accurate amount calculation. Prepare it quantitatively and use its known amount to determine another solution’s concentration where needed. Do not assume an arbitrary reagent bottle concentration is exact. The balanced equation supplies the mole ratio between standard and titrant. Standardisation and analysis are related titrations but answer different concentration questions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Endpoint requires careful technique and interpretation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Add titrant slowly near the endpoint while mixing, wash down flask walls and use a suitable indicator in a small amount. A rough titre locates the region; concordant accurate titres support a reliable average, following the tolerance specified by the task. The colour endpoint approximates the equivalence point but is not its definition. An air bubble leaving the burette tip can make the recorded delivered volume exceed the solution actually reaching the flask.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Rinsing and calculation bias", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Titration quality evaluation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Rinsing and calculation bias", "prompt": "The analyte pipette is rinsed only with water before transferring acid. Explain the bias in acid concentration calculated from a correctly standardised base titrant.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Residual water dilutes the acid aliquot. [1]", "The measured aliquot contains fewer acid moles than intended. [1]", "A smaller base titre is required. [1]", "The calculated original acid concentration is too low. Rinse the pipette with analyte. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Titration quality evaluation", "prompt": "A student uses titres 19.20, 19.22 and 19.19 mL but leaves a persistent air bubble in the burette tip at the first reading of every trial. Evaluate precision, bias and the inferred analyte concentration.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["The close titres indicate repeatability/precision. [1]", "They do not prove accuracy. [1]", "If part of the recorded delivery fills the tip, less titrant reaches the flask than the reading difference indicates. [1]", "The recorded titre is too high for the actual delivered amount. [1]", "For acid calculated from known base, inferred acid concentration is too high. [1]", "Fill and clear the tip before initial reading and verify the apparatus, rather than relying on repeats alone. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-titrations-technique-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain appropriate rinsing for a pipette, burette and conical flask in an acid–base titration. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Rinse the pipette with its analyte and the burette with titrant after cleaning to avoid residual-water dilution.", "[2] Rinse the conical flask with distilled water; do not add unmeasured analyte through rinsing.", "[2] Water in the flask does not change analyte moles, so stoichiometric titrant amount is unchanged under a suitable endpoint method."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-titrations-technique-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A bubble initially in the burette tip disappears during titration. Explain the likely effect on recorded titre and a concentration calculated from known analyte amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Some measured burette-volume decrease fills the tip rather than entering the flask.", "[2] Recorded titre is too large relative to actual titrant delivered.", "[2] If titrant concentration is calculated as known reacting moles divided by recorded titre, it is underestimated; explicitly state this calculation context."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can distilled water be added to a titration flask without changing equivalence titre?", "o": ["It does not change the analyte amount.", "It always cancels every error.", "It increases analyte moles by the same factor.", "It makes all indicators equivalent."], "a": 0, "why": "Stoichiometric equivalence depends on reacting amounts, not initial flask concentration alone."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-titrations-curves", "title": "Titration curves, indicators and equivalence", "scope": "Equivalence and endpoint are distinct · Explain each region using species · Match the indicator to the actual jump", "parent": "che12-titrations", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Titration curves, indicators and equivalence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Equivalence and endpoint are distinct</li><li>Explain each region using species</li><li>Match the indicator to the actual jump</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Endpoint versus equivalence point", "kind": "learn", "text": "Equivalence point is the theoretical stoichiometric point. Endpoint is the observed signal, such as a persistent indicator colour change. A suitable indicator changes over the steep pH region near equivalence, making endpoint a useful approximation. The two points are not conceptually identical. Add titrant slowly near the endpoint, swirl and rinse solution from flask walls with distilled water. Excess titrant gives a high recorded titre relative to the desired endpoint. A colour judged too early gives a low titre. A pH meter can trace the curve but needs calibration and sensible sampling near the steep region.", "diagram": "titration-curve"}, {"title": "Indicators as equilibrium systems", "kind": "learn", "text": "An acid-form indicator may be represented HIn ⇌ H⁺ + In⁻, where HIn and In⁻ have different colours. Added acid favours HIn; added base removes H⁺ and favours In⁻. Colour transition occurs over a range rather than at one exact universal pH. Common approximate ranges are methyl orange 3.1–4.4, bromothymol blue 6.0–7.6 and phenolphthalein 8.2–10.0. Use supplied ranges when choosing an indicator. A good selection refers to the curve’s steep region and expected equivalence pH, not simply “this indicator is used for acids.” Excess indicator can itself influence a very small analyte amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong acid–strong base curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a strong monoprotic acid titrated with strong hydroxide at 25 °C, the initial pH is low, then rises as H⁺ is consumed. Near equivalence the slope is steep and equivalence pH is approximately 7 if the resulting salt does not hydrolyse appreciably. Beyond equivalence, excess OH⁻ controls pH. The volume at equivalence follows the balanced ratio and concentrations. The curve’s position is not determined by acid strength alone because amount and concentration matter. Use the actual direction of titration: adding acid to base gives a descending curve with corresponding regions.", "diagram": "strong-titration"}, {"title": "Weak acid–strong base curve", "kind": "learn", "text": "A weak acid begins at a higher pH than an equally concentrated strong acid. As base is added, weak acid and conjugate base coexist in a buffer region. At equivalence, the conjugate-base salt hydrolyses to form OH⁻, so pH is above 7 at 25 °C. Beyond equivalence excess strong base dominates. Phenolphthalein often fits the steep region. Half-equivalence has equal amounts of weak acid and its conjugate base and provides useful equilibrium interpretation, though numerical buffer formulas beyond syllabus requirements are optional extension. Do not conclude that the weak acid requires fewer hydroxide moles for complete neutralisation merely because it ionises partly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Strong acid–weak base and weak–weak cases", "kind": "learn", "text": "When a strong acid titrates a weak base, the conjugate-acid salt formed at equivalence can make pH below 7. Indicator selection should match the relevant steep region. A weak acid–weak base titration may lack a sufficiently sharp transition for reliable visual endpoint detection. Equivalence pH depends on relative acid/base equilibrium tendencies and concentration, not on a universal pH-7 rule. State whether acid or base is being added before describing the curve’s direction. A named salt’s hydrolysis equation explains the equivalence pH more clearly than labelling the reagents weak or strong alone.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Equivalence and endpoint are distinct", "kind": "learn", "text": "Equivalence occurs when reacting amounts satisfy the balanced equation ratio. The endpoint is an observed signal such as an indicator colour change. Choose an indicator whose transition falls within the sufficiently steep part of the relevant curve near equivalence. Equivalence pH is not always seven: conjugate species formed by a weak-acid/strong-base or strong-acid/weak-base titration can hydrolyse. Identify the titration system before selecting the indicator.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Explain each region using species", "kind": "learn", "text": "Before equivalence, the initial species and any conjugate pair determine pH; a weak-acid titration can include a buffer region. At equivalence, the product species and water chemistry matter. After equivalence, excess strong titrant commonly dominates the simple calculation. A curve’s shape depends on the species and conditions, not merely the acid being on the left of a sketch. Label titrant volume and pH and distinguish the pH jump from the amount at equivalence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Match the indicator to the actual jump", "kind": "learn", "text": "An indicator is itself a weak acid/base system with differently coloured forms. Its transition range spans a pH interval, not one exact universal point. A range far outside the equivalence jump causes a systematic mismatch between endpoint and equivalence. More indicator is not automatically more accurate; it can add reacting species and obscure the transition. Explain choice using the supplied curve or ranges rather than memorising a colour without context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Interactive strong-acid titration", "kind": "interactive", "model": "titration"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Indicator choice", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Indicator choice", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Indicator choice", "prompt": "A weak monoprotic acid is titrated with NaOH. Equivalence lies at pH 8.8, with a steep region from 7.6 to 10.2. Choose between methyl orange 3.1–4.4 and phenolphthalein 8.2–10.0 and justify.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Choose phenolphthalein. [1]", "Its transition lies within the steep region near equivalence. [1]", "Methyl orange changes too early, outside that region. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-titrations-curves-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A weak acid is titrated with strong base. Explain why equivalence can be basic and how an indicator should be selected. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] At equivalence the weak acid has largely become its conjugate base.", "[2] That base can react with water to form OH⁻, giving a basic equivalence solution.", "[2] Choose a transition range within the steep equivalence region of the supplied curve, not automatically around pH 7."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-titrations-curves-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "An indicator changes colour well before equivalence on a given curve. Evaluate the measured titre and identify the information required for a better choice. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The colour endpoint occurs at too little titrant, underestimating the equivalence titre.", "[2] This can systematically bias concentration calculated from the titre, with direction depending on what is being calculated.", "[2] Use the actual titration curve and indicator transition ranges to place the change within the equivalence jump."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Is equivalence pH always 7?", "o": ["No: product-species hydrolysis can make it acidic or basic.", "Yes, regardless of acid/base strength.", "Only the indicator creates equivalence.", "Equivalence means equal starting volumes."], "a": 0, "why": "Equivalence is a stoichiometric condition, and the resulting species determine pH."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-titrations-direct", "title": "Direct titration, dilution and composition", "scope": "Carry the aliquot through the calculation · Track preparation and analysis separately · Report the requested composition with a defensible basis", "parent": "che12-titrations", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Direct titration, dilution and composition</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Carry the aliquot through the calculation</li><li>Track preparation and analysis separately</li><li>Report the requested composition with a defensible basis</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "A direct titration calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Calculate titrant moles from concentration × titre in litres. Use the balanced coefficient ratio to find analyte moles in the aliquot. Divide by aliquot volume for its concentration. If that solution was diluted from an original sample, apply the dilution factor afterward. Show chemical labels on each mole quantity. A compact expression can obscure an incorrect ratio, so write at least the equation and ratio step in an exam. Check whether the question asks concentration of the diluted solution, the original sample or mass of a component in a larger batch.", "diagram": "titration-path"}, {"title": "Purity and percentage composition", "kind": "learn", "text": "To determine sample purity, use titration moles to find the reacting compound amount in the entire prepared solution, then convert to mass. Divide the mass of the target pure compound by the original impure sample mass and multiply by 100. Scale aliquot moles by flask volume/aliquot volume before calculating total mass. A nominally pure result above 100% suggests error, water/impurity assumptions or an incorrect reaction interpretation. Not every impurity is inert: an additional acid- or base-reactive impurity can bias the result. The question must specify or justify which components react under the conditions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Exam timing and written evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Before calculation, annotate the unknown, standard concentration, aliquot, titre and dilution factor. Write the reaction and coefficient ratio, convert units and keep intermediate digits. For explanatory questions, refer to specific apparatus or curve regions and state consequences in the calculated result. For a multi-part problem, use an earlier numerical result consistently and make assumptions explicit. Suggested marking guidance here rewards chemically correct equivalent methods and consequential calculation reasoning; it is original practice guidance rather than an official SCSA marking key. Review why an incorrect answer happened, not just the final number.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Showing all working and drawing evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a structure-drawing task, use paper or the scratch space and show the required atoms, bonds and lone pairs explicitly. Condensed text is not an adequate substitute when a displayed formula is requested. For titration calculations, show numeric mole amounts, ratios, aliquot scaling and units rather than arrows referring to an earlier answer. Marking guidance rewards the intended chemical steps but does not mean a memorised list alone will earn every extended-response mark. Write connected explanations using the actual experiment, including why the salt affects equivalence pH.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Carry the aliquot through the calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use titrant concentration and titre volume to calculate titrant amount, apply the balanced coefficient ratio, then divide the analyte amount by aliquot volume if concentration is requested. The aliquot is the portion actually titrated. If it came from a diluted stock, reverse that preparation using its volume factor. Do not use the entire prepared flask volume as the aliquot denominator unless that entire volume was analysed.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Track preparation and analysis separately", "kind": "learn", "text": "A sample may be weighed, dissolved to a volumetric flask, aliquoted and titrated. The aliquot result scales to the full prepared solution before calculating sample composition. Draw a short amount map: original sample, prepared volume, measured aliquot and reacting amount. This prevents applying a dilution factor twice or ignoring it. State any assumptions about complete dissolution, transfer and reaction specificity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Report the requested composition with a defensible basis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Mass percentage uses identified analyte mass divided by original sample mass times 100. Concentration in a diluted flask is a different result and cannot replace that percentage. Include units and show each substitution rather than writing a final number alone. An endpoint overshoot changes recorded titre; explain error direction through the exact calculation, since the concentration of an unknown analyte and an unknown titrant respond differently.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Direct acid concentration", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Direct acid concentration", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Diprotic acid with dilution", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Diprotic acid with dilution", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Carbonate purity", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Carbonate purity", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Direct acid concentration", "prompt": "25.00 mL HCl requires 18.60 mL of 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Determine HCl concentration.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O gives 1:1. [1]", "n(NaOH) = 0.1000 × 0.01860 = 0.001860 mol. [1]", "n(HCl) = 0.001860 mol. [1]", "c(HCl) = 0.001860/0.02500 = 0.07440 mol L⁻¹. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Diprotic acid with dilution", "prompt": "10.00 mL original H₂SO₄ is diluted to 250.0 mL. A 25.00 mL aliquot requires 20.00 mL 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH for full neutralisation. Find original acid concentration.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. [1]", "n(NaOH) = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "n(H₂SO₄) in aliquot = 0.001000 mol. [1]", "Diluted acid c = 0.001000/0.02500 = 0.04000 mol L⁻¹. [1]", "Dilution factor = 250.0/10.00 = 25.00. [1]", "Original c = 1.000 mol L⁻¹. Full stoichiometric neutralisation does not require assuming both acid ionisations are complete initially. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Carbonate purity", "prompt": "1.500 g impure Na₂CO₃ is dissolved to 250.0 mL. A 25.00 mL aliquot requires 20.00 mL 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ HCl. Assuming inert impurities, find purity using M = 106.0 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O. [1]", "n(HCl) = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "n(Na₂CO₃) in aliquot = 0.001000 mol. [1]", "Total amount = 0.001000 × 10.00 = 0.01000 mol. [1]", "Pure mass = 0.01000 × 106.0 = 1.060 g. [1]", "Purity = 1.060/1.500 × 100 = 70.67%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-titrations-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-titrations-direct-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "25.0 mL of monoprotic acid requires 20.0 mL of 0.100 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Calculate acid concentration under complete 1:1 neutralisation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(NaOH) = 0.100 × 0.0200 = 0.00200 mol.", "[2] 1:1 stoichiometry gives n(acid) = 0.00200 mol.", "[2] c(acid) = 0.00200/0.0250 = 0.0800 mol L⁻¹."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-titrations-direct-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A 10.0 mL stock sample is diluted to 100.0 mL. The diluted monoprotic acid has concentration 0.0800 mol L⁻¹ from titration. Find stock concentration and explain the dilution relation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Solute amount is conserved during dilution without reaction.", "[2] cstock × 10.0 = 0.0800 × 100.0 in matched volume units.", "[2] cstock = 0.800 mol L⁻¹; distinguish original stock from the titrated dilution."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which volume divides analyte moles when finding concentration of a titrated aliquot?", "o": ["The analyte aliquot volume.", "The burette’s full capacity.", "The original flask volume regardless of aliquot size.", "The titre plus every rinse volume."], "a": 0, "why": "The moles obtained by titration belong to the analysed aliquot."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-titrations-back", "title": "Back titration and direction of calculation errors", "scope": "Back titration measures what remained · Follow every ratio and aliquot · Determine error direction through subtraction", "parent": "che12-titrations", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Back titration and direction of calculation errors</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Back titration measures what remained</li><li>Follow every ratio and aliquot</li><li>Determine error direction through subtraction</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Back titration as a calculation strategy", "kind": "learn", "text": "When direct titration is unsuitable, a known excess reagent can react with a sample and the remaining excess can be titrated. Target consumption = initially added reagent moles − leftover reagent moles. Then apply the target reaction’s ratio. This is a useful multi-step exam strategy where the data and reactions are supplied. Do not equate the second titre directly with target amount: it measures leftover reagent. State assumptions about completion, selectivity and loss of reagent. Separate the two balanced equations, label intermediate amounts and check that consumed amount is positive and smaller than initial amount.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Error direction: trace the amount pathway", "kind": "learn", "text": "If the endpoint is overshot while calculating an acid from known base, the titre is too large, calculated base moles too high and inferred acid concentration too high. If the burette tip contains air initially and fills during delivery, part of the recorded volume fills the tip rather than entering the flask, again exaggerating delivered volume. If an analyte pipette contains water, the aliquot is diluted, requiring less titrant and underestimating the original analyte concentration. Error directions depend on which quantity is unknown and the analysis pathway. Follow the actual calculation rather than memorising a disconnected table.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Back titration measures what remained", "kind": "learn", "text": "Add a known excess of reagent to react with the sample, then titrate the unreacted excess. Subtract remaining reagent amount from the initial amount to find what the sample consumed. Apply the sample-reagent coefficient ratio only after this subtraction. Back titration can suit slow reactions, insoluble samples or situations without a convenient direct endpoint, but it still requires known reactions and quantitative handling.", "diagram": "focused-back"}, {"title": "Follow every ratio and aliquot", "kind": "learn", "text": "The back titration may use a different stoichiometric ratio from the sample reaction. Write both equations and keep their amounts separate. If only an aliquot of the remaining reagent is analysed, scale its result to the total mixture before subtracting. The initial added amount and measured remaining amount must refer to the same full system. A negative consumed amount indicates an error in units, ratios, aliquot scaling or assumptions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Determine error direction through subtraction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Overestimating remaining reagent causes the calculated consumed amount to be too small. For a fixed sample stoichiometry, that generally underestimates sample analyte amount. This is the opposite of some direct titration overshoot contexts. Explain the subtraction explicitly rather than memorising that every high titre gives high concentration. Any incomplete sample reaction also leaves excess reagent that can make analyte content appear too low.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Back-titration amount", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Back-titration amount", "prompt": "A carbonate sample reacts with 0.01000 mol HCl. Leftover acid requires 30.00 mL of 0.1000 mol L⁻¹ NaOH. Find carbonate amount assuming CO₃²⁻ consumes two H⁺.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Leftover acid = n(NaOH) = 0.003000 mol. [1]", "Acid consumed = 0.01000 − 0.003000 = 0.00700 mol. [1]", "Carbonate amount is half the consumed acid amount. [1]", "n(CO₃²⁻) = 0.00350 mol. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-titrations-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-titrations-back-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A carbonate sample receives 0.0100 mol HCl. Back titration shows 0.00400 mol HCl remains. Use CO₃²⁻ + 2H⁺ → CO₂ + H₂O to calculate carbonate amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] HCl consumed = 0.0100 − 0.00400 = 0.00600 mol.", "[2] Two H⁺ react per carbonate, so n(carbonate) = 0.00600/2.", "[2] n(carbonate) = 0.00300 mol; the remaining acid is not the amount consumed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-titrations-back-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain how an overestimated back titre of residual acid affects carbonate amount calculated by initial acid minus remaining acid. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] A high back titre overestimates residual acid under the stated calibration.", "[2] Subtracting an excessive remaining amount underestimates acid consumed by the sample.", "[2] Applying the 2:1 acid/carbonate ratio then underestimates carbonate amount."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is subtracted in a back titration?", "o": ["Remaining excess reagent from initially added reagent.", "Initial amount from remaining amount.", "Only the two pH values.", "Sample mass from titre volume without conversion."], "a": 0, "why": "The difference gives reagent consumed by the sample."}, {"q": "Equivalence means", "o": ["Always equal acid/base concentrations", "Stoichiometric amounts match the balanced equation", "pH must always be 7", "Indicator is necessarily exactly colourless"], "a": 1, "why": "Coefficient ratios determine equivalent amounts."}, {"q": "A conical flask may contain extra distilled water because", "o": ["It adds analyte moles", "It changes volume without changing analyte moles", "It increases acid strength", "It standardises the burette"], "a": 1, "why": "The reacting amount is unchanged by pure water."}, {"q": "A weak acid–strong base equivalence solution is commonly", "o": ["Basic from conjugate-base hydrolysis", "Always neutral", "A pure strong acid", "Unable to contain ions"], "a": 0, "why": "The weak-acid conjugate base reacts with water to form OH⁻."}, {"q": "A standard solution has", "o": ["Verified known concentration", "Any printed bottle label only", "No ions", "A mandatory pH of 7"], "a": 0, "why": "Its concentration must be established reliably."}, {"q": "A burette titre equals", "o": ["Initial minus final", "Final minus initial", "Final reading alone always", "Aliquot volume times pH"], "a": 1, "why": "Delivered volume is the difference of the two readings."}], "subtitle": "12 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-redox-numbers", "title": "Redox roles and oxidation-number evidence", "scope": "Oxidation numbers are an accounting model · Oxidation and reduction occur together · Distinguish disproportionation and ordinary transfer", "parent": "che12-redox", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Redox roles and oxidation-number evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Oxidation numbers are an accounting model</li><li>Oxidation and reduction occur together</li><li>Distinguish disproportionation and ordinary transfer</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Oxidation and reduction as electron transfer", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain. They occur together because electrons transferred from one species are received by another. Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ is oxidation; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu is reduction. An oxidising agent causes another species to be oxidised and is itself reduced. A reducing agent causes reduction and is itself oxidised. The terms describe roles in a specified reaction. A metal can be a reducing agent even though its oxidised metal ion can participate differently in another system. Write electrons explicitly in half-equations and ensure they cancel in the overall equation.", "diagram": "electron-transfer"}, {"title": "Oxidation numbers: a bookkeeping system", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation numbers assign electrons by a formal rule to track oxidation changes. An uncombined element has oxidation number 0. A monatomic ion has its ionic charge. Hydrogen is usually +1 and oxygen usually −2 in the course examples, with known exceptions such as peroxides. The sum equals the species charge. In MnO₄⁻, Mn + 4(−2) = −1, so Mn is +7. Oxidation number is not necessarily a measured charge on an atom in a covalent molecule. It is useful for comparing before and after, especially where direct electron transfer is hidden in a balanced equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recognising oxidation-number changes", "kind": "learn", "text": "An oxidation-number increase indicates oxidation; a decrease indicates reduction. In Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺, iron rises from +2 to +3 and loses one electron. In MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺, manganese falls from +7 to +2 and gains five electrons per manganese. Track the specific atom and account for how many of those atoms appear. A reaction can transfer electrons even if oxygen is not involved. Conversely, acid–base proton transfer is not automatically redox. Identify changes rather than relying on the presence of an acid, gas or colour change as proof.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Selecting oxidising and reducing agents", "kind": "learn", "text": "For two potential couples, identify the proposed species actually present. A reduction potential belongs to a pair, not to an isolated element name without oxidation state. Cu²⁺ is the oxidising-agent candidate in Cu²⁺/Cu, while Cu metal is the reducing-agent candidate. A more positive reduction is favoured at the cathode when paired appropriately. Comparing only metal names can reverse agent identification. Write the reduction and the reversed oxidation, then evaluate the proposed net tendency. A reagent cannot take a role merely because its ion appears somewhere in the table if that species is not part of the mixture.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combustion as redox", "kind": "learn", "text": "In complete combustion of a hydrocarbon, carbon is generally oxidised toward +4 in CO₂ while oxygen is reduced from 0 in O₂ to −2 in the products. In incomplete combustion, carbon may end at +2 in CO or 0 in soot, so the electron changes differ. Combustion remains redox even when no simple ionic electron transfer is visible. Oxidation-number bookkeeping connects covalent reactions to the electron-transfer model. Use average carbon oxidation number cautiously when different carbon atoms in an organic molecule have different environments. The balanced overall equation and individual structural context govern the analysis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Oxidation numbers are an accounting model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation numbers assign electrons by rules and help identify changes; they are not always actual ionic charges within covalent molecules. The sum equals the overall species charge. Elements in their standard uncombined form have oxidation number zero. Apply the familiar oxygen and hydrogen rules with appropriate exceptions supplied in context, and solve the remaining value algebraically rather than assuming an atom keeps one number in every compound.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Oxidation and reduction occur together", "kind": "learn", "text": "Oxidation increases oxidation number and corresponds to electron loss; reduction decreases it and corresponds to electron gain. The reducing agent causes another species to be reduced and is itself oxidised. The oxidising agent is itself reduced. Name the whole reacting species where required, not only an element symbol stripped from its compound. Electron conservation connects the two changes in the balanced reaction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish disproportionation and ordinary transfer", "kind": "learn", "text": "A species can be both oxidised and reduced in a disproportionation reaction, producing products where the same element has higher and lower oxidation numbers. Acid–base reactions transfer protons and need not be redox. Check oxidation-number changes rather than classifying every reaction involving ions as electron transfer. A correct answer supports agent labels with the before-and-after values or half-equations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Oxidation-number calculation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Oxidation-number calculation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Oxidation-number calculation", "prompt": "Determine chromium oxidation number in Cr₂O₇²⁻ and manganese oxidation number in MnO₄⁻.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Let Cr be x: 2x + 7(−2) = −2. [1]", "x = +6. [1]", "Let Mn be y: y + 4(−2) = −1. [1]", "y = +7. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-redox-numbers-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "For Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s), identify oxidation, reduction and both agents. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Zn changes from 0 to +2 and is oxidised.", "[2] Cu changes from +2 to 0 and is reduced.", "[2] Zn is the reducing agent and Cu²⁺ the oxidising agent; label the reacting species."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-redox-numbers-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Find Mn oxidation number in MnO₄⁻ using O = −2, then compare with Mn²⁺ to identify the change. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] x + 4(−2) = −1.", "[2] x = +7 in permanganate and +2 in Mn²⁺.", "[2] The decrease of five is reduction, corresponding to five-electron gain per manganese in the appropriate half-equation."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What happens to the oxidising agent?", "o": ["It is reduced.", "It must itself lose electrons.", "Its oxidation number always increases.", "It cannot appear as an ion."], "a": 0, "why": "It accepts electrons while causing another species to be oxidised."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-redox-equations", "title": "Acidic half-equations and balanced redox reactions", "scope": "Balance atoms before cancelling electrons · Combine with an equal electron count · Use the stated medium and state symbols", "parent": "che12-redox", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Acidic half-equations and balanced redox reactions</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Balance atoms before cancelling electrons</li><li>Combine with an equal electron count</li><li>Use the stated medium and state symbols</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Simple metal and halogen half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "A metal oxidation equation is M → Mᶻ⁺ + ze⁻. Halogen reduction is X₂ + 2e⁻ → 2X⁻. When magnesium reduces copper(II), Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ and Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu combine directly. Halogens can oxidise less strongly oxidising halide partners according to their reduction tendencies: Cl₂ can oxidise Br⁻ to Br₂. Describe the species that loses electrons and the species that gains them, then identify the agents. A displacement equation must preserve atoms and charge, not simply exchange element names in a formula.", "diagram": "displacement"}, {"title": "Balancing acidic half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "The SCSA Year 12 redox treatment uses acidic conditions. Balance non-H/O atoms first. Add H₂O to balance oxygen, H⁺ to balance hydrogen, then electrons to balance total charge. Finally check every atom and charge. For MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺, add 4H₂O on the right and 8H⁺ on the left. The left charge is +7 and right +2, so add five electrons to the left: MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. Electron placement should agree with the reduction in oxidation number. Do not introduce OH⁻ in an acidic-only method.", "diagram": "redox-balance"}, {"title": "Dichromate reduction and characteristic colours", "kind": "learn", "text": "For dichromate in acidic solution: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. Each Cr falls from +6 to +3, giving six electrons for two chromium atoms. The SCSA booklet lists dichromate orange and Cr³⁺ deep green. Permanganate is purple and Mn²⁺ pale pink, often appearing nearly colourless when dilute. Colour evidence supports a reaction but does not replace a balanced equation. MnO₄⁻ can produce other manganese species under different conditions, so acidic conditions are essential to the stated equation and observation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combining half-equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiply half-equations so electrons gained equal electrons lost, add and cancel identical species on opposite sides. MnO₄⁻ reduction gains five electrons while Fe²⁺ oxidation loses one. Multiply iron oxidation by five, producing MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. Check total charge: left −1 + 8 + 10 = +17, right +2 + 15 = +17. Electrons do not remain in the overall equation. Keep the smallest whole-number coefficients. Do not multiply electrode potential values when multiplying a half-equation; potential is not an extensive amount of energy.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Checks that prevent equation errors", "kind": "learn", "text": "Check element counts, charge counts, electron cancellation and expected electron-change direction. In acidic equations, H₂O and H⁺ are balancing species, not arbitrary decorations. An oxidising agent gains electrons; a reducing agent loses them. Potentials use the reduction-table convention and are not scaled by coefficient multipliers. Match observation to the correct product and conditions. In an extended answer, distinguish thermodynamic tendency from observed reaction rate and identify uncertainty in a standard-state prediction. These checks catch common errors even when a calculator produces a plausible number.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Balance atoms before cancelling electrons", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an acidic half-equation, balance non-hydrogen/non-oxygen atoms, add H₂O to balance oxygen, H⁺ to balance hydrogen and electrons to balance charge. The method represents the specified acidic medium. Check the final half-equation rather than assuming the steps guarantee correctness. Electrons appear on the reactant side for reduction and product side for oxidation; charge conservation is as necessary as atom conservation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combine with an equal electron count", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiply entire half-equations so electrons lost equal electrons gained. Add them, cancel electrons and any common species, then recheck all atoms and net charge. Never multiply a standard reduction potential by the electron-balancing factor; voltage is not an extensive amount. Leave no electrons in the final overall ionic equation unless the task specifically asks for a half-equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the stated medium and state symbols", "kind": "learn", "text": "Permanganate products can depend on conditions; Mn²⁺ is appropriate to the standard acidic reduction used here, not automatically every medium. Acidic balancing does not justify adding arbitrary H⁺ to a neutral or basic reaction without interpretation. Give the relevant aqueous and solid states where specified and retain charges clearly. A visually balanced formula that has mismatched charge is still an incorrect chemical equation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-redox-check"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Acidic permanganate half-equation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Acidic permanganate half-equation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Dichromate with iron", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Acidic permanganate half-equation", "prompt": "Balance MnO₄⁻ → Mn²⁺ in acidic solution and explain the electron count.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Balance oxygen using 4H₂O on the right. [1]", "Balance hydrogen with 8H⁺ on the left. [1]", "Add 5e⁻ on the left to balance charge. [1]", "MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O. [1]", "Mn falls from +7 to +2, a gain of five electrons. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Dichromate with iron", "prompt": "Combine acidic dichromate reduction with Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ to obtain the net ionic equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Dichromate requires six electrons: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O. [1]", "Multiply iron oxidation by six. [1]", "Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O + 6Fe³⁺. [1]", "Atoms and total charge (+24 on each side) balance. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-redox-equations-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Balance acidic reduction of MnO₄⁻ to Mn²⁺ and demonstrate atom and charge checks. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O.", "[2] Mn:1, O:4 and H:8 are conserved.", "[2] Left charge −1 + 8 − 5 = +2 equals right +2; electrons are gained in reduction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-redox-equations-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Combine MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5e⁻ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O with Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻ in acidic solution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Multiply the iron oxidation half-equation by five.", "[2] MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺.", "[2] Electrons cancel; charge is +17 each side and atoms are balanced."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why are electrons balanced before half-equations are added?", "o": ["Electrons lost must equal electrons gained.", "Electrons are destroyed during reduction.", "Only oxygen counts need conservation.", "It allows ignoring charge on the final equation."], "a": 0, "why": "The overall reaction conserves electron transfer and charge."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-redox-potentials", "title": "Reduction potentials, displacement and observations", "scope": "Read tabulated potentials as reductions · Scaling the equation does not scale voltage · Describe evidence before naming the reaction", "parent": "che12-redox", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Reduction potentials, displacement and observations</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Read tabulated potentials as reductions</li><li>Scaling the equation does not scale voltage</li><li>Describe evidence before naming the reaction</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Standard reduction potentials", "kind": "learn", "text": "The data booklet lists reduction half-equations and standard electrode potentials E°. A more positive E° indicates a greater tendency for the written reduction under standard conditions, relative to the reference electrode. The species on the reactant side can therefore be a stronger oxidising agent. A strongly negative reduction potential corresponds to a product-side species that can be a strong reducing agent when oxidised. Conditions matter: standard potentials refer to specified states, concentrations, pressures and temperature, and real reaction behaviour can differ outside them. Use the current SCSA table rather than mixing signs from an oxidation table.", "diagram": "potential-scale"}, {"title": "E°cell and standard reaction tendency", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a proposed reaction, E°cell = E°reduction(cathode) − E°reduction(anode). Using Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn −0.76 V gives +1.10 V for zinc oxidation and copper-ion reduction. A positive result supports spontaneous tendency under the stated standard conditions. A negative result indicates the proposed direction is not favoured under those conditions and may require an external energy source. E° values do not multiply with stoichiometric coefficients. A favourable potential does not guarantee a fast visible reaction because kinetics, passivation and conditions can limit observation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Metal displacement and observations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) predicts zinc dissolving and a copper deposit, with blue copper-ion colour decreasing if the reaction proceeds. The observed deposit’s appearance can differ from a polished metal surface. A metal with a less favourable reduction pairing may not displace the ion under standard assumptions. Assess the actual concentrations and any protective oxide layers if results conflict with a simple prediction. An exam response should name changes in both species, identify agents and support tendency through potential comparison rather than merely saying one metal is “more reactive.”", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Halogen displacement and solvent colour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Cl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ is a redox displacement. Chlorine is reduced and acts as oxidising agent; bromide is oxidised and acts as reducing agent. Halogen colours depend on phase and solvent: the data booklet distinguishes aqueous and organic-solvent colours. Bromine is orange in aqueous solution, and iodine is brown in water but purple in many organic solvents as listed. A coloured organic layer is analytical evidence of halogen distribution, not a new elemental identity. State which layer and solvent you mean and refer to supplied data for exact observations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A full observation is different from an inference", "kind": "learn", "text": "For zinc in copper(II) solution, an observation can describe a grey solid changing, a reddish deposit appearing and blue solution colour becoming paler. Naming zinc, copper or copper ions explains identity inferred from the setup and chemistry, rather than directly describing appearance. Gas evolution is observed as bubbles or effervescence; its chemical identity requires inference or a test. In equation-and-observation questions, give the visible before/after change and separately the balanced chemistry. Use the SCSA booklet for the specified species colours and attend to excess reagent and physical state.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Checking a reduction-table prediction", "kind": "learn", "text": "Suppose couple A has E° = +0.50 V and B has −0.20 V. If the oxidised A species and reduced B species are present, A reduction with B oxidation gives +0.70 V. If only the reduced A form and oxidised B form are mixed, that proposed reverse direction gives −0.70 V. The table alone does not supply absent reactants. Identify actual species first, then decide directions. A positive value supports standard thermodynamic tendency but cannot guarantee rate, complete conversion or identical behaviour at different concentrations.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read tabulated potentials as reductions", "kind": "learn", "text": "The data book lists standard reduction half-equations and potentials. For a proposed cell, choose the reduction and reverse the other half-equation for oxidation, then calculate E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode using both tabulated reduction values. A positive standard cell potential supports thermodynamic favourability under standard conditions in the written direction. It does not guarantee a rapid visible reaction or identical behaviour under every concentration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Scaling the equation does not scale voltage", "kind": "learn", "text": "Multiplying coefficients to balance electrons multiplies amounts but leaves the half-cell potential unchanged. Reversing a half-equation changes the sign if expressing its oxidation potential; the subtraction convention avoids confusion by keeping table values as reductions. Record which convention is used and do not both reverse the sign and subtract it again. Match the exact species, charge and conditions to the table entry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Describe evidence before naming the reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "A displacement can show a metal deposit, fading solution colour or dissolving metal, depending on species and amount. These are observations; identifying copper or another product is an inference supported by the reaction model or tests. Surface coatings and kinetic barriers can hinder a favourable reaction. A well-supported answer combines potential calculation, correct agents and a species-specific observation with an appropriate limitation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A displacement potential", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A displacement potential", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Tendency and rate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A displacement potential", "prompt": "Using Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V and Zn²⁺/Zn −0.76 V, calculate E° for Zn reacting with Cu²⁺ and identify both agents.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Cu²⁺ is reduced and Zn is oxidised. [1]", "E° = +0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V. [1]", "Cu²⁺ is the oxidising agent. [1]", "Zn is the reducing agent; positive E° supports standard spontaneous tendency. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Tendency and rate", "prompt": "A proposed redox reaction has positive E°cell but little visible change. Explain why this does not automatically invalidate the potential calculation.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["Positive E° supports thermodynamic tendency under standard conditions. [1]", "A large activation barrier or passivating surface can make the reaction slow. [1]", "Actual concentrations and conditions may also differ from standard values. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-redox-potentials-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Using E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V, calculate standard potential for zinc reducing copper ions. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Copper ions are reduced at the cathode and zinc is oxidised at the anode.", "[2] E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V.", "[2] Positive E° supports the written direction under standard conditions; coefficient scaling does not multiply potential."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-redox-potentials-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why multiplying a half-equation by two does not double E°, then give a suitable observation for copper deposition from blue Cu²⁺ solution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Potential is an intensive voltage difference rather than an amount proportional to coefficients.", "[2] Electron balancing changes reaction amounts but not the tabulated half-cell potential.", "[2] A reddish-brown deposit may appear and blue colour can fade as Cu²⁺ is consumed; describe observations before identifying the deposit."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What happens to E° when a half-equation is multiplied to balance electrons?", "o": ["It remains unchanged.", "It is multiplied by the same factor.", "It becomes zero.", "It becomes a concentration unit."], "a": 0, "why": "Voltage is intensive, while stoichiometric amounts scale."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-redox-analysis", "title": "Redox stoichiometry and quantitative analysis", "scope": "Use the overall ionic mole ratio · Interpret endpoint and sample preparation · Track mass and percentage after the chemical calculation", "parent": "che12-redox", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Redox stoichiometry and quantitative analysis</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Use the overall ionic mole ratio</li><li>Interpret endpoint and sample preparation</li><li>Track mass and percentage after the chemical calculation</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Redox quantities and stoichiometry", "kind": "learn", "text": "The balanced redox equation gives mole ratios for quantitative analysis. One MnO₄⁻ in acidic solution oxidises five Fe²⁺. Use n = cV for a titrant, apply the ratio and then scale aliquots or find sample mass. Oxidation-number changes help derive the coefficients but do not replace the final balanced equation. Redox volumetric examples are original multi-step practice that uses supplied reaction information. Pay attention to reagent concentration units, sample dilution and whether all iron was prepared in the relevant oxidation state. Additional oxidisable substances can interfere and invalidate a simple one-component assumption.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the overall ionic mole ratio", "kind": "learn", "text": "A redox titration’s coefficient ratio comes from the balanced overall equation, not simply the number of species listed. Acidic permanganate consumes five Fe²⁺ per MnO₄⁻ in the relevant reaction. Find titrant amount from concentration and volume, calculate analyte amount by the ratio, then apply aliquot/dilution scaling. Keep the reaction conditions visible because different products can give different ratios.", "diagram": "focused-analysis"}, {"title": "Interpret endpoint and sample preparation", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a suitable acidic permanganate titration, the first persistent faint excess permanganate colour can signal the endpoint because the reagent is strongly coloured. Its meaning depends on complete reaction and appropriate medium. Air oxidation of a prepared Fe²⁺ sample can reduce the amount actually available to titrate. A concentration result then reflects the sample at analysis, not automatically the original unaltered material.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Track mass and percentage after the chemical calculation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Convert the calculated element or compound amount to mass with the requested molar mass. A sample’s purity uses original sample mass as denominator. Report which species is being quantified and whether all its relevant forms are measured. A titre overshoot can overestimate an unknown analyte amount in this direct-calibration context; explain through titrant moles and ratio rather than assigning the same error direction to every titration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: An iron analysis", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: An iron analysis", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Charge balance and observations", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "An iron analysis", "prompt": "20.00 mL of 0.02000 mol L⁻¹ MnO₄⁻ oxidises Fe²⁺ in acidic solution. Use a 1:5 ratio and find the Fe²⁺ amount and iron mass, M(Fe) = 55.85 g mol⁻¹.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.02000 × 0.02000 = 0.0004000 mol. [1]", "n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 0.0004000 = 0.002000 mol. [1]", "m(Fe) = 0.002000 × 55.85. [1]", "m = 0.1117 g for the analysed portion. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-redox-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Charge balance and observations", "prompt": "In acid, permanganate oxidises Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺. Write the net equation, identify both agents and describe an expected colour consequence using supplied SCSA colours.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. [2: species/coefficient and atom/charge balance]", "MnO₄⁻ gains electrons and is the oxidising agent. [1]", "Fe²⁺ loses electrons and is the reducing agent. [1]", "Purple permanganate is consumed toward pale-pink Mn²⁺, often very weakly coloured when dilute. [1]", "Iron ions also change from pale-green Fe²⁺ toward pale-brown Fe³⁺; the mixture and concentration influence overall appearance. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-redox-q7", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-redox-analysis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "20.0 mL of 0.0200 mol L⁻¹ acidic permanganate reacts with Fe²⁺ using a 1:5 ratio. Calculate Fe²⁺ amount. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.0200 × 0.0200 = 0.000400 mol.", "[2] n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 0.000400.", "[2] n(Fe²⁺) = 0.00200 mol in the analysed sample."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-redox-analysis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A 25.0 mL Fe²⁺ aliquot contains 0.00200 mol by redox titration. Calculate concentration and explain how prior air oxidation of Fe²⁺ could bias a claim about the original sample. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] c(Fe²⁺) = 0.00200/0.0250 = 0.0800 mol L⁻¹.", "[2] Air oxidation converts some Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ before the measured Fe²⁺ reaction.", "[2] The titre can underestimate original Fe²⁺ if that loss is ignored; specify the measured oxidation state."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Where does the analyte/titrant ratio come from?", "o": ["The balanced overall redox equation.", "Only the burette size.", "An assumed universal 1:1 ratio.", "The colour intensity alone."], "a": 0, "why": "Electron balancing establishes the actual stoichiometry."}, {"q": "An oxidising agent is itself", "o": ["Oxidised", "Reduced", "Always neutral", "Always a metal"], "a": 1, "why": "It accepts electrons while causing oxidation of another species."}, {"q": "Mn in MnO₄⁻ has oxidation number", "o": ["−1", "+2", "+7", "+8"], "a": 2, "why": "x + 4(−2) = −1 gives +7."}, {"q": "Multiplying a half-equation by two makes its E°", "o": ["Double", "Half", "Unchanged", "Always negative"], "a": 2, "why": "Potential is an intensive quantity."}, {"q": "A redox equation must conserve", "o": ["Atoms only", "Charge only", "Both atoms and charge", "Only colour"], "a": 2, "why": "Electron cancellation and balance ensure conservation."}, {"q": "The larger reduction potential usually identifies the stronger", "o": ["Reducing agent on the reactant side", "Oxidising agent on the reactant side", "Acid in every case", "Catalyst"], "a": 1, "why": "A more favourable reduction means greater electron-accepting tendency."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-electrochemistry-galvanic", "title": "Galvanic cells: reactions, flow and salt bridges", "scope": "Locate oxidation and reduction first · The salt bridge preserves electrical neutrality · Link the diagram to measured changes", "parent": "che12-electrochemistry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Galvanic cells: reactions, flow and salt bridges</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Locate oxidation and reduction first</li><li>The salt bridge preserves electrical neutrality</li><li>Link the diagram to measured changes</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Separating half-reactions into a cell", "kind": "learn", "text": "A galvanic cell separates oxidation and reduction so electrons travel through an external conductor and can do electrical work. The anode is where oxidation occurs and the cathode where reduction occurs in every electrochemical cell. For Zn/Cu, Zn atoms become Zn²⁺ at the anode and Cu²⁺ becomes Cu at the cathode. Solutions provide ionic conduction and a salt bridge or other separator supports charge balance. Electron flow through the wire differs from ion migration through solution. A disconnected circuit or loss of charge balance prevents sustained operation. The cell converts chemical energy from a spontaneous reaction into electrical energy.", "diagram": "galvanic"}, {"title": "Electrode signs and electron flow", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a galvanic cell, the anode is negative relative to the cathode because oxidation supplies electrons to the external circuit. Electrons travel anode → cathode. Conventional current in the external wire is opposite electron flow. In an electrolytic cell, a power source drives a non-spontaneous reaction, with a positive anode and negative cathode. The oxidation/reduction definitions do not change. Do not memorise “anode is always negative.” Determine cell type and reaction. A diagram should label signs, reactions and directions separately so one does not substitute for the others.", "diagram": "cell-signs"}, {"title": "Salt bridge and electrolyte", "kind": "learn", "text": "The salt bridge supplies an ionic path and helps prevent charge accumulation while avoiding direct mixing of the main solutions. At a Zn anode, production of Zn²⁺ increases positive charge tendency, so bridge anions migrate toward that half-cell. At a Cu cathode, Cu²⁺ is removed, so bridge cations migrate toward it. Electrons do not flow through the salt bridge as the normal transport mechanism. Use an electrolyte that does not precipitate or react appreciably with the cell species. A porous separator can fulfil a similar charge-balance role in other designs. Ion motion inside each half-cell also contributes to maintaining electroneutrality.", "diagram": "salt-bridge"}, {"title": "Cell notation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A conventional galvanic notation places the anode on the left and cathode on the right. Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) || Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s) uses a single line for a phase boundary and a double line for the junction between half-cells. Species within the same phase may be separated by commas. An inert platinum or graphite electrode can provide a conducting surface where the reacting couple has no suitable solid conductor. Notation is a compact representation and does not show every construction detail. Include states and specified concentrations when needed, and pair the notation with half-equations to make electron transfer unambiguous.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Standard voltage and conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Use E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode with both entries taken as reduction potentials. A Zn/Cu cell gives +1.10 V from +0.34 and −0.76 V. Standard values apply to specified standard conditions, commonly 25 °C with unit activities and standard gas pressure. Actual voltage changes with conditions and load. A measured open-circuit voltage is not the same as power output: power also depends on current and internal resistance. Do not multiply a voltage by the number of transferred electrons when balancing. The number of electrons matters for charge and energy quantities, while voltage is energy per charge.", "diagram": "cell-voltage"}, {"title": "Evaluating an electrochemical diagram", "kind": "learn", "text": "Check oxidation at anode, reduction at cathode, external electron direction, electrode signs appropriate to cell type and ionic charge balance. Label actual species and reactions rather than only generic plus/minus symbols. State whether the electrode is inert or consumed. Use cell potentials only with correctly oriented reduction entries. For corrosion and fuel-cell evaluation, connect the diagram to the chemical mechanism and practical limitations. A schematic is not to scale and does not show all internal resistance, side reactions or engineering components. Identifying those limits helps explain differences between calculated and measured performance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Inert electrodes and ion-only couples", "kind": "learn", "text": "A half-cell such as Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ has no conducting metal product to serve automatically as an electrode. An inert conducting surface such as platinum can exchange electrons with the ions without being the net reactant in the idealised process. A cell notation includes that solid electrode and lists ions in the same phase with a comma. The solution still requires an ionic conduction path. Distinguish a chemically active electrode such as copper in refining from an inert one; their anode reactions can be different even when the same solution is used.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Locate oxidation and reduction first", "kind": "learn", "text": "A galvanic cell uses a favourable redox reaction to provide electrical energy. Oxidation occurs at the anode and reduction at the cathode in every electrochemical-cell type; the electrode signs depend on whether the cell is galvanic or electrolytic. In a galvanic zinc–copper cell the zinc anode is negative and copper cathode positive. Electrons move through the external conductor from anode to cathode, not through the salt bridge as the main solution charge carriers.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The salt bridge preserves electrical neutrality", "kind": "learn", "text": "As zinc forms Zn²⁺, negative ions from a suitable bridge migrate towards the anodic compartment to counter increasing positive charge. As Cu²⁺ is consumed, positive bridge ions migrate towards the cathodic compartment to maintain balance. The ions must be compatible with the cell and not introduce unwanted reactions. A bridge completes the internal ionic pathway; it does not supply the electrons travelling through the wire.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Link the diagram to measured changes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Write both half-equations, label electron direction and identify changes in electrode mass and solution species. A metal anode dissolving can lose mass while a deposited metal cathode gains mass. Inert electrodes provide conductive surfaces without being consumed as the intended reactant. A diagram’s cell potential must match the actual half-cells and standard or nonstandard conditions; electrode sign alone is not an explanation of chemical direction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Cell voltage and notation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Cell voltage and notation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: A failed galvanic cell", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A complete Zn/Cu cell explanation", "prompt": "State both half-equations, electron flow and salt-bridge ion migration for a Zn/Cu galvanic cell.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Anode: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. [1]", "Cathode: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. [1]", "Electrons flow from Zn to Cu through the external circuit. [1]", "Bridge anions migrate toward the Zn half-cell. [1]", "Bridge cations migrate toward the Cu half-cell. [1]", "The bridge maintains charge balance through ions, not electron transport. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Cell voltage and notation", "prompt": "Using Ag⁺/Ag +0.80 V and Cu²⁺/Cu +0.34 V, write a spontaneous galvanic cell notation and calculate E°cell.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Cu is oxidised and Ag⁺ reduced. [1]", "Cu(s) | Cu²⁺(aq) || Ag⁺(aq) | Ag(s). [1]", "E° = +0.80 − +0.34. [1]", "E°cell = +0.46 V; do not double it when using two Ag⁺. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A failed galvanic cell", "prompt": "A Zn/Cu galvanic cell is assembled with a wire but without a functioning salt bridge. Explain why sustained current fails, provide the electrode equations and recommend a suitable correction.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ at the anode. [1]", "Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu at the cathode. [1]", "Electrons initially move through the external circuit from Zn toward Cu. [1]", "Charge imbalance develops without a sustained ionic path. [1]", "Provide a functioning bridge/separator with an appropriate non-interfering electrolyte. [1]", "Anions support balance toward the anode and cations toward the cathode; electrons do not use the bridge. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-galvanic-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain electron flow and salt-bridge migration in a galvanic Zn/Zn²⁺ and Cu²⁺/Cu cell. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ occurs at the anode; Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu at the cathode.", "[2] Electrons travel externally from zinc to copper.", "[2] Bridge anions migrate towards the zinc compartment and cations towards copper’s to maintain charge balance."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-galvanic-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Evaluate the claim that electrons cross the salt bridge and the zinc electrode gains mass during discharge. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The bridge mainly carries ionic movement, while electrons pass through the external conductor.", "[2] Zinc oxidation releases Zn²⁺ into solution and tends to decrease zinc electrode mass.", "[2] Copper reduction can deposit Cu and increase cathode mass; the reactions support the directions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Where does oxidation occur?", "o": ["At the anode.", "Always at the positive electrode regardless of cell type.", "In the salt bridge only.", "At the cathode."], "a": 0, "why": "Anode is defined by oxidation in both galvanic and electrolytic cells."}], "subtitle": "21 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-electrochemistry-batteries", "title": "Batteries, lead-acid cells and hydrogen fuel cells", "scope": "A battery’s chemistry depends on operating mode · Fuel cells require continuing reactant supply · Evaluate performance with a clear system boundary", "parent": "che12-electrochemistry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Batteries, lead-acid cells and hydrogen fuel cells</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A battery’s chemistry depends on operating mode</li><li>Fuel cells require continuing reactant supply</li><li>Evaluate performance with a clear system boundary</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Primary and secondary cells", "kind": "learn", "text": "A primary cell is intended for discharge without practical repeated recharging, such as the Leclanché-type cell context. A secondary cell uses chemistry that can be driven substantially backward during charging, such as a lead-acid accumulator. In discharge, the device acts galvanically; during charge, an external source drives electrolytic reversal. Reaction direction and electrode roles must be considered at each stage. A battery consists of one or more cells and its voltage and capacity depend on design. Evaluate materials, energy density, safety, lifespan, waste and recycling rather than claiming one cell chemistry is universally best for every use.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Lead-acid accumulator as a model", "kind": "learn", "text": "During lead-acid discharge, lead and lead dioxide react with sulfuric acid overall to form lead sulfate and water: Pb + PbO₂ + 2H₂SO₄ → 2PbSO₄ + 2H₂O. Lead metal is oxidised and Pb in PbO₂ is reduced. Charging drives the overall chemistry backward using supplied electrical energy. The reaction is a useful secondary-cell example rather than a requirement to memorise every construction detail. Changes in electrolyte concentration can provide evidence of state of charge. Practical degradation, side reactions and operating constraints limit perfect reversibility, so a model of ideal recharge does not guarantee unlimited battery life.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydrogen fuel cells", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fuel cell continuously receives fuel and oxidant rather than storing all reactants internally. An acidic hydrogen cell can use anode 2H₂ → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ and cathode O₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂O, giving 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. An alkaline design uses different ion-bearing half-equations but the same net reaction. At the point of use, water is the main chemical product; full environmental impact depends on how hydrogen and electricity for production are sourced. Assess efficiency, infrastructure and storage as well as direct emissions.", "diagram": "fuel-cell"}, {"title": "A battery’s chemistry depends on operating mode", "kind": "learn", "text": "During discharge a battery operates galvanically, supplying energy through a favourable reaction. A rechargeable battery is driven in the opposite overall direction during charging, using external electrical energy where the chemistry permits restoration. Electrode roles as oxidation and reduction sites follow the actual reaction direction. Do not assume the same physical electrode must retain the same anode/cathode role through both modes.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Fuel cells require continuing reactant supply", "kind": "learn", "text": "A hydrogen fuel cell uses supplied hydrogen and oxygen to form water through separated half-reactions. Electrons follow the external pathway and ions move through the electrolyte or membrane according to the cell’s chemistry. Unlike a sealed finite reactant store, continuing operation depends on reactant feed and product removal. The overall equation does not by itself reveal whether the electrolyte is acidic or alkaline; use the half-equations appropriate to the stated design.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate performance with a clear system boundary", "kind": "learn", "text": "Useful comparisons consider voltage, energy per mass, rechargeability, lifetime, reactant sourcing and environmental impacts. Hydrogen use can produce water locally, while upstream production may involve emissions and energy costs. Lead-acid cells involve hazardous materials and recycling considerations. A technology can offer a practical benefit without being impact-free; support the comparison with the specific criterion and supplied evidence, rather than treating clean as a complete chemical explanation. The syllabus names the International Partnership for Hydrogen and Fuel Cells in the Economy as an example of international cooperation in research, infrastructure information and common codes or standards. Cooperation can support compatible equipment, shared evidence and safer implementation, but scientific feasibility is only one part of adoption. Cost, storage, transport, energy sources and public priorities also matter. Distinguish a demonstration of cell chemistry from an evaluation of a transport system. Compare benefits and constraints using evidence and state whether the boundary includes only vehicle operation or upstream hydrogen production.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Fuel-cell environmental claim", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Fuel-cell environmental claim", "prompt": "Evaluate “a hydrogen fuel cell has no environmental impact because it makes water.” Include its net equation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. [1]", "Water is the main reaction product at use. [1]", "Hydrogen production and energy sourcing can create emissions and resource impacts. [1]", "Storage, transport and infrastructure also matter, so the absolute claim is unjustified. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-batteries-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Give the overall hydrogen–oxygen fuel-cell reaction and explain why a continuing feed differs from a finite stored-reactant battery. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O is the balanced overall reaction.", "[2] The fuel cell requires continuing hydrogen/oxygen supply and appropriate product removal to continue operating.", "[2] A stored-reactant battery has finite internal reacting amounts per discharge unless recharged or replaced."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-batteries-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain how charging a rechargeable battery differs from discharge, then qualify a claim that hydrogen fuel cells have no environmental impact. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Discharge is galvanic; charging uses external electrical energy to drive an appropriate reverse reaction.", "[2] Oxidation/reduction sites depend on operating direction, not an unchanging physical-electrode label.", "[2] Local water production does not account for hydrogen production, energy source, manufacture or disposal impacts."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What sustains a fuel cell’s operation?", "o": ["A suitable continuing reactant supply.", "Creation of hydrogen from nothing.", "The salt bridge supplying electrons indefinitely.", "A guarantee of zero upstream energy use."], "a": 0, "why": "Fuel and oxidant must be supplied for continued reaction."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-electrochemistry-electrolysis", "title": "Electrolysis, refining and electroplating", "scope": "External energy drives the chosen reaction · Electroplating places the object at the reduction site · Refining separates selected material through controlled reactions", "parent": "che12-electrochemistry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Electrolysis, refining and electroplating</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>External energy drives the chosen reaction</li><li>Electroplating places the object at the reduction site</li><li>Refining separates selected material through controlled reactions</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Electrolytic cells and external energy", "kind": "learn", "text": "Electrolysis uses a power supply to drive a reaction whose proposed direction is non-spontaneous under the given conditions. The source withdraws electrons from the anode and supplies them to the cathode. Oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode. The positive and negative terminal connections are essential to a diagram. In a simple molten salt, cations are reduced at the cathode and anions oxidised at the anode. In aqueous solutions, water and other species can compete, so products depend on the actual ions, electrodes and conditions. Do not assume every aqueous metal ion plates as metal.", "diagram": "electrolytic"}, {"title": "Molten versus aqueous products", "kind": "learn", "text": "Molten sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻, so ideal electrolysis gives Na at the cathode and Cl₂ at the anode. In aqueous NaCl, water is a possible reduction reactant and sodium metal is not the normal cathode product. The course’s aqueous product predictions should use supplied reduction data and stated conditions, noting concentration and overpotential limitations where relevant. An active electrode can itself oxidise, changing the anode reaction. Compare candidates before writing equations. Standard potentials offer a useful tendency guide but do not uniquely capture every practical electrode surface effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Copper electrolytic refining", "kind": "learn", "text": "In copper refining, impure copper serves as anode and a suitable pure copper sheet as cathode. Anode: Cu(s) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻. Cathode: Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s). Copper transfers from anode to cathode under electrical drive. Some impurities dissolve but are not deposited under chosen conditions, while less easily oxidised material can remain as anode sludge. The electrolyte supplies copper ions, and the ideal net copper-ion concentration can remain approximately stable when matching copper processes occur. Explain electrode mass decrease/increase and the separation of impurities rather than saying current makes every impurity disappear.", "diagram": "copper-refining"}, {"title": "Silver electroplating", "kind": "learn", "text": "The object to be plated is the cathode because metal ions must gain electrons and deposit there. For a simplified silver-ion system, Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag at the object. A silver anode can dissolve by Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻ to replenish ions. Appropriate electrolytes and operating conditions are chosen in practice; do not treat this learning diagram as a complete laboratory recipe. The object must be conductive or appropriately prepared. Coating thickness and uniformity depend on current distribution, time and surface preparation. Swapping the object to the anode would promote oxidation instead of the desired metal deposition.", "diagram": "silver-plating"}, {"title": "External energy drives the chosen reaction", "kind": "learn", "text": "An electrolytic cell uses an external supply to drive a reaction not favourable in that direction under the operating conditions. Oxidation remains at the anode and reduction at the cathode, but the usual electrolytic anode is positive and cathode negative. Cations move towards the cathode and anions towards the anode. The intended products depend on the electrolyte, water, concentrations, electrodes and kinetic effects; aqueous and molten salts need not give identical products.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Electroplating places the object at the reduction site", "kind": "learn", "text": "To coat an object with a metal, place it at the cathode where metal ions can gain electrons and deposit. A suitable metal anode can dissolve to replenish ions, while an inert anode requires another oxidation reaction. Clean surfaces and controlled conditions influence adhesion and uniformity. The external supply transfers energy and electrons through the circuit; it does not change conservation of atoms and charge.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Refining separates selected material through controlled reactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "In suitable copper refining, an impure copper anode dissolves while copper deposits at the cathode. Different impurities may remain, dissolve or form anode sludge depending on their chemistry. Avoid claiming every impurity follows one pathway. For molten sodium chloride, Na⁺ can be reduced to sodium, whereas aqueous sodium chloride involves competing water chemistry. Use specified conditions and supplied information instead of predicting solely from ion attraction.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Electroplating an object", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Electroplating an object", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Copper refining evidence", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Electroplating an object", "prompt": "Explain where to connect a metal object for silver electroplating and give the cathode and soluble-anode half-equations.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["The object connects as the cathode to the negative terminal in an electrolytic cell. [1]", "Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag at the object. [1]", "Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻ at a silver anode. [1]", "The anode can replenish silver ions while the coating grows. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Copper refining evidence", "prompt": "Describe changes at an impure copper anode and pure copper cathode during ideal electrolytic refining, including the role of impurities.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Copper at the anode oxidises to Cu²⁺ and anode mass decreases. [1]", "Cu²⁺ reduces at the cathode and cathode mass increases. [1]", "Both processes involve two electrons per copper atom. [1]", "Some impurities remain in solution under selected conditions. [1]", "Less readily oxidised impurities can collect as anode sludge. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-electrolysis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Describe electrode placement and half-equation when electroplating an object with copper from Cu²⁺ solution. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The object is the cathode connected to the negative side of the supply in this electrolytic setup.", "[2] Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu deposits metal on the object.", "[2] A suitable copper anode can oxidise as Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ to replenish ions."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-electrolysis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare molten and aqueous sodium chloride electrolysis and explain why the same cathode product cannot be assumed. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Molten salt contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ without water, permitting sodium formation at the cathode.", "[2] In aqueous solution water supplies competing reduction chemistry, commonly giving hydrogen under the stated school context.", "[2] Products depend on available species and operating conditions, not just the sign of an ion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Where is the object placed for metal electroplating?", "o": ["At the cathode.", "At the anode so it loses the coating.", "Inside the external power supply.", "Outside the electrolyte with no circuit."], "a": 0, "why": "Metal ions gain electrons and deposit at the reduction site."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 3 – Equilibrium, acids and redox", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-electrochemistry-corrosion", "title": "Corrosion, coatings and sacrificial protection", "scope": "Corrosion involves connected redox regions · Barriers and sacrificial protection differ · Protection has operating limits", "parent": "che12-electrochemistry", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Corrosion, coatings and sacrificial protection</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Corrosion involves connected redox regions</li><li>Barriers and sacrificial protection differ</li><li>Protection has operating limits</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Iron corrosion as an electrochemical process", "kind": "learn", "text": "Different regions of a moist iron surface can act as local anodes and cathodes. Iron oxidation is Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻. In aerated near-neutral water, oxygen reduction can be represented O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻. Later reactions form hydrated iron oxide products associated with rust. Oxygen, water and ionic conduction allow the process to continue; salts can increase conductivity. Rust is not a simple uniform Fe₂O₃ coating with no hydration complexity. A local-cell model explains why damage can occur even without a separate visible battery or external wire.", "diagram": "corrosion"}, {"title": "Coatings and sacrificial protection", "kind": "learn", "text": "Paint or a suitable intact coating limits access of water and oxygen. A sacrificial metal such as zinc or magnesium is more readily oxidised than iron and supplies electrons, helping keep iron cathodic. Protection can continue at a scratch if electrical contact and conditions are suitable. A less readily oxidised coating may protect as a barrier when intact but can accelerate local iron corrosion when damaged through galvanic coupling. Explain the difference between exclusion and cathodic protection. An impressed-current system uses an external source to maintain the protected structure as a cathode. Material choice depends on environment and maintenance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Context-specific corrosion protection", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a submerged iron jetty structure, a sacrificial metal can be attached in electrical contact and replaced as it is consumed. The surrounding water provides ionic conduction, while protective coatings reduce access to corrosive conditions. An impressed-current system is another cathodic approach when appropriately engineered. A suggestion must match the context: a paint coating may need maintenance at scratches, and a disconnected sacrificial piece cannot supply electrons to the iron. Explain which component is oxidised and why the protected iron is maintained as a cathode. Do not claim the sacrificial material lasts indefinitely.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Corrosion involves connected redox regions", "kind": "learn", "text": "In iron corrosion, anodic regions oxidise iron while cathodic regions support reduction, commonly involving oxygen and water in the relevant environment. Electron conduction through metal and ionic conduction through moisture connect the processes. Rust is not explained merely by saying iron touches oxygen; conditions such as water and dissolved salts affect pathways and rate. Use the actual context when assessing protection.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Barriers and sacrificial protection differ", "kind": "learn", "text": "Paint can isolate the metal from corrosive surroundings while intact, but a scratch exposes it. A more readily oxidised connected metal such as zinc can act sacrificially, providing protection even with suitable local damage while enough protective metal remains and the electrical/ionic connection works. A less readily oxidised coating can protect as a barrier but may accelerate exposed-iron attack in a damaged galvanic arrangement. Explain mechanism rather than ranking coatings by appearance.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Protection has operating limits", "kind": "learn", "text": "A sacrificial anode is consumed and requires inspection or replacement. Electrical connection and appropriate electrolyte access matter; an isolated piece of zinc nearby is not equivalent to a functioning system. A coating’s durability, adhesion and environment affect performance. An exam comparison should describe intact and scratched conditions separately and use oxidation tendencies or potentials where supplied. Do not claim one method prevents corrosion permanently in every environment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Sacrificial protection", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Sacrificial protection", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Sacrificial protection", "prompt": "A scratched zinc coating can still protect iron. Explain the mechanism and contrast it with paint.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Zinc is more readily oxidised under suitable conditions. [1]", "It supplies electrons and helps keep iron cathodic. [1]", "Electrical contact and an electrolyte pathway are needed for protection. [1]", "Paint mainly excludes water and oxygen, so a scratch can remove its local barrier protection. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-electrochemistry-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-corrosion-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare intact paint with a connected zinc coating on iron after each is scratched in a suitable moist environment. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Intact paint is a barrier, but a scratch exposes iron and can permit corrosion.", "[2] Zinc can oxidise preferentially and act sacrificially when correctly connected.", "[2] Zinc protection can continue at an exposed region while suitable connection and zinc remain; it is not permanent."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-electrochemistry-corrosion-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why a sacrificial anode requires replacement and why merely placing an isolated zinc object nearby is insufficient. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The sacrificial metal is oxidised and consumed.", "[2] Electron transfer requires an effective electrical connection to the protected metal.", "[2] Suitable ionic pathways/environment also matter; proximity alone does not establish the protective circuit."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is consumed in sacrificial protection?", "o": ["The more readily oxidised protective metal.", "Only paint pigment in every system.", "The protected metal must always oxidise first.", "Nothing because the anode is immortal."], "a": 0, "why": "The protective anode supplies electrons by its own oxidation."}, {"q": "Oxidation occurs at the", "o": ["Anode in every cell", "Cathode in every cell", "Salt bridge", "Negative terminal in every cell"], "a": 0, "why": "Anode/cathode definitions are based on reaction type."}, {"q": "Electrons normally move through the external wire", "o": ["Cathode to anode", "Anode to cathode", "Through the salt bridge first", "Only in electrolytic cells"], "a": 1, "why": "Oxidation supplies electrons and reduction consumes them."}, {"q": "In an electrolytic cell the cathode is", "o": ["Negative", "Positive", "Always neutral", "An oxidation site"], "a": 0, "why": "The supply delivers electrons to its negative-connected cathode."}, {"q": "The object in metal electroplating is", "o": ["The anode", "The cathode", "The salt bridge", "Necessarily dissolved entirely"], "a": 1, "why": "Reduction deposits metal on the cathode surface."}, {"q": "Sacrificial protection requires a metal that is", "o": ["More readily oxidised than the protected iron", "Always a noble metal", "Unable to conduct", "Unconnected to the iron"], "a": 0, "why": "Its oxidation supplies electrons that suppress iron oxidation."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-organic-groups", "title": "Functional groups, formulae and systematic names", "scope": "Connectivity carries chemical meaning · Name the parent and functional positions · Use representation requested in the question", "parent": "che12-organic", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Functional groups, formulae and systematic names</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Connectivity carries chemical meaning</li><li>Name the parent and functional positions</li><li>Use representation requested in the question</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Functional groups organise organic chemistry", "kind": "learn", "text": "Organic compounds contain carbon frameworks and may include functional groups that govern characteristic reactions. The course includes alkenes, alcohols, aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, esters, amines and amides. An alkene contains C=C. An alcohol has a hydroxy group bonded to an appropriate carbon. Aldehydes and ketones contain carbonyl C=O in different positions. Carboxylic acids contain –C(=O)OH. Esters contain –C(=O)O– linked to another carbon group. Amines have a nitrogen group, and amides contain –C(=O)N–. Identify the complete group, not a single oxygen or nitrogen atom in isolation.", "diagram": "functional-groups"}, {"title": "Reading condensed and displayed formulae", "kind": "learn", "text": "CH₃CH₂OH shows an O–H bond in ethanol, while CH₃OCH₃ represents an ether with different connectivity. CH₃CHO is an aldehyde, CH₃COCH₃ a ketone and CH₃COOH a carboxylic acid. In a displayed formula, show the carbonyl double bond explicitly. A condensed formula must still communicate which atoms attach where. Check carbon has four bond units, neutral oxygen usually two and neutral nitrogen commonly three plus a lone pair. A molecular formula alone cannot identify a functional group because different arrangements can share it. Draw an unfamiliar condensed structure before inferring its chemistry.", "diagram": "carbonyl"}, {"title": "Naming alcohols and alkenes", "kind": "learn", "text": "Choose the appropriate longest parent chain containing the principal group or double bond and apply its locant and suffix. Alcohol examples include propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol. Alkene examples include but-1-ene and 3-methylpent-1-ene. In simple single-functional-group course cases, number to give that group the appropriate lowest locant before ordinary alkyl branches. Show a locant where it is needed to distinguish positions. A name should reconstruct the exact connectivity. The SCSA scope includes parents up to eight carbons with simple branching and one listed functional group, so highly complex nomenclature is outside the core sequence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming aldehydes, ketones and acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "An aldehyde’s carbonyl carbon is terminal and part of the parent chain, as in ethanal CH₃CHO. Ketones use -one and a carbonyl locant, such as pentan-2-one CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃. Carboxylic acids use -oic acid, such as propanoic acid CH₃CH₂COOH, with the acid carbon counted in the chain. A carboxyl group is not simply an alcohol group plus an unrelated carbonyl: its combined structure gives acid chemistry. When drawing a named compound, count the carbonyl carbon among the parent carbons. Missing that atom produces the wrong homologous member.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Naming esters", "kind": "learn", "text": "An ester name has the alkyl group from the alcohol first, followed by the carboxylate-derived name from the acid. Ethyl ethanoate is CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃: the ethyl group is attached through oxygen and the ethanoate section includes the carbonyl carbon. Methyl propanoate is CH₃CH₂C(=O)OCH₃. Split the structure at the carbonyl–oxygen connection conceptually to identify the two parts. Do not reverse the acid and alcohol names. An ester is usually not an ionic ethanoate salt: its oxygen connects covalently to an organic group. Use complete structural formulae when demonstrating ester formation.", "diagram": "ester"}, {"title": "Amines and amides", "kind": "learn", "text": "An amine such as ethanamine CH₃CH₂NH₂ has nitrogen that can accept a proton through its lone pair. A simple primary amide such as ethanamide CH₃CONH₂ has nitrogen attached to a carbonyl carbon. In an amide, electron delocalisation involving the carbonyl changes nitrogen’s basic behaviour relative to an ordinary amine. Nitrogen-containing molecules can also form hydrogen bonds if appropriate N–H donors and acceptor sites are available. Naming may include positions and N-substitution where relevant; follow the simple structures and conventions specified. Recognising –CONH– rather than merely “contains N” is essential for peptide and polymer questions.", "diagram": "amine-amide"}, {"title": "A naming reference with complete examples", "kind": "learn", "text": "Examples within the simple course scope include ethanol CH₃CH₂OH; propanal CH₃CH₂CHO; butan-2-one CH₃COCH₂CH₃; pentanoic acid CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂COOH; methyl butanoate CH₃CH₂CH₂COOCH₃; propan-1-amine CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂; and butanamide CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂. Each carbonyl carbon belongs to its parent chain. Amines can be named with an appropriate locant where positional distinction matters. A displayed formula must connect bonds to the correct atoms and show the requested hydrogens. These condensed examples are naming aids rather than substitutes for every required drawn representation.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Connectivity carries chemical meaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "A molecular formula gives atom totals but not their connections. Structural and condensed formulae must make functional groups and bonds unambiguous. An alcohol has a hydroxyl on the appropriate carbon framework; an aldehyde contains a terminal carbonyl hydrogen; a ketone has carbon groups on both sides of its carbonyl. A carboxylic acid and ester have different oxygen connectivity. Labeling a molecule by its atoms alone loses the distinctions that control its reactions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Name the parent and functional positions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Choose the appropriate parent chain containing the principal functional group, number to assign the relevant lowest positions under the course naming conventions and identify substituents. Count the carbonyl carbon as part of an acid or aldehyde parent chain. For an ester, the alcohol-derived alkyl part and acid-derived alkanoate part identify opposite sides of the ester linkage. Check that a name can be reconstructed into the intended structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use representation requested in the question", "kind": "learn", "text": "A skeletal formula uses endpoints and vertices for carbon and omits many attached hydrogens, but heteroatoms and necessary hydrogens must remain clear. An abbreviated group can be appropriate in a condensed formula but may not satisfy a request to show all atoms and bonds. In exam answers draw correct connectivity first, then check valence and total formula. Do not turn a carbonyl double bond into a single bond simply to fit a label.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Naming an ester", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Naming an ester", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Functional-group discrimination", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Naming an ester", "prompt": "Name CH₃CH₂C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ and identify its acid and alcohol precursors.", "marks": 3, "steps": ["The acid-derived part is propanoate. [1]", "The oxygen-linked alkyl group is ethyl: ethyl propanoate. [1]", "Precursors are propanoic acid and ethanol. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Functional-group discrimination", "prompt": "Two candidates are propanal and propanone. Explain a suitable oxidising test to distinguish them, including one limitation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Use an appropriate controlled aldehyde oxidation test or suitable oxidant under stated conditions. [1]", "Propanal is oxidised toward propanoic acid. [1]", "Propanone does not undergo the same easy oxidation under mild course conditions. [1]", "Other oxidisable impurities or harsher conditions can interfere, so control conditions and compare references. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-organic-groups-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Identify the functional group and name CH₃CH₂CHO, then distinguish it from CH₃COCH₃. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CH₃CH₂CHO has a terminal aldehyde group and is propanal.", "[2] CH₃COCH₃ has an internal carbonyl and is propanone, a ketone.", "[2] Both have C₃H₆O but different connectivity and chemical class."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-organic-groups-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain the name ethyl ethanoate by identifying its alcohol-derived and acid-derived parts, and write a clear condensed formula. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ethyl is the alcohol-derived CH₃CH₂– group attached through oxygen.", "[2] Ethanoate is the acid-derived CH₃C(=O)– portion.", "[2] CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ shows correct ester connectivity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Can a molecular formula alone distinguish propanal from propanone?", "o": ["No: they share atom totals but differ in connectivity.", "Yes: every formula has only one possible structure.", "Only because both have different carbon counts.", "No molecule can have a carbonyl group."], "a": 0, "why": "Functional arrangement requires structural information."}], "subtitle": "19 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-organic-isomers", "title": "Structural and cis–trans isomerism", "scope": "Test structural isomers by connectivity · Cis–trans needs restricted rotation and suitable groups · Use geometry to explain a consequence", "parent": "che12-organic", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Structural and cis–trans isomerism</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Test structural isomers by connectivity</li><li>Cis–trans needs restricted rotation and suitable groups</li><li>Use geometry to explain a consequence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Structural isomerism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chain isomers differ in carbon skeleton; position isomers differ in placement of a bond or functional group. Butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol share C₄H₁₀O and differ in O–H group position. Two different functional classes can also share molecular formula, so a formula alone may leave several possibilities. Draw every distinct connectivity, check valency and eliminate duplicate structures related only by rotation of the drawing. Give the requested isomer type rather than calling every difference cis–trans. Physical properties can differ because the structures affect forces, shape and packing, even when molecular mass is identical.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cis–trans isomerism", "kind": "learn", "text": "Rotation around C=C is restricted. Geometric isomerism is possible when each double-bonded carbon has two different attached groups. In but-2-ene, the methyl groups can be on the same side, cis, or opposite sides, trans. But-1-ene does not meet the condition because its terminal double-bond carbon has two hydrogens. A flat cis–trans drawing represents the approximate planar region around the double bond. Reorienting the entire diagram does not change the isomer. Geometric isomers have the same connectivity but different spatial arrangement and can have different properties. Do not count free rotation about an ordinary single bond as a new cis–trans isomer.", "diagram": "cis-trans"}, {"title": "Test structural isomers by connectivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in atom connections. Different rotations or drawings of the same connected structure are not new structural isomers. Chain, positional and functional-group differences can produce valid examples within the relevant formula. Check valence, atom totals and duplicates systematically. A branching sketch that quietly adds a carbon is a different formula, not an isomer of the original.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Cis–trans needs restricted rotation and suitable groups", "kind": "learn", "text": "For simple alkene cis–trans isomerism, the carbon–carbon double bond restricts rotation and each double-bond carbon must have two different substituents. But-2-ene can meet the condition, whereas propene does not because one end has two hydrogens. Apply the course’s suitable matching-group examples rather than claiming every alkene has cis and trans forms. These forms retain the same atom connectivity but differ in spatial arrangement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use geometry to explain a consequence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Different spatial arrangements can affect dipole cancellation and physical properties, but trends must be tied to the actual structure and property. A higher boiling point does not guarantee the same melting-point order because packing also matters. Draw substituents explicitly on both sides of the double bond and avoid ambiguous crosses or labels. Compare models as representations of the same connectivity with different locked geometry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Isomer requirement", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Isomer requirement", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Isomer requirement", "prompt": "Explain why but-2-ene has cis–trans isomers while but-1-ene does not.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["C=C restricts rotation. [1]", "Each but-2-ene double-bond carbon has H and CH₃ as different groups. [1]", "These allow same-side and opposite-side methyl arrangements. [1]", "But-1-ene has two H on one double-bond carbon, preventing distinct cis–trans arrangements. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-organic-isomers-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain why but-2-ene can show cis–trans isomerism but propene cannot. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The double bond restricts rotation.", "[2] In but-2-ene each double-bond carbon has a hydrogen and methyl group, allowing distinct relative arrangements.", "[2] In propene one double-bond carbon has two identical hydrogens, so the required distinction is absent."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-organic-isomers-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why redrawing the same molecule after rotating a single bond does not necessarily give a new structural isomer, and state the formula test for a valid pair. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Structural isomers require different atom connectivity, not only a changed drawing orientation.", "[2] Single-bond rotation can give different conformations of the same connected molecule.", "[2] A valid structural-isomer pair must have identical molecular formulae and different connections."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is required on each double-bond carbon for the simple alkene cis–trans cases taught here?", "o": ["Two different substituents.", "Two identical substituents.", "No carbon bonds.", "An unrestricted single bond instead."], "a": 0, "why": "Different groups at each end make distinct locked relative arrangements possible."}], "subtitle": "13 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-organic-properties", "title": "Organic physical properties and solubility", "scope": "Name all relevant intermolecular interactions · Compare similar molecules before drawing a trend · Physical change preserves the molecular framework", "parent": "che12-organic", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Organic physical properties and solubility</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Name all relevant intermolecular interactions</li><li>Compare similar molecules before drawing a trend</li><li>Physical change preserves the molecular framework</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Functional groups and physical properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Boiling point depends on the combined intermolecular attractions and molecular structure. Alcohols and carboxylic acids can donate and accept hydrogen bonds. Aldehydes, ketones and esters have polar carbonyl groups and can accept hydrogen bonds from water but lack O–H donor groups in the simple examples. Primary amides can form strong hydrogen-bonding networks. Dispersion occurs in all. A larger hydrocarbon region can reduce water solubility even when the functional group interacts with water. Compare molecules of similar size when isolating group effects and avoid assuming one named force universally dominates every comparison.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Solubility: explicit group and chain reasoning", "kind": "learn", "text": "Methanol can donate and accept hydrogen bonds with water and has a small non-polar carbon region. A long-chain carboxylic acid still has a polar hydrogen-bonding group, but its large non-polar hydrocarbon region makes favourable water interaction less extensive relative to the molecule’s size. Thus a functional-group label alone does not guarantee high water solubility. Explain the actual solute–water forces and the chain’s effect, not merely “polar dissolves in polar.” Dissolution competes with solute–solute and water–water attraction and includes entropy effects, so data strengthen a comparison.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Name all relevant intermolecular interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "All molecular substances have dispersion attractions. Polar groups can add dipole–dipole interactions; suitable donor and acceptor sites permit hydrogen bonding between particular species. Alcohols can donate and accept appropriate hydrogen bonds, while many carbonyl compounds can accept from water but cannot donate to themselves without an appropriate bonded hydrogen. Distinguish interactions between identical solute molecules from interactions with water.", "diagram": "focused-organic-forces"}, {"title": "Compare similar molecules before drawing a trend", "kind": "learn", "text": "For comparable structures, stronger overall intermolecular attractions can increase boiling point. Chain size, surface contact and branching also affect dispersion and packing. Water solubility reflects competition between molecular regions and solvent interactions: a polar functional group does not guarantee a long-chain molecule is highly soluble. State the comparison and conditions instead of using one-force explanations universally.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Physical change preserves the molecular framework", "kind": "learn", "text": "Boiling usually separates molecules without breaking their covalent skeletons. A claim that a molecule boils high because its intramolecular carbonyl bond is strong confuses bond types. Melting adds the importance of crystal packing, so a boiling comparison cannot automatically be extended to melting. Use structure → named interaction → property reasoning, with a limit or competing factor where the evidence requires one.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"id": "che12-organic-properties-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare ethanol and dimethyl ether as same-formula molecules for hydrogen bonding between their own molecules and likely boiling behaviour. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ethanol has O–H groups and can form intermolecular hydrogen bonds with itself.", "[2] Dimethyl ether has oxygen acceptor sites but no O–H donor, so it lacks the same self-hydrogen-bond network.", "[2] Ethanol’s stronger overall attractions support higher boiling point in this comparison; both also have dispersion."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-organic-properties-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why a carbonyl compound may hydrogen-bond with water without hydrogen-bonding to its own molecules in the same way. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Carbonyl oxygen can accept a hydrogen bond from water’s O–H group.", "[2] A molecule lacking a suitable O–H/N–H donor cannot donate that bond to an identical molecule.", "[2] Solute–water and solute–solute interactions must therefore be distinguished in solubility/boiling explanations."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why is a strong covalent carbonyl bond not itself the usual explanation for boiling point?", "o": ["Boiling separates molecules rather than ordinarily breaking that bond.", "Every boiling process destroys the molecule into atoms.", "Carbonyl compounds have no dispersion forces.", "Only atomic mass matters in every case."], "a": 0, "why": "The relevant attractions are between molecules during ordinary boiling."}], "subtitle": "11 screens · 2 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-organic-oxidation", "title": "Alcohol classes, oxidation and test evidence", "scope": "Classify the carbon carrying hydroxyl · Conditions determine which product is recovered · Separate oxidation tests from broad identification", "parent": "che12-organic", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Alcohol classes, oxidation and test evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Classify the carbon carrying hydroxyl</li><li>Conditions determine which product is recovered</li><li>Separate oxidation tests from broad identification</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols", "kind": "learn", "text": "Classify the carbon bearing OH by how many other carbons attach to it. A primary alcohol usually has one, a secondary two and a tertiary three; methanol is handled as the simplest primary oxidation case. Propan-1-ol is primary, propan-2-ol secondary and 2-methylpropan-2-ol tertiary. Classification is not determined by total molecular carbon count or OH locant alone. Draw the OH carbon and its neighbours. Under common course oxidising conditions, primary alcohols can yield aldehydes then acids, secondary alcohols yield ketones and tertiary alcohols do not undergo the same simple oxidation without C–C cleavage.", "diagram": "alcohol-classes"}, {"title": "Oxidising alcohols: products and conditions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Controlled primary-alcohol oxidation can give an aldehyde, often removed by distillation to limit further oxidation. With excess oxidant under reflux, a carboxylic acid can form. Secondary alcohols form ketones. A shorthand [O] equation tracks organic transformation: CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O, followed by CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH. This shorthand is not a full ionic redox equation. Where asked, balance oxidation and oxidant reduction half-equations explicitly. Conditions and oxidation-agent observations form part of the chemical answer rather than being assumed.", "diagram": "alcohol-oxidation"}, {"title": "Oxidant colours and full equations", "kind": "learn", "text": "Acidified dichromate changes from orange toward green as Cr(VI) reduces to Cr(III); acidified permanganate loses its purple colour as Mn(VII) reduces to Mn(II). These changes show oxidant consumption but alone may not uniquely identify the organic reactant. Ethanol-to-ethanal oxidation can be written CH₃CH₂OH → CH₃CHO + 2H⁺ + 2e⁻. Ethanol-to-ethanoic-acid oxidation is CH₃CH₂OH + H₂O → CH₃COOH + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. Combine with the appropriate acidic reduction half-equation and cancel species. Make sure your final equation accounts for the oxygen incorporated into the product.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Acids, amines and functional tests", "kind": "learn", "text": "Carboxylic acids react with hydroxide to form carboxylate and water, and with carbonate to release CO₂. Amines accept H⁺ to form ammonium-type ions. An alkene can decolourise bromine through addition, while an alcohol may consume a suitable oxidant depending on its class. Use a set of tests to distinguish candidates. An aldehyde can also oxidise, so oxidant colour change is not unique to alcohols. A carbonate effervescence test supports an acidic group but must be interpreted within the candidate list. State reagent, conditions, observation and a chemical explanation rather than treating a single colour as a complete identification.", "diagram": "organic-tests"}, {"title": "Classify the carbon carrying hydroxyl", "kind": "learn", "text": "Primary, secondary and tertiary alcohol classes refer to how many carbon groups attach to the hydroxyl-bearing carbon. Do not classify by the total number of carbons in the molecule or by the oxygen’s neighbours alone. This local structure influences oxidation: primary alcohols can form aldehydes and further carboxylic acids, secondary alcohols ketones, while tertiary alcohols resist the standard mild school oxidation pathway without carbon–carbon cleavage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conditions determine which product is recovered", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a primary alcohol, distillation can remove the aldehyde and reduce further oxidation, while reflux with suitable oxidant can favour the acid. A reaction arrow should show relevant conditions. Colour change of an oxidant is an observation supporting reaction, not complete proof of one particular organic product. Structural identification may require additional evidence. Keep the carbon skeleton and functional connectivity correct in product drawings.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Separate oxidation tests from broad identification", "kind": "learn", "text": "A negative test can reflect an unsuitable condition, a resistant class or procedural limitations. Acidified dichromate and permanganate have specific expected changes under suitable conditions; use the given reagent and medium. Do not claim every alcohol yields a carboxylic acid or every carbonyl is an aldehyde. A full explanation links the hydroxyl-bearing carbon’s class to products and observed evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Alcohol oxidation pathway", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Alcohol oxidation pathway", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Alcohol oxidation pathway", "prompt": "Give products of controlled oxidation and further oxidation of ethanol, with condensed equations and an oxidant observation.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Controlled product: ethanal. [1]", "CH₃CH₂OH + [O] → CH₃CHO + H₂O. [1]", "Further product: ethanoic acid; CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH. [1]", "Acidified dichromate changes orange to green, or permanganate loses purple under the stated acidic conditions. [1]", "Distillation can remove aldehyde, while excess oxidant/reflux favours the acid. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-organic-oxidation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Classify propan-2-ol and state its product under suitable school oxidation conditions, with structural reasoning. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Its hydroxyl-bearing carbon is attached to two other carbons, so it is secondary.", "[2] Oxidation gives propanone, CH₃C(=O)CH₃.", "[2] A ketone forms at that carbon; total carbon count alone did not determine class."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-organic-oxidation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare distillation and reflux when oxidising ethanol and identify why an oxidant colour change alone is incomplete product identification. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Distillation can remove ethanal as it forms, limiting further oxidation.", "[2] Reflux with suitable excess oxidant can favour ethanoic acid.", "[2] A colour change demonstrates reagent change but does not uniquely establish the organic product without conditions/additional evidence."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What defines a secondary alcohol?", "o": ["Two carbon groups attached to the hydroxyl-bearing carbon.", "Two oxygen atoms anywhere.", "Two carbons in the whole molecule.", "An O–O bond."], "a": 0, "why": "Classification uses the local carbon attached to hydroxyl."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-organic-synthesis", "title": "Esterification, formula calculations and identification", "scope": "Show the ester linkage and source fragments · Yield and separation are different considerations · Combine quantitative and structural evidence", "parent": "che12-organic", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Esterification, formula calculations and identification</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Show the ester linkage and source fragments</li><li>Yield and separation are different considerations</li><li>Combine quantitative and structural evidence</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Esterification and reversible condensation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A carboxylic acid and alcohol can react to form an ester and water, usually with acid catalysis and heating. CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O produces ethyl ethanoate. It is a condensation because a small molecule forms as the organic reactants join. The reaction is reversible, so yield can improve by using excess reagent or removing a product under appropriate conditions. A catalyst speeds approach to equilibrium without changing Kc. In a structural model, identify which acid OH and alcohol H contribute to water and trace the retained atoms into the ester.", "diagram": "esterification"}, {"title": "Empirical and molecular formulae", "kind": "learn", "text": "An empirical formula gives the simplest whole-number atom ratio. Convert each element’s mass or percentage into moles, divide by the smallest and scale near-simple fractional ratios to integers. For a molecular formula, divide measured molar mass by empirical-formula mass and multiply all subscripts by the resulting whole number. Do not round 1.50 to 2 prematurely; multiply the entire ratio by two. Combustion data give carbon from CO₂ and hydrogen from H₂O, with oxygen often found by mass difference when appropriate. State assumptions about composition and completeness. A formula does not establish one unique structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Identifying structure from combined evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Start with molecular formula or empirical data, use reactions to narrow functional groups and then consider isomer candidates. A C₃H₆O compound that oxidises readily may be propanal rather than propanone under suitable conditions. Boiling/solubility evidence can support, but not always uniquely determine, an assignment. If analytical spectra or other secondary data are supplied, interpret the stated features and avoid inventing an unstated peak rule. SCSA requires structure inferred from chemical reactions and other analytical data; advanced standalone instrumental theory is not assumed here. Clearly state when several structures remain consistent with the evidence.", "diagram": "structure-evidence"}, {"title": "Show the ester linkage and source fragments", "kind": "learn", "text": "A carboxylic acid and alcohol can react reversibly to form an ester and water under suitable acidic conditions. The ester contains R–C(=O)–O–R′, with the carbonyl retained. Identify acid-derived and alcohol-derived portions when drawing or naming the product. The linking oxygen position matters: R–O–C–R′ with no carbonyl is not the ester linkage. Use a reversible arrow for the equilibrium where appropriate.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Yield and separation are different considerations", "kind": "learn", "text": "An acid catalyst increases approach rate without changing fixed-temperature equilibrium constant. Excess reactant or removal of a product can improve conversion under suitable conditions; purification determines how much usable product is isolated. Percentage yield compares actual isolated amount with theoretical amount from the limiting reagent. Product loss during transfer can reduce yield without changing reaction stoichiometry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Combine quantitative and structural evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "An empirical formula gives a simplest ratio and a molecular mass can scale it to a molecular formula, but neither alone fixes connectivity. Combine formula, functional tests and specified data to evaluate candidate structures. A fruity smell is a broad observation associated with some esters, not a unique identity proof or a reason to smell unknown reagents directly. Show atom and valence checks in the final structure.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Composition to formula", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Composition to formula", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Ester equilibrium", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: A synthesis identification problem", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 3}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 4}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Composition to formula", "prompt": "A compound is 40.0% C, 6.71% H and 53.3% O by mass. Use C 12.01, H 1.008 and O 16.00. Its molar mass is approximately 60.05 g mol⁻¹. Determine empirical and molecular formulae.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Take 100 g: C moles 40.0/12.01 ≈ 3.33; H 6.71/1.008 ≈ 6.66; O 53.3/16.00 ≈ 3.33. [1]", "Divide by approximately 3.33 to get 1:2:1. [1]", "Empirical formula CH₂O, mass ≈ 30.03 g mol⁻¹. [1]", "Molecular multiplier = 60.05/30.03 ≈ 2. [1]", "Molecular formula C₂H₄O₂. Multiple structures may share it. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-organic-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Ester equilibrium", "prompt": "Write the equation for ethanol and ethanoic acid and explain how using excess ethanol differs from adding more catalyst.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["CH₃COOH + CH₃CH₂OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ + H₂O. [2: reactant/product structures and atom balance]", "Excess ethanol favours net ester formation and can increase equilibrium yield. [1]", "A catalyst supplies a faster pathway. [1]", "It does not change equilibrium composition at fixed temperature. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q6", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A synthesis identification problem", "prompt": "An unknown C₃H₈O compound oxidises to a ketone. Identify a likely structure, give the oxidation product, and explain how its hydrogen bonding differs from the product.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["The likely alcohol is propan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₃. [2: identity and structure]", "It is secondary and oxidises to propanone, CH₃COCH₃. [1]", "CH₃CH(OH)CH₃ + [O] → CH₃COCH₃ + H₂O. [1]", "The alcohol can donate and accept hydrogen bonds. [1]", "The ketone accepts from water but lacks a donor for ordinary self hydrogen bonding. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-organic-q7", "allocations": [2, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-organic-synthesis-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Write the esterification of ethanoic acid with ethanol using condensed structures, identify the ester and state the catalyst’s equilibrium effect. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] CH₃COOH + HOCH₂CH₃ ⇌ CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ + H₂O.", "[2] The ester is ethyl ethanoate with its carbonyl and linking oxygen correctly placed.", "[2] An acid catalyst speeds approach but does not change K at fixed temperature."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-organic-synthesis-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "An esterification has theoretical product mass 10.0 g and isolated mass 7.50 g. Calculate yield and distinguish a purification loss from a changed balanced ratio. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Percentage yield = actual/theoretical × 100.", "[2] Yield = 7.50/10.0 × 100 = 75.0%.", "[2] Transfer or purification loss lowers isolated mass but does not alter the balanced reaction’s stoichiometric coefficients."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which linkage identifies an ester?", "o": ["–C(=O)–O–.", "–O–O– only.", "–C–C– with no oxygen.", "Any molecule with two unrelated oxygen atoms."], "a": 0, "why": "The carbonyl carbon is bonded to the linking oxygen."}, {"q": "CH₃COCH₃ contains", "o": ["A ketone", "An aldehyde", "An ester", "An amine"], "a": 0, "why": "The carbonyl carbon is bonded to two carbon groups."}, {"q": "The alcohol part of ethyl ethanoate supplies", "o": ["Ethyl attached through O", "The carbonyl carbon", "Every oxygen atom", "The ethanoate carbon skeleton"], "a": 0, "why": "The ester oxygen links to the alcohol-derived ethyl group."}, {"q": "A secondary alcohol usually oxidises to", "o": ["A ketone", "An alkane directly", "An amide", "An ester without another reagent"], "a": 0, "why": "The OH-bearing carbon has two carbon neighbours and forms a ketone carbonyl."}, {"q": "An empirical formula represents", "o": ["A unique structure", "The simplest whole-number atom ratio", "Only ionic charges", "The full molecule necessarily"], "a": 1, "why": "A molecular formula may be an integer multiple of it."}, {"q": "Tertiary alcohols under typical mild oxidation conditions", "o": ["Always become aldehydes", "Do not undergo the simple primary/secondary oxidation pathway", "Lose one carbon automatically", "Always form acids"], "a": 1, "why": "The OH-bearing carbon lacks the hydrogen needed for that simple transformation."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 5 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-addition", "title": "Addition polymers and repeat-unit evidence", "scope": "A repeat unit must preserve connectivity · Recover the monomer from the backbone · Properties follow chain architecture and interactions", "parent": "che12-polymers-proteins", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Addition polymers and repeat-unit evidence</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>A repeat unit must preserve connectivity</li><li>Recover the monomer from the backbone</li><li>Properties follow chain architecture and interactions</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Monomers, polymers and repeat units", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polymer contains many repeating structural units linked into long molecules. A monomer is a molecule that can be used to make the polymer. A repeat unit shows the smallest or a convenient recurring structural pattern; it is not necessarily identical in formula to an intact monomer, especially in condensation polymerisation. Brackets and a subscript n indicate repetition, with bonds extending through the brackets. A drawing of three units represents a segment, not the exact full chain length. Polymer samples commonly contain a distribution of molecular lengths. Distinguish the covalent backbone within a chain from intermolecular attractions between chains.", "diagram": "polymer"}, {"title": "Addition polymerisation of ethene", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethene monomers join when the C=C double bond is converted into single-bond connections in the chain. nCH₂=CH₂ → [–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ represents polyethene. No small molecule is eliminated in this simplified addition model. Carbon atoms retain four bond units: the former double bond becomes single while each carbon gains a backbone connection. Bonds must extend beyond both sides of the repeat-unit brackets. Do not leave C=C in a conventional polyethene backbone or show unconnected CH₂ groups. Initiation and detailed mechanism are not represented by the simple overall equation.", "diagram": "addition-polymer"}, {"title": "PTFE and substituted addition monomers", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tetrafluoroethene CF₂=CF₂ gives poly(tetrafluoroethene), [–CF₂–CF₂–]ₙ. Propene gives [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ: the methyl group remains a side group rather than becoming an extra backbone carbon. To recover an alkene monomer from an addition repeat unit, identify the two backbone carbons formed from its double bond and reconnect them as C=C while retaining attached groups. More complex copolymers need additional information. PTFE’s chemical resistance and surface properties arise from its fluorinated structure and material organisation; they are not explained merely by calling it “strong plastic.”", "diagram": "ptfe"}, {"title": "A repeat unit must preserve connectivity", "kind": "learn", "text": "Addition polymerisation of a suitable alkene opens the double bond’s π component and links monomer units into a chain. Substituents remain attached to the corresponding backbone carbons. Show the repeat unit in brackets with bonds extending through both sides and n outside. A drawing with a retained backbone C=C in ordinary poly(ethene) misrepresents the addition product. Do not delete substituents simply because the monomer has become a repeat unit.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recover the monomer from the backbone", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a simple alkene addition polymer, identify the two-carbon repeat backbone and restore the double bond between those carbons to reconstruct the monomer. This inference depends on the supplied repeat-unit structure and polymer class. A condensation polymer requires a different method because its linkage reflects functional-group reactions. The molecular mass of a whole polymer depends on chain length, not merely repeat-unit mass.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Properties follow chain architecture and interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chain length, branching, crystallinity, crosslinking and substituents affect material properties. Flexible chains can still pack and interact differently, so not every addition polymer has the same softening behaviour or recyclability. A repeat unit explains connectivity but does not fully specify a real sample’s chain-length distribution. Connect the structural feature named in the question to the specific observed property rather than labelling every polymer strong and light.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Addition repeat unit", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Addition repeat unit", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Addition repeat unit", "prompt": "Draw the repeat unit from CH₂=CHCH₃ and explain what changes at the double bond.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Repeat unit [–CH₂–CH(CH₃)–]ₙ with bonds extending through brackets. [2]", "The C=C becomes C–C single bonding in the backbone. [1]", "New links join units without elimination of a small molecule in the addition model. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q1", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-addition-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Describe the repeat unit of poly(propene) formed from CH₂=CHCH₃ and identify what happens to the methyl group. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The backbone repeat is –CH₂–CH(CH₃)– with outward chain bonds and brackets/n.", "[2] The double bond becomes a backbone single bond through addition polymerisation.", "[2] The methyl substituent remains attached to the corresponding carbon; it is not removed."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-addition-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A simple addition-polymer repeat unit is –CH₂–CHCl–. Reconstruct the monomer and explain why retaining C=C in that ordinary repeat is incorrect. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Restore the double bond between the repeat’s two backbone carbons.", "[2] The monomer is CH₂=CHCl.", "[2] Addition uses the double-bond component to link the chain; the ordinary resulting repeat backbone contains single bonds."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What happens to substituents during simple alkene addition polymerisation?", "o": ["They remain on the corresponding backbone carbons.", "They all become water.", "They are deleted from the formula.", "They always move to the opposite carbon."], "a": 0, "why": "Addition links the alkene carbons without discarding their substituents."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-condensation", "title": "Polyesters, polyamides and recovering monomers", "scope": "Functional groups create the linkage · Recover monomers by opening the correct bond · Count small molecules with chain ends in mind", "parent": "che12-polymers-proteins", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Polyesters, polyamides and recovering monomers</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Functional groups create the linkage</li><li>Recover monomers by opening the correct bond</li><li>Count small molecules with chain ends in mind</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Condensation polymerisation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Condensation joins monomers with complementary reactive groups and releases small molecules. A diol and dicarboxylic acid can form ester links, producing a polyester and water. A diamine and dicarboxylic acid can form amide links, producing a polyamide and water. For long chains, monomers need appropriate functionality to continue growth at both ends. Repeat-unit drawings commonly omit end groups, so exact water counts for a finite chain can differ from a simplified large-n representation. Identify the linkage and the atoms removed, and do not copy an addition-polymer method that merely opens a double bond.", "diagram": "condensation-polymer"}, {"title": "Polyester repeat units and PET", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polyester contains –C(=O)O– links in its backbone. PET can be formed from ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid in a simplified condensation model. Its repeating backbone includes –OCH₂CH₂O– and –C(=O)C₆H₄C(=O)– sections joined as esters. Aromatic rings and carbonyl-containing segments influence rigidity and interactions. PET is used in fibres and containers, while actual performance depends on processing, crystallinity and additives. A schematic aromatic ring must retain its two opposite attachment points in this example. Draw both monomer structures and the resulting links rather than naming PET without showing chemical relationships.", "diagram": "pet"}, {"title": "Polyamides and nylon", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polyamide has –C(=O)NH– links in its backbone. A dicarboxylic acid and diamine can form a nylon-type polymer. N–H groups can donate hydrogen bonds to carbonyl oxygens on neighbouring chains, contributing to strength and melting behaviour. To recover monomers, cut the amide link conceptually and restore OH on the carbonyl section and H on the nitrogen section for the acid/amine condensation route. Account for functionality at both ends. Nylon is a family of polyamides, not a single universal chain. Use the provided repeat unit to identify the actual monomers in a question.", "diagram": "nylon"}, {"title": "Polymer structures and material properties", "kind": "learn", "text": "Longer chains can have more entanglement and more total intermolecular contact. Branching can reduce close packing, affecting density, crystallinity and flexibility. Cross-links connect different chains covalently and restrict motion. Stronger interchain attractions can increase resistance to separation or deformation. These features interact, so one-factor explanations require matched comparisons. Polyethene is non-polar and mainly experiences dispersion between chains, while nylon can hydrogen bond. Thermal and mechanical properties also depend on additives and processing. Link each stated property to a specific structural feature and identify whether the force is covalent within/across chains or intermolecular.", "diagram": "polymer-structure"}, {"title": "Thermoplastics, cross-linking and evaluation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Many polymers with separate chains can soften when heated because chains gain mobility without destroying every covalent backbone. Extensive cross-linking can prevent simple remelting; sufficiently high temperature may instead cause decomposition. This helps explain different reuse and recycling pathways but does not guarantee any labelled plastic is easy to recycle locally. Evaluate collection, separation, contamination, material degradation and processing energy. “Biodegradable” needs conditions and timescale, not an assumption of harmless disappearance anywhere. The chemistry supports informed choices, while economic and infrastructure factors shape practical outcomes. Use evidence rather than equating all polymers with one environmental effect.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recovering monomers from polyester links", "kind": "learn", "text": "For a polyester segment containing –O–R–O–C(=O)–R′–C(=O)–, identify the diol as HO–R–OH and the dicarboxylic acid as HOOC–R′–COOH in the acid/diol condensation model. Restore H to oxygen and OH to the carbonyl section when conceptually hydrolysing each ester link. Count atoms carefully and preserve aromatic attachment positions if present. For a polyamide, restore amine and carboxylic acid groups instead. A polymer can sometimes be made by another synthetic route, so infer the requested condensation monomers from the supplied linkage and question context.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Functional groups create the linkage", "kind": "learn", "text": "A polyester can form from suitable difunctional alcohol and carboxylic-acid monomers; a polyamide from suitable amine and acid functionality. The ester linkage is –C(=O)–O– and the amide linkage –C(=O)–NH– in the common examples. Monomers need appropriate repeated functionality to support chain growth. A single ester molecule is not automatically a polymer, and the carbonyl must remain visible in the repeat unit.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recover monomers by opening the correct bond", "kind": "learn", "text": "To reconstruct polyester monomers, conceptually hydrolyse the ester linkages and restore alcohol and acid groups at the cut sites. For a polyamide, restore amine and acid functionality. Count the carbonyl carbon within the acid fragment rather than losing it during the split. The supplied polymer diagram may span portions of more than one monomer, so identify linkage boundaries before assigning a repeat fragment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Count small molecules with chain ends in mind", "kind": "learn", "text": "Condensation link formation commonly releases a small molecule such as water for the stated monomers. For a finite linear chain the number of links differs from simply the number of monomers; chain ends remain. A schematic repeating-unit equation often ignores end groups for large n. Use the exact finite chain requested when calculating water amount, rather than assuming every monomer independently loses one complete water molecule regardless of connectivity.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Condensation monomer recovery", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Condensation monomer recovery", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: A nylon–polyethene comparison", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Condensation monomer recovery", "prompt": "A polymer segment is –NH(CH₂)₆NHC(=O)(CH₂)₄C(=O)–. Identify the two condensation monomers and linkage type.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Diamine H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂. [1]", "Dicarboxylic acid HOOC(CH₂)₄COOH. [1]", "The polymer contains amide links and is a polyamide. [1]", "Acid/amine joining releases water in the simplified condensation route. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "A nylon–polyethene comparison", "prompt": "Explain why nylon and polyethene can differ in interchain attraction and relate this to a material property. State one comparison limitation.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Nylon contains amide N–H donors and carbonyl acceptors. [1]", "It can hydrogen bond between chains, while polyethene mainly has dispersion interactions. [1]", "Stronger interchain attraction can increase resistance to separation or deformation under matched conditions. [1]", "Chain length, packing, crystallinity, cross-links and processing also affect properties. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-condensation-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Identify the linkage and monomer functional groups required for a polyester made from a diol and dicarboxylic acid. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The repeat contains ester –C(=O)–O– linkages.", "[2] The diol supplies two hydroxyl groups and the diacid two carboxyl groups.", "[2] Suitable acid–alcohol condensation joins units with water release; preserve carbonyl and oxygen connectivity."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-condensation-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Four difunctional monomer molecules form one simple linear chain by three condensation links, each releasing one water. Find water amount per mole of such chains and explain the chain-end issue. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Four connected units in a simple linear chain have three links.", "[2] Three waters form per chain, so 3 mol H₂O per mole of these chains.", "[2] End groups remain; finite-chain counting is not the same as assigning one link to each monomer."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which linkage identifies a common polyamide?", "o": ["–C(=O)–NH–.", "–O–O–.", "A carbonyl-free –C–NH– presented as identical.", "Only –CH₂–CH₂–."], "a": 0, "why": "The amide linkage includes the carbonyl carbon bonded to nitrogen."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-amino", "title": "Amino acids, zwitterions and peptide bonds", "scope": "Amino acids have linked acid/base functionality · A peptide bond is an amide linkage · Sequence and side chains carry information", "parent": "che12-polymers-proteins", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Amino acids, zwitterions and peptide bonds</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Amino acids have linked acid/base functionality</li><li>A peptide bond is an amide linkage</li><li>Sequence and side chains carry information</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "General structure of an α-amino acid", "kind": "learn", "text": "An α-amino acid has amino and carboxyl groups attached to the same central α carbon, commonly represented H₂N–CH(R)–COOH. R is a side chain determining identity and influencing properties. Glycine has R = H, while alanine has R = CH₃. The general diagram is a chemical structure, not a complete three-dimensional protein model. Many amino acids have ionisable or polar side chains that affect behaviour, so use the SCSA amino-acid data pages when given specific examples. Count functional groups and identify where peptide links form rather than assuming side chains are part of every backbone bond.", "diagram": "amino-acid"}, {"title": "Zwitterions and pH-dependent charge", "kind": "learn", "text": "In a common aqueous form, the amino group is protonated and the carboxyl group deprotonated, giving ⁺H₃N–CH(R)–COO⁻. This zwitterion contains both positive and negative formal charges with zero net charge for a simple non-ionising side chain. It is not an uncharged structure with no charged sites. At low pH, a carboxylate can gain H⁺, tending toward a net positive form. At high pH, ammonium can lose H⁺, tending toward a net negative form. Side-chain ionisation can alter net charge. Always specify the amino acid and pH context rather than claiming every amino acid has net zero charge in every solution.", "diagram": "zwitterion"}, {"title": "Peptide bond formation", "kind": "learn", "text": "Two α-amino acids join through condensation between a carboxyl group and an amino group, forming a –C(=O)NH– peptide link and water. A dipeptide retains an amino end and carboxyl end and contains two side chains. The peptide bond is an amide bond. H₂NCH(R₁)COOH + H₂NCH(R₂)COOH → H₂NCH(R₁)CONHCH(R₂)COOH + H₂O is a simplified neutral-form equation. Joining the same two different amino acids in reverse sequence can give a different dipeptide. Identify the linkage atoms explicitly and distinguish it from an intermolecular hydrogen bond.", "diagram": "peptide"}, {"title": "Zwitterions and melting behaviour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Amino acids commonly exist with opposite charged sites in a solid zwitterionic arrangement. Strong electrostatic interactions between sites can contribute to relatively high melting/decomposition behaviour compared with similarly sized simple molecular substances. A net-zero zwitterion still has full formal charges, so “non-polar because zero total charge” is an incorrect inference. Hydrogen bonding can also contribute. In a comparison, identify the relevant solid form and interactions and acknowledge that some amino acids decompose before simple melting. Compare provided data rather than assuming every amino acid has one universal melting point.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amino acids have linked acid/base functionality", "kind": "learn", "text": "The common α-amino-acid structure contains an amino group and carboxyl group associated with the same central carbon framework, plus a side chain. In suitable aqueous conditions proton transfer can give a zwitterion with NH₃⁺ and COO⁻ groups and overall neutral charge when other ionisable groups are absent. Overall neutrality does not mean no formal charges. pH changes can alter protonation and net charge.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "A peptide bond is an amide linkage", "kind": "learn", "text": "Condensation between one amino acid’s carboxyl group and another’s amino group creates –C(=O)–NH– and releases water in the simplified equation. Retain the carbonyl oxygen and identify which atoms form the water. A chain of n amino-acid residues linked linearly has n − 1 peptide bonds in the simple unbranched model. Hydrolysis can reverse the linkage to recover amino-acid functionality.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Sequence and side chains carry information", "kind": "learn", "text": "Peptide sequence specifies residue order, not merely the total number of each amino acid. Different order can alter folding and function. Side chains influence acid/base behaviour and interactions, so a diagram of a neutral generic amino acid does not settle every real residue’s charge at every pH. Show the requested species at the stated conditions, with charges visible, and distinguish a covalent peptide bond from noncovalent folding interactions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-peptide"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: A dipeptide structure", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: A dipeptide structure", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Zwitterion interpretation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Zwitterion interpretation", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "A dipeptide structure", "prompt": "Write a neutral-form condensed structure for glycine followed by alanine and identify the peptide link.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Glycine has R = H and alanine R = CH₃. [1]", "H₂NCH₂C(=O)NHCH(CH₃)COOH. [2: sequence/connectivity and retained terminal groups]", "The peptide link is –C(=O)NH–; one water forms on joining. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q3", "allocations": [1, 2, 1]}, {"title": "Zwitterion interpretation", "prompt": "Draw alanine’s simple zwitterion and state what happens to its main groups in strongly acidic solution.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["⁺H₃N–CH(CH₃)–COO⁻. [2: both correct charged sites and connectivity]", "It has zero net charge but charged internal groups in this form. [1]", "Acid can protonate COO⁻ to COOH, yielding a net-positive form with NH₃⁺ retained. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q4", "allocations": [2, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-amino-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Describe a zwitterion of a simple amino acid and explain why net zero charge does not mean the molecule is uncharged everywhere. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The amino group can be NH₃⁺ and the carboxyl group COO⁻.", "[2] Positive and negative formal charges coexist within the same species.", "[2] Their sum can be zero for the stated simple case, but pH and additional ionisable side chains can alter net charge."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-amino-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A linear peptide contains five amino-acid residues. Give its number of peptide bonds and waters released in the simplified condensation, then state the linkage. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Five residues have four connecting peptide bonds in a simple linear chain.", "[2] Four H₂O molecules are released per chain in that simplified condensation.", "[2] The peptide linkage is –C(=O)–NH–, with its carbonyl retained."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the covalent peptide linkage?", "o": ["–C(=O)–NH–.", "A hydrogen bond only.", "–O–O–.", "A linkage that removes every carbonyl oxygen."], "a": 0, "why": "A peptide bond is an amide bond between amino-acid-derived groups."}], "subtitle": "18 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-proteins", "title": "Protein structure, interactions and denaturation", "scope": "Distinguish levels of protein structure · Use the actual interaction and groups · Denaturation differs from complete hydrolysis", "parent": "che12-polymers-proteins", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Protein structure, interactions and denaturation</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Distinguish levels of protein structure</li><li>Use the actual interaction and groups</li><li>Denaturation differs from complete hydrolysis</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Primary protein structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence connected by peptide bonds. Sequence determines which side chains occur and where they are positioned along the polypeptide. Changing one residue can affect later folding or function, although consequences depend on its position and chemical character. A chain drawn as beads is useful for sequence but hides the actual amide backbone and side-chain chemistry. The N and C ends give the chain direction. Do not define primary structure as the first shape formed or as a collection of hydrogen bonds. It is the covalent order of the amino-acid residues.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Secondary structure: helix and sheet", "kind": "learn", "text": "Secondary structures include α-helices and β-pleated sheets stabilised by hydrogen bonding involving backbone amide N–H and carbonyl O groups. In an α-helix, interactions occur along different positions of the same chain in the characteristic helical pattern. β-sheet hydrogen bonds connect adjacent aligned strands, which may belong to separate chains or folded regions of one chain. Side chains project away from the backbone arrangements. A ribbon model simplifies many atoms to reveal recurring shape; it does not show all bonds. Hydrogen bonds stabilise secondary organisation, while covalent peptide bonds maintain the backbone itself.", "diagram": "protein-secondary"}, {"title": "Tertiary structure and side-chain interactions", "kind": "learn", "text": "Tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional folding of a polypeptide. Side-chain interactions can include hydrogen bonding, dipole–dipole and dispersion attractions, ionic interactions and covalent disulfide bridges between suitable cysteine residues. Hydrophobic effects favour clustering of non-polar regions away from water in many proteins. A disulfide bridge is stronger covalent linking and should not be labelled a hydrogen bond. The resulting shape influences access to binding or active sites. Protein structures are not static sculptures; molecular motion remains possible. Match each depicted interaction to the actual side-chain chemistry supplied.", "diagram": "protein-tertiary"}, {"title": "Denaturation, function and shared structural evidence", "kind": "learn", "text": "Heat or unsuitable pH can disrupt folding and interactions, changing a protein’s function. Ordinary denaturation need not break every peptide bond, so primary sequence may remain while secondary/tertiary structure changes. Enzyme activity depends on a suitable shape and chemical environment at the active site. The Protein Data Bank provides shared structural evidence that scientists contribute to and use internationally. Experimental models have resolution and uncertainty limits; a ribbon or space-filling display emphasises different features. Explain how structure relates to function and which evidence supports a claim, rather than assuming every loss of activity is permanent chemical destruction of the entire molecule.", "diagram": "denaturation"}, {"title": "Distinguish levels of protein structure", "kind": "learn", "text": "Primary structure is amino-acid sequence connected by peptide bonds. Secondary structure includes local regular patterns such as α-helices and β-sheets supported by backbone hydrogen bonding. Tertiary structure is an overall three-dimensional arrangement of a polypeptide, influenced by side-chain interactions and environment. Quaternary structure involves multiple subunits where present. Not every protein must have the same number of subunits or one identical folded pattern.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Use the actual interaction and groups", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hydrogen bonding, ionic attractions, dispersion/hydrophobic associations and covalent disulfide links can contribute in relevant structures. A disulfide bridge is not a hydrogen bond, and a peptide bond is not the same as a temporary ionic attraction. Changes in pH can alter side-chain charge and interactions; heating can disrupt the balance sustaining a folded form. Name the affected groups or interaction in the specified example.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Denaturation differs from complete hydrolysis", "kind": "learn", "text": "Denaturation changes higher-order structure and can reduce function without necessarily breaking the peptide backbone into individual amino acids. Hydrolysis breaks covalent peptide links. Some unfolding can be reversible, while aggregation or other changes can prevent recovery. Explain function through the relevant binding or active-site shape rather than saying the protein dies. A model supports structure–function reasoning but does not prove every protein has the same response.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Protein levels and denaturation", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Interpreting a protein model", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Protein levels and denaturation", "prompt": "Distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary structure and explain why denaturation need not destroy primary structure.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Primary is amino-acid sequence joined by peptide bonds. [1]", "Secondary is recurring helix/sheet organisation supported by backbone hydrogen bonds. [1]", "Tertiary is overall three-dimensional folding involving side-chain interactions. [1]", "Heat/pH can disrupt those stabilising interactions and folding. [1]", "Peptide bonds may remain intact, retaining the primary sequence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Interpreting a protein model", "prompt": "A ribbon model shows a helix, sheet and a link between two cysteine side chains. Identify the structural levels and interactions shown, then explain one limitation of using this image to infer function.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Helix and sheet are secondary structures. [1]", "Backbone N–H/carbonyl hydrogen bonds stabilise them. [1]", "The overall folded arrangement is tertiary structure. [1]", "The cysteine link can be a covalent disulfide bridge. [1]", "Primary structure is the amino-acid sequence, not fully visible from a simple ribbon alone. [1]", "A ribbon omits atom details, dynamics and experimental uncertainty; function needs additional structural/chemical evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-polymers-proteins-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-proteins-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Distinguish denaturation from peptide hydrolysis and explain one possible consequence for an enzyme. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Denaturation alters higher-order folding without necessarily cleaving peptide bonds.", "[2] Hydrolysis breaks the covalent peptide backbone into smaller peptides/amino-acid functionality.", "[2] Altered active-site arrangement can impair substrate binding or catalysis; this links shape change to function."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-polymers-proteins-proteins-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain how pH change can affect tertiary structure, and distinguish a disulfide link from a hydrogen bond. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] pH can alter ionisation of relevant side chains and change ionic interactions.", "[2] Changed interactions can disturb the folded arrangement.", "[2] A disulfide link is covalent S–S, while a hydrogen bond is a different noncovalent interaction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Does denaturation necessarily break every peptide bond?", "o": ["No: higher-order structure can change while the backbone remains.", "Yes: it always produces only free amino acids.", "It cannot affect function.", "It means sequence and folding are the same concept."], "a": 0, "why": "Denaturation and covalent hydrolysis are distinct processes."}, {"q": "Addition polymerisation normally", "o": ["Eliminates water from each alkene", "Consumes C=C to link the backbone", "Leaves every C=C unchanged", "Requires amino acids"], "a": 1, "why": "The alkene double bond becomes backbone single connections."}, {"q": "A peptide bond is a type of", "o": ["Ester", "Amide", "Ionic pair only", "Metallic bond"], "a": 1, "why": "Its linkage is –C(=O)NH–."}, {"q": "A zwitterion can have", "o": ["No charges anywhere", "Both positive and negative groups", "Only positive groups", "No functional groups"], "a": 1, "why": "A simple amino-acid zwitterion has opposite formal charges and zero net charge."}, {"q": "Protein primary structure is", "o": ["Overall folding", "Amino-acid sequence", "Only an α-helix", "Every hydrogen bond"], "a": 1, "why": "Sequence is the covalent order of residues."}, {"q": "A disulfide bridge is", "o": ["A covalent interaction", "A hydrogen bond", "A free electron", "An ester link"], "a": 0, "why": "It is a covalent S–S link between suitable residues."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-synthesis-yield", "title": "Synthesis design, limiting amounts and percentage yield", "scope": "Choose a route using both chemistry and constraints · Calculate the theoretical maximum from the limiting amount · Multi-stage yields compound", "parent": "che12-synthesis", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Synthesis design, limiting amounts and percentage yield</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Choose a route using both chemistry and constraints</li><li>Calculate the theoretical maximum from the limiting amount</li><li>Multi-stage yields compound</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Synthesis pathways and design choices", "kind": "learn", "text": "Chemical synthesis constructs a desired product through one or more reactions. A useful plan identifies starting materials, functional-group transformations, reagents, conditions and separation steps. Selecting a pathway involves yield, rate, selectivity, energy use, hazards, feedstock availability and waste. High percentage yield alone does not establish a sustainable process. A series of steps multiplies losses: two 80% yield stages give 64% overall yield before other losses. Relate chosen conditions to the chemistry rather than treating an industrial flowsheet as a set of boxes to memorise. A reaction equation describes chemical change but not every engineering operation.", "diagram": "synthesis-path"}, {"title": "Theoretical amount and limiting reagent", "kind": "learn", "text": "For each reactant, calculate moles and divide by its balanced coefficient. The smaller value limits reaction extent. Theoretical product = limiting extent × product coefficient. If some reactant is impure, calculate the reactive portion first. If a solution is used, obtain moles from concentration and volume. A yield calculation using the non-limiting reagent gives an incorrect denominator even when arithmetic is accurate. State which reagent limits and how much excess remains when asked. This method applies equally to gas synthesis, esterification and supplied biodiesel equations, provided the reaction and assumptions are clear.", "diagram": "limiting"}, {"title": "Percentage yield and practical losses", "kind": "learn", "text": "Percentage yield = actual product amount / theoretical product amount × 100, using comparable units for the same substance. Low yield can result from incomplete conversion, equilibrium limitation, competing reactions or losses during isolation. Distinguish reaction conversion from isolated yield: complete reactant consumption can still give low target-product yield if side products form. An apparent yield above 100% may reflect retained solvent, water or impurity. Report purity assumptions. Improving separation can increase isolated yield without changing Kc, whereas removing a product during a reversible reaction can also affect composition. Diagnose a cause before proposing an improvement.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Rate and yield are distinct goals", "kind": "learn", "text": "A fast reaction need not have a high equilibrium product fraction, and a product-favoured reaction can be too slow for production. Temperature often improves rate but may reduce equilibrium yield for an exothermic forward reaction. Catalysts increase rate without directly changing equilibrium composition. Pressure, reagent ratios and product removal can change equilibrium extent under suitable conditions. A practical optimum balances throughput, equipment costs and energy with yield and safety. When a question asks “explain the chosen conditions,” address both kinetic and equilibrium consequences and name the actual reaction rather than giving generic high-temperature statements.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Choose a route using both chemistry and constraints", "kind": "learn", "text": "A synthesis route must connect starting material to the intended product through feasible reactions and conditions. Evaluate selectivity, equilibrium, rate, hazards, purification and resource use. A short route is not automatically best if it has low yield or difficult separation. Draw each functional-group change explicitly so an intermediate’s actual structure, not only its name, justifies the next stage.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Calculate the theoretical maximum from the limiting amount", "kind": "learn", "text": "Convert reactant quantities to moles, divide by coefficients to compare reaction extents and identify the limiting reagent. Use that extent to find theoretical product amount. Actual isolated yield can be lower because of equilibrium, side reactions and handling losses. Percentage yield is actual/theoretical × 100 using the same quantity basis; it does not tell which loss mechanism occurred.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Multi-stage yields compound", "kind": "learn", "text": "For sequential stages whose stated yields apply to the amount entering each stage, multiply yield fractions rather than add percentages. Two stages of 80% yield give 64% overall, not 160% or automatically 80%. Intermediate purification and recycling can complicate real production, so state the simplified basis. Yield alone is not atom economy or a complete environmental score; wasted mass and energy require other evidence.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Read and apply the chemical model", "kind": "learn", "text": "Read the labelled species and quantities, then connect each part of the model to the explanation in this lesson. Check charge, atom connectivity or units where appropriate. Use the model to justify a prediction or calculation in the written tasks, and state the supplied model limit rather than interpreting a schematic as a literal scale drawing.", "diagram": "focused-stages"}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Two-stage synthesis", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Ammonia limiting reagent and yield", "prompt": "For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, start with 0.500 mol N₂ and 1.20 mol H₂. Actual NH₃ collected is 0.600 mol. Find limiting reagent, theoretical amount and yield.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n/coefficient: N₂ = 0.500; H₂ = 1.20/3 = 0.400. [1]", "H₂ limits. [1]", "Theoretical NH₃ = 2 × 0.400 = 0.800 mol. [1]", "Yield = 0.600/0.800 × 100. [1]", "Yield = 75.0%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q1", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Two-stage synthesis", "prompt": "An initial feedstock could theoretically produce 0.500 mol intermediate. Stage 1 has 80.0% yield, then a 1:1 product step has 75.0% yield. Find final product amount and overall yield.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Intermediate actually available = 0.500 × 0.800 = 0.400 mol. [1]", "Final product = 0.400 × 0.750 = 0.300 mol. [1]", "Overall fraction = 0.800 × 0.750 = 0.600. [1]", "Overall yield = 60.0%, rather than adding the percentages. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q5", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-yield-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "A two-stage synthesis has 80.0% yield in each stage. Calculate overall yield and final product amount from a theoretical 0.100 mol basis with 1:1 stage ratios. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Overall fraction = 0.800 × 0.800 = 0.640.", "[2] Overall percentage yield = 64.0%.", "[2] Final amount = 0.100 × 0.640 = 0.0640 mol on the stated basis."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-yield-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A theoretical product mass is 12.0 g, but 9.00 g is isolated. Calculate yield and give two chemically distinct reasons it could be below 100%. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Yield = (9.00/12.0) × 100 = 75.0%.", "[2] Equilibrium limitation or competing reactions can reduce product formation.", "[2] Transfer/purification losses can reduce isolated product even when formed; yield alone does not identify the cause."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "How are successive stage yield fractions combined in a simple route?", "o": ["Multiply them.", "Add them without limit.", "Use only the largest.", "Subtract them from the reaction coefficients."], "a": 0, "why": "Each stage acts on the amount reaching that stage."}], "subtitle": "17 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-synthesis-industry", "title": "Haber and Contact processes: justified conditions", "scope": "Conditions balance equilibrium with useful rate · The Contact process has another equilibrium example · Recycling and removal improve practical conversion", "parent": "che12-synthesis", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Haber and Contact processes: justified conditions</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Conditions balance equilibrium with useful rate</li><li>The Contact process has another equilibrium example</li><li>Recycling and removal improve practical conversion</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Haber process: the chemical equilibrium", "kind": "learn", "text": "N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) is exothermic. Higher pressure favours ammonia because the product side has fewer gas moles. Lower temperature favours ammonia equilibrium fraction, while higher temperature improves rate. An iron-based catalyst supplies a more accessible pathway. Industrial conditions commonly use an elevated-temperature compromise and high pressure, with actual values depending on plant design. Treat approximate example conditions as illustrative rather than a universal plant specification. Condensing ammonia removes product and unreacted gases can be recycled, increasing overall utilisation beyond a single-pass equilibrium conversion.", "diagram": "haber"}, {"title": "Explaining Haber compromises", "kind": "learn", "text": "Very low temperature can give favourable equilibrium composition but unacceptable rate. Very high pressure can improve equilibrium yield but requires stronger equipment and more compression energy. A catalyst allows viable rates under a chosen compromise but does not move the equilibrium by itself. Product separation and recycling reduce waste and improve feedstock use. A complete exam evaluation links each condition to a separate chemical and practical reason, then gives a reasoned recommendation. Do not describe recycling as increasing Kc. Kc depends on temperature; the flowsheet changes the material handled and allows repeated passes. Hydrogen sourcing also matters to life-cycle emissions.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Contact process: the equilibrium stage", "kind": "learn", "text": "The Contact process includes sulfur oxidation to SO₂ and catalytic conversion 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), which is exothermic. Lower temperature favours SO₃, while a moderate elevated temperature gives a useful rate with a vanadium(V)-oxide-based catalyst. Higher pressure favours fewer gas moles but practical gains and costs must be assessed; many implementations do not use the extreme pressure typical of Haber. Specific numerical conditions vary. Explain reaction stoichiometry and enthalpy before discussing the compromise. A catalyst changes the rate, not equilibrium yield at fixed conditions.", "diagram": "contact"}, {"title": "Sulfuric acid production and absorption", "kind": "learn", "text": "SO₃ is commonly absorbed into concentrated sulfuric acid to form oleum, which is then carefully reacted with water to obtain the desired acid concentration. Direct SO₃ addition to water is difficult to control and can generate an acid mist rather than a readily collected product. A simplified model is SO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂S₂O₇, followed by H₂S₂O₇ + H₂O → 2H₂SO₄. These equations show chemical relationships, not a student procedure. Compare feedstock purification, catalyst protection, absorption, energy recovery and emission control when evaluating the full process. The equilibrium stage is only one part of industrial synthesis.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Conditions balance equilibrium with useful rate", "kind": "learn", "text": "For the exothermic Haber reaction N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, lower temperature favours equilibrium ammonia proportion but can make rate impractically low. Higher pressure favours the side with fewer gaseous coefficient units, while cost and engineering constraints limit pressure. A catalyst enables a useful rate at a compromise temperature without changing K at that temperature. Explain each condition through its own mechanism.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "The Contact process has another equilibrium example", "kind": "learn", "text": "The conversion 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ is exothermic in the standard industrial description. Temperature, catalyst and gas composition influence useful production. Do not assume a diagram of the whole plant means every later absorption step is the same equilibrium reaction. Identify the actual stage asked about and distinguish a gas-conversion equilibrium from subsequent handling and acid production.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Recycling and removal improve practical conversion", "kind": "learn", "text": "Separating product and recycling unreacted feed can increase overall utilisation without changing the fixed-temperature equilibrium constant. A single-pass equilibrium yield differs from total plant conversion after repeated passes. Industrial claims require energy, material and safety boundaries: a high-yield process can still use large energy inputs. Use supplied operating data and practical constraints rather than proposing infinitely high pressure or zero temperature.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Haber conditions", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Haber conditions", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Industrial recommendation", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 2}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 3}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Haber conditions", "prompt": "Explain why a Haber plant uses a temperature compromise, elevated pressure, a catalyst and recycling.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Lower temperature favours exothermic ammonia equilibrium yield. [1]", "Higher temperature increases rate, so a compromise is chosen. [1]", "Higher pressure favours the two-gas-mole product side over four reactant moles. [1]", "Compression and strong equipment cost energy/money. [1]", "A catalyst improves rate without changing Kc at fixed temperature. [1]", "Removing ammonia and recycling reactants improves overall utilisation. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q2", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"title": "Industrial recommendation", "prompt": "Compare base-catalysed and lipase-catalysed biodiesel routes for a feedstock with variable water/free-fatty-acid content. Propose evidence needed for a justified selection and distinguish yield from sustainability.", "marks": 6, "steps": ["Base routes can be fast/economical but water/free acids can promote difficulties including soap formation. [1]", "A lipase route may offer milder conditions or different feedstock tolerance. [1]", "Compare measured conversion/isolated yield for the actual feedstock. [1]", "Compare catalyst cost, recovery/reuse, lifetime and rate. [1]", "Assess separation, energy, waste and feedstock impacts. [1]", "High yield alone does not establish the most sustainable route; select based on the combined evidence. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q7", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-industry-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Justify a compromise temperature, increased pressure and catalyst in the Haber process. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Lower temperature favours exothermic equilibrium yield but reduces rate, so a compromise supports useful production.", "[2] Increased pressure favours NH₃ because four gaseous coefficient units become two, subject to cost/safety limits.", "[2] A catalyst increases approach rate without changing fixed-temperature K or equilibrium yield."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-industry-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Explain why ammonia removal and reactant recycling can improve overall production without changing K at fixed temperature. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Removing product alters current composition and can support further net product formation.", "[2] Recycling gives unreacted feed additional opportunities to react.", "[2] These operating changes do not alter the reaction’s fixed-temperature equilibrium constant; overall conversion differs from single-pass yield."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why not simply choose the lowest possible temperature for Haber production?", "o": ["Equilibrium benefit can be offset by an impractically slow rate.", "Temperature has no effect on rate.", "A catalyst eliminates all engineering constraints.", "Lowest temperature always gives maximum output per time."], "a": 0, "why": "Production must balance thermodynamics and kinetics."}], "subtitle": "16 screens · 4 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-synthesis-ethanol", "title": "Ethanol pathways and linking synthesis stages", "scope": "Hydration and fermentation use different starting materials · Keep the balanced fermentation equation · Link ethanol to further synthesis accurately", "parent": "che12-synthesis", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Ethanol pathways and linking synthesis stages</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Hydration and fermentation use different starting materials</li><li>Keep the balanced fermentation equation</li><li>Link ethanol to further synthesis accurately</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Ethanol by ethene hydration", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethene can react with steam: CH₂=CH₂(g) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CH₃CH₂OH(g). Acid catalysis and elevated temperature/pressure support production, with recovery and recycling of unreacted feedstock. The forward reaction is exothermic and reduces gas mole count in the written model, so equilibrium favours lower temperature and higher pressure while rate needs a compromise. The process can be continuous and gives a relatively defined chemical feedstock path. Evaluate the source of ethene, energy and separation requirements. A route based on fossil-derived ethene and one using bio-derived feedstock need not have identical environmental profiles.", "diagram": "ethanol-path"}, {"title": "Ethanol by fermentation", "kind": "learn", "text": "A simplified fermentation equation is C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂. Enzymes in microorganisms catalyse conversion under suitable relatively mild conditions, with oxygen availability and temperature controlled for the desired process. Ethanol accumulation and other conditions can limit biological performance. Product separation commonly requires distillation and energy input. Fermentation can use renewable feedstocks, but land use, fertiliser, water, transport and competing food uses also matter. Compare this with hydration through actual process features: biological specificity and mild conditions versus throughput, feedstock, conversion and purification. “Natural” is not itself a chemical proof of sustainability.", "diagram": "fermentation"}, {"title": "Linking ethanol to an ester product", "kind": "learn", "text": "A multi-step route can hydrate ethene to ethanol, then react ethanol with ethanoic acid to form ethyl ethanoate. State the two equations and conditions separately. The final esterification is reversible and may use excess reactant or suitable product removal to improve yield. If the first stage has 75% yield and the second 80%, the idealised overall yield relative to the initial stoichiometric potential is 0.75 × 0.80 = 60%, assuming compatible amount ratios. Keep track of which substance limits each stage. A common error applies the second percentage to the original feedstock instead of to the intermediate actually available.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hydration and fermentation use different starting materials", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hydration adds water across ethene’s double bond under suitable acid-catalysed conditions to form ethanol. Fermentation converts suitable sugars through biological pathways, with ethanol and carbon dioxide in the simplified glucose equation. Each route has different feedstock, conditions, rate and separation requirements. Do not present a catalyst as changing the balanced product ratio or assume both routes use the same equipment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Keep the balanced fermentation equation", "kind": "learn", "text": "C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂ conserves carbon, hydrogen and oxygen in the simplified overall account. Biological systems involve many steps and limits, so the equation gives theoretical stoichiometry rather than a guarantee of complete usable yield. Temperature, substrate availability and ethanol accumulation affect the process. Ethanol recovery from aqueous mixture requires separation and energy.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Link ethanol to further synthesis accurately", "kind": "learn", "text": "Ethanol can undergo oxidation, esterification or other specified transformations. A multi-step pathway needs conditions and correct structures at each arrow. Fermentation feedstock can be renewable, but land use, processing energy and separation still matter. Compare routes using supplied criteria and a clear boundary rather than asserting renewable means zero impact. Distinguish ethanol produced from ethanol purified to a useful concentration.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Fermentation yield", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Fermentation yield", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Fermentation yield", "prompt": "Calculate theoretical ethanol mass from 18.0 g glucose using C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, M(glucose) = 180 and M(ethanol) = 46.0 g mol⁻¹. Actual mass is 7.36 g. Find yield.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["n(glucose) = 18.0/180 = 0.100 mol. [1]", "n(ethanol) theoretical = 0.200 mol. [1]", "m(ethanol) theoretical = 0.200 × 46.0 = 9.20 g. [1]", "Yield = 7.36/9.20 × 100. [1]", "Yield = 80.0%. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q3", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-ethanol-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Use the simplified fermentation equation to find theoretical ethanol amount from 0.500 mol glucose and state one reason isolated amount can be lower. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.", "[2] n(ethanol) = 2 × 0.500 = 1.00 mol theoretically.", "[2] Incomplete conversion, biological limits or recovery loss can reduce isolated amount; the equation is a stoichiometric maximum."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-ethanol-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Compare ethene hydration and glucose fermentation as routes to ethanol, giving starting material, reaction type and one practical difference. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ethene hydration adds H₂O across C=C using appropriate acid-catalysed conditions.", "[2] Fermentation converts suitable sugar biologically and also forms CO₂ in the simplified equation.", "[2] Feedstock, conditions, rate or purification requirements differ; justify the selected practical distinction."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "What is the ethanol/glucose mole ratio in the simplified fermentation equation?", "o": ["2:1.", "1:2.", "1:1 for every carbohydrate.", "A ratio determined only by flask size."], "a": 0, "why": "One glucose gives two ethanol molecules in the balanced simplified equation."}], "subtitle": "14 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-synthesis-lipids", "title": "Triglycerides, biodiesel, enzymes and saponification", "scope": "Triglycerides are triesters · Distinguish biodiesel from saponification · Evaluate the whole production system", "parent": "che12-synthesis", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Triglycerides, biodiesel, enzymes and saponification</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Triglycerides are triesters</li><li>Distinguish biodiesel from saponification</li><li>Evaluate the whole production system</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Triglycerides and fatty acids", "kind": "learn", "text": "A triglyceride contains glycerol joined through three ester bonds to fatty-acid-derived chains. The chains may differ and may contain C=C bonds. A simplified formula represents the three acyl groups as RCO–, but R is not always identical in a real fat. An ester linkage is –C(=O)O–. The hydrocarbon tails contribute non-polar character, while ester groups contain polar bonds. Hydrolysis or transesterification changes the ester connections. Distinguish triglycerides from free fatty acids and from soaps, which are fatty-acid salts. A model should label glycerol-derived backbone, carbonyl groups and hydrocarbon chains.", "diagram": "triglyceride"}, {"title": "Biodiesel by transesterification", "kind": "learn", "text": "A triglyceride reacts with an alcohol, commonly methanol, to give three fatty-acid methyl esters and glycerol in the simplified stoichiometry. The methyl esters are biodiesel components. This is transesterification: the ester’s alcohol-derived part changes. Base catalysis can give a fast economical process but feedstock water and free fatty acids can cause difficulties including soap formation. Excess alcohol can favour conversion, and product separation/recovery matters. A base catalyst is not itself the source of every atom in the product. The environmental assessment depends on oil source, land use and processing, not only its biological origin.", "diagram": "biodiesel"}, {"title": "Lipase-catalysed biodiesel", "kind": "learn", "text": "Lipases are enzyme catalysts that can support biodiesel production under milder conditions and may tolerate some feedstocks differently from a base process. Their protein structure governs activity, so temperature, pH or solvent effects can alter performance. Cost, stability, reaction time, reuse and separation influence feasibility. An immobilised enzyme may be recovered and used repeatedly, but activity can decline. Compare a base and lipase route through specific evidence rather than declaring the enzyme automatically superior. A catalyst improves a pathway’s rate; final yield still depends on reaction conditions, equilibrium, side reactions and isolation.", "diagram": "enzyme"}, {"title": "Saponification: making soap", "kind": "learn", "text": "Base hydrolysis of a triglyceride produces glycerol and carboxylate salts. With sodium hydroxide, three ester links require three hydroxide amounts in the simplified equation: triglyceride + 3NaOH → glycerol + 3RCOO⁻Na⁺. These salts of long-chain fatty acids are soaps. Saponification differs from methanol transesterification that produces biodiesel esters. Identify which bond breaks and which products form. The carboxylate head is ionic, while the long hydrocarbon tail is non-polar. Write a general structure with an explained R group rather than inventing one exact fatty acid for every oil.", "diagram": "saponification"}, {"title": "Specific links in biodiesel and soap products", "kind": "learn", "text": "For an acyl chain R, a methyl ester is R–C(=O)OCH₃, while a soap anion is R–C(=O)O⁻ with a counterion such as Na⁺. The methyl ester is a neutral molecular product; the carboxylate salt contains ionic species. In transesterification, alcohol-derived connections exchange and glycerol is released. In base hydrolysis, carboxylate formation changes the acid-derived end into its salt. A diagram of a triglyceride must connect its three glycerol carbons by C–C bonds, not show three separate ester molecules. Real feedstocks contain a mixture of chains, so one R model represents a class of products.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Triglycerides are triesters", "kind": "learn", "text": "A triglyceride contains glycerol linked through three ester groups to fatty-acid-derived chains. Hydrolysis opens these ester links and restores the relevant alcohol and acid functionality under specified conditions. Different chain lengths and unsaturation change physical properties. A lipid model should show the carbonyls and ester oxygens correctly rather than using three generic lines that erase functional chemistry.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Distinguish biodiesel from saponification", "kind": "learn", "text": "Transesterification with a suitable alcohol produces fatty-acid alkyl esters, used as biodiesel, and glycerol in the simplified triglyceride reaction. Alkaline saponification produces glycerol and carboxylate salts, which can function as soaps. These are different product classes. Name the reagent and resulting head group rather than calling every lipid reaction biodiesel formation. Enzyme catalysis can offer selectivity under suitable conditions but still requires practical assessment.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Evaluate the whole production system", "kind": "learn", "text": "Biodiesel feedstock, reaction conditions, purification and by-products affect suitability and impact. Renewable feed does not remove energy inputs or land-use concerns, while waste-oil use raises its own quality and processing issues. Unsaturation can affect oxidation stability and cold-flow behaviour. Compare using the question’s evidence and avoid claiming all fatty-acid esters have identical properties. Stoichiometry gives a product ceiling, not a complete life-cycle conclusion.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Practice: Biodiesel versus soap", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Biodiesel versus soap", "prompt": "Compare products from triglyceride reaction with methanol and from base hydrolysis. Explain one trade-off of lipase rather than base catalysis for biodiesel.", "marks": 5, "steps": ["Methanol transesterification gives fatty-acid methyl esters and glycerol. [1]", "Base hydrolysis gives fatty-acid carboxylate salts, soaps, and glycerol. [1]", "The processes change ester connections differently. [1]", "Lipase can operate under milder conditions or offer different feedstock tolerance. [1]", "Its cost, stability, reuse and reaction rate must be assessed rather than assuming a universal benefit. [1]"], "practice": true, "id": "che12-synthesis-q6", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-lipids-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Compare alkaline saponification and transesterification of a triglyceride, identifying both product classes. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Saponification gives glycerol and fatty-acid carboxylate salts under alkaline conditions.", "[2] Suitable alcohol transesterification gives glycerol and fatty-acid alkyl esters.", "[2] The salts can be soaps; the alkyl esters are the biodiesel product class, so the pathways are not interchangeable."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-lipids-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "A simplified triglyceride transesterification forms three fatty-acid methyl esters per triglyceride. Calculate ester amount from 0.200 mol triglyceride with excess methanol and explain a yield limitation. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] The stated ratio is three ester molecules per triglyceride.", "[2] Theoretical n(esters) = 3 × 0.200 = 0.600 mol.", "[2] Incomplete conversion, equilibrium or purification losses can lower isolated ester amount."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Which product class forms in alkaline saponification?", "o": ["Fatty-acid carboxylate salts.", "Only neutral methyl esters.", "Ethene from every chain.", "No glycerol-related product."], "a": 0, "why": "Base hydrolysis gives carboxylate salts and glycerol."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}, {"year": "12", "unit": "Unit 4 – Organic chemistry and synthesis", "icon": "🧪", "id": "che12-synthesis-surfactants", "title": "Soap, detergents, hard water and green chemistry", "scope": "Amphiphilic structure supports cleaning · Hard-water ions change soap behaviour · Environmental evaluation needs several criteria", "parent": "che12-synthesis", "screens": [{"title": "Focus and learning goals", "kind": "reference", "html": "<h3>Soap, detergents, hard water and green chemistry</h3><p>Learn the concepts, apply them to unfamiliar examples and justify your conclusions with evidence.</p><ul><li>Amphiphilic structure supports cleaning</li><li>Hard-water ions change soap behaviour</li><li>Environmental evaluation needs several criteria</li></ul><p>Work through the explained models, attempt the written questions before revealing the answers, then use the concept check to find ideas to revisit.</p>"}, {"title": "Soap, detergent and micelles", "kind": "learn", "text": "A soap molecule has a non-polar hydrocarbon tail and charged carboxylate head. In water, suitable aggregates can place tails inward around oil and heads outward interacting with water. Agitation helps disperse oily material so it can be carried away. Anionic detergents derived from dodecylbenzene have a long non-polar region and a sulfonate head. A micelle drawing represents an aggregate arrangement, not a single molecule changing into water. Cleaning involves interfaces, emulsification and transport as well as molecular attraction. Use hydrophobic/hydrophilic descriptions with the actual structural groups identified.", "diagram": "micelle"}, {"title": "Hard water and calcium salts", "kind": "learn", "text": "Hard water contains appreciable calcium and/or magnesium ions. Calcium can form poorly soluble salts of soap carboxylates: Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s). This consumes useful soap and forms scum, reducing cleaning effectiveness. Many corresponding sulfonate detergent calcium salts are more soluble, so the detergent can perform better in hard water. Use the solubility difference rather than claiming detergents contain no charged groups. The extent depends on formulation and conditions. A lather observation is not a direct precise measure of every hardness ion without calibration and controlled sample amounts.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Green chemistry and justified decisions", "kind": "learn", "text": "A synthesis evaluation compares atom use, unwanted products, solvent/reagent hazards, energy, renewable feedstocks, purification and catalyst recovery. Atom economy, when given or used as extension, concerns the fraction of stoichiometric reactant mass retained in target product; percentage yield concerns actual product relative to theoretical product. They are distinct. An enzyme route may reduce temperature but increase catalyst cost or solvent demands. A high-yield route can still generate harmful waste. Conclude using the supplied evidence and acknowledge trade-offs. Recommendations should be specific to the process rather than broad claims that one category of chemicals is always green.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Forces in cleaning: a complete explanation", "kind": "learn", "text": "The non-polar soap tail experiences dispersion interactions with the oily material. The charged carboxylate head interacts with polar water through ion–dipole attraction. Suitable aggregates orient tails toward oil and heads toward water, helping disperse droplets so agitation and rinsing can carry them away. Tail-to-water “hydrogen bonding” is not the mechanism. The sulfonate head of an anionic detergent is also charged and water-interacting. Include a labelled diagram and name each region’s force when asked. Hard-water precipitation is a separate chemical consumption of soap that can reduce the amount available for this interfacial action.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Amphiphilic structure supports cleaning", "kind": "learn", "text": "A surfactant has a water-compatible head and a comparatively nonpolar hydrocarbon region. In suitable aggregates, hydrocarbon regions associate with nonpolar material while heads interact with water. A micelle is an assembly of many molecules, not a new covalent polymer. Explain the orientation and interactions rather than saying soap destroys grease molecules. Agitation and rinsing help disperse and remove material.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Hard-water ions change soap behaviour", "kind": "learn", "text": "Calcium and magnesium ions can form poorly soluble salts with soap carboxylate ions, reducing useful dissolved surfactant and producing scum. Some detergent head groups avoid the same precipitation problem under appropriate conditions, but detergent is not one universal structure. Use a balanced ionic example such as Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s). Distinguish a visible precipitate from an unsupported claim that all cleaning action disappears instantly.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Environmental evaluation needs several criteria", "kind": "learn", "text": "Biodegradability, aquatic effects, feedstock, waste, energy and concentration all matter. A substance described as natural is not automatically harmless at every dose or environment. Green-chemistry improvements should address a specific burden, such as reducing hazardous reagents, waste or energy, while acknowledging trade-offs. A strong exam response links molecular structure to cleaning/hard-water behaviour and uses evidence to assess the environmental claim.", "diagram": null}, {"title": "Check the key distinction", "kind": "check", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Hard-water mechanism", "kind": "question", "q": 0}, {"title": "Solution: Hard-water mechanism", "kind": "solution", "q": 0}, {"title": "Worked question: Apply and explain", "kind": "question", "q": 1}, {"title": "Solution: Apply and explain", "kind": "solution", "q": 1}, {"title": "Practice: Transfer and evaluate", "kind": "question", "q": 2}, {"title": "Official sources and further exam practice", "kind": "sources", "html": "<p>These are original ATAR-style learning questions and suggested marking points, not official SCSA questions or an endorsed textbook. Use the official paper and marking key for additional timed practice. Draw requested structures and graphs on paper and compare them carefully with the explanations.</p><p>Year 11 follows the syllabus effective from 2026. Year 12 follows the syllabus effective from 2024. Currency checked 8 October 2026. The data booklet linked here is the 2026 edition; use the booklet supplied for your examination.</p><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/syllabus-and-support-materials/science/chemistry\">SCSA Chemistry course page</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1232519/_Chemistry-ATAR-Year-11-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-2026.PDF\">Year 11 syllabus: teaching from 2026</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/1089527/Chemistry-ATAR-Year-12-Syllabus-for-teaching-from-January-2024_pdf.PDF\">Year 12 syllabus: teaching from 2024</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1230654/2026-CHE-Data-Book.PDF\">2026 Chemistry data booklet</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0008/1231010/2025-CHE-Examination.PDF\">2025 official examination</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/1231011/2025-CHE-Ratified-Marking-Key.PDF\">2025 official marking key</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/1242531/2025-ATAR-course-examination-report-CHE-public-version.PDF\">2025 examination report</a></li></ul><p>Repository support: Kennedy Year 11 atomic structure, bonding, organic chemistry, intermolecular forces/gases and reaction-rate PowerPoints; Safety Bay acids and bases PowerPoint. Older slide statements were checked and re-explained rather than copied wholesale.</p><p><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://senior-secondary.scsa.wa.edu.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/1264098/2026-CHE-Examination-Front-Cover.PDF\">2026 official examination front cover</a>: 10 minutes reading and 3 hours working. Multiple choice has 25 raw marks/25%; short answer 72 raw marks/35%; extended answer 94 raw marks/40%. Raw totals differ from section weights. Original lesson allocations are practice marks, not official examination questions.</p>"}], "questions": [{"title": "Hard-water mechanism", "prompt": "Explain why soap can form scum in hard water and why an anionic sulfonate detergent can remain more effective.", "marks": 4, "steps": ["Ca²⁺ or Mg²⁺ can react with soap carboxylate ions. [1]", "Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s). [1]", "Precipitation removes available soap and forms scum. [1]", "Many corresponding detergent calcium salts are more soluble, leaving more cleaning-active material. [1]"], "practice": false, "id": "che12-synthesis-q4", "allocations": [1, 1, 1, 1]}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-surfactants-worked", "title": "Apply and explain", "prompt": "Explain micelle orientation around nonpolar grease in water and distinguish the assembly from a covalent polymer. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Nonpolar tails associate with grease/nonpolar regions and water-compatible heads face the surrounding water.", "[2] This supports dispersion and removal through suitable interactions, not necessarily chemical destruction of grease.", "[2] A micelle is an aggregate of surfactant molecules, not a chain created by new covalent polymer bonds."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": false, "marks": 6}, {"id": "che12-synthesis-surfactants-practice", "title": "Transfer and evaluate", "prompt": "Write an ionic equation for calcium precipitating soap and assess the claim that natural soap must have no environmental impact. (6 marks)", "steps": ["[2] Ca²⁺ + 2RCOO⁻ → Ca(RCOO)₂(s), conserving atoms and charge.", "[2] Precipitation removes dissolved soap ions and can reduce effectiveness/produce scum.", "[2] Natural origin alone does not establish harmlessness; assess dose, biodegradation, aquatic effects and production impacts."], "allocations": [2, 2, 2], "practice": true, "marks": 6}], "bank": [{"q": "Why can hard water reduce soap effectiveness?", "o": ["Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺ can form poorly soluble soap salts.", "Hard water has no dissolved ions.", "Every tail becomes covalently bonded to water.", "Soap must become a gas."], "a": 0, "why": "Precipitation removes useful dissolved carboxylate surfactant."}, {"q": "Percentage yield compares", "o": ["Actual with theoretical product", "Catalyst mass with solvent volume", "Kc with pH", "Product with pressure"], "a": 0, "why": "Use amounts of the same product on a consistent basis."}, {"q": "Higher pressure favours ammonia because", "o": ["Products have fewer gas moles", "It lowers nuclear charge", "The catalyst disappears", "It must raise Kc at constant T"], "a": 0, "why": "N₂ + 3H₂ gives four gaseous amounts to two in NH₃."}, {"q": "Biodiesel transesterification with methanol gives", "o": ["Fatty-acid methyl esters", "Only sodium soaps", "Only hydrocarbons", "Ammonia"], "a": 0, "why": "The triglyceride ester groups exchange their alcohol-derived connections."}, {"q": "A soap’s hydrophilic region is", "o": ["Its long non-polar tail", "Its charged carboxylate head", "An absent oxygen atom", "A metallic core"], "a": 1, "why": "The ionic head interacts with water."}, {"q": "Two successive 80% yields give overall yield", "o": ["160%", "80%", "64%", "40%"], "a": 2, "why": "Multiply fractions: 0.80 × 0.80 = 0.64."}], "subtitle": "15 screens · 3 written tasks · explained models"}];
